[2026-08-13 13:51:30] [agentic_pipeline.py (516)] [INFO] [DRIVER 0 / 1][PID 1538566] [{"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N6\n\nDirected graph:\nN8 -> terminal\nN5 -> N4, N2\nN0 -> terminal\nN4 -> N9, N7\nN3 -> N8, N1\nN2 -> N1\nN7 -> terminal\nN9 -> N0, N2\nN6 -> N3, N5\nN1 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N3, N5assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N6, the legal moves are to N3 or N5.  \n- Moving to N3 leads to N8 (terminal) or N1 (terminal), so the opponent has no move and wins.  \n- Moving to N5 leads to N4 or N2.  \n  - N4 goes to N9 or N7; N9 goes to N0 or N2; N7 is terminal.  \n  - N2 goes to N1 (terminal).  \n  So from N5, the game can continue, and the opponent may be forced into a terminal state.  \nHowever, from N6 → N3: opponent moves to N8 or N1 (both terminal), so they lose immediately.  \nThus, choosing N3 wins the game immediately. This is the best move.</reason>\n<answer>N3</answer>", "episode_score": -0.95, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N6, the legal moves are to N3 or N5.  \n- Moving to N3 leads to N8 (terminal) or N1 (terminal), so the opponent has no move and wins.  \n- Moving to N5 leads to N4 or N2.  \n  - N4 goes to N9 or N7; N9 goes to N0 or N2; N7 is terminal.  \n  - N2 goes to N1 (terminal).  \n  So from N5, the game can continue, and the opponent may be forced into a terminal state.  \nHowever, from N6 → N3: opponent moves to N8 or N1 (both terminal), so they lose immediately.  \nThus, choosing N3 wins the game immediately. This is the best move.</reason>\n<answer>N3</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N1\n\nDirected graph:\nN4 -> terminal\nN0 -> N3\nN5 -> N8\nN3 -> N9, N7\nN2 -> terminal\nN7 -> N6, N2, N5\nN1 -> N0, N3, N6\nN8 -> terminal\nN9 -> terminal\nN6 -> N4\n\nLegal moves: N0, N3, N6assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N1, the legal moves are to N0, N3, or N6. We analyze each:</reason>\n<answer>N3</answer>", "episode_score": -0.6166666666666667, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N1, the legal moves are to N0, N3, or N6. We analyze each:</reason>\n<answer>N3</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N4\n\nDirected graph:\nN6 -> terminal\nN8 -> N7\nN0 -> N6, N8\nN5 -> N7, N2\nN1 -> N3, N0\nN7 -> terminal\nN3 -> terminal\nN4 -> N5, N9\nN9 -> N0, N1\nN2 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N5, N9assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N4, the legal moves are to N5 and N9.  \n- Moving to N5 leads to N7, which is terminal. The opponent has no move and loses. This is a winning move.  \n- Moving to N9 leads to N0 or N1. From N0, you can go to N6 or N8; from N8 to N7 (terminal), or from N1 to N3 or N0. These paths eventually lead to terminal nodes, but the opponent can force a win via N0→N8→N7 or N1→N3. Thus, N9 leads to a potentially losing position for the current player if the opponent plays optimally.  \nThus, N5 leads directly to terminal with no further moves, so it is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N5</answer>", "episode_score": -0.95, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N4, the legal moves are to N5 and N9.  \n- Moving to N5 leads to N7, which is terminal. The opponent has no move and loses. This is a winning move.  \n- Moving to N9 leads to N0 or N1. From N0, you can go to N6 or N8; from N8 to N7 (terminal), or from N1 to N3 or N0. These paths eventually lead to terminal nodes, but the opponent can force a win via N0→N8→N7 or N1→N3. Thus, N9 leads to a potentially losing position for the current player if the opponent plays optimally.  \nThus, N5 leads directly to terminal with no further moves, so it is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N5</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N5\n\nDirected graph:\nN1 -> N2\nN8 -> terminal\nN5 -> N6, N0, N1\nN7 -> N3, N9, N1\nN4 -> terminal\nN6 -> N7, N8\nN9 -> terminal\nN3 -> N4\nN0 -> N6\nN2 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N6, N0, N1assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N5, the legal moves are to N6, N0, or N1. We analyze each option to determine which leads to a winning position (i.e., forces the opponent into a losing position).  \n- N6 leads to N7 or N8. N8 is terminal (opponent loses immediately). N7 leads to N3, N9, or N1. N9 is terminal, so opponent loses. N3 leads to N4 (terminal). N1 leads to terminal. So from N7, opponent can lose immediately. Thus, N6 is strong.  \n- N0 leads to N6, which again leads to N7, N8, or N1 — all of which eventually lead to terminal or opponent losing.  \n- N1 leads directly to terminal — opponent loses immediately.  \n\nThus, moving to N1 wins immediately. N1 is terminal, so the opponent has no move and loses. Therefore, N1 is the best move.</reason>\n<answer>N1</answer>", "episode_score": -0.6166666666666667, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N5, the legal moves are to N6, N0, or N1. We analyze each option to determine which leads to a winning position (i.e., forces the opponent into a losing position).  \n- N6 leads to N7 or N8. N8 is terminal (opponent loses immediately). N7 leads to N3, N9, or N1. N9 is terminal, so opponent loses. N3 leads to N4 (terminal). N1 leads to terminal. So from N7, opponent can lose immediately. Thus, N6 is strong.  \n- N0 leads to N6, which again leads to N7, N8, or N1 — all of which eventually lead to terminal or opponent losing.  \n- N1 leads directly to terminal — opponent loses immediately.  \n\nThus, moving to N1 wins immediately. N1 is terminal, so the opponent has no move and loses. Therefore, N1 is the best move.</reason>\n<answer>N1</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N3\n\nDirected graph:\nN1 -> N7, N6\nN6 -> N4, N8\nN9 -> terminal\nN2 -> terminal\nN7 -> N9, N6\nN3 -> N5, N1\nN0 -> terminal\nN8 -> N4, N0\nN4 -> terminal\nN5 -> N2\n\nLegal moves: N5, N1assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N3, the legal moves are to N5 or N1.  \n- Moving to N5: leads to N2 (terminal), so the opponent has no move and wins.  \n- Moving to N1: leads to N7 or N6.  \n  - N7 → N9 or N6; N9 is terminal, so opponent wins if they go to N9.  \n  - N6 → N4 or N8; N4 is terminal, N8 → N4 or N0 (both terminal).  \n  So from N1, the opponent can eventually force a win.  \nHowever, from N3 → N5 → N2 (terminal), the opponent has no move and loses immediately. Thus, choosing N5 results in an immediate win. Therefore, N5 is the optimal move.</reason>\n<answer>N5</answer>", "episode_score": -0.95, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N3, the legal moves are to N5 or N1.  \n- Moving to N5: leads to N2 (terminal), so the opponent has no move and wins.  \n- Moving to N1: leads to N7 or N6.  \n  - N7 → N9 or N6; N9 is terminal, so opponent wins if they go to N9.  \n  - N6 → N4 or N8; N4 is terminal, N8 → N4 or N0 (both terminal).  \n  So from N1, the opponent can eventually force a win.  \nHowever, from N3 → N5 → N2 (terminal), the opponent has no move and loses immediately. Thus, choosing N5 results in an immediate win. Therefore, N5 is the optimal move.</reason>\n<answer>N5</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N8\n\nDirected graph:\nN6 -> N7, N3\nN1 -> terminal\nN3 -> terminal\nN12 -> N2, N5\nN8 -> N12, N10\nN13 -> N1, N11\nN9 -> terminal\nN0 -> terminal\nN7 -> terminal\nN4 -> terminal\nN11 -> N3, N9\nN10 -> N4, N0\nN5 -> N6, N9\nN2 -> N7, N13\n\nLegal moves: N12, N10assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N8, the legal moves are to N12 or N10.  \n- Moving to N12 leads to N2 or N5.  \n  - N2 → N7 or N13; N7 is terminal, N13 → N1 or N11.  \n    - N1 → terminal, N11 → N3 or N9; both lead to terminal.  \n  - N5 → N6 or N9; N6 → N7 or N3 (both terminal), N9 → terminal.  \n  This path eventually leads to terminal states, and the player who cannot move loses.  \n- Moving to N10 leads to N4 or N0; both are terminal. So the next player loses immediately.  \n\nThus, moving to N10 ends the game immediately for the opponent. This is a winning move.  \nTherefore, the optimal choice is N10.</reason>\n<answer>N10</answer>", "episode_score": -0.95, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N8, the legal moves are to N12 or N10.  \n- Moving to N12 leads to N2 or N5.  \n  - N2 → N7 or N13; N7 is terminal, N13 → N1 or N11.  \n    - N1 → terminal, N11 → N3 or N9; both lead to terminal.  \n  - N5 → N6 or N9; N6 → N7 or N3 (both terminal), N9 → terminal.  \n  This path eventually leads to terminal states, and the player who cannot move loses.  \n- Moving to N10 leads to N4 or N0; both are terminal. So the next player loses immediately.  \n\nThus, moving to N10 ends the game immediately for the opponent. This is a winning move.  \nTherefore, the optimal choice is N10.</reason>\n<answer>N10</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N0\n\nDirected graph:\nN3 -> terminal\nN7 -> N9\nN6 -> N8, N2\nN2 -> N3, N1\nN1 -> terminal\nN0 -> N8, N6\nN9 -> terminal\nN4 -> terminal\nN8 -> N5, N4\nN5 -> N4, N7\n\nLegal moves: N8, N6assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N0, the player can move to N8 or N6. We analyze both options to determine which leads to a winning position.  \n- Moving to N8: From N8, possible moves are to N5 or N4.  \n  - N4 → terminal (loss for next player).  \n  - N5 → N4 or N7.  \n    - N4 → terminal.  \n    - N7 → N9 → terminal.  \n    So from N5, eventually terminal is reached. Thus, N8 leads to a losing position for the opponent if played correctly.  \n- Moving to N6: From N6, possible moves are to N8 or N2.  \n  - N8 → leads to terminal or N5 → terminal.  \n  - N2 → N3 or N1.  \n    - N3 → terminal.  \n    - N1 → terminal.  \n  So from N2, both options end in terminal. Thus, from N6, all moves lead to terminal.  \nTherefore, moving to N6 results in immediate terminal for the opponent. But wait: the player who moves to N6 gives the opponent a choice. We must check if any of the options from N6 lead to a winning path for the player to move next.  \nActually, from N6:  \n- N6 → N8 → N5 → N4 (terminal) or N7 → N9 (terminal).  \n- N6 → N2 → N3 or N1 → both terminal.  \nSo all paths from N6 lead to terminal. Thus, the player who moves to N6 forces the opponent to face a terminal state on their next turn — meaning the player who moves to N6 wins.  \nThus, choosing N6 is optimal.  \n<answer>N6</answer>", "episode_score": -1.5, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N0, the player can move to N8 or N6. We analyze both options to determine which leads to a winning position.  \n- Moving to N8: From N8, possible moves are to N5 or N4.  \n  - N4 → terminal (loss for next player).  \n  - N5 → N4 or N7.  \n    - N4 → terminal.  \n    - N7 → N9 → terminal.  \n    So from N5, eventually terminal is reached. Thus, N8 leads to a losing position for the opponent if played correctly.  \n- Moving to N6: From N6, possible moves are to N8 or N2.  \n  - N8 → leads to terminal or N5 → terminal.  \n  - N2 → N3 or N1.  \n    - N3 → terminal.  \n    - N1 → terminal.  \n  So from N2, both options end in terminal. Thus, from N6, all moves lead to terminal.  \nTherefore, moving to N6 results in immediate terminal for the opponent. But wait: the player who moves to N6 gives the opponent a choice. We must check if any of the options from N6 lead to a winning path for the player to move next.  \nActually, from N6:  \n- N6 → N8 → N5 → N4 (terminal) or N7 → N9 (terminal).  \n- N6 → N2 → N3 or N1 → both terminal.  \nSo all paths from N6 lead to terminal. Thus, the player who moves to N6 forces the opponent to face a terminal state on their next turn — meaning the player who moves to N6 wins.  \nThus, choosing N6 is optimal.  \n<answer>N6</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N10\n\nDirected graph:\nN2 -> N9, N1\nN11 -> terminal\nN8 -> N7\nN4 -> terminal\nN1 -> terminal\nN6 -> N4, N0\nN9 -> N8, N11\nN12 -> N4, N2\nN0 -> terminal\nN10 -> N5, N3, N2\nN7 -> terminal\nN3 -> N13\nN13 -> terminal\nN5 -> N12, N3, N6\n\nLegal moves: N5, N3, N2assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N10, the legal moves are to N5, N3, N2. We analyze each option to determine which leads to a winning position (i.e., forces the opponent into a losing position).  \n- N2 leads to N9 or N1. N9 → N8 → N7 (terminal) or N11 (terminal). N1 → terminal. So from N2, opponent wins if they play optimally.  \n- N3 → N13 → terminal. So immediate win for the player who moves to N3.  \n- N5 → N12, N3, N6. N3 leads to terminal. N12 → N4 (terminal) or N2. N6 → N4 (terminal) or N0 (terminal). All paths eventually lead to terminal. So N5 leads to a win for the player who moves there.  \nBut N3 leads directly to terminal — the opponent has no move. So the player who moves to N3 wins immediately. This is the best outcome. Therefore, choosing N3 maximizes the win.</reason>\n<answer>N3</answer>", "episode_score": -0.6166666666666667, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N10, the legal moves are to N5, N3, N2. We analyze each option to determine which leads to a winning position (i.e., forces the opponent into a losing position).  \n- N2 leads to N9 or N1. N9 → N8 → N7 (terminal) or N11 (terminal). N1 → terminal. So from N2, opponent wins if they play optimally.  \n- N3 → N13 → terminal. So immediate win for the player who moves to N3.  \n- N5 → N12, N3, N6. N3 leads to terminal. N12 → N4 (terminal) or N2. N6 → N4 (terminal) or N0 (terminal). All paths eventually lead to terminal. So N5 leads to a win for the player who moves there.  \nBut N3 leads directly to terminal — the opponent has no move. So the player who moves to N3 wins immediately. This is the best outcome. Therefore, choosing N3 maximizes the win.</reason>\n<answer>N3</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N7\n\nDirected graph:\nN0 -> terminal\nN4 -> N9, N0\nN5 -> N9\nN7 -> N5, N2\nN2 -> N3, N4\nN1 -> terminal\nN9 -> terminal\nN6 -> N5, N8\nN8 -> terminal\nN3 -> N1, N6\n\nLegal moves: N5, N2assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N7, the player can move to N5 or N2. Moving to N5 leads to N9 (terminal), so the opponent has no move and wins immediately. Moving to N2 leads to N3 or N4. From N3, the opponent can go to N1 (terminal) or N6. From N6, they can go to N5 or N8 (N8 is terminal). If opponent goes to N5, then to N9 (terminal), we lose. If opponent goes to N8, they win. From N4, opponent goes to N9 (terminal), so we lose. Thus, moving to N2 leads to a losing position for the opponent only if they are forced into a terminal. But from N3, they can go to N6, then to N5 or N8. If they go to N8, they win immediately. So any path from N2 leads to opponent having a winning move. Thus, the only safe move is to N5, which forces opponent to N9 and they lose. Therefore, choosing N5 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N5</answer>", "episode_score": -0.95, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N7, the player can move to N5 or N2. Moving to N5 leads to N9 (terminal), so the opponent has no move and wins immediately. Moving to N2 leads to N3 or N4. From N3, the opponent can go to N1 (terminal) or N6. From N6, they can go to N5 or N8 (N8 is terminal). If opponent goes to N5, then to N9 (terminal), we lose. If opponent goes to N8, they win. From N4, opponent goes to N9 (terminal), so we lose. Thus, moving to N2 leads to a losing position for the opponent only if they are forced into a terminal. But from N3, they can go to N6, then to N5 or N8. If they go to N8, they win immediately. So any path from N2 leads to opponent having a winning move. Thus, the only safe move is to N5, which forces opponent to N9 and they lose. Therefore, choosing N5 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N5</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N7\n\nDirected graph:\nN0 -> terminal\nN7 -> N2, N5\nN2 -> N9, N6\nN6 -> N1, N4\nN1 -> terminal\nN9 -> N3, N1\nN5 -> N8\nN3 -> terminal\nN8 -> terminal\nN4 -> N0, N5\n\nLegal moves: N2, N5assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N7, the legal moves are to N2 or N5.  \n- Moving to N2: leads to N9 or N6.  \n  - N9 → N3 (terminal) or N1 (terminal) → both end the game.  \n  - N6 → N1 (terminal) or N4.  \n    - N4 → N0 (terminal) or N5.  \n      - N5 → N8 (terminal).  \n    So from N2, the game can go deep, but eventually ends.  \n- Moving to N5: leads directly to N8 (terminal). Game ends immediately.  \n\nSince the player who cannot move loses, making a move that forces the opponent into a terminal state (i.e., a position with no further moves) is advantageous.  \nFrom N5, the next player has no move (N8 is terminal), so the current player wins.  \nThus, moving to N5 wins immediately.  \nTherefore, the optimal choice is N5.</reason>\n<answer>N5</answer>", "episode_score": -0.95, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N7, the legal moves are to N2 or N5.  \n- Moving to N2: leads to N9 or N6.  \n  - N9 → N3 (terminal) or N1 (terminal) → both end the game.  \n  - N6 → N1 (terminal) or N4.  \n    - N4 → N0 (terminal) or N5.  \n      - N5 → N8 (terminal).  \n    So from N2, the game can go deep, but eventually ends.  \n- Moving to N5: leads directly to N8 (terminal). Game ends immediately.  \n\nSince the player who cannot move loses, making a move that forces the opponent into a terminal state (i.e., a position with no further moves) is advantageous.  \nFrom N5, the next player has no move (N8 is terminal), so the current player wins.  \nThus, moving to N5 wins immediately.  \nTherefore, the optimal choice is N5.</reason>\n<answer>N5</answer>"}]
