[2026-08-13 13:53:40] [agentic_pipeline.py (516)] [INFO] [DRIVER 0 / 1][PID 1538566] [{"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N6\n\nDirected graph:\nN3 -> N2, N9\nN1 -> N5\nN5 -> terminal\nN8 -> terminal\nN6 -> N3, N4, N1\nN7 -> N0\nN4 -> N1, N3\nN9 -> terminal\nN2 -> N7, N8\nN0 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N3, N4, N1assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N6, legal moves are to N3, N4, or N1. We analyze each:  \n- N3 → N2 or N9; N9 is terminal (loss for next player), N2 → N7 or N8; both lead to terminal. So from N3, the opponent wins if they play optimally.  \n- N4 → N1 or N3; both lead to recursive paths eventually to terminal.  \n- N1 → N5 → terminal. So moving to N1 forces immediate win (opponent has no move).  \n\nThus, moving to N1 leads to terminal for opponent immediately. This is the best outcome.  \n</reason>\n<answer>N1</answer>", "episode_score": -0.6166666666666667, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N6, legal moves are to N3, N4, or N1. We analyze each:  \n- N3 → N2 or N9; N9 is terminal (loss for next player), N2 → N7 or N8; both lead to terminal. So from N3, the opponent wins if they play optimally.  \n- N4 → N1 or N3; both lead to recursive paths eventually to terminal.  \n- N1 → N5 → terminal. So moving to N1 forces immediate win (opponent has no move).  \n\nThus, moving to N1 leads to terminal for opponent immediately. This is the best outcome.  \n</reason>\n<answer>N1</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N1\n\nDirected graph:\nN3 -> N2, N9\nN1 -> N5\nN5 -> terminal\nN8 -> terminal\nN6 -> N3, N4, N1\nN7 -> N0\nN4 -> N1, N3\nN9 -> terminal\nN2 -> N7, N8\nN0 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N5assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N1, the only legal move is to N5. N5 leads to a terminal node, so the player who moves to N5 wins immediately. Thus, the first player wins if they move to N5. There are no other options from N1. Since no other move is available, and moving to N5 ends the game in a win for the first player, this is the only and best choice.</reason>\n<answer>N5</answer>", "episode_score": 0.05, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N1, the only legal move is to N5. N5 leads to a terminal node, so the player who moves to N5 wins immediately. Thus, the first player wins if they move to N5. There are no other options from N1. Since no other move is available, and moving to N5 ends the game in a win for the first player, this is the only and best choice.</reason>\n<answer>N5</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N9\n\nDirected graph:\nN3 -> N7, N0\nN8 -> terminal\nN5 -> terminal\nN9 -> N6, N1\nN4 -> terminal\nN0 -> terminal\nN1 -> N8, N5\nN6 -> N3, N2\nN7 -> N5\nN2 -> N4, N1\n\nLegal moves: N6, N1assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N9, the player can move to N6 or N1. Evaluating both options:  \n- Moving to N6 leads to N3 or N2. N3 goes to N7 or N0 (both terminal), N7 to N5 (terminal), N2 to N4 or N1. N4 is terminal, N1 leads to N8 or N5 (both terminal). This creates a path that eventually ends in terminal nodes.  \n- Moving to N1 leads to N8 or N5 (both terminal), so the opponent has no moves and loses immediately.  \nThus, moving to N1 immediately ends the game with a win. N1 is the best choice.</reason>\n<answer>N1</answer>", "episode_score": -0.95, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N9, the player can move to N6 or N1. Evaluating both options:  \n- Moving to N6 leads to N3 or N2. N3 goes to N7 or N0 (both terminal), N7 to N5 (terminal), N2 to N4 or N1. N4 is terminal, N1 leads to N8 or N5 (both terminal). This creates a path that eventually ends in terminal nodes.  \n- Moving to N1 leads to N8 or N5 (both terminal), so the opponent has no moves and loses immediately.  \nThus, moving to N1 immediately ends the game with a win. N1 is the best choice.</reason>\n<answer>N1</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N1\n\nDirected graph:\nN3 -> N7, N0\nN8 -> terminal\nN5 -> terminal\nN9 -> N6, N1\nN4 -> terminal\nN0 -> terminal\nN1 -> N8, N5\nN6 -> N3, N2\nN7 -> N5\nN2 -> N4, N1\n\nLegal moves: N8, N5assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N1, the possible moves are to N8 or N5. N8 and N5 are terminal nodes, so moving to either ends the game immediately. If a player moves to a terminal node, the opponent has no legal move and loses. Therefore, any move from N1 to a terminal node (N8 or N5) wins immediately. Both N8 and N5 lead to immediate loss for the opponent. Since both are winning moves, either is valid. Choosing N5 is safe and direct. However, N9 → N1 creates a cycle, but since we start at N1, we don't need to consider that unless it's a response. The goal is to maximize outcome — so any move that ends the game immediately is optimal. Both N8 and N5 are terminal. Choose one. N5 is a valid and direct choice.</reason>\n<answer>N5</answer>", "episode_score": 0.05, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N1, the possible moves are to N8 or N5. N8 and N5 are terminal nodes, so moving to either ends the game immediately. If a player moves to a terminal node, the opponent has no legal move and loses. Therefore, any move from N1 to a terminal node (N8 or N5) wins immediately. Both N8 and N5 lead to immediate loss for the opponent. Since both are winning moves, either is valid. Choosing N5 is safe and direct. However, N9 → N1 creates a cycle, but since we start at N1, we don't need to consider that unless it's a response. The goal is to maximize outcome — so any move that ends the game immediately is optimal. Both N8 and N5 are terminal. Choose one. N5 is a valid and direct choice.</reason>\n<answer>N5</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N8\n\nDirected graph:\nN4 -> terminal\nN9 -> N0, N12\nN13 -> N6, N3\nN7 -> N11, N10\nN0 -> N1, N13, N5\nN10 -> N9, N4\nN1 -> N2\nN8 -> N0, N11, N7\nN12 -> terminal\nN3 -> terminal\nN6 -> terminal\nN5 -> terminal\nN2 -> terminal\nN11 -> N4\n\nLegal moves: N0, N11, N7assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N8, the legal moves are to N0, N11, or N7. We analyze each option to determine which leads to a winning position (i.e., forces the opponent into a losing position).  \n- N0 leads to N1, N13, or N5. N1 → N2 (terminal), N13 → N6 or N3 (both terminal), N5 → terminal. All paths end quickly. This gives the opponent a losing position if we can force them into a terminal state.  \n- N11 → N4 → terminal. Immediate win for the player who moves to N11.  \n- N7 → N11 or N10. N11 → N4 (terminal), N10 → N9 → N0 or N12 (both lead to terminal). So from N7, all paths eventually end.  \n\nThe key is to choose a move that leads to a terminal state immediately or forces the opponent into a position with no winning moves. N11 leads directly to terminal (N4), so the next player has no legal move and loses. This is optimal. Therefore, choosing N11 is the best move.</reason>\n<answer>N11</answer>", "episode_score": -0.6166666666666667, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N8, the legal moves are to N0, N11, or N7. We analyze each option to determine which leads to a winning position (i.e., forces the opponent into a losing position).  \n- N0 leads to N1, N13, or N5. N1 → N2 (terminal), N13 → N6 or N3 (both terminal), N5 → terminal. All paths end quickly. This gives the opponent a losing position if we can force them into a terminal state.  \n- N11 → N4 → terminal. Immediate win for the player who moves to N11.  \n- N7 → N11 or N10. N11 → N4 (terminal), N10 → N9 → N0 or N12 (both lead to terminal). So from N7, all paths eventually end.  \n\nThe key is to choose a move that leads to a terminal state immediately or forces the opponent into a position with no winning moves. N11 leads directly to terminal (N4), so the next player has no legal move and loses. This is optimal. Therefore, choosing N11 is the best move.</reason>\n<answer>N11</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are pla