[2026-08-13 14:00:13] [agentic_pipeline.py (516)] [INFO] [DRIVER 0 / 1][PID 1538566] [{"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N6\n\nDirected graph:\nN0 -> terminal\nN1 -> N9, N8, N0\nN6 -> N4, N2, N1\nN2 -> N5\nN8 -> N3\nN3 -> terminal\nN7 -> terminal\nN9 -> terminal\nN5 -> N1, N7\nN4 -> N2\n\nLegal moves: N4, N2, N1assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N6, the legal moves are to N4, N2, or N1. We analyze each:\n\n- N4 → N2 → N5 → (N1 or N7). N7 is terminal, so if reach N7, current player loses. If go to N1, it continues. N5 → N1 → leads to N9 or N8 → N3 → terminal. So chains can go deep.\n\n- N2 → N5 → N1 or N7. Again, N7 ends the game immediately. N1 leads to N9, N8, or N0. N8 → N3 → terminal. N9 → terminal. N0 → terminal. So from N5, player can go to N7 (lose) or N1 (continue).\n\n- N1 → N9, N8, N0 — all lead to terminal nodes directly. So any move from N1 ends the game immediately for the player who just moved.\n\nThus, moving to N1 immediately ends the game (opponent loses). So choosing N1 is a winning move.\n\nBut we must ensure the player who moves to N1 causes the opponent to have no legal move — which is true, since all neighbors of N1 are terminal.\n\nTherefore, choosing N1 is optimal: it forces the opponent to lose immediately.\n\n<answer>N1</answer>", "episode_score": -1.5, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N6, the legal moves are to N4, N2, or N1. We analyze each:\n\n- N4 → N2 → N5 → (N1 or N7). N7 is terminal, so if reach N7, current player loses. If go to N1, it continues. N5 → N1 → leads to N9 or N8 → N3 → terminal. So chains can go deep.\n\n- N2 → N5 → N1 or N7. Again, N7 ends the game immediately. N1 leads to N9, N8, or N0. N8 → N3 → terminal. N9 → terminal. N0 → terminal. So from N5, player can go to N7 (lose) or N1 (continue).\n\n- N1 → N9, N8, N0 — all lead to terminal nodes directly. So any move from N1 ends the game immediately for the player who just moved.\n\nThus, moving to N1 immediately ends the game (opponent loses). So choosing N1 is a winning move.\n\nBut we must ensure the player who moves to N1 causes the opponent to have no legal move — which is true, since all neighbors of N1 are terminal.\n\nTherefore, choosing N1 is optimal: it forces the opponent to lose immediately.\n\n<answer>N1</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N5\n\nDirected graph:\nN9 -> terminal\nN8 -> N3\nN6 -> N2, N8\nN1 -> N4, N9\nN3 -> N7\nN7 -> terminal\nN2 -> N1, N0\nN4 -> terminal\nN5 -> N6, N8, N3\nN0 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N6, N8, N3assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N5, the legal moves are to N6, N8, and N3. We analyze each destination to determine which leads to a winning position.  \n- N3 leads to N7, then terminal — the opponent has no move after that, so we win.  \n- N8 leads to N3, then N7, terminal — opponent wins after our move.  \n- N6 leads to N2 or N8. N2 leads to N1 or N0; N1 leads to N4 or N9 (both terminal), N0 is terminal. So from N6, the opponent can move to N2, then to N1 or N0 (winning for them). Thus, N6 leads to opponent winning.  \nN3 directly leads to terminal after one move — we win immediately. Therefore, choosing N3 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N3</answer>", "episode_score": -0.6166666666666667, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N5, the legal moves are to N6, N8, and N3. We analyze each destination to determine which leads to a winning position.  \n- N3 leads to N7, then terminal — the opponent has no move after that, so we win.  \n- N8 leads to N3, then N7, terminal — opponent wins after our move.  \n- N6 leads to N2 or N8. N2 leads to N1 or N0; N1 leads to N4 or N9 (both terminal), N0 is terminal. So from N6, the opponent can move to N2, then to N1 or N0 (winning for them). Thus, N6 leads to opponent winning.  \nN3 directly leads to terminal after one move — we win immediately. Therefore, choosing N3 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N3</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N3\n\nDirected graph:\nN9 -> terminal\nN8 -> N3\nN6 -> N2, N8\nN1 -> N4, N9\nN3 -> N7\nN7 -> terminal\nN2 -> N1, N0\nN4 -> terminal\nN5 -> N6, N8, N3\nN0 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N7assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N3, the only legal move is to N7. N7 leads to terminal, so the player who moves to N7 wins immediately. Thus, moving to N7 results in an immediate win. No other move is available from N3. Therefore, N7 is the optimal choice.</reason>\n<answer>N7</answer>", "episode_score": 0.05, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N3, the only legal move is to N7. N7 leads to terminal, so the player who moves to N7 wins immediately. Thus, moving to N7 results in an immediate win. No other move is available from N3. Therefore, N7 is the optimal choice.</reason>\n<answer>N7</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N5\n\nDirected graph:\nN2 -> N8, N6\nN0 -> terminal\nN7 -> N9, N0\nN6 -> N9\nN9 -> terminal\nN3 -> N7, N1\nN5 -> N6, N3\nN8 -> terminal\nN4 -> terminal\nN1 -> N2, N4\n\nLegal moves: N6, N3assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N5, the legal moves are to N6 or N3.  \n- Moving to N6: leads to N9 (terminal), so the opponent has no move and wins.  \n- Moving to N3: leads to N7 or N1.  \n  - N7 → N9 or N0 (both terminal).  \n  - N1 → N2 or N4 (both lead to terminal eventually).  \nAll paths from N3 eventually reach terminal nodes. Thus, any move from N5 leads to a terminal state in one or two steps. Since the player who cannot move loses, the first player to make a move wins if the opponent is left with no legal move.  \nHowever, from N5, both N6 and N3 lead to terminal states in one or two steps. The key is: after your move, the opponent must move from the new node.  \n- N6 → N9 (terminal): opponent cannot move → opponent loses → you win.  \n- N3 → N7 → N9 or N0: opponent moves, then you win.  \nBut in both cases, the opponent eventually loses.  \nSo both moves lead to a win for the first player. However, we must choose the one that maximizes outcome.  \nSince N6 leads directly to terminal (one move), the opponent has no move and immediately loses. This is optimal.  \nThus, choosing N6 wins immediately.  \n<answer>N6</answer>", "episode_score": -1.5, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N5, the legal moves are to N6 or N3.  \n- Moving to N6: leads to N9 (terminal), so the opponent has no move and wins.  \n- Moving to N3: leads to N7 or N1.  \n  - N7 → N9 or N0 (both terminal).  \n  - N1 → N2 or N4 (both lead to terminal eventually).  \nAll paths from N3 eventually reach terminal nodes. Thus, any move from N5 leads to a terminal state in one or two steps. Since the player who cannot move loses, the first player to make a move wins if the opponent is left with no legal move.  \nHowever, from N5, both N6 and N3 lead to terminal states in one or two steps. The key is: after your move, the opponent must move from the new node.  \n- N6 → N9 (terminal): opponent cannot move → opponent loses → you win.  \n- N3 → N7 → N9 or N0: opponent moves, then you win.  \nBut in both cases, the opponent eventually loses.  \nSo both moves lead to a win for the first player. However, we must choose the one that maximizes outcome.  \nSince N6 leads directly to terminal (one move), the opponent has no move and immediately loses. This is optimal.  \nThus, choosing N6 wins immediately.  \n<answer>N6</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N7\n\nDirected graph:\nN3 -> N9\nN9 -> N4, N1\nN7 -> N6, N3\nN2 -> terminal\nN6 -> N2, N0\nN0 -> terminal\nN8 -> N5, N2\nN5 -> terminal\nN1 -> N5, N8\nN4 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N6, N3assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N7, the player can move to N6 or N3. Moving to N3 leads to N9, then to N4 or N1. N4 and N1 both eventually lead to terminal nodes (N4 → terminal, N1 → N5 → terminal or N8 → N2 → terminal), so all paths from N3 eventually end with the opponent having no moves. Moving to N6 leads to N2 or N0, both of which are terminal. So N6 ends the game immediately. However, since the player who cannot move loses, the player who moves to a terminal node (i.e., forces the opponent to have no move) wins. Thus, moving to N6 leads to an immediate win for the current player, as the opponent has no legal move. Therefore, choosing N6 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N6</answer>", "episode_score": -0.95, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N7, the player can move to N6 or N3. Moving to N3 leads to N9, then to N4 or N1. N4 and N1 both eventually lead to terminal nodes (N4 → terminal, N1 → N5 → terminal or N8 → N2 → terminal), so all paths from N3 eventually end with the opponent having no moves. Moving to N6 leads to N2 or N0, both of which are terminal. So N6 ends the game immediately. However, since the player who cannot move loses, the player who moves to a terminal node (i.e., forces the opponent to have no move) wins. Thus, moving to N6 leads to an immediate win for the current player, as the opponent has no legal move. Therefore, choosing N6 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N6</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N9\n\nDirected graph:\nN8 -> N1, N5\nN9 -> N4, N7\nN3 -> N6, N0\nN7 -> N3, N8\nN5 -> terminal\nN2 -> terminal\nN6 -> N4, N2\nN4 -> N1\nN0 -> terminal\nN1 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N4, N7assistant\n", "response": "<reason>The current node is N9. From N9, the legal moves are to N4 and N7.  \n- Moving to N4: Next node is N4, which has only one outgoing edge to N1, which leads to terminal. So the next player (opponent) will have no move after that — they lose. So this is a winning move.  \n- Moving to N7: From N7, the moves are to N3 and N8.  \n  - N3 → N6, N0 (both lead to terminal or further moves).  \n  - N8 → N1, N5 (N5 is terminal, N1 is terminal).  \n  This creates a path that may continue, giving the opponent a chance to move.  \nThus, moving to N4 ends the game immediately for the opponent, ensuring a win. Therefore, N4 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N4</answer>", "episode_score": -0.95, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>The current node is N9. From N9, the legal moves are to N4 and N7.  \n- Moving to N4: Next node is N4, which has only one outgoing edge to N1, which leads to terminal. So the next player (opponent) will have no move after that — they lose. So this is a winning move.  \n- Moving to N7: From N7, the moves are to N3 and N8.  \n  - N3 → N6, N0 (both lead to terminal or further moves).  \n  - N8 → N1, N5 (N5 is terminal, N1 is terminal).  \n  This creates a path that may continue, giving the opponent a chance to move.  \nThus, moving to N4 ends the game immediately for the opponent, ensuring a win. Therefore, N4 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N4</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N6\n\nDirected graph:\nN3 -> N9\nN9 -> N4, N1\nN7 -> N6, N3\nN2 -> terminal\nN6 -> N2, N0\nN0 -> terminal\nN8 -> N5, N2\nN5 -> terminal\nN1 -> N5, N8\nN4 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N2, N0assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N6, the legal moves are to N2 or N0. Both N2 and N0 lead to terminal nodes, meaning the player who moves to either wins immediately (the opponent has no move). Thus, moving to N2 or N0 ends the game on the next turn, and the current player wins. Since both options result in an immediate win, either is optimal. However, N0 leads to terminal state directly, and N2 also leads to terminal. Both are equivalent in outcome. Choosing N0 is safe and terminal, so it maximizes outcome.</reason>\n<answer>N0</answer>", "episode_score": 0.05, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N6, the legal moves are to N2 or N0. Both N2 and N0 lead to terminal nodes, meaning the player who moves to either wins immediately (the opponent has no move). Thus, moving to N2 or N0 ends the game on the next turn, and the current player wins. Since both options result in an immediate win, either is optimal. However, N0 leads to terminal state directly, and N2 also leads to terminal. Both are equivalent in outcome. Choosing N0 is safe and terminal, so it maximizes outcome.</reason>\n<answer>N0</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N4\n\nDirected graph:\nN8 -> N1, N5\nN9 -> N4, N7\nN3 -> N6, N0\nN7 -> N3, N8\nN5 -> terminal\nN2 -> terminal\nN6 -> N4, N2\nN4 -> N1\nN0 -> terminal\nN1 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N1assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N4, the only legal move is to N1. N1 leads to terminal, so the player who moves to N1 wins immediately. Thus, moving to N1 ends the game on the next player's turn, meaning the current player wins. No other moves are available from N4. Therefore, N1 is the optimal choice.</reason>\n<answer>N1</answer>", "episode_score": 0.05, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N4, the only legal move is to N1. N1 leads to terminal, so the player who moves to N1 wins immediately. Thus, moving to N1 ends the game on the next player's turn, meaning the current player wins. No other moves are available from N4. Therefore, N1 is the optimal choice.</reason>\n<answer>N1</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N2\n\nDirected graph:\nN6 -> terminal\nN4 -> terminal\nN7 -> terminal\nN8 -> N9, N3\nN1 -> N0, N6\nN3 -> N6, N9\nN0 -> terminal\nN2 -> N8, N5\nN9 -> N1, N4\nN5 -> N7\n\nLegal moves: N8, N5assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N2, the legal moves are to N8 and N5.  \n- Moving to N5 leads to N7, which is terminal. The player who moves to N7 wins immediately, as the opponent has no move.  \n- Moving to N8 leads to N9 or N3.  \n  - From N9: can go to N1 or N4 (both lead to terminal).  \n  - From N3: can go to N6 or N9.  \n  N3 and N9 can loop, but eventually lead to terminal nodes. However, from N8, the opponent can force a win via N9 → N4 or N9 → N1 → N0 (terminal).  \nThus, N5 leads directly to terminal, and the current player wins immediately. N8 leads to a position where the opponent can win. Therefore, choosing N5 maximizes the outcome.</reason>\n<answer>N5</answer>", "episode_score": -0.95, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N2, the legal moves are to N8 and N5.  \n- Moving to N5 leads to N7, which is terminal. The player who moves to N7 wins immediately, as the opponent has no move.  \n- Moving to N8 leads to N9 or N3.  \n  - From N9: can go to N1 or N4 (both lead to terminal).  \n  - From N3: can go to N6 or N9.  \n  N3 and N9 can loop, but eventually lead to terminal nodes. However, from N8, the opponent can force a win via N9 → N4 or N9 → N1 → N0 (terminal).  \nThus, N5 leads directly to terminal, and the current player wins immediately. N8 leads to a position where the opponent can win. Therefore, choosing N5 maximizes the outcome.</reason>\n<answer>N5</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N4\n\nDirected graph:\nN8 -> N6, N3\nN0 -> N2, N8\nN3 -> terminal\nN6 -> terminal\nN1 -> N5, N0\nN2 -> N9, N8\nN9 -> N7\nN7 -> terminal\nN5 -> terminal\nN4 -> N2, N1\n\nLegal moves: N2, N1assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N4, the player can move to N2 or N1. Moving to N2 leads to N9 → N7 → terminal, so the opponent has no move and wins. Moving to N1 leads to N5 → terminal, again opponent has no move and wins. Either way, the opponent wins immediately. But since we want to maximize our outcome, we must consider if either move leads to a winning position. However, both N2 and N1 lead to terminal nodes in one step. Thus, the player who moves from N4 forces the opponent to face a terminal node immediately. Therefore, the current player wins. Since both moves lead to immediate win for the current player, either is valid. Choosing N1 is safe and leads to terminal. However, N2 also leads to terminal. Both are equivalent. Pick one. N1 is chosen.</reason>\n<answer>N1</answer>", "episode_score": -0.95, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N4, the player can move to N2 or N1. Moving to N2 leads to N9 → N7 → terminal, so the opponent has no move and wins. Moving to N1 leads to N5 → terminal, again opponent has no move and wins. Either way, the opponent wins immediately. But since we want to maximize our outcome, we must consider if either move leads to a winning position. However, both N2 and N1 lead to terminal nodes in one step. Thus, the player who moves from N4 forces the opponent to face a terminal node immediately. Therefore, the current player wins. Since both moves lead to immediate win for the current player, either is valid. Choosing N1 is safe and leads to terminal. However, N2 also leads to terminal. Both are equivalent. Pick one. N1 is chosen.</reason>\n<answer>N1</answer>"}]
