[2026-08-13 14:02:22] [agentic_pipeline.py (516)] [INFO] [DRIVER 0 / 1][PID 1538566] [{"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N7\n\nDirected graph:\nN5 -> N6, N0\nN9 -> N2\nN7 -> N4, N3\nN1 -> terminal\nN3 -> N6, N8\nN2 -> terminal\nN6 -> terminal\nN8 -> terminal\nN0 -> N1, N3\nN4 -> N9, N5\n\nLegal moves: N4, N3assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N7, the legal moves are to N4 or N3.  \n- Moving to N4: From N4, moves to N9 or N5.  \n  - N9 → N2 (terminal), so player who moves to N9 wins immediately.  \n  - N5 → N6 or N0.  \n    - N6 → terminal.  \n    - N0 → N1 or N3.  \n      - N1 → terminal.  \n      - N3 → N6 or N8 (both terminal).  \n    So from N5, opponent can eventually force a win.  \n- Moving to N3: From N3 → N6 (terminal) or N8 (terminal), so immediate win.  \nThus, moving to N3 leads to immediate terminal state for opponent — a winning move.  \nTherefore, choosing N3 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N3</answer>", "episode_score": -0.95, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N7, the legal moves are to N4 or N3.  \n- Moving to N4: From N4, moves to N9 or N5.  \n  - N9 → N2 (terminal), so player who moves to N9 wins immediately.  \n  - N5 → N6 or N0.  \n    - N6 → terminal.  \n    - N0 → N1 or N3.  \n      - N1 → terminal.  \n      - N3 → N6 or N8 (both terminal).  \n    So from N5, opponent can eventually force a win.  \n- Moving to N3: From N3 → N6 (terminal) or N8 (terminal), so immediate win.  \nThus, moving to N3 leads to immediate terminal state for opponent — a winning move.  \nTherefore, choosing N3 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N3</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N3\n\nDirected graph:\nN5 -> N6, N0\nN9 -> N2\nN7 -> N4, N3\nN1 -> terminal\nN3 -> N6, N8\nN2 -> terminal\nN6 -> terminal\nN8 -> terminal\nN0 -> N1, N3\nN4 -> N9, N5\n\nLegal moves: N6, N8assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N3, the legal moves are to N6 or N8. Both N6 and N8 lead to terminal nodes (win for the player who moves there). Since the player who cannot move loses, moving to a terminal node ends the game immediately and wins. Thus, either N6 or N8 is a winning move. Both are terminal, so either choice wins. Choosing N8 is valid and terminal. However, since both are equivalent in outcome, any is acceptable. We pick N8 as it is a direct terminal move.</reason>\n<answer>N8</answer>", "episode_score": 0.05, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N3, the legal moves are to N6 or N8. Both N6 and N8 lead to terminal nodes (win for the player who moves there). Since the player who cannot move loses, moving to a terminal node ends the game immediately and wins. Thus, either N6 or N8 is a winning move. Both are terminal, so either choice wins. Choosing N8 is valid and terminal. However, since both are equivalent in outcome, any is acceptable. We pick N8 as it is a direct terminal move.</reason>\n<answer>N8</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N7\n\nDirected graph:\nN5 -> terminal\nN8 -> N9, N4\nN9 -> N3, N6\nN4 -> terminal\nN6 -> terminal\nN1 -> N2, N6\nN3 -> N5\nN7 -> N0, N1\nN0 -> N3, N8\nN2 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N0, N1assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N7, the player can move to N0 or N1. Moving to N1 leads to N2 or N6, both of which are terminal (win for the player who moves there). Moving to N0 leads to N3 or N8. N3 goes to N5 (terminal), and N8 goes to N9 or N4; N9 goes to N3 or N6 (N6 terminal), N4 is terminal. The key is to avoid giving the opponent a winning path. From N7, moving to N1 immediately ends the game on a terminal node (N2 or N6), so the current player wins. Thus, N1 is the best move.</reason>\n<answer>N1</answer>", "episode_score": -0.95, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N7, the player can move to N0 or N1. Moving to N1 leads to N2 or N6, both of which are terminal (win for the player who moves there). Moving to N0 leads to N3 or N8. N3 goes to N5 (terminal), and N8 goes to N9 or N4; N9 goes to N3 or N6 (N6 terminal), N4 is terminal. The key is to avoid giving the opponent a winning path. From N7, moving to N1 immediately ends the game on a terminal node (N2 or N6), so the current player wins. Thus, N1 is the best move.</reason>\n<answer>N1</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N1\n\nDirected graph:\nN5 -> terminal\nN8 -> N9, N4\nN9 -> N3, N6\nN4 -> terminal\nN6 -> terminal\nN1 -> N2, N6\nN3 -> N5\nN7 -> N0, N1\nN0 -> N3, N8\nN2 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N2, N6assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N1, the player can move to N2 or N6. Moving to N6 leads directly to terminal, so the opponent has no move and wins immediately. Moving to N2 leads to terminal, also ending the game immediately. However, both N2 and N6 are terminal moves, so the player who moves to either wins. Since both are winning moves, either is valid. But N6 is a direct terminal move from N1, and N2 is also terminal. However, N1 also has a move to N6, which is terminal, so the first player wins by choosing N6. Thus, choosing N6 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N6</answer>", "episode_score": 0.05, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N1, the player can move to N2 or N6. Moving to N6 leads directly to terminal, so the opponent has no move and wins immediately. Moving to N2 leads to terminal, also ending the game immediately. However, both N2 and N6 are terminal moves, so the player who moves to either wins. Since both are winning moves, either is valid. But N6 is a direct terminal move from N1, and N2 is also terminal. However, N1 also has a move to N6, which is terminal, so the first player wins by choosing N6. Thus, choosing N6 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N6</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N6\n\nDirected graph:\nN2 -> terminal\nN3 -> N5, N7, N2\nN9 -> N3\nN5 -> N4\nN6 -> N1, N9, N8\nN7 -> terminal\nN4 -> terminal\nN8 -> N5, N0\nN1 -> N9\nN0 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N1, N9, N8assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N6, the legal moves are to N1, N9, or N8. We analyze each:\n\n- N1 → N9 → N3 → (N5, N7, N2). N7 and N2 lead to terminal (win for opponent if they reach terminal). N3 can go to N5 → N4 (terminal), or N8 → N5 → N4, or N0 → terminal. So from N3, all paths eventually end. If a player reaches a terminal node, they win. Thus, if we go to N1, we eventually reach N9 → N3 → then opponent can move to terminal. So opponent wins.\n\n- N9 → N3 → same as above: opponent can force terminal. So N9 leads to opponent winning.\n\n- N8 → N5 → N4 (terminal), or N8 → N0 (terminal). Both terminal. So if we go to N8, we move to a terminal node, meaning we win immediately.\n\nThus, choosing N8 leads to immediate win. All other moves allow opponent to win. Therefore, best move is N8.</reason>\n<answer>N8</answer>", "episode_score": -0.6166666666666667, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N6, the legal moves are to N1, N9, or N8. We analyze each:\n\n- N1 → N9 → N3 → (N5, N7, N2). N7 and N2 lead to terminal (win for opponent if they reach terminal). N3 can go to N5 → N4 (terminal), or N8 → N5 → N4, or N0 → terminal. So from N3, all paths eventually end. If a player reaches a terminal node, they win. Thus, if we go to N1, we eventually reach N9 → N3 → then opponent can move to terminal. So opponent wins.\n\n- N9 → N3 → same as above: opponent can force terminal. So N9 leads to opponent winning.\n\n- N8 → N5 → N4 (terminal), or N8 → N0 (terminal). Both terminal. So if we go to N8, we move to a terminal node, meaning we win immediately.\n\nThus, choosing N8 leads to immediate win. All other moves allow opponent to win. Therefore, best move is N8.</reason>\n<answer>N8</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N8\n\nDirected graph:\nN2 -> terminal\nN3 -> N5, N7, N2\nN9 -> N3\nN5 -> N4\nN6 -> N1, N9, N8\nN7 -> terminal\nN4 -> terminal\nN8 -> N5, N0\nN1 -> N9\nN0 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N5, N0assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N8, the legal moves are to N5 or N0. N0 leads directly to terminal, so the player who moves to N0 wins immediately (the opponent has no move). Thus, moving to N0 is a winning move. Moving to N5 leads to N4, which is terminal, so the opponent wins. Therefore, choosing N0 maximizes the outcome.</reason>\n<answer>N0</answer>", "episode_score": 1.05, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N8, the legal moves are to N5 or N0. N0 leads directly to terminal, so the player who moves to N0 wins immediately (the opponent has no move). Thus, moving to N0 is a winning move. Moving to N5 leads to N4, which is terminal, so the opponent wins. Therefore, choosing N0 maximizes the outcome.</reason>\n<answer>N0</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N12\n\nDirected graph:\nN10 -> terminal\nN12 -> N6, N3, N4\nN13 -> terminal\nN3 -> N5, N1, N11\nN6 -> N5\nN1 -> N6, N9\nN0 -> N7\nN11 -> terminal\nN4 -> N2\nN5 -> N9, N13, N0, N8\nN9 -> N10\nN8 -> terminal\nN2 -> terminal\nN7 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N6, N3, N4assistant\n", "response": "<reason>The current node is N12. From N12, the legal moves are to N6, N3, or N4. We analyze each:\n\n- N6 → leads to N5 → from N5, possible moves to N9, N13, N0, N8. N13 and N8 are terminal, so the game ends. N9 → N10 (terminal), N0 → N7 (terminal). So all paths from N5 eventually end. Thus, N6 leads to a terminal state in one or two moves — the player who moves to N6 will eventually lose if the opponent plays optimally, since all paths end.\n\n- N3 → leads to N5, N1, N11. N11 is terminal. N1 → N6, N9. N6 → N5, N9 → N10. All paths eventually end. So N3 leads to a chain that ends. The player who moves to N3 can force a path that ends after a few moves.\n\n- N4 → leads to N2 (terminal). So the game ends immediately. Thus, the player who moves to N4 wins immediately — the opponent has no move.\n\nThus, moving to N4 ends the game immediately, giving the current player a win. This is the best outcome.</reason>\n<answer>N4</answer>", "episode_score": -0.6166666666666667, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>The current node is N12. From N12, the legal moves are to N6, N3, or N4. We analyze each:\n\n- N6 → leads to N5 → from N5, possible moves to N9, N13, N0, N8. N13 and N8 are terminal, so the game ends. N9 → N10 (terminal), N0 → N7 (terminal). So all paths from N5 eventually end. Thus, N6 leads to a terminal state in one or two moves — the player who moves to N6 will eventually lose if the opponent plays optimally, since all paths end.\n\n- N3 → leads to N5, N1, N11. N11 is terminal. N1 → N6, N9. N6 → N5, N9 → N10. All paths eventually end. So N3 leads to a chain that ends. The player who moves to N3 can force a path that ends after a few moves.\n\n- N4 → leads to N2 (terminal). So the game ends immediately. Thus, the player who moves to N4 wins immediately — the opponent has no move.\n\nThus, moving to N4 ends the game immediately, giving the current player a win. This is the best outcome.</reason>\n<answer>N4</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternat