[2026-08-13 14:11:08] [agentic_pipeline.py (516)] [INFO] [DRIVER 0 / 1][PID 1538566] [{"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N8\n\nDirected graph:\nN5 -> N3, N6\nN4 -> terminal\nN1 -> N7, N9\nN7 -> terminal\nN0 -> N2, N1\nN3 -> N4, N0\nN9 -> terminal\nN8 -> N5, N6\nN6 -> N4\nN2 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N5, N6assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N8, the player can move to N5 or N6. We analyze each option:  \n- Moving to N5: From N5, player can go to N3 or N6.  \n  - N3 leads to N4 (terminal) or N0.  \n    - N0 leads to N2 (terminal) or N1.  \n      - N1 leads to N7 (terminal) or N9 (terminal).  \n    - N6 leads to N4 (terminal).  \n  - This creates branching with no immediate loss.  \n- Moving to N6: From N6, only move is to N4 (terminal). Opponent has no move and loses immediately.  \nThus, moving to N6 forces immediate win. This is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N6</answer>", "episode_score": -0.95, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N8, the player can move to N5 or N6. We analyze each option:  \n- Moving to N5: From N5, player can go to N3 or N6.  \n  - N3 leads to N4 (terminal) or N0.  \n    - N0 leads to N2 (terminal) or N1.  \n      - N1 leads to N7 (terminal) or N9 (terminal).  \n    - N6 leads to N4 (terminal).  \n  - This creates branching with no immediate loss.  \n- Moving to N6: From N6, only move is to N4 (terminal). Opponent has no move and loses immediately.  \nThus, moving to N6 forces immediate win. This is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N6</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N6\n\nDirected graph:\nN5 -> N3, N6\nN4 -> terminal\nN1 -> N7, N9\nN7 -> terminal\nN0 -> N2, N1\nN3 -> N4, N0\nN9 -> terminal\nN8 -> N5, N6\nN6 -> N4\nN2 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N4assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N6, the only legal move is to N4. From N4, the next player must move to terminal, meaning the current player (who moved to N4) wins because the opponent has no legal move. Thus, moving to N4 is the only option and results in an immediate win for the current player. No other move is available. Therefore, N4 is the optimal choice.</reason>\n<answer>N4</answer>", "episode_score": 0.05, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N6, the only legal move is to N4. From N4, the next player must move to terminal, meaning the current player (who moved to N4) wins because the opponent has no legal move. Thus, moving to N4 is the only option and results in an immediate win for the current player. No other move is available. Therefore, N4 is the optimal choice.</reason>\n<answer>N4</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N0\n\nDirected graph:\nN6 -> terminal\nN0 -> N5, N9, N1\nN5 -> N1, N2\nN8 -> terminal\nN1 -> N6, N9\nN3 -> N4, N7\nN2 -> terminal\nN9 -> N3\nN4 -> N8\nN7 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N5, N9, N1assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N0, the player can move to N5, N9, or N1. We analyze each option to determine which leads to a winning position.  \n- N5 → N1 or N2: N2 is terminal (win for opponent if they reach it), so if opponent reaches N2, they lose. But from N1, player can go to N6 or N9. N6 leads to terminal, so the player who moves to N6 wins immediately. N9 leads to N3, which leads to N4 → N8 (terminal). So chains are long.  \n- N9 → N3 → N4 → N8 (terminal), so opponent wins.  \n- N1 → N6 or N9: N6 is terminal, so if player moves to N6, they win immediately. So choosing N1 allows immediate win.  \nThus, moving to N1 is best: player moves to N6 (terminal), opponent has no move. This is a winning move.</reason>\n<answer>N1</answer>", "episode_score": -0.6166666666666667, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N0, the player can move to N5, N9, or N1. We analyze each option to determine which leads to a winning position.  \n- N5 → N1 or N2: N2 is terminal (win for opponent if they reach it), so if opponent reaches N2, they lose. But from N1, player can go to N6 or N9. N6 leads to terminal, so the player who moves to N6 wins immediately. N9 leads to N3, which leads to N4 → N8 (terminal). So chains are long.  \n- N9 → N3 → N4 → N8 (terminal), so opponent wins.  \n- N1 → N6 or N9: N6 is terminal, so if player moves to N6, they win immediately. So choosing N1 allows immediate win.  \nThus, moving to N1 is best: player moves to N6 (terminal), opponent has no move. This is a winning move.</reason>\n<answer>N1</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N1\n\nDirected graph:\nN6 -> terminal\nN0 -> N5, N9, N1\nN5 -> N1, N2\nN8 -> terminal\nN1 -> N6, N9\nN3 -> N4, N7\nN2 -> terminal\nN9 -> N3\nN4 -> N8\nN7 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N6, N9assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N1, the available moves are to N6 or N9. N6 leads to terminal, so the player who moves to N6 wins immediately. N9 leads to N3, which branches to N4 (to N8, terminal) or N7 (terminal). Either path eventually ends in terminal. However, moving to N6 immediately ends the game in a win for the current player. Thus, choosing N6 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N6</answer>", "episode_score": 0.05, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N1, the available moves are to N6 or N9. N6 leads to terminal, so the player who moves to N6 wins immediately. N9 leads to N3, which branches to N4 (to N8, terminal) or N7 (terminal). Either path eventually ends in terminal. However, moving to N6 immediately ends the game in a win for the current player. Thus, choosing N6 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N6</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N4\n\nDirected graph:\nN11 -> N13, N7\nN8 -> terminal\nN6 -> terminal\nN4 -> N3, N9, N11\nN5 -> terminal\nN1 -> N9, N10\nN3 -> N1, N5, N2, N0\nN2 -> N1, N8\nN0 -> terminal\nN12 -> terminal\nN7 -> N6\nN9 -> N12\nN13 -> N8\nN10 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N3, N9, N11assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N4, the legal moves are to N3, N9, or N11. We analyze each:\n\n- N9 → N12 (terminal): immediate win for the player who moves here, as the opponent has no move. So this is a winning move.\n- N11 → N13 → N8 (terminal): path leads to terminal, opponent wins after two moves. Not optimal.\n- N3 → N1, N5, N2, N0: N3 leads to branching, but N0 and N5 go to terminal, so it's risky. From N3, opponent can go to N1 or N2, which can loop or lead to terminal.\n\nBut N9 leads directly to terminal. Thus, moving to N9 ends the game immediately, and the opponent cannot move. This is a forced win.\n\nTherefore, choosing N9 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N9</answer>", "episode_score": -0.6166666666666667, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N4, the legal moves are to N3, N9, or N11. We analyze each:\n\n- N9 → N12 (terminal): immediate win for the player who moves here, as the opponent has no move. So this is a winning move.\n- N11 → N13 → N8 (terminal): path leads to terminal, opponent wins after two moves. Not optimal.\n- N3 → N1, N5, N2, N0: N3 leads to branching, but N0 and N5 go to terminal, so it's risky. From N3, opponent can go to N1 or N2, which can loop or lead to terminal.\n\nBut N9 leads directly to terminal. Thus, moving to N9 ends the game immediately, and the opponent cannot move. This is a forced win.\n\nTherefore, choosing N9 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N9</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N9\n\nDirected graph:\nN11 -> N13, N7\nN8 -> terminal\nN6 -> terminal\nN4 -> N3, N9, N11\nN5 -> terminal\nN1 -> N9, N10\nN3 -> N1, N5, N2, N0\nN2 -> N1, N8\nN0 -> terminal\nN12 -> terminal\nN7 -> N6\nN9 -> N12\nN13 -> N8\nN10 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N12assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N9, the only legal move is to N12, which is a terminal node. The player who moves to a terminal node ends the game, and the opponent has no legal move afterward. Thus, moving to N12 wins immediately. No other option exists, so this is the only and best choice.</reason>\n<answer>N12</answer>", "episode_score": 0.05, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N9, the only legal move is to N12, which is a terminal node. The player who moves to a terminal node ends the game, and the opponent has no legal move afterward. Thus, moving to N12 wins immediately. No other option exists, so this is the only and best choice.</reason>\n<answer>N12</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N5\n\nDirected graph:\nN8 -> N0, N6\nN0 -> N7, N2\nN9 -> N1, N8\nN5 -> N7, N9\nN2 -> terminal\nN3 -> terminal\nN7 -> N2\nN4 -> terminal\nN6 -> terminal\nN1 -> N3, N4\n\nLegal moves: N7, N9assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N5, the legal moves are to N7 or N9. N7 leads to N2, which is terminal, so the player who moves to N7 wins immediately (opponent has no move). N9 leads to N1, which branches to N3 or N4 (both terminal). From N1, the next player moves to a terminal node and wins. Thus, moving to N7 results in an immediate win (the opponent has no move after reaching N2). Moving to N9 gives the opponent a choice, but they can eventually force a win. Therefore, N7 is the optimal choice.</reason>\n<answer>N7</answer>", "episode_score": -0.95, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N5, the legal moves are to N7 or N9. N7 leads to N2, which is terminal, so the player who moves to N7 wins immediately (opponent has no move). N9 leads to N1, which branches to N3 or N4 (both terminal). From N1, the next player moves to a terminal node and wins. Thus, moving to N7 results in an immediate win (the opponent has no move after reaching N2). Moving to N9 gives the opponent a choice, but they can eventually force a win. Therefore, N7 is the optimal choice.</reason>\n<answer>N7</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N7\n\nDirected graph:\nN8 -> N0, N6\nN0 -> N7, N2\nN9 -> N1, N8\nN5 -> N7, N9\nN2 -> terminal\nN3 -> terminal\nN7 -> N2\nN4 -> terminal\nN6 -> terminal\nN1 -> N3, N4\n\nLegal moves: N2assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N7, the legal moves are to N2 or N7→N2. However, N7→N2 leads directly to terminal (N2 is terminal). Since the player who cannot move loses, moving to a terminal node means the current player has just completed the move and the opponent has no move — thus, the current player wins. From the graph, N7 has only one outgoing edge to N2, which is terminal. Therefore, the only legal move from N7 is to N2, which ends the game immediately. Since the player who moves to a terminal node wins (as the opponent has no legal move), choosing N2 is optimal. Thus, the best move is to N2.</reason>\n<answer>N2</answer>", "episode_score": 0.05, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N7, the legal moves are to N2 or N7→N2. However, N7→N2 leads directly to terminal (N2 is terminal). Since the player who cannot move loses, moving to a terminal node means the current player has just completed the move and the opponent has no move — thus, the current player wins. From the graph, N7 has only one outgoing edge to N2, which is terminal. Therefore, the only legal move from N7 is to N2, which ends the game immediately. Since the player who moves to a terminal node wins (as the opponent has no legal move), choosing N2 is optimal. Thus, the best move is to N2.</reason>\n<answer>N2</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N7\n\nDirected graph:\nN9 -> terminal\nN6 -> terminal\nN1 -> N9\nN2 -> N1, N5\nN5 -> terminal\nN7 -> N4, N3\nN3 -> N0, N9\nN0 -> terminal\nN4 -> N1, N8\nN8 -> N6, N2\n\nLegal moves: N4, N3assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N7, the legal moves are to N4 or N3. Evaluating each:\n\n- N4 leads to N1, then to N9 (terminal), or to N8. From N8, options are N6 (terminal) or N2. N2 leads to N1 or N5 (terminal). N1 leads to N9 (terminal). So paths from N4 eventually reach terminal, and the player who starts from N4 will eventually lose if the opponent plays optimally, because all paths end in terminal.\n\n- N3 leads to N0 (terminal) or N9 (terminal). Both are terminal. So from N3, the next player has no move.\n\nThus, moving to N3 immediately ends the game on the next player's turn — they have no legal move. Therefore, choosing N3 wins the game.\n\nHence, N3 is the optimal move.</reason>\n<answer>N3</answer>", "episode_score": -0.95, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N7, the legal moves are to N4 or N3. Evaluating each:\n\n- N4 leads to N1, then to N9 (terminal), or to N8. From N8, options are N6 (terminal) or N2. N2 leads to N1 or N5 (terminal). N1 leads to N9 (terminal). So paths from N4 eventually reach terminal, and the player who starts from N4 will eventually lose if the opponent plays optimally, because all paths end in terminal.\n\n- N3 leads to N0 (terminal) or N9 (terminal). Both are terminal. So from N3, the next player has no move.\n\nThus, moving to N3 immediately ends the game on the next player's turn — they have no legal move. Therefore, choosing N3 wins the game.\n\nHence, N3 is the optimal move.</reason>\n<answer>N3</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N3\n\nDirected graph:\nN9 -> terminal\nN6 -> terminal\nN1 -> N9\nN2 -> N1, N5\nN5 -> terminal\nN7 -> N4, N3\nN3 -> N0, N9\nN0 -> terminal\nN4 -> N1, N8\nN8 -> N6, N2\n\nLegal moves: N0, N9assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N3, the legal moves are to N0 or N9. Both N0 and N9 lead to terminal nodes, meaning the player who moves to either wins immediately (the opponent has no move). Thus, both moves end the game on the next turn. Since the player who makes the move to a terminal node wins (as the opponent cannot move), either choice is winning. However, since both lead directly to terminal states, either is optimal. Choosing N9 is safe and direct. Therefore, the best choice is N9.</reason>\n<answer>N9</answer>", "episode_score": 0.05, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N3, the legal moves are to N0 or N9. Both N0 and N9 lead to terminal nodes, meaning the player who moves to either wins immediately (the opponent has no move). Thus, both moves end the game on the next turn. Since the player who makes the move to a terminal node wins (as the opponent cannot move), either choice is winning. However, since both lead directly to terminal states, either is optimal. Choosing N9 is safe and direct. Therefore, the best choice is N9.</reason>\n<answer>N9</answer>"}]
