minal) → win for the player who reaches terminal.  \n  - N2 → N5 (terminal) or N8 → N12 or N9.  \n    - N8 → N12 → N7 (terminal) or N13 → N7 or N6 (terminal).  \n    - So from N2, eventually reach terminal.  \n- Moving to N4: leads to N11 or N1, both terminal.  \nThus, N4 leads directly to terminal on the next move.  \nSince the player who cannot move loses, moving to N4 ends the game immediately.  \nTherefore, choosing N4 maximizes immediate win.  \n</reason>  \n<answer>N4</answer>", "episode_score": -0.95, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N10, the legal moves are to N3 or N4.  \n- Moving to N3: leads to N0 or N2.  \n  - N0 → N6 (terminal) or N11 (terminal) → win for the player who reaches terminal.  \n  - N2 → N5 (terminal) or N8 → N12 or N9.  \n    - N8 → N12 → N7 (terminal) or N13 → N7 or N6 (terminal).  \n    - So from N2, eventually reach terminal.  \n- Moving to N4: leads to N11 or N1, both terminal.  \nThus, N4 leads directly to terminal on the next move.  \nSince the player who cannot move loses, moving to N4 ends the game immediately.  \nTherefore, choosing N4 maximizes immediate win.  \n</reason>  \n<answer>N4</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N4\n\nDirected graph:\nN2 -> N5, N8\nN6 -> terminal\nN0 -> N6, N11\nN5 -> terminal\nN3 -> N0, N2\nN9 -> terminal\nN13 -> N7, N6\nN4 -> N11, N1\nN8 -> N12, N9\nN10 -> N3, N4\nN12 -> N7, N13\nN1 -> terminal\nN11 -> terminal\nN7 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N11, N1assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N4, the legal moves are to N11 or N1. Both N11 and N1 lead to terminal nodes, meaning the player who moves to either wins immediately (the opponent has no move). Thus, both moves end the game on the next turn. Since the player who makes the move wins (the opponent loses), either move is winning. However, choosing N11 or N1 is equivalent in outcome—both result in an immediate win. Since the goal is to maximize final game outcome and both are winning moves, either is valid. We select N11 as it appears earlier in the list and is a terminal move.</reason>\n<answer>N11</answer>", "episode_score": 0.05, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N4, the legal moves are to N11 or N1. Both N11 and N1 lead to terminal nodes, meaning the player who moves to either wins immediately (the opponent has no move). Thus, both moves end the game on the next turn. Since the player who makes the move wins (the opponent loses), either move is winning. However, choosing N11 or N1 is equivalent in outcome—both result in an immediate win. Since the goal is to maximize final game outcome and both are winning moves, either is valid. We select N11 as it appears earlier in the list and is a terminal move.</reason>\n<answer>N11</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N1\n\nDirected graph:\nN9 -> terminal\nN4 -> terminal\nN7 -> N6, N8\nN3 -> N0, N7\nN0 -> N5, N2\nN8 -> N6, N9\nN2 -> N4\nN1 -> N3, N0\nN5 -> terminal\nN6 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N3, N0assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N1, the player can move to N3 or N0. We analyze both options for winning potential.  \n- Moving to N3: leads to N0 or N7.  \n  - N0 → N5 (terminal) or N2 → N4 (terminal). Both end in terminal, so player wins if opponent cannot respond.  \n  - N7 → N6 or N8, both of which lead to terminal eventually.  \n  - From N7, opponent can go to N6 or N8 → both terminal. So if you go to N7, opponent wins.  \n  - From N0, if opponent moves to N5 or N2, both end in terminal. So paths from N0 lead to immediate end.  \n- Moving to N0: leads to N5 or N2. Both end in terminal. So opponent has no move.  \nThus, N0 leads to immediate end for opponent. N3 leads to branching but allows opponent to go to N7 → N6/N8 → terminal. So N0 is better.  \nBest move: N0, which ends the game immediately for opponent.</reason>  \n<answer>N0</answer>", "episode_score": -0.95, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N1, the player can move to N3 or N0. We analyze both options for winning potential.  \n- Moving to N3: leads to N0 or N7.  \n  - N0 → N5 (terminal) or N2 → N4 (terminal). Both end in terminal, so player wins if opponent cannot respond.  \n  - N7 → N6 or N8, both of which lead to terminal eventually.  \n  - From N7, opponent can go to N6 or N8 → both terminal. So if you go to N7, opponent wins.  \n  - From N0, if opponent moves to N5 or N2, both end in terminal. So paths from N0 lead to immediate end.  \n- Moving to N0: leads to N5 or N2. Both end in terminal. So opponent has no move.  \nThus, N0 leads to immediate end for opponent. N3 leads to branching but allows opponent to go to N7 → N6/N8 → terminal. So N0 is better.  \nBest move: N0, which ends the game immediately for opponent.</reason>  \n<answer>N0</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not 