inal state (win for current player). Hence, choosing N0 ends the game immediately, forcing the opponent to have no move. This is optimal. Therefore, the best move is N0.</reason>\n<answer>N0</answer>", "episode_score": -0.95, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N4, the legal moves are to N8 or N0. N8 leads to N7 or N6; N7 leads to N6 or N1 (N1 is terminal), and N6 leads to N1 or N5. N5 leads to N9 (terminal) or N3 (terminal). N0 leads to N2 (terminal). Thus, moving to N8 allows the opponent to potentially reach a winning branch. Moving to N0 results in immediate terminal state (win for current player). Hence, choosing N0 ends the game immediately, forcing the opponent to have no move. This is optimal. Therefore, the best move is N0.</reason>\n<answer>N0</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N0\n\nDirected graph:\nN4 -> N8, N0\nN3 -> terminal\nN5 -> N9, N3\nN6 -> N1, N5\nN8 -> N7, N6\nN0 -> N2\nN7 -> N6, N1\nN9 -> terminal\nN1 -> terminal\nN2 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N2assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N0, the only legal move is to N2. N2 leads to terminal, so the player who moves to N2 wins immediately. Thus, moving to N2 results in an immediate win. No other move is available from N0. Therefore, N2 is the optimal choice.</reason>\n<answer>N2</answer>", "episode_score": 0.05, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N0, the only legal move is to N2. N2 leads to terminal, so the player who moves to N2 wins immediately. Thus, moving to N2 results in an immediate win. No other move is available from N0. Therefore, N2 is the optimal choice.</reason>\n<answer>N2</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N7\n\nDirected graph:\nN3 -> N0, N9\nN1 -> N8, N3\nN9 -> N2, N8\nN7 -> N4, N5\nN0 -> terminal\nN6 -> terminal\nN4 -> N6\nN2 -> terminal\nN5 -> N1, N3\nN8 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N4, N5assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N7, the legal moves are to N4 or N5. N4 leads to N6, which is terminal, so the player who moves to N4 wins immediately (opponent has no move). N5 leads to N1 or N3. From N1, paths go to N8 (terminal) or N3; from N3, paths go to N0 or N9. N0 and N9 eventually lead to terminal states. Since N4 results in immediate terminal, the move to N4 ends the game on the next turn, forcing the opponent to lose. Thus, moving to N4 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N4</answer>", "episode_score": -0.95, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N7, the legal moves are to N4 or N5. N4 leads to N6, which is terminal, so the player who moves to N4 wins immediately (opponent has no move). N5 leads to N1 or N3. From N1, paths go to N8 (terminal) or N3; from N3, paths go to N0 or N9. N0 and N9 eventually lead to terminal states. Since N4 results in immediate terminal, the move to N4 ends the game on the next turn, forcing the opponent to lose. Thus, moving to N4 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N4</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N4\n\nDirected graph:\nN3 -> N0, N9\nN1 -> N8, N3\nN9 -> N2, N8\nN7 -> N4, N5\nN0 -> terminal\nN6 -> terminal\nN4 -> N6\nN2 -> terminal\nN5 -> N1, N3\nN8 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N6assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N4, the only legal move is to N6. N6 leads to terminal, so the player who moves to N6 wins immediately (the opponent has no move). Thus, moving to N6 is the only option and results in an immediate win. No other move is available. Therefore, N6 is the optimal choice.</reason>\n<answer>N6</answer>", "episode_score": 0.05, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N4, the only legal move is to N6. N6 leads to terminal, so the player who moves to N6 wins immediately (the opponent has no move). Thus, moving to N6 is the only option and results in an immediate win. No other move is available. Therefore, N6 is the optimal choice.</reason>\n<answer>N6</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N5\n\nDirected graph:\nN8 -> terminal\nN6 -> terminal\nN0 -> N6, N8\nN5 -> N2, N0\nN7 -> N9\nN4 -> N7, N1\nN3 -> N8, N4\nN2 -> N3, N0\nN9 -> terminal\nN1 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N2, N0assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N5, the legal moves are to N2 or N0. Evaluating outcomes:  \n- N0 leads to N6 or N8 (both terminal), so the player who moves to N0 wins immediately (opponent has no move).  \n- N2 leads to N3 or N0:  \n   - N3 leads to N8 or N4.  \n     - N4 leads to N7 or N1 (N1 terminal, so opponent can move to N7 → N9 terminal → win).  \n     - So path from N2 can branch into long chains, but eventually leads to terminal.  \n   - However, since the game ends when no legal move is available, the player who moves to a node with only terminal edges wins.  \nN0 gives immediate terminal options for the opponent (they cannot move). Thus, moving to N0 forces opponent into terminal.  \nBut from N5 → N0, opponent chooses between N6 and N8 (both terminal), meaning they have no move → they lose.  \nThus, N5 → N0 wins immediately.  \nThus, best move is N0.</reason>\n<answer>N0</answer>", "episode_score": -0.95, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N5, the legal moves are to N2 or N0. Evaluating outcomes:  \n- N0 leads to N6 or N8 (both terminal), so the player who moves to N0 wins immediately (opponent has no move).  \n- N2 leads to N3 or N0:  \n   - N3 leads to N8 or N4.  \n     - N4 leads to N7 or N1 (N1 terminal, so opponent can move to N7 → N9 terminal → win).  \n     - So path from N2 can branch into long chains, but eventually leads to terminal.  \n   - However, since the game ends when no legal move is available, the player who moves to a node with only terminal edges wins.  \nN0 gives immediate terminal options for the opponent (they cannot move). Thus, moving to N0 forces opponent into terminal.  \nBut from N5 → N0, opponent chooses between N6 and N8 (both terminal), meaning they have no move → they lose.  \nThus, N5 → N0 wins immediately.  \nThus, best move is N0.</reason>\n<answer>N0</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N0\n\nDirected graph:\nN8 -> terminal\nN6 -> terminal\nN0 -> N6, N8\nN5 -> N2, N0\nN7 -> N9\nN4 -> N7, N1\nN3 -> N8, N4\nN2 -> N3, N0\nN9 -> terminal\nN1 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N6, N8assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N0, the player can move to N6 or N8. Both N6 and N8 lead directly to terminal nodes, so moving to either ends the game immediately for the opponent. Thus, whichever move is chosen, the current player wins. Since both moves result in an immediate win, either is optimal. Choosing N6 or N8 is equivalent. We select N6 as it appears first in the list.</reason>\n<answer>N6</answer>", "episode_score": 0.05, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N0, the player can move to N6 or N8. Both N6 and N8 lead directly to terminal nodes, so moving to either ends the game immediately for the opponent. Thus, whichever move is chosen, the current player wins. Since both moves result in an immediate win, either is optimal. Choosing N6 or N8 is equivalent. We select N6 as it appears first in the list.</reason>\n<answer>N6</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N5\n\nDirected graph:\nN6 -> terminal\nN2 -> terminal\nN12 -> terminal\nN3 -> N10\nN11 -> N6, N3, N2\nN0 -> N13, N8, N7, N4\nN4 -> N11\nN9 -> N2\nN1 -> terminal\nN8 -> N1\nN10 -> terminal\nN13 -> terminal\nN7 -> N9, N12\nN5 -> N4, N8, N0\n\nLegal moves: N4, N8, N0assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N5, the legal moves are to N4, N8, or N0. We analyze the outcome of each move:  \n- N4 → N11 → leads to N6, N3, or N2 (all terminal), so the next player can win immediately from any of these. From N11, the opponent will win. So moving to N4 gives the opponent a winning position.  \n- N8 → N1 → terminal. Player moves to terminal, opponent has no move → current player wins. So this is a winning move.  \n- N0 → N13, N8, N7, N4. From here, it branches: N13 (terminal), N8 (leads to N1, win), N7 → N9 → N2 (terminal), or N4 (as above). From N0, the opponent can play to N8 and win. So from N0, the opponent gains a strong position.  \n\nBest outcome: N8 leads to immediate terminal, opponent cannot move — thus, current player wins. This is a direct win. Hence, N8 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N8</answer>", "episode_score": -0.6166666666666667, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N5, the legal moves are to N4, N8, or N0. We analyze the outcome of each move:  \n- N4 → N11 → leads to N6, N3, or N2 (all terminal), so the next player can win immediately from any of these. From N11, the opponent will win. So moving to N4 gives the opponent a winning position.  \n- N8 → N1 → terminal. Player moves to terminal, opponent has no move → current player wins. So this is a winning move.  \n- N0 → N13, N8, N7, N4. From here, it branches: N13 (terminal), N8 (leads to N1, win), N7 → N9 → N2 (terminal), or N4 (as above). From N0, the opponent can play to N8 and win. So from N0, the opponent gains a strong position.  \n\nBest outcome: N8 leads to immediate terminal, opponent cannot move — thus, current player wins. This is a direct win. Hence, N8 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N8</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output 