[2026-08-13 14:24:29] [agentic_pipeline.py (516)] [INFO] [DRIVER 0 / 1][PID 1538566] [{"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N8\n\nDirected graph:\nN1 -> N6, N3\nN8 -> N1, N9\nN4 -> N5, N0\nN6 -> terminal\nN0 -> N5, N2\nN5 -> terminal\nN7 -> terminal\nN3 -> terminal\nN2 -> N7\nN9 -> N4, N0\n\nLegal moves: N1, N9assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N8, the legal moves are to N1 or N9.  \n- Moving to N1: From N1, moves are to N6 or N3, both terminal → immediate win for the player who moves to them. So the opponent has no move and loses. Thus, moving to N1 wins.  \n- Moving to N9: From N9, moves to N4 or N0.  \n   - N4 → N5 or N0 → eventually leads to terminal or cycles (N0 → N5 → terminal, N0 → N2 → N7 → terminal). Both paths end in terminal, so opponent eventually wins.  \n   Thus, moving to N9 leads to opponent having control and winning eventually.  \nTherefore, choosing N1 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N1</answer>", "episode_score": -0.95, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N8, the legal moves are to N1 or N9.  \n- Moving to N1: From N1, moves are to N6 or N3, both terminal → immediate win for the player who moves to them. So the opponent has no move and loses. Thus, moving to N1 wins.  \n- Moving to N9: From N9, moves to N4 or N0.  \n   - N4 → N5 or N0 → eventually leads to terminal or cycles (N0 → N5 → terminal, N0 → N2 → N7 → terminal). Both paths end in terminal, so opponent eventually wins.  \n   Thus, moving to N9 leads to opponent having control and winning eventually.  \nTherefore, choosing N1 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N1</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N1\n\nDirected graph:\nN1 -> N6, N3\nN8 -> N1, N9\nN4 -> N5, N0\nN6 -> terminal\nN0 -> N5, N2\nN5 -> terminal\nN7 -> terminal\nN3 -> terminal\nN2 -> N7\nN9 -> N4, N0\n\nLegal moves: N6, N3assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N1, the legal moves are to N6 or N3. Both N6 and N3 lead directly to terminal nodes. Moving to N6 or N3 ends the game immediately for the opponent, meaning the current player wins. Thus, either move is winning. Since both are equivalent in outcome, either is optimal. We choose N3 as it is listed first in the options.</reason>\n<answer>N3</answer>", "episode_score": 0.05, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N1, the legal moves are to N6 or N3. Both N6 and N3 lead directly to terminal nodes. Moving to N6 or N3 ends the game immediately for the opponent, meaning the current player wins. Thus, either move is winning. Since both are equivalent in outcome, either is optimal. We choose N3 as it is listed first in the options.</reason>\n<answer>N3</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N2\n\nDirected graph:\nN2 -> N9, N1, N4\nN9 -> N1\nN1 -> N3, N5, N7\nN8 -> terminal\nN4 -> N7, N6\nN7 -> terminal\nN0 -> terminal\nN6 -> N8\nN3 -> terminal\nN5 -> N0\n\nLegal moves: N9, N1, N4assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N2, the player can move to N9, N1, or N4. Moving to N9 leads to N1, which then leads to N3, N5, or N7. N3 and N7 are terminal, so the next player loses if they move to them. N5 leads to N0 (terminal). So from N1, all paths eventually end with a terminal move. From N1, the opponent will eventually have no legal move. From N4, leads to N7 or N6. N7 is terminal; N6 leads to N8 (terminal). All branches from N4 also end. The key is that N1 leads into branching paths, and all those paths eventually end. The player moving to N1 can force a sequence where the opponent is in the last move. Thus, moving to N1 maximizes winning chances. Therefore, optimal move is to N1.</reason>\n<answer>N1</answer>", "episode_score": -0.6166666666666667, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N2, the player can move to N9, N1, or N4. Moving to N9 leads to N1, which then leads to N3, N5, or N7. N3 and N7 are terminal, so the next player loses if they move to them. N5 leads to N0 (terminal). So from N1, all paths eventually end with a terminal move. From N1, the opponent will eventually have no legal move. From N4, leads to N7 or N6. N7 is terminal; N6 leads to N8 (terminal). All branches from N4 also end. The key is that N1 leads into branching paths, and all those paths eventually end. The player moving to N1 can force a sequence where the opponent is in the last move. Thus, moving to N1 maximizes winning chances. Therefore, optimal move is to N1.</reason>\n<answer>N1</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N1\n\nDirected graph:\nN2 -> N9, N1, N4\nN9 -> N1\nN1 -> N3, N5, N7\nN8 -> terminal\nN4 -> N7, N6\nN7 -> terminal\nN0 -> terminal\nN6 -> N8\nN3 -> terminal\nN5 -> N0\n\nLegal moves: N3, N5, N7assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N1, the legal moves are to N3, N5, or N7. N3 and N7 lead directly to terminal nodes (win for the player who moves there), so those are winning moves. N5 leads to N0, which is terminal, also a winning move. All three moves end the game immediately for the opponent. Thus, any of these moves wins. Since the goal is to maximize final outcome and all are winning, choosing any is optimal. N7 is a direct terminal move and safe. We pick N7.</reason>\n<answer>N7</answer>", "episode_score": 0.7166666666666668, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N1, the legal moves are to N3, N5, or N7. N3 and N7 lead directly to terminal nodes (win for the player who moves there), so those are winning moves. N5 leads to N0, which is terminal, also a winning move. All three moves end the game immediately for the opponent. Thus, any of these moves wins. Since the goal is to maximize final outcome and all are winning, choosing any is optimal. N7 is a direct terminal move and safe. We pick N7.</reason>\n<answer>N7</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N13\n\nDirected graph:\nN7 -> terminal\nN1 -> terminal\nN12 -> N2, N8\nN9 -> terminal\nN3 -> N9, N1\nN8 -> N9, N4\nN13 -> N5, N10\nN11 -> N4, N12\nN10 -> N7, N0\nN4 -> terminal\nN2 -> terminal\nN0 -> terminal\nN5 -> N6, N3\nN6 -> N11, N0\n\nLegal moves: N5, N10assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N13, the legal moves are to N5 and N10. Moving to N5 leads to N6, which branches to N11 or N0 (N11 leads to N4 or N12; N12 leads to N2 or N8, both eventually leading to terminals or loops with high risk). Moving to N10 leads to N7 or N0, both terminal. N10 gives immediate terminal on N0 or N7, thus ending the game quickly. Since the player who cannot move loses, the goal is to force the opponent into a terminal move. The move to N5 is more complex, involving deeper game paths. However, N10 leads to a guaranteed immediate terminal from either path. Since the goal is to maximize outcome and avoid giving opponent a move, the best move is to N10 which results in immediate terminal, ending the game fast and ensuring the opponent cannot move. Thus, N10 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N10</answer>", "episode_score": -0.95, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N13, the legal moves are to N5 and N10. Moving to N5 leads to N6, which branches to N11 or N0 (N11 leads to N4 or N12; N12 leads to N2 or N8, both eventually leading to terminals or loops with high risk). Moving to N10 leads to N7 or N0, both terminal. N10 gives immediate terminal on N0 or N7, thus ending the game quickly. Since the player who cannot move loses, the goal is to force the opponent into a terminal move. The move to N5 is more complex, involving deeper game paths. However, N10 leads to a guaranteed immediate terminal from either path. Since the goal is to maximize outcome and avoid giving opponent a move, the best move is to N10 which results in immediate terminal, ending the game fast and ensuring the opponent cannot move. Thus, N10 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N10</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N10\n\nDirected graph:\nN7 -> terminal\nN1 -> terminal\nN12 -> N2, N8\nN9 -> terminal\nN3 -> N9, N1\nN8 -> N9, N4\nN13 -> N5, N10\nN11 -> N4, N12\nN10 -> N7, N0\nN4 -> terminal\nN2 -> terminal\nN0 -> terminal\nN5 -> N6, N3\nN6 -> N11, N0\n\nLegal moves: N7, N0assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N10, the legal moves are to N7 and N0. Both N7 and N0 lead to terminal nodes, so whichever edge is taken, the current player wins immediately (the opponent has no move). Thus, both moves result in an immediate win for the player who moves. Since both options end the game immediately, either is equivalent. Choosing N0 is safe and terminal. Therefore, N0 is a valid and optimal choice.</reason>\n<answer>N0</answer>", "episode_score": 0.05, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N10, the legal moves are to N7 and N0. Both N7 and N0 lead to terminal nodes, so whichever edge is taken, the current player wins immediately (the opponent has no move). Thus, both moves result in an immediate win for the player who moves. Since both options end the game immediately, either is equivalent. Choosing N0 is safe and terminal. Therefore, N0 is a valid and optimal choice.</reason>\n<answer>N0</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N3\n\nDirected graph:\nN4 -> terminal\nN0 -> terminal\nN5 -> N0, N8\nN7 -> N6\nN8 -> N4, N1\nN9 -> N7, N5\nN1 -> N2\nN6 -> terminal\nN3 -> N5, N8, N9\nN2 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N5, N8, N9assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N3, the player can move to N5, N8, or N9. We evaluate the outcomes of each:\n\n- N5 leads to N0 (terminal), so the opponent cannot move and wins immediately (this is a losing move for the current player).\n- N8 leads to N4 or N1:\n  - N4 → terminal: immediate win for next player (so current player loses).\n  - N1 → N2 → terminal: again, next player wins, so current player loses.\n- N9 leads to N7 → N6 (terminal), so after N9 → N7 → N6, the chain ends. So the current player forces the next move into a terminal, meaning they win.\n\nSo only N9 leads to a win (via N7 → N6, terminal), while all others lead to the opponent winning. Thus, N9 is the optimal choice.</reason>\n<answer>N9</answer>", "episode_score": 1.3833333333333333, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N3, the player can move to N5, N8, or N9. We evaluate the outcomes of each:\n\n- N5 leads to N0 (terminal), so the opponent cannot move and wins immediately (this is a losing move for the current player).\n- N8 leads to N4 or N1:\n  - N4 → terminal: immediate win for next player (so current player loses).\n  - N1 → N2 → terminal: again, next player wins, so current player loses.\n- N9 leads to N7 → N6 (terminal), so after N9 → N7 → N6, the chain ends. So the current player forces the next move into a terminal, meaning they win.\n\nSo only N9 leads to a win (via N7 → N6, terminal), while all others lead to the opponent winning. Thus, N9 is the optimal choice.</reason>\n<answer>N9</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N7\n\nDirected graph:\nN2 -> N5\nN4 -> N2, N5\nN3 -> terminal\nN9 -> terminal\nN1 -> N9, N0\nN5 -> N8, N1\nN7 -> N4, N6, N2\nN6 -> N3\nN0 -> terminal\nN8 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N4, N6, N2assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N7, the legal moves are to N4, N6, and N2.  \n- N4 leads to N2 or N5.  \n  - N2 → N5 → N8 (win), or N5 → N8 (win). Eventually leads to terminal.  \n  - N2 → N5 → N1 → N0 or N9 → terminal (all terminal).  \n- N6 → N3 → terminal (win for next player).  \n- N2 → N5 → N1 → N0 or N9 (terminal).  \nBest is to move to N6, which leads to N3 (terminal), meaning opponent has no move and loses. So N6 wins. Therefore, choosing N6 maximizes outcome.</reason>\n<answer>N6</answer>", "episode_score": -0.6166666666666667, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N7, the legal moves are to N4, N6, and N2.  \n- N4 leads to N2 or N5.  \n  - N2 → N5 → N8 (win), or N5 → N8 (win). Eventually leads to terminal.  \n  - N2 → N5 → N1 → N0 or N9 → terminal (all terminal).  \n- N6 → N3 → terminal (win for next player).  \n- N2 → N5 → N1 → N0 or N9 (terminal).  \nBest is to move to N6, which leads to N3 (terminal), meaning opponent has no move and loses. So N6 wins. Therefore, choosing N6 maximizes outcome.</reason>\n<answer>N6</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N9\n\nDirected graph:\nN4 -> terminal\nN0 -> terminal\nN5 -> N0, N8\nN7 -> N6\nN8 -> N4, N1\nN9 -> N7, N5