[2026-08-13 14:30:56] [agentic_pipeline.py (516)] [INFO] [DRIVER 0 / 1][PID 1538566] [{"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N7\n\nDirected graph:\nN0 -> terminal\nN1 -> N0\nN4 -> N1, N5\nN8 -> N4, N9, N6\nN7 -> N8, N1, N5\nN3 -> terminal\nN5 -> N2\nN6 -> terminal\nN2 -> N3\nN9 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N8, N1, N5assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N7, the legal moves are to N8, N1, or N5.  \n- N1 leads to N0 (terminal), so the player who moves to N1 wins immediately (opponent has no move).  \n- N5 leads to N2 → N3 (terminal), again the chain ends.  \n- N8 leads to N4, N9, N6. N9 and N6 go directly to terminal. N4 → N1, which leads back into a losing path due to N1’s terminal loop.\n\nThus, moving to N1 or N5 both result in a terminal win immediately after, meaning they are winning moves. However, N1 and N5 are terminal in their chains — they don't give the opponent a move. So either move wins directly. But among them, choosing N1 leads to terminal, so opponent can't move — thus immediate win.\n\nSince both are winning, the best choice is N1 as it leads directly to a winning end. Hence, the optimal move is to N1.</reason>\n<answer>N1</answer>", "episode_score": -0.6166666666666667, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N7, the legal moves are to N8, N1, or N5.  \n- N1 leads to N0 (terminal), so the player who moves to N1 wins immediately (opponent has no move).  \n- N5 leads to N2 → N3 (terminal), again the chain ends.  \n- N8 leads to N4, N9, N6. N9 and N6 go directly to terminal. N4 → N1, which leads back into a losing path due to N1’s terminal loop.\n\nThus, moving to N1 or N5 both result in a terminal win immediately after, meaning they are winning moves. However, N1 and N5 are terminal in their chains — they don't give the opponent a move. So either move wins directly. But among them, choosing N1 leads to terminal, so opponent can't move — thus immediate win.\n\nSince both are winning, the best choice is N1 as it leads directly to a winning end. Hence, the optimal move is to N1.</reason>\n<answer>N1</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N1\n\nDirected graph:\nN0 -> terminal\nN1 -> N0\nN4 -> N1, N5\nN8 -> N4, N9, N6\nN7 -> N8, N1, N5\nN3 -> terminal\nN5 -> N2\nN6 -> terminal\nN2 -> N3\nN9 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N0assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N1, the only legal move is to N0. From N0, the move goes to terminal, so the player who moves to N0 wins immediately (the opponent has no move). Thus, moving from N1 to N0 is a winning move. No other move is available from N1. Therefore, the optimal choice is to go to N0.</reason>\n<answer>N0</answer>", "episode_score": 0.05, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N1, the only legal move is to N0. From N0, the move goes to terminal, so the player who moves to N0 wins immediately (the opponent has no move). Thus, moving from N1 to N0 is a winning move. No other move is available from N1. Therefore, the optimal choice is to go to N0.</reason>\n<answer>N0</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N4\n\nDirected graph:\nN6 -> N9, N8\nN8 -> N1, N2\nN5 -> N8, N6\nN3 -> terminal\nN4 -> N5, N0\nN1 -> N3\nN7 -> terminal\nN9 -> terminal\nN0 -> N9, N7\nN2 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N5, N0assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N4, the legal moves are to N5 and N0.  \n- Moving to N5 leads to N8 or N6.  \n   - N8 leads to N1 → N3 (terminal) or N2 (terminal) — all losing moves for the opponent.\n   - N6 leads to N9 or N8 (both eventually terminal paths or win).  \n   Thus, from N5, control is passed into paths with good outcomes.  \n- Moving to N0 leads to N9 (terminal) or N7 (terminal). This ends the game immediately, so player to move after that (i.e., opponent) will lose — meaning we win if we choose N0.  \nN0 is a winning move as it immediately leads to terminal states for the opponent.  \nThus, choosing N0 gives immediate game end. Hence, optimal move is to N0.</reason>\n<answer>N0</answer>", "episode_score": -0.95, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N4, the legal moves are to N5 and N0.  \n- Moving to N5 leads to N8 or N6.  \n   - N8 leads to N1 → N3 (terminal) or N2 (terminal) — all losing moves for the opponent.\n   - N6 leads to N9 or N8 (both eventually terminal paths or win).  \n   Thus, from N5, control is passed into paths with good outcomes.  \n- Moving to N0 leads to N9 (terminal) or N7 (terminal). This ends the game immediately, so player to move after that (i.e., opponent) will lose — meaning we win if we choose N0.  \nN0 is a winning move as it immediately leads to terminal states for the opponent.  \nThus, choosing N0 gives immediate game end. Hence, optimal move is to N0.</reason>\n<answer>N0</answer>"}, {"prompt