[2026-08-13 14:37:45] [agentic_pipeline.py (516)] [INFO] [DRIVER 0 / 1][PID 1538566] [{"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N8\n\nDirected graph:\nN6 -> terminal\nN1 -> terminal\nN0 -> N5, N7\nN2 -> N4, N6\nN9 -> N0, N1\nN8 -> N2, N3\nN5 -> N7\nN4 -> terminal\nN7 -> terminal\nN3 -> N9, N5\n\nLegal moves: N2, N3assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N8, the legal moves are to N2 or N3. Moving to N2 leads to N4 or N6. N4 leads to terminal (win for opponent), and N6 leads to terminal. Moving to N3 leads to N9 or N5. N9 leads to N0 or N1, both of which are paths leading to terminal. N5 leads directly to N7 (terminal). From N3 to N5 results in immediate loss due to chain of terminal nodes. N2 is better because N6 to terminal results in opponent's loss; thus, moving to N2 lets player force the sequence where the opponent eventually faces a forced terminal. Thus, choosing N2 maximizes final outcome.</reason>\n<answer>N2</answer>", "episode_score": -0.95, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N8, the legal moves are to N2 or N3. Moving to N2 leads to N4 or N6. N4 leads to terminal (win for opponent), and N6 leads to terminal. Moving to N3 leads to N9 or N5. N9 leads to N0 or N1, both of which are paths leading to terminal. N5 leads directly to N7 (terminal). From N3 to N5 results in immediate loss due to chain of terminal nodes. N2 is better because N6 to terminal results in opponent's loss; thus, moving to N2 lets player force the sequence where the opponent eventually faces a forced terminal. Thus, choosing N2 maximizes final outcome.</reason>\n<answer>N2</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N2\n\nDirected graph:\nN6 -> terminal\nN1 -> terminal\nN0 -> N5, N7\nN2 -> N4, N6\nN9 -> N0, N1\nN8 -> N2, N3\nN5 -> N7\nN4 -> terminal\nN7 -> terminal\nN3 -> N9, N5\n\nLegal moves: N4, N6assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N2, the legal moves are to N4 and N6. N4 leads to terminal (win for the player who moves there), so the opponent has no move and loses. N6 also leads to terminal. Thus, both moves end the game immediately. Since the player who makes the move wins (opponent has no legal move), either move is winning. However, choosing N4 is safer as it directly ends the game with no risk. Both are winning, but N4 is a direct terminal move. Thus, N4 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N4</answer>", "episode_score": 0.05, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N2, the legal moves are to N4 and N6. N4 leads to terminal (win for the player who moves there), so the opponent has no move and loses. N6 also leads to terminal. Thus, both moves end the game immediately. Since the player who makes the move wins (opponent has no legal move), either move is winning. However, choosing N4 is safer as it directly ends the game with no risk. Both are winning, but N4 is a direct terminal move. Thus, N4 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N4</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N2\n\nDirected graph:\nN4 -> N9\nN6 -> N7, N1\nN9 -> terminal\nN0 -> terminal\nN8 -> N4, N3\nN7 -> terminal\nN3 -> terminal\nN2 -> N6, N5\nN5 -> N6, N8\nN1 -> N9, N0\n\nLegal moves: N6, N5assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N2, the player can move to N6 or N5. We analyze the outcomes from each choice. Moving to N6 leads to a path through N6 → N7 or N6 → N1. Both N7 and N1 lead to terminal states, so those are losing positions for the opponent. From N5: N5 → N6 (to N7 or N1) or N5 → N8 (N8 → N4 → N9, which ends in terminal). Any such move leads eventually to terminal, so no immediate gain. However, N2's options lead to terminal moves, suggesting all transitions are losing positions from next player's turn. So optimal play is to avoid giving opponent an immediate terminal. But all routes are leading to a forced terminal chain. Therefore, the best move is toward a branch that does not immediately lose control — N6 gives more control and terminal options to opponent. Thus N6 is safer in long-term. Thus, choosing N6 ensures opponent plays into a losing path. So N6 is best.</reason>\n<answer>N6</answer>", "episode_score": -0.95, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N2, the player can move to N6 or N5. We analyze the outcomes from each choice. Moving to N6 leads to a path through N6 → N7 or N6 → N1. Both N7 and N1 lead to terminal states, so those are losing positions for the opponent. From N5: N5 → N6 (to N7 or N1) or N5 → N8 (N8 → N4 → N9, which ends in terminal). Any such move leads eventually to terminal, so no immediate gain. However, N2's options lead to terminal moves, suggesting all transitions are losing positions from next player's turn. So optimal play is to avoid giving opponent an immediate terminal. But all routes are leading to a forced terminal chain. Therefore, the best move is toward a branch that does not immediately lose control — N6 gives more control and terminal options to opponent. Thus N6 is safer in long-term. Thus, choosing N6 ensures opponent plays into a losing path. So N6 is best.</reason>\n<answer>N6</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N6\n\nDirected graph:\nN4 -> N9\nN6 -> N7, N1\nN9 -> terminal\nN0 -> terminal\nN8 -> N4, N3\nN7 -> terminal\nN3 -> terminal\nN2 -> N6, N5\nN5 -> N6, N8\nN1 -> N9, N0\n\nLegal moves: N7, N1assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N6, the legal moves are to N7 and N1. N7 leads to terminal (win for the player who moves there, so opponent loses). N1 leads to N9 or N0, both of which are terminal. Thus, moving to N7 immediately ends the game for the opponent. Choosing N7 is a winning move. Therefore, the optimal choice is N7.</reason>\n<answer>N7</answer>", "episode_score": 1.05, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N6, the legal moves are to N7 and N1. N7 leads to terminal (win for the player who moves there, so opponent loses). N1 leads to N9 or N0, both of which are terminal. Thus, moving to N7 immediately ends the game for the opponent. Choosing N7 is a winning move. Therefore, the optimal choice is N7.</reason>\n<answer>N7</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N2\n\nDirected graph:\nN6 -> N4\nN2 -> N3, N6, N1\nN5 -> terminal\nN1 -> N5\nN0 -> terminal\nN8 -> N1, N9\nN9 -> terminal\nN3 -> N7\nN7 -> terminal\nN4 -> N0, N8, N3\n\nLegal moves: N3, N6, N1assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N2, the legal moves are to N3, N6, or N1. We analyze each successor's outcome:  \n- N3 leads to N7 (terminal) → losing move for the opponent → good outcome.  \n- N6 leads to N4, which has path N4→N0 (terminal) or N4→N8 (then N8→N1 and eventually terminal), and further, N4→N3 to terminal, all ending in terminal, so paths lead to opponent win unless player can force. But N4 has branching paths; deeper analysis shows no immediate terminal control. But, since N6 leads through N4, it's not a strong choice.  \n- N1 → N5 → terminal, immediate end of chain. Thus, opponent loses when moving into N1, which is a good outcome.  \nFrom N2 move:  \nN1 → opponent gets a direct terminal win after N5 move, meaning the player who chooses this path will win.  \nThus, move to N1 gives immediate control and forces opponent to lose. This is best.  \nHence N1 is optimal.  \n</reason>\n<answer>N1</answer>", "episode_score": -0.6166666666666667, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N2, the legal moves are to N3, N6, or N1. We analyze each successor's outcome:  \n- N3 leads to N7 (terminal) → losing move for the opponent → good outcome.  \n- N6 leads to N4, which has path N4→N0 (terminal) or N4→N8 (then N8→N1 and eventually terminal), and further, N4→N3 to terminal, all ending in terminal, so paths lead to opponent win unless player can force. But N4 has branching paths; deeper analysis shows no immediate terminal control. But, since N6 leads through N4, it's not a strong choice.  \n- N1 → N5 → terminal, immediate end of chain. Thus, opponent loses when moving into N1, which is a good outcome.  \nFrom N2 move:  \nN1 → opponent gets a direct terminal win after N5 move, meaning the player who chooses this path will win.  \nThus, move to N1 gives immediate control and forces opponent to lose. This is best.  \nHence N1 is optimal.  \n</reason>\n<answer>N1</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N1\n\nDirected graph:\nN6 -> N4\nN2 -> N3, N6, N1\nN5 -> terminal\nN1 -> N5\nN0 -> terminal\nN8 -> N1, N9\nN9 -> terminal\nN3 -> N7\nN7 -> terminal\nN4 -> N0, N8, N3\n\nLegal moves: N5assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N1, the only legal move is to N5. N5 leads to terminal, so the player who moves to N5 wins immediately (the opponent has no move). Thus, moving to N5 is a winning move. No other move from N1 is available. Therefore, the optimal choice is to move to N5.</reason>\n<answer>N5</answer>", "episode_score": 0.05, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N1, the only legal move is to N5. N5 leads to terminal, so the player who moves to N5 wins immediately (the opponent has no move). Thus, moving to N5 is a winning move. No other move from N1 is available. Therefore, the optimal choice is to move to N5.</reason>\n<answer>N5</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N4\n\nDirected graph:\nN8 -> terminal\nN5 -> terminal\nN4 -> N0, N7, N9\nN0 -> N9\nN1 -> terminal\nN9 -> N6, N5\nN6 -> N2, N3\nN7 -> N8, N1\nN3 -> terminal\nN2 -> N1\n\nLegal moves: N0, N7, N9assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N4, the legal moves are to N0, N7, or N9. Evaluating each move:\n- N4 → N0: leads to N9 from N0. Then N9 leads to N6 or N5. \n   - From N6 → N2 → N1 → terminal → losing path for opponent; N3 → terminal → also losing.\n   → Ultimately leads to terminal. \n- N4 → N7: goes to N8 or N1. N1 → terminal → losing move; N8 → terminal → opponent wins immediately.\n- N4 → N9 → leads to a fork (N5, N6). N5 leads directly to terminal, N6 to N2 → terminal path.\n  Ultimately N9 → N6 → N2 → N1 → terminal, so terminal is reached.\nAll paths lead to terminal but key: N7 leads to N1 and N8 which are terminal, so opponent wins fast. The only move that forces opponent to lose immediately or forces a win is avoiding terminal early.\nUpon deeper analysis, N7 leads to terminal paths (via both edges), while N0 or N9 can extend the path, and N9 is best since it gives more control over branching.\nBut N4 to N9 gives control of the N9 path to terminal and can win by forcing the opponent in losing terminal states.\nThus, move to N9 ensures that the game is played in a more favorable terminal control. Therefore, N9 is best.</reason>\n<answer>N9</answer>", "episode_score": -0.6166666666666667, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N4, the legal moves are to N0, N7, or N9. Evaluating each move:\n- N4 → N0: leads to N9 from N0. Then N9 leads to N6 or N5. \n   - From N6 → N2 → N1 → terminal → losing path for opponent; N3 → terminal → also losing.\n   → Ultimately leads to terminal. \n- N4 → N7: goes to N8 or N1. N1 → terminal → losing move; N8 → terminal → opponent wins immediately.\n- N4 → N9 → leads to a fork (N5, N6). N5 leads directly to terminal, N6 to N2 → terminal path.\n  Ultimately N9 → N6 → N2 → N1 → terminal, so terminal is reached.\nAll paths lead to terminal but key: N7 leads to N1 and N8 which are terminal, so opponent wins fast. The only move that forces opponent to lose immediately or forces a win is avoiding terminal early.\nUpon deeper analysis, N7 leads to terminal paths (via both edges), while N0 or N9 can extend the path, and N9 is best since it gives more control over branching.\nBut N4 to N9 gives control of the N9 path to terminal and can win by forcing the opponent in losing terminal states.\nThus, move to N9 ensures that the game is played in a more favorable terminal control. Therefore, N9 is best.</reason>\n<answer>N9</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N9\n\nDirected graph:\nN8 -> terminal\nN5 -> terminal\nN4 -> N0, N7, N9\nN0 -> N9\nN1 -> terminal\nN9 -> N6, N5\nN6 -> N2, N3\nN7 -> N8, N1\nN3 -> terminal\nN2 -> N1\n\nLegal moves: N6, N5assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N9, the legal moves are to N6 or N5. Moving to N5 leads to terminal (loss for the next player, so win for current). Thus, moving to N5 is optimal. Therefore, the best first move is to N5.</reason>\n<answer>N5</answer>", "episode_score": 1.05, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N9, the legal moves are to N6 or N5. Moving to N5 leads to terminal (loss for the next player, so win for current). Thus, moving to N5 is optimal. Therefore, the best first move is to N5.</reason>\n<answer>N5</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N4\n\nDirected graph:\nN3 -> terminal\nN2 -> N6, N13\nN1 -> N11, N7\nN8 -> N2, N1\nN9 -> N5, N10\nN6 -> terminal\nN11 -> N9, N0\nN7 -> terminal\nN4 -> N8, N12\nN5 -> terminal\nN10 -> N13, N5\nN13 -> terminal\nN0 -> terminal\nN12 -> N3, N7\n\nLegal moves: N8, N12assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N4, the moves are to N8 and N12. Moving to N8 leads to N2 or N1, both of which lead to longer paths with potential to reach terminal nodes. Moving to N12 leads to N3 or N7, both of which are terminal. However, from N12, either N3 or N7 results in terminal, meaning the next player has no move and wins. But if N4 → N8 → N1, N1 → N11 → N9 → N10 → N13, N13 is terminal. The key is to control the path that forces the opponent into losing positions. However, N12 gives immediate access to N3 → terminal, or N7 → terminal, so the opponent gets no move after that. Hence, choosing N12 immediately leads the opponent to terminal path and forces them into a losing position. This gives an edge to the player who picks N12. Therefore, selecting N12 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N12</answer>", "episode_score": -0.95, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N4, the moves are to N8 and N12. Moving to N8 leads to N2 or N1, both of which lead to longer paths with potential to reach terminal nodes. Moving to N12 leads to N3 or N7, both of which are terminal. However, from N12, either N3 or N7 results in terminal, meaning the next player has no move and wins. But if N4 → N8 → N1, N1 → N11 → N9 → N10 → N13, N13 is terminal. The key is to control the path that forces the opponent into losing positions. However, N12 gives immediate access to N3 → terminal, or N7 → terminal, so the opponent gets no move after that. Hence, choosing N12 immediately leads the opponent to terminal path and forces them into a losing position. This gives an edge to the player who picks N12. Therefore, selecting N12 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N12</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N12\n\nDirected graph:\nN3 -> terminal\nN2 -> N6, N13\nN1 -> N11, N7\nN8 -> N2, N1\nN9 -> N5, N10\nN6 -> terminal\nN11 -> N9, N0\nN7 -> terminal\nN4 -> N8, N12\nN5 -> terminal\nN10 -> N13, N5\nN13 -> terminal\nN0 -> terminal\nN12 -> N3, N7\n\nLegal moves: N3, N7assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N12, the legal moves are to N3 and N7. N3 leads to terminal, so the player who moves to N3 wins immediately (the opponent has no move). Similarly, N7 also leads to terminal. Both moves end the game immediately for the opponent. Since both options end the game on the next move, either is a win. However, choosing N7 is safer as it directly ends the game with no risk. Thus, N7 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N7</answer>", "episode_score": 0.05, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N12, the legal moves are to N3 and N7. N3 leads to terminal, so the player who moves to N3 wins immediately (the opponent has no move). Similarly, N7 also leads to terminal. Both moves end the game immediately for the opponent. Since both options end the game on the next move, either is a win. However, choosing N7 is safer as it directly ends the game with no risk. Thus, N7 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N7</answer>"}]
