al. So, from N1, moving to N2 gives control over N4/N6, where reaching N6 ends the game immediately for opponent. Thus, N2 is a winning move as it gives the ability to force the opponent into losing paths. Therefore, choosing N2 maximizes the chance of winning.</reason>\n<answer>N2</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N2\n\nDirected graph:\nN4 -> N5, N3\nN8 -> N9, N0\nN2 -> N4, N6\nN6 -> terminal\nN9 -> terminal\nN1 -> N2, N7\nN3 -> N5\nN7 -> N2, N8\nN0 -> terminal\nN5 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N4, N6assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N2, the legal moves are to N4 and N6. N6 leads directly to terminal, so the player who moves to N6 wins immediately (the opponent has no move). Moving to N4 leads to N5 or N3. N5 and N3 both eventually lead to terminal. Thus, moving to N6 results in an immediate win. Therefore, the optimal move is to N6.</reason>\n<answer>N6</answer>", "episode_score": 1.05, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N2, the legal moves are to N4 and N6. N6 leads directly to terminal, so the player who moves to N6 wins immediately (the opponent has no move). Moving to N4 leads to N5 or N3. N5 and N3 both eventually lead to terminal. Thus, moving to N6 results in an immediate win. Therefore, the optimal move is to N6.</reason>\n<answer>N6</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking pr