rected Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N3\n\nDirected graph:\nN6 -> N10, N0\nN7 -> N4\nN2 -> terminal\nN1 -> terminal\nN12 -> N1, N13\nN4 -> terminal\nN8 -> N2, N7, N10\nN0 -> terminal\nN13 -> N11\nN5 -> N1, N8\nN9 -> N12, N5\nN3 -> N13, N6, N9\nN11 -> terminal\nN10 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N13, N6, N9assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N3, the legal moves are to N13, N6, and N9.  \n- N13 leads to N11 (terminal) → opponent wins if this is a winning move, so we must consider deeper outcomes.  \n- N6 leads to N10 → N10 is terminal. Opponent will lose immediately; we win.  \n- N9 leads to N12 or N5.  \n  - N12 leads to N1 or N13 → both lead eventually to terminal (win for opponent).  \n  - N5 → N8 (N8 to N2/N7/N10) → N2 → terminal (win), so N2 is a terminal, meaning N7 leads to N4 (also terminal), so all branches eventually end with opponent's loss.  \n\nHowever, N6 → terminal directly, thus forcing a loss to the opponent (they now have no move). So, from N3, move to N6 wins immediately. All other options lead to opponent having better or terminal options. N6 is the only move to a terminal directly. Hence, best move is N6.</reason>  \n<answer>N6</answer>", "episode_score": -0.6166666666666667, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N3, the legal moves are to N13, N6, and N9.  \n- N13 leads to N11 (terminal) → opponent wins if this is a winning move, so we must consider deeper outcomes.  \n- N6 leads to N10 → N10 is terminal. Opponent will lose immediately; we win.  \n- N9 leads to N12 or N5.  \n  - N12 leads to N1 or N13 → both lead eventually to terminal (win for opponent).  \n  - N5 → N8 (N8 to N2/N7/N10) → N2 → terminal (win), so N2 is a terminal, meaning N7 leads to N4 (also terminal), so all branches eventually end with opponent's loss.  \n\nHowever, N6 → terminal directly, thus forcing a loss to the opponent (they now have no move). So, from N3, move to N6 wins immediately. All other options lead to opponent having better or terminal options. N6 is the only move to a terminal directly. Hence, best move is N6.</reason>  \n<answer>N6</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N6\n\nDirected graph:\nN6 -> N10, N0\nN7 -> N4\nN2 -> terminal\nN1 -> terminal\nN12 -> N1, N13\nN4 -> terminal\nN8 -> N2, N7, N10\nN0 -> terminal\nN13 -> N11\nN5 -> N1, N8\nN9 -> N12, N5\nN3 -> N13, N6, N9\nN11 -> terminal\nN10 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N10, N0assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N6, the legal moves are to N10 and N0. Both N10 and N0 lead to terminal nodes, meaning the player who moves to either wins immediately (the opponent has no move). Thus, both moves end the game on the next turn. Since the player who makes the move wins (opponent loses), choosing either N10 or N0 is a winning move. However, since both result in an immediate end, either is optimal. N0 leads to terminal immediately, as does N10. There is no strategic advantage between them. Thus, either is valid. We choose one.</reason>\n<answer>N10</answer>", "episode_score": 0.05, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N6, the legal moves are to N10 and N0. Both N10 and N0 lead to terminal nodes, meaning the player who moves to either wins immediately (the opponent has no move). Thus, both moves end the game on the next turn. Since the player who makes the move wins (opponent loses), choosing either N10 or N0 is a winning move. However, since both result in an immediate end, either is optimal. N0 leads to terminal immediately, as does N10. There is no strategic advantage between them. Thus, either is valid. We choose one.</reason>\n<answer>N10</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N7\n\nDirected graph:\nN9 -> N0, N8\nN2 -> terminal\nN1 -> N3, N5\nN5 -> N0, N3\nN3 -> N6, N4\nN8 -> terminal\nN0 -> terminal\nN4 -> terminal\nN6 -> N2\nN7 -> N1, N9\n\nLegal moves: N1, N9assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N7, the legal moves are to N1 or N9. Moving to N9 leads to N0 or N8, both of which are terminal, so the opponent wins immediately. Moving to N1 leads to N3 or N5. From N3, we can reach N6 (which leads to N2, terminal), or N4 (terminal). From N5, we reach N0 (terminal) or N3 (which continues). The path N1 → N3 → N6 → N2 ends with N2 terminal, meaning the player who just played loses. Thus, choosing N1 forces the opponent to face the game from a position with a winning continuation. The choice to move to N1 gives a better chance of winning. Therefore, N1 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N1</answer>", "episode_score": 1.05, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N7, the legal moves are to N1 or N9. Moving to N9 leads to N0 or N8, both of which are terminal, so the opponent wins immediately. Moving to N1 leads to N3 or N5. From N3, we can reach N6 (which leads to N2, terminal), or N4 (terminal). From N5, we reach N0 (terminal) or N3 (which continues). The path N1 → N3 → N6 → N2 ends with N2 terminal, meaning the player who just played loses. Thus, choosing N1 forces the opponent to face the game from a position with a winning continuation. The choice to move to N1 gives a better chance of winning. Therefore, N1 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N1</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N6\n\nDirected graph:\nN3 -> N4, N2\nN1 -> terminal\nN4 -> N1, N7\nN5 -> terminal\nN7 -> terminal\nN8 -> N9, N0\nN6 -> N3, N4\nN2 -> N8, N5\nN0 -> N9\nN9 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N3, N4assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N6, the legal moves are to N3 or N4. Moving to N3 leads to N4 or N2. N4 leads to N1 or N7, both of which are terminal or lead to terminal (losing). N2 leads to N8 and N5 (N5 is terminal). N8 leads to N9 (terminal). So all paths from N3 or N4 eventually lead to terminal nodes. Thus, the key is to avoid immediate loss. If the opponent plays optimally, any path from N6 leads to a chain ending in terminal nodes. The goal is to maximize the chance to force a win. However, all nodes from N6 onward lead eventually to terminal nodes, and since it's a zero-sum, no immediate move wins. The only safe play is to go to N4 because it gives more control in terms of avoiding immediate terminal loss, and N4 leads to terminal nodes, thus forcing the opponent to lose. But both N3 and N4 result in terminal paths. So both are losing for the player who moves there. However, the player who takes N4 gives the opponent a chance to go into N7 or N1 — both losing. But N3 leads to N2, which leads to N5 (terminal). So N3 is slightly better as it reduces opponent's options. However, both are losing. Since N6 leads to a cycle, we must find the winning node. But from analysis, every edge leads eventually to terminal. The correct optimal node choice is to pick N4 because from N4, the path to terminal is direct and avoids longer chains, reducing opponent's flexibility. Final choice: N4.</reason>\n<answer>N4</answer>", "episode_score": -0.95, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N6, the legal moves are to N3 or N4. Moving to N3 leads to N4 or N2. N4 leads to N1 or N7, both of which are terminal or lead to terminal (losing). N2 leads to N8 and N5 (N5 is terminal). N8 leads to N9 (terminal). So all paths from N3 or N4 eventually lead to terminal nodes. Thus, the key is to avoid immediate loss. If the opponent plays optimally, any path from N6 leads to a chain ending in terminal nodes. The goal is to maximize the chance to force a win. However, all nodes from N6 onward lead eventually to terminal nodes, and since it's a zero-sum, no immediate move wins. The only safe play is to go to N4 because it gives more control in terms of avoiding immediate terminal loss, and N4 leads to terminal nodes, thus forcing the opponent to lose. But both N3 and N4 result in terminal paths. So both are losing for the player who moves there. However, the player who takes N4 gives the opponent a chance to go into N7 or N1 — both losing. But N3 leads to N2, which leads to N5 (terminal). So N3 is slightly better as it reduces opponent's options. However, both are losing. Since N6 leads to a cycle, we must find the winning node. But from analysis, every edge leads eventually to terminal. The correct optimal node choice is to pick N4 because from N4, the path to terminal is direct and avoids longer chains, reducing opponent's flexibility. Final choice: N4.</reason>\n<answer>N4</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N1\n\nDirected graph:\nN9 -> N0, N8\nN2 -> terminal\nN1 -> N3, N5\nN5 -> N0, N3\nN3 -> N6, N4\nN8 -> terminal\nN0 -> terminal\nN4 -> terminal\nN6 -> N2\nN7 -> N1, N9\n\nLegal moves: N3, N5assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N1, the legal moves are to N3 or N5. From N3, the player can move to N6 or N4, both of which lead to terminal nodes (i.e., the next player loses). From N5, moves go to N0 or N3. N0 is terminal, so moving to N3 from N5 continues the game. From N3 to N6 leads to N2 (terminal). From N6 to N2 leads to end. Therefore, the critical analysis is: moving to N5 gives opponent the chance to move to N3 and continue the game, while moving directly to N3 gives the opponent immediate access to a path that can lead to a losing condition for the opponent. Thus, choosing N3 initially gives more control in the game. Further, from N3 to N6 (to N2), or to N4 (terminal), the game ends quickly. The optimal first choice is N3 to maintain control and restrict opponent options.</reason>\n<answer>N3</answer>", "episode_score": 0.05, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N1, the legal moves are to N3 or N5. From N3, the player can move to N6 or N4, both of which lead to terminal nodes (i.e., the next player loses). From N5, moves go to N0 or N3. N0 is terminal, so moving to N3 from N5 continues the game. From N3 to N6 leads to N2 (terminal). From N6 to N2 leads to end. Therefore, the critical analysis is: moving to N5 gives opponent the chance to move to N3 and continue the game, while moving directly to N3 gives the opponent immediate access