nswer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N3\n\nDirected graph:\nN5 -> terminal\nN7 -> terminal\nN3 -> N1, N2\nN4 -> N6, N7\nN0 -> terminal\nN6 -> N0, N8\nN1 -> N5, N9\nN9 -> terminal\nN8 -> N0\nN2 -> N4, N8\n\nLegal moves: N1, N2assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N3, the legal moves are to N1 or N2. We analyze the outcome of each move. Moving to N1 leads to a path: N1 → N5 (terminal), or N1 → N9 (terminal), both terminal for the opponent — so the player wins. Moving to N2 leads to N2 → N4, which then goes to N6 or N8. N6 → N0 → terminal (loses), or N6 → N8 → N0 → terminal; N8 → N0 → terminal. All routes from N2 eventually lead to terminal states. Since N1 has terminal options, it leads to immediate win for the current player. Thus, choosing N1 maximizes the outcome.</reason>\n<answer>N1</answer>", "episode_score": -0.95, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N3, the legal moves are to N1 or N2. We analyze the outcome of each move. Moving to N1 leads to a path: N1 → N5 (terminal), or N1 → N9 (terminal), both terminal for the opponent — so the player wins. Moving to N2 leads to N2 → N4, which then goes to N6 or N8. N6 → N0 → terminal (loses), or N6 → N8 → N0 → terminal; N8 → N0 → terminal. All routes from N2 eventually lead to terminal states. Since N1 has terminal options, it leads to immediate win for the current player. Thus, choosing N1 maximizes the outcome.</reason>\n<answer>N1</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N1\n\nDirected graph:\nN5 -> terminal\nN7 -> terminal\nN3 -> N1, N2\nN4 -> N6, N7\nN0 -> terminal\nN6 -> N0, N8\nN1 -> N5, N9\nN9 -> terminal\nN8 -> N0\nN2 -> N4, N8\n\nLegal moves: N5, N9assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N1, the legal moves are to N5 or N9. Both N5 and N9 lead to terminal nodes, so moving to either results in an immediate win for the opponent (since the player who moves to a terminal node ends the game and the other player has no move). Thus, both moves lose immediately. However, since the player who cannot move loses, moving to N5 or N9 ends the game on the next player's turn, meaning the current player wins. Therefore, either move is winning. Since both are terminal, either is optimal. Choosing N9 is safer as it directly ends the game without branching. However, both are equivalent in outcome. We pick one. N9 is chosen.</reason>\n<answer>N9</answer>", "episode_score": 0.05, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N1, the legal moves are to N5 or N9. Both N5 and N9 lead to terminal nodes, so moving to either results in an immediate win for the opponent (since the player who moves to a terminal node ends the game and the other player has no move). Thus, both moves lose immediately. However, since the player who cannot move loses, moving to N5 or N9 ends the game on the next player's turn, meaning the current player wins. Therefore, either move is winning. Since both are terminal, either is optimal. Choosing N9 is safer as it directly ends the game without branching. However, both are equivalent in outcome. We pick one. N9 is chosen.</reason>\n<answer>N9</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Direc