     Thus, opponent can force a loss from N8 if N7 is taken via N8. But since N7 gives a terminal move, N1 is safe (N1 → terminal). So if the player moves to N7 → N1, opponent has no move.  \n- N9 leads to N7 or N8:  \n   - From N9, the opponent gets to choose a path from N7/N8, and they will force terminal.  \n   - From N7 → opponent can reach N8 again, and eventually N2 or N5.  \n   → N9 allows opponent to keep the game alive longer with options, so it's worse.  \n- N6 → leads to N4 → terminal (so opponent has only one option and eventually ends in terminal). So N6 gives opponent an early end.  \n\nBest outcome: Move to N7 and force opponent to lose immediately. N7 leads to a win. So the optimal choice is to pick N7.</reason>\n<answer>N7</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N7\n\nDirected graph:\nN2 -> terminal\nN9 -> N7, N8\nN1 -> terminal\nN3 -> terminal\nN5 -> N3\nN8 -> N5, N2\nN4 -> terminal\nN7 -> N1, N8\nN0 -> N7, N9, N6\nN6 -> N4\n\nLegal moves: N1, N8assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N7, the legal moves are to N1 and N8. N1 leads to terminal (win for the player who moves there, so opponent loses). N8 leads to N5 or N2, both of which eventually lead to terminal. Thus, moving to N1 immediately ends the game for the opponent. Choosing N1 is a winning move. Therefore, the optimal choice is N1.</reason>\n<answer>N1</answer>", "episode_score": 1.05, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N7, the legal moves are to N1 and N8. N1 leads to terminal (win for the player who moves there, so opponent loses). N8 le