stant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N8, the player can move to N11 or N12. If the token goes to N11, the next state is N5 or N6. From N5, paths lead to N13 (terminal) or N10 (terminal); from N6, paths go to N9 or N10. N9 leads to N2 or N1, both eventually to terminal. Thus, from N11, the opponent gains a winning path. If moving to N12, paths go to N0 or N7, both terminal. The opponent will have no legal move. Thus, moving to N12 leads to immediate win for the current player. Hence, N12 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N12</answer>", "episode_score": -0.95, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N8, the player can move to N11 or N12. If the token goes to N11, the next state is N5 or N6. From N5, paths lead to N13 (terminal) or N10 (terminal); from N6, paths go to N9 or N10. N9 leads to N2 or N1, both eventually to terminal. Thus, from N11, the opponent gains a winning path. If moving to N12, paths go to N0 or N7, both terminal. The opponent will have no legal move. Thus, moving to N12 leads to immediate win for the current player. Hence, N12 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N12</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N12\n\nDirected graph:\nN2 -> terminal\nN4 -> terminal\nN10 -> terminal\nN11 -> N5, N6\nN8 -> N11, N12\nN3 -> N4, N2\nN1 -> N3, N0\nN9 -> N2, N1\nN0 -> terminal\nN5 -> N13, N10\nN7 -> terminal\nN13 -> terminal\nN6 -> N9, N10\nN12 -> N0, N7\n\nLegal moves: N0, N7assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N12, the legal moves are to N0 or N7. Both N0 and N7 lead to terminal nodes, so moving to either results in 