ds to a potentially winning position for the opponent. Thus, moving to N2 results in immediate loss. Moving to N9 gives the opponent control of a branching path, but N9 leads to N5 which eventually can lead back to terminal through N7 → N1. However, from N4 → N0 → terminal, N4 is a safe terminal path. The only way to avoid losing is to avoid letting opponent get to N1 or N4. The best move from N8 is to N2 because it immediately avoids terminal paths and forces the opponent to play from N6 which ends immediately. Thus, N2 is a safe, terminal-avoiding choice that maximizes winning chance.</reason>\n<answer>N2</answer>", "episode_score": -0.95, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N8, the legal moves are to N9 or N2. If the player moves to N2, the opponent can move to N6 (winning the game, as N6 leads to terminal). If the player moves to N9, the opponent faces N5, which leads to N7 or N3. N7 leads to terminal through N1 or N4, so N9 leads to a potentially winning position for the opponent. Thus, moving to N2 results in immediate loss. Moving to N9 gives the opponent control of a branching path, but N9 leads to N5 which eventually can lead back to terminal through N7 → N1. However, from N4 → N0 → terminal, N4 is a safe terminal path. The only way to avoid losing is to avoid letting opponent get to N1 or N4. The best move from N8 is to N2 because it immediately avoids terminal paths and forces the opponent to play from N6 which ends immediately. Thus, N2 is a safe, terminal-avoiding choice that maximizes winning chance.</reason>\n<answer>N2</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N2\n\nDirected graph:\nN2 -> N6, N0\nN6 -> terminal\nN8 -> N9, N2\nN5 -> N7, N3\nN1 -> terminal\nN9 -> N5, N7\nN7 -> N1, N4\nN0 -> terminal\nN3 -> terminal\nN4 -> N0\n\nLegal moves: N6, N0assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N2, the legal moves are to N6 or N0. N6 leads to terminal (win for the player who moves there), so that is a winning move. N0 also leads to terminal. Both moves end the game immediately for the opponent. Since both moves result in immediate loss for the opponent, either is valid. However, choosing N6 is safer as it does not loop back to N2. But since both are terminal, either is optimal. Choosing N0 is simpler and direct. Thus, N0 is selected.</reason>\n<answer>N0</answer>", "episode_score": 0.05, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N2, the legal moves are to N6 or N0. N6 leads to terminal (win for the player who moves there), so that is a winning move. N0 also leads to terminal. Both moves end the game immediately for the opponent. Since both moves result in immediate loss for the opponent, either is valid. However, choosing N6 is safer as it does not loop back to N2. But since both are terminal, either is optimal. Choosing N0 is simpler and direct. Thus, N0 is selected.</reason>\n<answer>N0</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N13\n\nDirected graph:\nN11 -> terminal\nN4 -> N8, N3\nN0 -> N7, N1\nN12 -> N0, N4\nN7 -> N2, N3\nN13 -> N6, N0\nN8 -> terminal\nN9 -> N12, N11\nN6 -> N9, N8\nN3 -> terminal\nN10 -> terminal\nN2 -> terminal\nN1 -> N5, N10\nN5 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N6, N0assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N13, the legal moves are to N6 or N0. If the token goes to N0, the opponent faces N0, which has moves to N7 or N1. From N7, paths lead to terminal nodes (N2, N3) or N1 (which leads to N5 or N10—both terminal). From N1 leads to terminal. Thus, all moves from N0 eventually lead to terminal nodes. From N6, the path continues to N9 or N8. N8 is terminal; N9 leads to N12 (to N0 or N4). N4 leads to N3 (terminal) or N8 (terminal). N6 path ends in terminal, so both paths are losing for the opponent. Thus, choosing N6 leads to better game outcome as it gives opponent a forced move into losing situations. Therefore, N6 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N6</answer>", "episode_score": -0.95, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N13, the legal moves are to N6 or N0. If the token goes to N0, the opponent faces N0, which has moves to N7 or N1. From N7, paths lead to terminal nodes (N2, N3) or N1 (which leads to N5 or N10—both terminal). From N1 leads to terminal. Thus, all moves from N0 eventually lead to terminal nodes. From N6, the path continues to N9 or N8. N8 is terminal; N9 leads to N12 (to N0 or N4). N4 leads to N3 (terminal) or N8 (terminal). N6 path ends in terminal, so both paths are losing for the opponent. Thus, choosing N6 leads to better game outcome as it gives opponent a forced move into losing situations. Therefore, N6 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N6</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N6\n\nDirected graph:\nN11 -> terminal\nN4 -> N8, N3\nN0 -> N7, N1\nN12 -> N0, N4\nN7 -> N2, N3\nN13 -> N6, N0\nN8 -> terminal\nN9 -> N12, N11\nN6 -> N9, N8\nN3 -> terminal\nN10 -> terminal\nN2 -> terminal\nN1 -> N5, N10\nN5 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N9, N8assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N6, the legal moves are to N9 and N8. N8 leads directly to terminal, so the player who moves to N8 wins immediately (the opponent has no move). Thus, moving to N8 is a winning move. Moving to N9 leads to N12 or N11; N11 is terminal, so the opponent wins if they move there. From N12, the path continues to N0 or N4, both of which have paths to terminal. Thus, N9 leads to a position where the opponent can force a win. Therefore, choosing N8 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N8</answer>", "episode_score": 1.05, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N6, the legal moves are to N9 and N8. N8 leads directly to terminal, so the player who moves to N8 wins immediately (the opponent has no move). Thus, moving to N8 is a winning move. Moving to N9 leads to N12 or N11; N11 is terminal, so the opponent wins if they move there. From N12, the path continues to N0 or N4, both of which have paths to terminal. Thus, N9 leads to a position where the opponent can force a win. Therefore, choosing N8 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N8</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N1\n\nDirected graph:\nN0 -> terminal\nN11 -> N8, N13\nN5 -> N3, N2\nN8 -> N3, N5\nN9 -> N6, N10\nN1 -> N7, N9\nN12 -> terminal\nN7 -> N4, N13\nN4 -> terminal\nN6 -> N12, N2\nN13 -> terminal\nN2 -> terminal\nN10 -> N0, N11\nN3 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N7, N9assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N1, the legal moves are to N7 or N9. From N7, the next moves are to N4 (terminal) or N13 (terminal), both of which end the game immediately for the opponent. From N9, moves go to N6 or N10. N6 leads to N12 (terminal) or N2 (terminal); N10 leads to N0 or N11. N11 leads to N8 or N13, which are both terminal. Since all such paths eventually lead to terminal nodes, the player who moves to N7 or N9 will eventually force the opponent to lose. However, choosing N9 gives more branching paths, potentially allowing for longer play and better control. But in this zero-sum, first-move advantage game, both N7 and N9 are terminal-adjacent. Since N7 leads directly to immediate terminal states from the opponent’s perspective, choosing N7 immediately ends the game for the opponent. Thus, N7 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N7</answer>", "episode_score": -0.95, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N1, the legal moves are to N7 or N9. From N7, the next moves are to N4 (terminal) or N13 (terminal), both of which end the game immediately for the opponent. From N9, moves go to N6 or N10. N6 leads to N12 (terminal) or N2 (terminal); N10 leads to N0 or N11. N11 leads to N8 or N13, which are both terminal. Since all such paths eventually lead to terminal nodes, the player who moves to N7 or N9 will eventually force the opponent to lose. However, choosing N9 gives more branching paths, potentially allowing for longer play and better control. But in this zero-sum, first-move advantage game, both N7 and N9 are terminal-adjacent. Since N7 leads directly to immediate terminal states from the opponent’s perspective, choosing N7 immediately ends the game for the opponent. Thus, N7 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N7</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N7\n\nDirected graph:\nN0 -> terminal\nN11 -> N8, N13\nN5 -> N3, N2\nN8 -> N3, N5\nN9 -> N6, N10\nN1 -> N7, N9\nN12 -> terminal\nN7 -> N4, N13\nN4 -> terminal\nN6 -> N12, N2\nN13 -> terminal\nN2 -> terminal\nN10 -> N0, N11\nN3 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N4, N13assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N7, the legal moves are to N4 and N13. Both N4 and N13 lead to terminal nodes, so whichever edge is taken, the current player wins immediately (the opponent has no move). Thus, both moves end the game immediately. Since the player who makes the move wins, either choice is optimal. However, N13 also leads to terminal directly, as does N4. Both are winning moves. Choosing either is equivalent. We select N13 as it is a move available in a branch with higher node number, but both are equal. Still, since the goal is to maximize outcome and both end the game immediately, either is valid. We pick N13.</reason>\n<answer>N13</answer>", "episode_score": 0.05, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N7, the legal moves are to N4 and N13. Both N4 and N13 lead to terminal nodes, so whichever edge is taken, the current player wins immediately (the opponent has no move). Thus, both moves end the game immediately. Since the player who makes the move wins, either choice is optimal. However, N13 also leads to terminal directly, as does N4. Both are winning moves. Choosing either is equivalent. We select N13 as it is a move available in a branch with higher node number, but both are equal. Still, since the goal is to maximize outcome and both end the game immediately, either is valid. We pick N13.</reason>\n<answer>N13</answer>"}]
