ponse that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N1\n\nDirected graph:\nN0 -> N2, N7\nN6 -> terminal\nN1 -> N9, N2, N0\nN8 -> terminal\nN9 -> N10, N13\nN13 -> N5\nN12 -> terminal\nN5 -> terminal\nN10 -> N7, N3\nN7 -> N11, N6\nN2 -> N9\nN11 -> terminal\nN3 -> N4, N12\nN4 -> N8\n\nLegal moves: N9, N2, N0assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N1, the player can move to N9, N2, or N0. Moving to N0 leads to N2 or N7, both of which eventually lead to terminal nodes or loops. Moving to N2 leads to N9, which branches to N10 or N13; N13 leads to N5 (terminal), N10 leads to N7 or N3. N3 leads to N4 or N12; N4 leads to N8 (terminal), so this path risks looping or losing. N9 → N10 → N7 → N6 ends in terminal, or N9 → N10 → N3 → N4 → N8 also terminal. N1 → N9 → N13 → N5 ends immediately. N1 → N0 → N2 → N9 is a loop back to N9. Thus, to avoid losing immediately, avoid loops. N1 → N7 → N6 wins (N6 is terminal). N1 → N7 leads to N11 (win) or N6 (win). So N7 is a winning move. N1 → N2 → N9 → N10 → N3 → N12 is terminal. But N7 leads directly to terminal. So best first move is N7 which leads to terminal on N6, or N11, both terminal. N7 is a strong move. Thus, choosing N7 leads to terminal for opponent. Final outcome: optimal is N7.</reason>\n<answer>N7</answer>", "episode_score": -1.5, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N1, the player can move to N9, N2, or N0. Moving to N0 leads to N2 or N7, both of which eventually lead to terminal nodes or loops. Moving to N2 leads to N9, which branches to N10 or N13; N13 leads to N5 (terminal), N10 leads to N7 or N3. N3 leads to N4 or N12; N4 leads to N8 (terminal), so this path risks looping or losing. N9 → N10 → N7 → N6 ends in terminal, or N9 → N10 → N3 → N4 → N8 also terminal. N1 → N9 → N13 → N5 ends immediately. N1 → N0 → N2 → N9 is a loop back to N9. Thus, to avoid losing immediately, avoid loops. N1 → N7 → N6 wins (N6 is terminal). N1 → N7 leads to N11 (win) or N6 (win). So N7 is a winning move. N1 → N2 → N9 → N10 → N3 → N12 is terminal. But N7 leads directly to terminal. So best first move is N7 which leads to terminal on N6, or N11, both terminal. N7 is a strong move. Thus, choosing N7 leads to terminal for opponent. Final outcome: optimal is N7.</reason>\n<answer>N7</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N1\n\nDirected graph:\nN2 -> N5\nN0 -> terminal\nN5 -> N7, N9\nN1 -> N5, N6\nN9 -> N7, N4\nN8 -> terminal\nN7 -> N0, N3\nN6 -> N2, N8\nN4 -> terminal\nN3 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N5, N6assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N1, the player can move to N5 or N6. Moving to N6 leads to N2 or N8. N8 leads directly to terminal (win for opponent), so N2 is better. N2 leads to N5. From N5, the opponent can go to N7 or N9. N7 leads to N0 or N3 (both terminal), so opponent wins. N9 leads to N7 or N4; N4 is terminal. Thus, from N5, opponent can force a win. Moving to N5 first allows the opponent to move to N7 or N9, both of which eventually lead to terminal nodes for the current player. Thus, moving to N6 (via N2 to N5) leads to a win for the opponent. However, if we go to N6 and then N8, the opponent is forced to move on terminal. So, N6 → N8 leads to terminal on the next move, meaning the current player wins. Thus, N6 is better. So, optimal is to go to N6.</reason>\n<answer>N6</answer>", "episode_score": -0.95, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N1, the player can move to N5 or N6. Moving to N6 leads to N2 or N8. N8 leads directly to terminal (win for opponent), so N2 is better. N2 leads to N5. From N5, the opponent can go to N7 or N9. N7 leads to N0 or N3 (both terminal), so opponent wins. N9 leads to N7 or N4; N4 is terminal. Thus, from N5, opponent can force a win. Moving to N5 first allows the opponent to move to N7 or N9, both of which eventually lead to terminal nodes for the current player. Thus, moving to N6 (via N2 to N5) leads to a win for the opponent. However, if we go to N6 and then N8, the opponent is forced to move on terminal. So, N6 → N8 leads to terminal on the next move, meaning the current player wins. Thus, N6 is better. So, optimal is to go to N6.</reason>\n<answer>N6</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N6\n\nDirected graph:\nN2 -> N5\nN0 -> terminal\nN5 -> N7, N9\nN1 -> N5, N6\nN9 -> N7, N4\nN8 -> terminal\nN7 -> N0, N3\nN6 -> N2, N8\nN4 -> terminal\nN3 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N2, N8assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N6, the legal moves are to N2 and N8. N8 leads directly to terminal, so the player who moves to N8 wins immediately (the opponent has no move). N2 leads to N5, and from N5 the opponent can move to N7 or N9. Moving to N7 leads to N0 or N3, both terminal. Moving to N9 leads to N7 or N4 (N4 is terminal). Thus, from N5, the opponent can force a win. Therefore, moving to N2 allows the opponent to force a win. Choosing N8 results in an immediate win. Thus, the optimal move is to N8.</reason>\n<answer>N8</answer>", "episode_score": 1.05, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N6, the legal moves are to N2 and N8. N8 leads directly to terminal, so the player who moves to N8 wins immediately (the opponent has no move). N2 leads to N5, and from N5 the opponent can move to N7 or N9. Moving to N7 leads to N0 or N3, both terminal. Moving to N9 leads to N7 or N4 (N4 is terminal). Thus, from N5, the opponent can force a win. Therefore, moving to N2 allows the opponent to force a win. Choosing N8 results in an immediate win. Thus, the optimal move is to N8.</reason>\n<answer>N8</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N4\n\nDirected graph:\nN9 -> N0\nN3 -> N7\nN6 -> terminal\nN1 -> N8\nN2 -> N6, N3\nN7 -> terminal\nN5 -> terminal\nN8 -> terminal\nN0 -> N2, N5, N1\nN4 -> N0, N9, N2\n\nLegal moves: N0, N9, N2assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N4, the legal moves are to N0, N9, and N2. N9 leads to N0, which then leads to N2 or N5 or N1. N2 leads to N6 (terminal) or N3 (leads to N7, terminal). N0 and N2 both lead to terminal positions or cycles. However, N5 and N8 lead to terminal. Evaluating backwards, N6, N7, N8, and N5 are terminal (win for the opponent). The move to N2 leads to a path where either N6 (terminal) or N3 (to N7 terminal), so opponent loses. So N2 leads to a forced loss for the opponent. Thus, choosing N2 is optimal. Moving to N0 or N9 leads to positions where the opponent can move to terminal or win. Thus, N2 maximizes outcome.</reason>\n<answer>N2</answer>", "episode_score": -0.6166666666666667, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N4, the legal moves are to N0, N9, and N2. N9 leads to N0, which then leads to N2 or N5 or N1. N2 leads to N6 (terminal) or N3 (leads to N7, terminal). N0 and N2 both lead to terminal positions or cycles. However, N5 and N8 lead to terminal. Evaluating backwards, N6, N7, N8, and N5 are terminal (win for the opponent). The move to N2 leads to a path where either N6 (terminal) or N3 (to N7 terminal), so opponent loses. So N2 leads to a forced loss for the opponent. Thus, choosing N2 is optimal. Moving to N0 or N9 leads to positions where the opponent can move to terminal or win. Thus, N2 maximizes outcome.</reason>\n<answer>N2</answer>"}]
