, which is terminal. Thus, moving to N2 is a winning move. Choosing N2 maximizes the outcome.</reason>\n<answer>N2</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N8\n\nDirected graph:\nN1 -> N2\nN10 -> N9, N1\nN9 -> terminal\nN3 -> N10, N6\nN7 -> terminal\nN5 -> N3, N0, N10\nN11 -> terminal\nN12 -> N4, N5\nN2 -> terminal\nN0 -> terminal\nN8 -> N13, N5, N12\nN6 -> terminal\nN13 -> N7\nN4 -> N11, N6\n\nLegal moves: N13, N5, N12assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N8, the legal moves are to N13, N5, and N12. We analyze the outcomes of each move using the structure of the graph and terminal nodes.  \n- Move to N13: N13 → N7 → terminal ⇒ the opponent has no moves ⇒ win.  \n- Move to N5: N5 → N3, N0, or N10.  \n  - N0 or N10 → terminal ⇒ opponent wins immediately.  \n  - N3 → N6 (terminal), N10 → opponent can win via N10→N9→terminal or N10→N1→N2→terminal. From N3, all paths lead to opponent winning. So N5 leads to opponent win.  \n- Move to N12: N12 → N4 or N5.  \n  - N5: as above, leads to opponent win.  \n  - N4 → N6 (terminal) or N4 → N11 (terminal) ⇒ opponent wins. So again opponent wins.  \nThus, moving to N13 immediately forces the opponent into a position where they have no legal moves. This is optimal. Therefore, N13 is the best choice.</reason>\n<answer>N13</answer>", "episode_score": -0.6166666666666667, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N8, the legal moves are to N13, N5, and N12. We analyze the outcomes of each move using the structure of the graph and terminal nodes.  \n- Move to N13: N13 → N7 → terminal ⇒ the opponent has no