hat, I win. Therefore, the optimal choice is to move to N7.</reason>\n<answer>N7</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N8\n\nDirected graph:\nN9 -> terminal\nN2 -> N12, N1\nN0 -> N10, N6\nN5 -> terminal\nN12 -> terminal\nN11 -> N13, N5\nN1 -> terminal\nN13 -> terminal\nN6 -> terminal\nN8 -> N11, N7\nN10 -> N9, N3\nN4 -> N6, N12\nN7 -> N0, N4\nN3 -> N2, N1\n\nLegal moves: N11, N7assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N8, the player can move to N11 or N7. Moving to N11 leads to N13 or N5; both N13 and N5 are terminal, so the opponent wins immediately. Moving to N7 leads to N0 or N4. From N0, the opponent can go to N10 or N6; N6 is terminal, so if the opponent moves to N6, they win. From N10, they can go to N9 or N3; N9 is terminal. From N3, they can go to N2 or N1; both N2 and N1 eventually lead to terminal nodes. Thus, moving to N7 allows the opponent to eventually win unless we block them. However, from N7 → N4 → N12 (terminal) or N4 → N6 (terminal). So from N7, opponent has no safe move. Actually, any edge leads to terminal or forcing opponent to lose. But analyzing: N8 → N11 → N5 or N13: both terminal, so the opponent loses immediately. So N11 is winning. Therefore, choosing N11 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N11</answer>", "episode_score": -0.95, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N8, the player can move to N11 or N7. Moving to N11 leads to N13 or N5; both N13 and N5 are terminal, so the opponent wins immediately. Moving to N7 leads to N0 or N4. From N0, the opponent can go to N10 or N6; N6 is terminal, so if t