[2026-08-13 15:08:12] [agentic_pipeline.py (516)] [INFO] [DRIVER 0 / 1][PID 1538566] [{"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N0\n\nDirected graph:\nN8 -> terminal\nN9 -> N4, N1\nN5 -> N2, N8\nN0 -> N5, N6, N9\nN6 -> N7\nN3 -> terminal\nN2 -> N3\nN7 -> N5, N8\nN1 -> terminal\nN4 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N5, N6, N9assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N0, the player can move to N5, N6, or N9. We analyze each:  \n- N5 → N2, N8: N8 leads to terminal (win for opponent), N2 → N3 → terminal (opponent wins). So both paths lose for current player.  \n- N6 → N7 → either N5 or N8; both eventually lead to terminal, so losing.  \n- N9 → N4 (terminal) or N1 (terminal); both lead immediately to opponent's win.  \nAll moves lead to terminal or opponent winning. However, since all paths from any node eventually end in terminal, each move forces the opponent to face terminal. Thus, each option results in the current player losing. But since all are equivalent in outcome, any choice is equal. Choosing N9 is safe and ends immediately. However, N5 or N6 lead to deeper play but still loss. But the first move must maximize outcome. As all moves lead to opponent's win, any is equivalent. Pick N9 as it is a terminal move option, reducing game length. Final choice: N9.</reason>  \n<answer>N9</answer>", "episode_score": -0.6166666666666667, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N0, the player can move to N5, N6, or N9. We analyze each:  \n- N5 → N2, N8: N8 leads to terminal (win for opponent), N2 → N3 → terminal (opponent wins). So both paths lose for current player.  \n- N6 → N7 → either N5 or N8; both eventually lead to terminal, so losing.  \n- N9 → N4 (terminal) or N1 (terminal); both lead immediately to opponent's win.  \nAll moves lead to terminal or opponent winning. However, since all paths from any node eventually end in terminal, each move forces the opponent to face terminal. Thus, each option results in the current player losing. But since all are equivalent in outcome, any choice is equal. Choosing N9 is safe and ends immediately. However, N5 or N6 lead to deeper play but still loss. But the first move must maximize outcome. As all moves lead to opponent's win, any is equivalent. Pick N9 as it is a terminal move option, reducing game length. Final choice: N9.</reason>  \n<answer>N9</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N9\n\nDirected graph:\nN8 -> terminal\nN9 -> N4, N1\nN5 -> N2, N8\nN0 -> N5, N6, N9\nN6 -> N7\nN3 -> terminal\nN2 -> N3\nN7 -> N5, N8\nN1 -> terminal\nN4 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N4, N1assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N9, the legal moves are to N4 or N1. Both N4 and N1 lead directly to terminal nodes, so the player who moves to either wins immediately (the opponent has no move). Thus, both moves end the game immediately. Since the player who makes the move wins (the opponent loses), choosing either N4 or N1 is optimal. However, since both are terminal moves, either is equally good. We choose one. N1 is selected.</reason>\n<answer>N1</answer>", "episode_score": 0.05, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N9, the legal moves are to N4 or N1. Both N4 and N1 lead directly to terminal nodes, so the player who moves to either wins immediately (the opponent has no move). Thus, both moves end the game immediately. Since the player who makes the move wins (the opponent loses), choosing either N4 or N1 is optimal. However, since both are terminal moves, either is equally good. We choose one. N1 is selected.</reason>\n<answer>N1</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N4\n\nDirected graph:\nN8 -> N2, N9\nN7 -> terminal\nN0 -> N7, N1\nN4 -> N3, N8\nN2 -> N5\nN3 -> N2, N6\nN1 -> terminal\nN9 -> N6, N0\nN6 -> terminal\nN5 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N3, N8assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N4, the legal moves are to N3 or N8. Analyzing each:  \n- Move to N3: N3 → N2 → N5 → terminal (terminal win for the opponent). So, after N4→N3, opponent moves N2→N5 and wins.  \n- Move to N8: N8 → N2 → N5 (opponent wins again).  \nAny move from N4 leads opponent to terminal. Thus, both options result in the opponent winning. However, since both options are equivalent in outcome (loss), either choice is optimal. Choosing N3 is valid and safe. But since N8 leads to N9 path which loops back to N0 (N9→N0→N7→terminal), we examine further: N8→N9→N0→N7→terminal. Still opponent wins. So both paths lead to opponent winning. Choose one: N3 is safe and direct. However, N3 leads to N2→N5 which is terminal. So both are equal. Choosing N3 maximizes minimizing opponent advantage. Final decision: N3.</reason>\n<answer>N3</answer>", "episode_score": -0.95, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N4, the legal moves are to N3 or N8. Analyzing each:  \n- Move to N3: N3 → N2 → N5 → terminal (terminal win for the opponent). So, after N4→N3, opponent moves N2→N5 and wins.  \n- Move to N8: N8 → N2 → N5 (opponent wins again).  \nAny move from N4 leads opponent to terminal. Thus, both options result in the opponent winning. However, since both options are equivalent in outcome (loss), either choice is optimal. Choosing N3 is valid and safe. But since N8 leads to N9 path which loops back to N0 (N9→N0→N7→terminal), we examine further: N8→N9→N0→N7→terminal. Still opponent wins. So both paths lead to opponent winning. Choose one: N3 is safe and direct. However, N3 leads to N2→N5 which is terminal. So both are equal. Choosing N3 maximizes minimizing opponent advantage. Final decision: N3.</reason>\n<answer>N3</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N3\n\nDirected graph:\nN8 -> N2, N9\nN7 -> terminal\nN0 -> N7, N1\nN4 -> N3, N8\nN2 -> N5\nN3 -> N2, N6\nN1 -> terminal\nN9 -> N6, N0\nN6 -> terminal\nN5 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N2, N6assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N3, the legal moves are to N2 or N6. N6 leads directly to terminal (win for the player who moves there), so the opponent cannot move after that. Thus, moving to N6 wins immediately. Moving to N2 leads to N5, which is terminal, so the opponent wins. Therefore, choosing N6 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N6</answer>", "episode_score": 1.05, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N3, the legal moves are to N2 or N6. N6 leads directly to terminal (win for the player who moves there), so the opponent cannot move after that. Thus, moving to N6 wins immediately. Moving to N2 leads to N5, which is terminal, so the opponent wins. Therefore, choosing N6 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N6</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N5\n\nDirected graph:\nN5 -> N6, N1\nN2 -> N12, N7\nN12 -> terminal\nN8 -> terminal\nN13 -> terminal\nN9 -> N11, N8\nN4 -> N12, N0\nN7 -> N9, N8\nN3 -> terminal\nN6 -> N13, N3\nN11 -> terminal\nN1 -> N4, N2\nN10 -> terminal\nN0 -> N10, N11\n\nLegal moves: N6, N1assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N5, the legal moves are to N6 or N1. We evaluate each option:  \n- Moving to N6 leads to N13 or N3.  \n  - N3 → terminal → loss for the next player (current player wins if opponent loses).  \n  - N13 → terminal → same, opponent loses.  \n  N13 or N3 both end immediately via terminal nodes. So N6 is a winning move.  \n- Moving to N1 leads to N4 or N2.  \n  N4 → N12 (terminal) or N0  \n    N0 → N10 (terminal) or N11 (terminal) → both lead to terminal → all terminal.  \n  N2 → N12 (terminal) → opponent wins immediately.  \n  Any continuation from N1 ends via terminal nodes or back to N4/N0 → all paths terminate eventually.  \nThus, N6 leads to immediate terminal states, but the player who moves first from N6 wins, as all their moves end the game for opponent. Hence, choosing N6 is optimal.</reason>  \n<answer>N6</answer>", "episode_score": -0.95, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N5, the legal moves are to N6 or N1. We evaluate each option:  \n- Moving to N6 leads to N13 or N3.  \n  - N3 → terminal → loss for the next player (current player wins if opponent loses).  \n  - N13 → terminal → same, opponent loses.  \n  N13 or N3 both end immediately via terminal nodes. So N6 is a winning move.  \n- Moving to N1 leads to N4 or N2.  \n  N4 → N12 (terminal) or N0  \n    N0 → N10 (terminal) or N11 (terminal) → both lead to terminal → all terminal.  \n  N2 → N12 (terminal) → opponent wins immediately.  \n  Any continuation from N1 ends via terminal nodes or back to N4/N0 → all paths terminate eventually.  \nThus, N6 leads to immediate terminal states, but the player who moves first from N6 wins, as all their moves end the game for opponent. Hence, choosing N6 is optimal.</reason>  \n<answer>N6</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N6\n\nDirected graph:\nN5 -> N6, N1\nN2 -> N12, N7\nN12 -> terminal\nN8 -> terminal\nN13 -> terminal\nN9 -> N11, N8\nN4 -> N12, N0\nN7 -> N9, N8\nN3 -> terminal\nN6 -> N13, N3\nN11 -> terminal\nN1 -> N4, N2\nN10 -> terminal\nN0 -> N10, N11\n\nLegal moves: N13, N3assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N6, the legal moves are to N13 and N3. N13 and N3 are both terminal nodes, meaning the player who moves to them wins immediately (the opponent has no move). Thus, both moves end the game. Since the player who makes the move wins (the opponent loses), choosing either N13 or N3 is a winning move. Both are terminal, so either is optimal. However, since the goal is to maximize outcome and both lead to immediate win, either is valid. We choose one. N3 is selected.</reason>\n<answer>N3</answer>", "episode_score": 0.05, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N6, the legal moves are to N13 and N3. N13 and N3 are both terminal nodes, meaning the player who moves to them wins immediately (the opponent has no move). Thus, both moves end the game. Since the player who makes the move wins (the opponent loses), choosing either N13 or N3 is a winning move. Both are terminal, so either is optimal. However, since the goal is to maximize outcome and both lead to immediate win, either is valid. We choose one. N3 is selected.</reason>\n<answer>N3</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N6\n\nDirected graph:\nN2 -> N0, N9\nN1 -> terminal\nN3 -> terminal\nN5 -> N4\nN7 -> N3, N0\nN0 -> terminal\nN9 -> N5, N1\nN8 -> N7, N2\nN4 -> terminal\nN6 -> N8, N2\n\nLegal moves: N8, N2assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N6, the legal moves are to N8 or N2.  \n- Moving to N2: N2 → N0 (terminal) → player who moves to N0 wins immediately (the opponent has no move). So, if I move to N2, I force the opponent into a terminal state on their turn.  \n- Moving to N8: N8 → N7 or N2.  \n   - N7 → N3 or N0: N3 or N0 are terminal, so these lead to opponent losing.  \n   - N2 → N0, terminal.  \nThus, from N8, opponent has a winning path. But if we pick N2, the opponent is immediately forced into terminal states. So, N2 is a better move.  \nChoosing N2 leads to an immediate win for the current player. Thus, N2 is optimal.</reason>  \n<answer>N2</answer>", "episode_score": -0.95, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N6, the legal moves are to N8 or N2.  \n- Moving to N2: N2 → N0 (terminal) → player who moves to N0 wins immediately (the opponent has no move). So, if I move to N2, I force the opponent into a terminal state on their turn.  \n- Moving to N8: N8 → N7 or N2.  \n   - N7 → N3 or N0: N3 or N0 are terminal, so these lead to opponent losing.  \n   - N2 → N0, terminal.  \nThus, from N8, opponent has a winning path. But if we pick N2, the opponent is immediately forced into terminal states. So, N2 is a better move.  \nChoosing N2 leads to an immediate win for the current player. Thus, N2 is optimal.</reason>  \n<answer>N2</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N2\n\nDirected graph:\nN2 -> N0, N9\nN1 -> terminal\nN3 -> terminal\nN5 -> N4\nN7 -> N3, N0\nN0 -> terminal\nN9 -> N5, N1\nN8 -> N7, N2\nN4 -> terminal\nN6 -> N8, N2\n\nLegal moves: N0, N9assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N2, the legal moves are to N0 or N9. N0 leads directly to terminal (win for the player who moves there, as the opponent has no move). N9 leads to N5 or N1; N1 is terminal, so moving to N1 ends the game immediately. N5 leads to N4, which is terminal. Thus, moving to N9 gives the opponent a move to N1 or N4, both terminal. However, moving directly to N0 ends the game immediately. Therefore, choosing N0 is a winning move. The optimal choice is N0.</reason>\n<answer>N0</answer>", "episode_score": 1.05, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N2, the legal moves are to N0 or N9. N0 leads directly to terminal (win for the player who moves there, as the opponent has no move). N9 leads to N5 or N1; N1 is terminal, so moving to N1 ends the game immediately. N5 leads to N4, which is terminal. Thus, moving to N9 gives the opponent a move to N1 or N4, both terminal. However, moving directly to N0 ends the game immediately. Therefore, choosing N0 is a winning move. The optimal choice is N0.</reason>\n<answer>N0</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N6\n\nDirected graph:\nN0 -> N2\nN4 -> N9, N8\nN2 -> N5, N1, N8\nN5 -> N3\nN9 -> N7\nN7 -> terminal\nN8 -> terminal\nN3 -> terminal\nN6 -> N4, N0, N2\nN1 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N4, N0, N2assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N6, the legal moves are to N4, N0, or N2. We analyze each option to find the one that leads to a winning position (i.e., forces the opponent into a terminal or losing state). \n\n- From N4: Next options are N9 → N7 (terminal), N8 → terminal. Either way, the opponent eventually reaches a terminal, so this is a winning move. \n- From N0 → N2 → N5 → N3 (terminal), N1 (terminal). Both are terminal. So N0 leads to opponent winning.\n- From N2 → N5 → N3 (terminal), N1 (terminal), N8 → terminal. Again, all lead to terminal for opponent. Thus, N2 is losing.\n\nBest choice: N4, since all paths from N4 eventually lead to opponent winning. So N4 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N4</answer>", "episode_score": -0.6166666666666667, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N6, the legal moves are to N4, N0, or N2. We analyze each option to find the one that leads to a winning position (i.e., forces the opponent into a terminal or losing state). \n\n- From N4: Next options are N9 → N7 (terminal), N8 → terminal. Either way, the opponent eventually reaches a terminal, so this is a winning move. \n- From N0 → N2 → N5 → N3 (terminal), N1 (terminal). Both are terminal. So N0 leads to opponent winning.\n- From N2 → N5 → N3 (terminal), N1 (terminal), N8 → terminal. Again, all lead to terminal for opponent. Thus, N2 is losing.\n\nBest choice: N4, since all paths from N4 eventually lead to opponent winning. So N4 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N4</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N4\n\nDirected graph:\nN0 -> N2\nN4 -> N9, N8\nN2 -> N5, N1, N8\nN5 -> N3\nN9 -> N7\nN7 -> terminal\nN8 -> terminal\nN3 -> terminal\nN6 -> N4, N0, N2\nN1 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N9, N8assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N4, the legal moves are to N9 or N8. Both N9 and N8 lead to terminal nodes (win for the player who moves there, as the opponent has no move). Thus, whichever node is chosen, the current player wins immediately. Since both moves result in an immediate win, either is optimal. Choosing N8 is safe and direct. However, since both are winning moves, any is valid. We select N8 as it is a direct terminal move.</reason>\n<answer>N8</answer>", "episode_score": 1.05, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N4, the legal moves are to N9 or N8. Both N9 and N8 lead to terminal nodes (win for the player who moves there, as the opponent has no move). Thus, whichever node is chosen, the current player wins immediately. Since both moves result in an immediate win, either is optimal. Choosing N8 is safe and direct. However, since both are winning moves, any is valid. We select N8 as it is a direct terminal move.</reason>\n<answer>N8</answer>"}]
