[2026-08-13 15:12:32] [agentic_pipeline.py (516)] [INFO] [DRIVER 0 / 1][PID 1538566] [{"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N3\n\nDirected graph:\nN9 -> terminal\nN2 -> terminal\nN6 -> N0, N8\nN0 -> terminal\nN4 -> terminal\nN8 -> N0, N2\nN7 -> N4\nN5 -> N1\nN1 -> N9, N6\nN3 -> N8, N7, N5\n\nLegal moves: N8, N7, N5assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N3, the legal moves are to N8, N7, and N5. We analyze each option:\n\n- N3 → N5: N5 → N1 → leads to N1 branching to N9 or N6. N9 is terminal (win for opponent), N6 leads to N0 or N8. N0 and N8 lead to terminal. So, if opponent plays optimally, they will win from N6 or N1.\n- N3 → N7: N7 → N4 → terminal. Player with no legal move loses → this results in immediate win (opponent has no move).\n- N3 → N8: N8 → N0 or N2. N0 → terminal, N2 → terminal. Thus opponent can only play to terminal, and the player wins.\n\nSo, N7 leads to terminal directly; opponent cannot move after. That gives a win. N8 and N5 result in terminal but with more options. However, N7 leads to immediate terminal, which is optimal. Choosing N7 wins immediately. Thus, N7 is best.</reason>\n<answer>N7</answer>", "episode_score": -0.6166666666666667, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N3, the legal moves are to N8, N7, and N5. We analyze each option:\n\n- N3 → N5: N5 → N1 → leads to N1 branching to N9 or N6. N9 is terminal (win for opponent), N6 leads to N0 or N8. N0 and N8 lead to terminal. So, if opponent plays optimally, they will win from N6 or N1.\n- N3 → N7: N7 → N4 → terminal. Player with no legal move loses → this results in immediate win (opponent has no move).\n- N3 → N8: N8 → N0 or N2. N0 → terminal, N2 → terminal. Thus opponent can only play to terminal, and the player wins.\n\nSo, N7 leads to terminal directly; opponent cannot move after. That gives a win. N8 and N5 result in terminal but with more options. However, N7 leads to immediate terminal, which is optimal. Choosing N7 wins immediately. Thus, N7 is best.</reason>\n<answer>N7</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N7\n\nDirected graph:\nN9 -> terminal\nN2 -> terminal\nN6 -> N0, N8\nN0 -> terminal\nN4 -> terminal\nN8 -> N0, N2\nN7 -> N4\nN5 -> N1\nN1 -> N9, N6\nN3 -> N8, N7, N5\n\nLegal moves: N4assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N7, the only legal move is to N4, which leads to terminal. Thus, the first player (me) moves to N4 and wins immediately. No other move is available, so this is the only option. Since the opponent has no legal move after my move, I win. Therefore, the optimal choice is to move to N4.</reason>\n<answer>N4</answer>", "episode_score": 0.05, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N7, the only legal move is to N4, which leads to terminal. Thus, the first player (me) moves to N4 and wins immediately. No other move is available, so this is the only option. Since the opponent has no legal move after my move, I win. Therefore, the optimal choice is to move to N4.</reason>\n<answer>N4</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N3\n\nDirected graph:\nN0 -> terminal\nN9 -> N4, N8\nN5 -> terminal\nN3 -> N8, N2, N9\nN7 -> terminal\nN2 -> N5\nN1 -> N6\nN6 -> terminal\nN8 -> N0, N4\nN4 -> N1, N7\n\nLegal moves: N8, N2, N9assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N3, the legal moves are to N8, N2, or N9. We analyze each:\n\n- Moving to N8: Next state is N8, then opponent moves to N0 (loss) or N4. From N4: can go to N1 (to N6 terminal), or N7 (terminal). So opponent can win from N4. If opponent picks N4, then we are in N1 or N7 (terminal) and they win. So N8 leads to opponent winning.\n\n- Moving to N2: N2 → N5 (terminal). Player who starts after N2 wins the next round by forcing terminal. Thus, if we move to N2, we move opponent into terminal (they lose).\n\n- Moving to N9: N9 → N4 or N8. As above, N8 leads opponent to terminal, N4 leads to N4 → N7 (terminal). Thus, N9 leads to opponent being forced to terminal.\n\nThe best outcome is choosing N2, which immediately leads to opponent losing on their turn. So the optimal move is N2.</reason>\n<answer>N2</answer>", "episode_score": -0.6166666666666667, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N3, the legal moves are to N8, N2, or N9. We analyze each:\n\n- Moving to N8: Next state is N8, then opponent moves to N0 (loss) or N4. From N4: can go to N1 (to N6 terminal), or N7 (terminal). So opponent can win from N4. If opponent picks N4, then we are in N1 or N7 (terminal) and they win. So N8 leads to opponent winning.\n\n- Moving to N2: N2 → N5 (terminal). Player who starts after N2 wins the next round by forcing terminal. Thus, if we move to N2, we move opponent into terminal (they lose).\n\n- Moving to N9: N9 → N4 or N8. As above, N8 leads opponent to terminal, N4 leads to N4 → N7 (terminal). Thus, N9 leads to opponent being forced to terminal.\n\nThe best outcome is choosing N2, which immediately leads to opponent losing on their turn. So the optimal move is N2.</reason>\n<answer>N2</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N2\n\nDirected graph:\nN0 -> terminal\nN9 -> N4, N8\nN5 -> terminal\nN3 -> N8, N2, N9\nN7 -> terminal\nN2 -> N5\nN1 -> N6\nN6 -> terminal\nN8 -> N0, N4\nN4 -> N1, N7\n\nLegal moves: N5assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N2, the only legal move is to N5. N5 leads to terminal, so the player who moves to N5 wins immediately (the opponent has no move). Thus, moving to N5 is optimal and results in an immediate win. No other move is available from N2. Therefore, the best choice is N5.</reason>\n<answer>N5</answer>", "episode_score": 0.05, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N2, the only legal move is to N5. N5 leads to terminal, so the player who moves to N5 wins immediately (the opponent has no move). Thus, moving to N5 is optimal and results in an immediate win. No other move is available from N2. Therefore, the best choice is N5.</reason>\n<answer>N5</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N4\n\nDirected graph:\nN2 -> N5, N3\nN3 -> N6, N5\nN9 -> N0\nN8 -> N7, N2\nN5 -> terminal\nN4 -> N9, N8\nN0 -> N6, N1\nN7 -> terminal\nN6 -> terminal\nN1 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N9, N8assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N4, the legal moves are to N9 and N8. N9 leads to N0, and N0 leads to N6 or N1, both terminal. So from N9, the opponent wins. N8 leads to N7 or N2. N7 is terminal, so the opponent wins immediately. N2 leads to N5 or N3; N5 is terminal, so opponent wins. N3 leads to N6 (terminal) or N5 (terminal). So all moves from N4 lead to the opponent winning. Thus, any move from N4 results in loss. However, since we must choose a legal move that maximizes outcome, and all lead to loss, the optimal choice is arbitrary. We pick N8 as it avoids N9 which leads to N0 and N6/N1, both terminal. But both paths lead to opponent winning. Thus, no winning move exists. Still, to maximize outcome, we choose N8.</reason>\n<answer>N8</answer>", "episode_score": -0.95, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N4, the legal moves are to N9 and N8. N9 leads to N0, and N0 leads to N6 or N1, both terminal. So from N9, the opponent wins. N8 leads to N7 or N2. N7 is terminal, so the opponent wins immediately. N2 leads to N5 or N3; N5 is terminal, so opponent wins. N3 leads to N6 (terminal) or N5 (terminal). So all moves from N4 lead to the opponent winning. Thus, any move from N4 results in loss. However, since we must choose a legal move that maximizes outcome, and all lead to loss, the optimal choice is arbitrary. We pick N8 as it avoids N9 which leads to N0 and N6/N1, both terminal. But both paths lead to opponent winning. Thus, no winning move exists. Still, to maximize outcome, we choose N8.</reason>\n<answer>N8</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N8\n\nDirected graph:\nN2 -> N5, N3\nN3 -> N6, N5\nN9 -> N0\nN8 -> N7, N2\nN5 -> terminal\nN4 -> N9, N8\nN0 -> N6, N1\nN7 -> terminal\nN6 -> terminal\nN1 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N7, N2assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N8, the legal moves are to N7 or N2. N7 leads directly to terminal (win for the player who moves there, as the opponent has no move). N2 leads to N5 or N3. N5 and N3 both lead to terminal nodes. Thus, moving to N7 wins immediately. Choosing N7 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N7</answer>", "episode_score": 1.05, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N8, the legal moves are to N7 or N2. N7 leads directly to terminal (win for the player who moves there, as the opponent has no move). N2 leads to N5 or N3. N5 and N3 both lead to terminal nodes. Thus, moving to N7 wins immediately. Choosing N7 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N7</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N9\n\nDirected graph:\nN4 -> terminal\nN5 -> terminal\nN11 -> terminal\nN10 -> terminal\nN1 -> terminal\nN0 -> N4, N5\nN2 -> N8, N11\nN13 -> N3, N6\nN8 -> terminal\nN6 -> N5, N1\nN3 -> N8, N7\nN7 -> N10, N2\nN12 -> N0, N1\nN9 -> N12, N13\n\nLegal moves: N12, N13assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N9, the player can move to N12 or N13. From N12, the next move is to N0 or N1. N0 leads to N4 or N5 (terminal), so the opponent wins immediately. N1 leads directly to terminal. Thus, moving to N1 from N12 ends the game immediately for the opponent. From N13, the move is to N3 or N6. N3 leads to N8 or N7; N8 is terminal, so the opponent wins. N7 leads to N10 or N2. N10 is terminal. N2 leads to N8 or N11, both terminal. So all paths from N13 eventually end with the opponent winning. Thus, choosing N12 and then going to N1 (a terminal move) wins immediately. Therefore, the optimal move is to N12.</reason>\n<answer>N12</answer>", "episode_score": -0.95, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N9, the player can move to N12 or N13. From N12, the next move is to N0 or N1. N0 leads to N4 or N5 (terminal), so the opponent wins immediately. N1 leads directly to terminal. Thus, moving to N1 from N12 ends the game immediately for the opponent. From N13, the move is to N3 or N6. N3 leads to N8 or N7; N8 is terminal, so the opponent wins. N7 leads to N10 or N2. N10 is terminal. N2 leads to N8 or N11, both terminal. So all paths from N13 eventually end with the opponent winning. Thus, choosing N12 and then going to N1 (a terminal move) wins immediately. Therefore, the optimal move is to N12.</reason>\n<answer>N12</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N12\n\nDirected graph:\nN4 -> terminal\nN5 -> terminal\nN11 -> terminal\nN10 -> terminal\nN1 -> terminal\nN0 -> N4, N5\nN2 -> N8, N11\nN13 -> N3, N6\nN8 -> terminal\nN6 -> N5, N1\nN3 -> N8, N7\nN7 -> N10, N2\nN12 -> N0, N1\nN9 -> N12, N13\n\nLegal moves: N0, N1assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N12, the legal moves are to N0 or N1. N1 leads directly to terminal, so the player who moves to N1 wins immediately (the opponent has no move). Thus, choosing N1 is a winning move. Choosing N0 leads to N4 or N5, both terminal, so the opponent wins. Therefore, the optimal move is to N1.</reason>\n<answer>N1</answer>", "episode_score": 1.05, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N12, the legal moves are to N0 or N1. N1 leads directly to terminal, so the player who moves to N1 wins immediately (the opponent has no move). Thus, choosing N1 is a winning move. Choosing N0 leads to N4 or N5, both terminal, so the opponent wins. Therefore, the optimal move is to N1.</reason>\n<answer>N1</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N5\n\nDirected graph:\nN0 -> terminal\nN5 -> N2, N9, N6\nN9 -> N3\nN6 -> N0, N4\nN3 -> terminal\nN2 -> N1\nN7 -> terminal\nN4 -> N2, N9\nN1 -> N7, N8\nN8 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N2, N9, N6assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N5, the player can move to N2, N9, or N6. We need to evaluate each move's outcome to maximize our chance of winning. The goal is to force the opponent into a position where they have no legal move (i.e., they reach a terminal state or no outgoing edges). We analyze each option:\n\n- From N2: N2 → N1 → N8 or N7 (both terminal or leads to terminal via N1). N2 → N1 → N8 wins (N8 → terminal) for the opponent if it's their turn, meaning we lose if we start with N2.\n- From N9: N9 → N3 → terminal → opponent loses, so we win immediately.\n- From N6: N6 → N4 → N2 or N9 (leads to loops or terminal paths); N6 → N0 → terminal → opponent loses, so this is also a winning move.\n\nBest outcome: Moving to N9 leads directly to N3, which is terminal and opponent cannot move. N6 also wins (N6 → N0 terminal). But N9 results in immediate terminal for opponent without needing multiple moves. Both are winning, but N9 is a direct win. So N9 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N9</answer>", "episode_score": -0.6166666666666667, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N5, the player can move to N2, N9, or N6. We need to evaluate each move's outcome to maximize our chance of winning. The goal is to force the opponent into a position where they have no legal move (i.e., they reach a terminal state or no outgoing edges). We analyze each option:\n\n- From N2: N2 → N1 → N8 or N7 (both terminal or leads to terminal via N1). N2 → N1 → N8 wins (N8 → terminal) for the opponent if it's their turn, meaning we lose if we start with N2.\n- From N9: N9 → N3 → terminal → opponent loses, so we win immediately.\n- From N6: N6 → N4 → N2 or N9 (leads to loops or terminal paths); N6 → N0 → terminal → opponent loses, so this is also a winning move.\n\nBest outcome: Moving to N9 leads directly to N3, which is terminal and opponent cannot move. N6 also wins (N6 → N0 terminal). But N9 results in immediate terminal for opponent without needing multiple moves. Both are winning, but N9 is a direct win. So N9 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N9</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N9\n\nDirected graph:\nN0 -> terminal\nN5 -> N2, N9, N6\nN9 -> N3\nN6 -> N0, N4\nN3 -> terminal\nN2 -> N1\nN7 -> terminal\nN4 -> N2, N9\nN1 -> N7, N8\nN8 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N3assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N9, the only legal move is to N3. N3 leads to terminal, so the player who moves to N3 wins immediately (the opponent has no move). Thus, moving to N3 is optimal and results in an immediate win. No other move from N9 is available. Therefore, the best choice is N3.</reason>\n<answer>N3</answer>", "episode_score": 0.05, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N9, the only legal move is to N3. N3 leads to terminal, so the player who moves to N3 wins immediately (the opponent has no move). Thus, moving to N3 is optimal and results in an immediate win. No other move from N9 is available. Therefore, the best choice is N3.</reason>\n<answer>N3</answer>"}]
