[2026-08-13 15:14:46] [agentic_pipeline.py (516)] [INFO] [DRIVER 0 / 1][PID 1538566] [{"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N9\n\nDirected graph:\nN1 -> N8\nN0 -> N4, N2\nN5 -> N3\nN3 -> N7, N1\nN4 -> N5, N6\nN7 -> terminal\nN8 -> terminal\nN9 -> N5, N0, N4\nN2 -> terminal\nN6 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N5, N0, N4assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N9, the player can move to N5, N0, or N4. N0 and N4 lead to terminal nodes directly (via N2 or N6 or N5), but N5 leads to N3. From N3, the opponent can move to N1 or N7. N7 and N1 are terminal or lead to terminal states. Moving to N1 leads to a path to N8 (terminal), so the opponent wins. Moving to N3 (via N5) allows the opponent to win via N7 or N1. N0 leads to N2 (terminal), so the current player wins immediately. N4 leads to N5 or N6; N6 is terminal. From N5 again, opponent gains advantage. Thus, moving to N0 leads to immediate terminal for the opponent (they cannot move). Therefore, N0 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N0</answer>", "episode_score": -0.6166666666666667, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N9, the player can move to N5, N0, or N4. N0 and N4 lead to terminal nodes directly (via N2 or N6 or N5), but N5 leads to N3. From N3, the opponent can move to N1 or N7. N7 and N1 are terminal or lead to terminal states. Moving to N1 leads to a path to N8 (terminal), so the opponent wins. Moving to N3 (via N5) allows the opponent to win via N7 or N1. N0 leads to N2 (terminal), so the current player wins immediately. N4 leads to N5 or N6; N6 is terminal. From N5 again, opponent gains advantage. Thus, moving to N0 leads to immediate terminal for the opponent (they cannot move). Therefore, N0 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N0</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N0\n\nDirected graph:\nN1 -> N8\nN0 -> N4, N2\nN5 -> N3\nN3 -> N7, N1\nN4 -> N5, N6\nN7 -> terminal\nN8 -> terminal\nN9 -> N5, N0, N4\nN2 -> terminal\nN6 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N4, N2assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N0, the legal moves are to N4 or N2. N2 leads directly to terminal (win for the player who moves there, as the opponent has no move). Thus, moving to N2 wins immediately. Choosing N2 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N2</answer>", "episode_score": 1.05, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N0, the legal moves are to N4 or N2. N2 leads directly to terminal (win for the player who moves there, as the opponent has no move). Thus, moving to N2 wins immediately. Choosing N2 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N2</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N7\n\nDirected graph:\nN3 -> N10, N6\nN9 -> N0\nN5 -> N8\nN12 -> terminal\nN4 -> terminal\nN13 -> N6, N4\nN7 -> N5, N1, N13\nN6 -> terminal\nN0 -> terminal\nN11 -> N2, N3, N9\nN10 -> terminal\nN2 -> terminal\nN8 -> N12, N1\nN1 -> N2, N11\n\nLegal moves: N5, N1, N13assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N7, the legal moves are to N5, N1, or N13. We analyze each choice:  \n- To N5: N5 goes to N8. N8 has moves to terminal or N1. If to N1 (a losing state), opponent can go to N11 and eventually win; if N8 goes to terminal, we win. But N1 is a move to N11, which is winning. So N8 → N1 → N11 gives opponent a strong advantage.\n- To N1: N1 → N2 (terminal), opponent wins immediately. Or N1 → N11 → N2 or N3 → N6 → terminal (opponent may win). So N7 → N1 is risky; opponent can win quickly.  \n- To N13 → N6 or N4 both lead to terminal. Thus, the move to N6 is terminal and ends the game immediately.  \nN7 → N13 wins immediately by ending game on terminal. This is best move. Hence optimal choice is N13.</reason>  \n<answer>N13</answer>", "episode_score": -0.6166666666666667, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N7, the legal moves are to N5, N1, or N13. We analyze each choice:  \n- To N5: N5 goes to N8. N8 has moves to terminal or N1. If to N1 (a losing state), opponent can go to N11 and eventually win; if N8 goes to terminal, we win. But N1 is a move to N11, which is winning. So N8 → N1 → N11 gives opponent a strong advantage.\n- To N1: N1 → N2 (terminal), opponent wins immediately. Or N1 → N11 → N2 or N3 → N6 → terminal (opponent may win). So N7 → N1 is risky; opponent can win quickly.  \n- To N13 → N6 or N4 both lead to terminal. Thus, the move to N6 is terminal and ends the game immediately.  \nN7 → N13 wins immediately by ending game on terminal. This is best move. Hence optimal choice is N13.</reason>  \n<answer>N13</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N13\n\nDirected graph:\nN3 -> N10, N6\nN9 -> N0\nN5 -> N8\nN12 -> terminal\nN4 -> terminal\nN13 -> N6, N4\nN7 -> N5, N1, N13\nN6 -> terminal\nN0 -> terminal\nN11 -> N2, N3, N9\nN10 -> terminal\nN2 -> terminal\nN8 -> N12, N1\nN1 -> N2, N11\n\nLegal moves: N6, N4assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N13, the legal moves are to N6 and N4. Both N6 and N4 lead to terminal nodes, so the player who moves to either wins immediately (the opponent has no move). Thus, both moves end the game immediately. Since the player who makes the move wins (the opponent has no legal move), choosing either N6 or N4 is optimal. However, since both lead to terminal states, either is equivalent. We choose one. N4 is selected.</reason>\n<answer>N4</answer>", "episode_score": 0.05, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N13, the legal moves are to N6 and N4. Both N6 and N4 lead to terminal nodes, so the player who moves to either wins immediately (the opponent has no move). Thus, both moves end the game immediately. Since the player who makes the move wins (the opponent has no legal move), choosing either N6 or N4 is optimal. However, since both lead to terminal states, either is equivalent. We choose one. N4 is selected.</reason>\n<answer>N4</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N3\n\nDirected graph:\nN7 -> terminal\nN4 -> N2, N0\nN9 -> N7\nN1 -> N8, N5\nN5 -> N4, N6\nN2 -> terminal\nN0 -> terminal\nN8 -> N9, N6\nN3 -> N1, N5\nN6 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N1, N5assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N3, the player can move to N1 or N5. We analyze the outcome of each move. \n\n- From N1, the next moves are to N8 or N5:\n    - N8 leads to N9, then N7 (terminal), so the opponent wins if they reach terminal. \n    - N5 → N4 → N2 (terminal or N4 → N0, both terminal). So N5 is terminal. This gives a winning position if the next player reaches terminal. Since the game ends when a player cannot move, if a player reaches terminal from a path they can't extend, it's a loss. The opponent will win after a player's turn ends the chain.\n    \nWe evaluate the final outcome of terminal paths:\nFrom N→terminal: terminal = loss for the player whose turn it was before.\n- From N3 → N1 → N5 → (N5 can reach terminal via N4 or N6): both end in terminal, so it's a win for the opponent.\n- N3 → N5 → ends in terminal path: the opponent can't move after N3 → N5 → (N → terminal), thus the opponent will reach terminal in their turn → current player wins.\n\nHence, N3 → N5 leads to terminal for the next player. So current player can force a win by going to N5.\n\nThus, choosing N5 leads to optimal win outcome.</reason>\n<answer>N5</answer>", "episode_score": -0.95, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N3, the player can move to N1 or N5. We analyze the outcome of each move. \n\n- From N1, the next moves are to N8 or N5:\n    - N8 leads to N9, then N7 (terminal), so the opponent wins if they reach terminal. \n    - N5 → N4 → N2 (terminal or N4 → N0, both terminal). So N5 is terminal. This gives a winning position if the next player reaches terminal. Since the game ends when a player cannot move, if a player reaches terminal from a path they can't extend, it's a loss. The opponent will win after a player's turn ends the chain.\n    \nWe evaluate the final outcome of terminal paths:\nFrom N→terminal: terminal = loss for the player whose turn it was before.\n- From N3 → N1 → N5 → (N5 can reach terminal via N4 or N6): both end in terminal, so it's a win for the opponent.\n- N3 → N5 → ends in terminal path: the opponent can't move after N3 → N5 → (N → terminal), thus the opponent will reach terminal in their turn → current player wins.\n\nHence, N3 → N5 leads to terminal for the next player. So current player can force a win by going to N5.\n\nThus, choosing N5 leads to optimal win outcome.</reason>\n<answer>N5</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N5\n\nDirected graph:\nN7 -> terminal\nN4 -> N2, N0\nN9 -> N7\nN1 -> N8, N5\nN5 -> N4, N6\nN2 -> terminal\nN0 -> terminal\nN8 -> N9, N6\nN3 -> N1, N5\nN6 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N4, N6assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N5, the legal moves are to N4 or N6. N6 leads directly to terminal (win for the player who moves there, as the opponent has no move). N4 leads to N2 or N0, both of which are terminal. Thus, moving to N6 results in an immediate win (the opponent cannot move). Moving to N4 allows the opponent to move to a terminal node, meaning we lose. Therefore, the optimal move is to N6.</reason>\n<answer>N6</answer>", "episode_score": 1.05, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N5, the legal moves are to N4 or N6. N6 leads directly to terminal (win for the player who moves there, as the opponent has no move). N4 leads to N2 or N0, both of which are terminal. Thus, moving to N6 results in an immediate win (the opponent cannot move). Moving to N4 allows the opponent to move to a terminal node, meaning we lose. Therefore, the optimal move is to N6.</reason>\n<answer>N6</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N6\n\nDirected graph:\nN11 -> terminal\nN5 -> N7, N4\nN3 -> N9, N1\nN2 -> N8, N11\nN9 -> terminal\nN0 -> N5, N3\nN7 -> N10, N9\nN12 -> terminal\nN8 -> terminal\nN10 -> N2, N8\nN4 -> terminal\nN13 -> N12, N11\nN6 -> N0, N13\nN1 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N0, N13assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N6, the legal moves are to N0 or N13. We analyze each choice to find the one that leads to a win (i.e., forces the opponent into a losing position). \n\nN13 → N12 or N11 (both terminal), so the player moves to N6's edge leading directly to terminal (i.e. the game ends immediately). This means the next player can't move and loses — so the opponent loses immediately. That is a win for the current player. \n\nN0 → N5, N3 leads to branching. \n\nN5: leads to N7 or N4; N4 terminal, N7 → N10, N9 → terminal → N10 → N2 or N8 (N8 terminal). So if we reach N5 → N7 → N9, it's a terminal move by opponent. \n\nN3 → N9 → terminal; N3 → N1 → terminal. Both terminal. So any N0 path eventually reaches terminal or leads to a sequence where the other player can force a terminal. \n\nSince N6 → N13 leads to immediate terminal for opponent's turn, it's winning. Thus, choosing N13 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N13</answer>", "episode_score": -0.95, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N6, the legal moves are to N0 or N13. We analyze each choice to find the one that leads to a win (i.e., forces the opponent into a losing position). \n\nN13 → N12 or N11 (both terminal), so the player moves to N6's edge leading directly to terminal (i.e. the game ends immediately). This means the next player can't move and loses — so the opponent loses immediately. That is a win for the current player. \n\nN0 → N5, N3 leads to branching. \n\nN5: leads to N7 or N4; N4 terminal, N7 → N10, N9 → terminal → N10 → N2 or N8 (N8 terminal). So if we reach N5 → N7 → N9, it's a terminal move by opponent. \n\nN3 → N9 → terminal; N3 → N1 → terminal. Both terminal. So any N0 path eventually reaches terminal or leads to a sequence where the other player can force a terminal. \n\nSince N6 → N13 leads to immediate terminal for opponent's turn, it's winning. Thus, choosing N13 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N13</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N13\n\nDirected graph:\nN11 -> terminal\nN5 -> N7, N4\nN3 -> N9, N1\nN2 -> N8, N11\nN9 -> terminal\nN0 -> N5, N3\nN7 -> N10, N9\nN12 -> terminal\nN8 -> terminal\nN10 -> N2, N8\nN4 -> terminal\nN13 -> N12, N11\nN6 -> N0, N13\nN1 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N12, N11assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N13, the legal moves are to N12 or N11. N12 leads directly to terminal (win for the player who moves there, as the opponent has no move). N11 leads to terminal immediately. Both moves end the game immediately. Since the player who makes the last move wins (the opponent has no legal move), choosing either N12 or N11 results in an immediate win. However, since both lead to terminal, either is valid. Choosing N12 is safe and direct. Thus, the optimal move is t