move from N2 can move to N3, which ends the game immediately. So moving to N3 wins.\n\nThus, choosing N2 gives the opponent the only choice to go to N3 (terminal), which ends the game. So I pick N2 to win.</reason>\n<answer>N2</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N2\n\nDirected graph:\nN2 -> N5, N3\nN4 -> N7, N0, N2\nN0 -> N6, N5, N2\nN3 -> terminal\nN6 -> terminal\nN9 -> terminal\nN7 -> N8\nN5 -> N9\nN8 -> N1\nN1 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N5, N3assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N2, the legal moves are to N5 or N3. N3 leads directly to terminal (win for the player who moves there, as the opponent has no move). Thus, moving to N3 wins immediately. Moving to N5 leads to N9, which is terminal, so the opponent wins. Therefore, the optimal move is to N3.</reason>\n<answer>N3</answer>", "episode_score": 1.05, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N2, the legal moves are to N5 or N3. N3 leads directly to terminal (win for the player who moves there, as the opponent has no move). Thus, moving to N3 wins immediately. Moving to N5 leads to N9, which is terminal, so the opponent wins. Therefore, the optimal move is to N3.</reason>\n<answer>N3</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N1\n\nDirected graph:\nN7 -> N2\nN8 -> N4, N3\nN9 -> terminal\nN3 -> N2, N5\nN4 -> N6, N9\nN0 -> terminal\nN2 -> terminal\nN5 -> terminal\nN1 -> N8, N3\nN6 -> N0, N7\n\nLegal moves: N8, N3assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N1, the legal moves are to N8 or N3. We analyze each option to determine which leads to a winning position (i.e., forces the opponent into a losing position). \n\nFrom N8:\n- Move to N4 or N3. \n  - N4 → N6 or N9 → both lead to N6 (→ N0, N7) or N9 (terminal), which are terminal or winnable.\n  - N3 → N2 (terminal) or N5 (terminal) — immediate win for opponent.\n  N8 leads to terminal or terminal options, so opponent wins after few moves.\n\nFrom N3:\n- N3 → N2 or N5 → both terminal. Opponent has no move — immediate loss for opponent. So N3 is winning.\n\nThus, choosing N3 immediately forces the opponent to land on a terminal move (N2 or N5). The player who moves next after that loses. So playing N3 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N3</answer>", "episode_score": -0.95, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N1, the legal moves are to N8 or N3. We analyze each option to determine which leads to a winning position (i.e., forces the opponent into a losing position). \n\nFrom N8:\n- Move to N4 or N3. \n  - N4 → N6 or N9 → both lead to N6 (→ N0, N7) or N9 (terminal), which are terminal or winnable.\n  - N3 → N2 (terminal) or N5 (terminal) — immediate win for opponent.\n  N8 leads to terminal or terminal options, so opponent wins after few moves.\n\nFrom N3:\n- N3 → N2 or N5 → both terminal. Opponent has no move — immediate loss for opponent. So N3 is winning.\n\nThus, choosing N3 immediately forces the opponent to land on a terminal move (N2 or N5). The player who moves next after that loses. So playing N3 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N3</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N3\n\nDirected graph:\nN7 -> N2\nN8 -> N4, N3\nN9 -> terminal\nN3 -> N2, N5\nN4 -> N6, N9\nN0 -> terminal\nN2 -> terminal\nN5 -> terminal\nN1 -> N8, N3\nN6 -> N0, N7\n\nLegal moves: N2, N5assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N3, the legal moves are to N2 or N5. Both N2 and N5 lead directly to terminal nodes, so the player who moves to either wins immediately (the opponent has no move). Thus, both moves end the game immediately. Since the player who makes the move wins (the opponent has no legal move), choosing either N2 or N5 is optimal. However, since both lead to immediate victory, either is valid. We choose one. N5 is selected.</reason>\n<answer>N5</answer>", "episode_score": 0.05, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N3, the legal moves are to N2 or N5. Both N2 and N5 lead directly to terminal nodes, so the player who moves to either wins immediately (the opponent has no move). Thus, both moves end the game immediately. Since the player who makes the move wins (the opponent has no legal move), choosing either N2 or N5 is optimal. However, since both lead to immediate victory, either is valid. We choose one. N5 is selected.</reason>\n<answer>N5</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N4\n\nDirected graph:\nN2 -> N1, N6\nN6 -> terminal\nN0 -> N6\nN4 -> N5, N0\nN3 -> terminal\nN8 -> N7, N9\nN9 -> N0, N3\nN1 -> terminal\nN7 -> terminal\nN5 -> N8, N2\n\nLegal moves: N5, N0assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N4, the legal moves are to N5 or N0. We analyze each option:\n\n- Moving to N5: From N5, options are N8 and N2.\n  - N8 leads to N7 (terminal) or N9.\n    - N9 leads to N0 → N6 → terminal or N3 → terminal.\n    - N0 → N6 → terminal, so all paths from N8 lead to terminal eventually.\n    - N7 is terminal, so N8 can only be a move toward end.\n    - N2 leads to N1 or N6 (both terminal). Thus, N2 leads to terminal.\n  - All paths from N5 end the game eventually (no safe loop). So the opponent will win.\n  \n- Moving to N0: N0 → N6 → terminal immediately (game ends). This is a direct loss for the opponent (they have no move).\n\nSince N0 leads to terminal immediately, the player who moves to N0 will win (opponent has no move). Thus, choosing N0 is better than N5. Therefore, the optimal move is N0.</reason>\n<answer>N0</answer>", "episode_score": -0.95, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N4, the legal moves are to N5 or N0. We analyze each option:\n\n- Moving to N5: From N5, options are N8 and N2.\n  - N8 leads to N7 (terminal) or N9.\n    - N9 leads to N0 → N6 → terminal or N3 → terminal.\n    - N0 → N6 → terminal, so all paths from N8 lead to terminal eventually.\n    - N7 is terminal, so N8 can only be a move toward end.\n    - N2 leads to N1 or N6 (both terminal). Thus, N2 leads to terminal.\n  - All paths from N5 end the game eventually (no safe loop). So the opponent will win.\n  \n- Moving to N0: N0 → N6 → terminal immediately (game ends). This is a direct loss for the opponent (they have no move).\n\nSince N0 leads to terminal immediately, the player who moves to N0 will win (opponent has no move). Thus, choosing N0 is better than N5. Therefore, the optimal move is N0.</reason>\n<answer>N0</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N0\n\nDirected graph:\nN2 -> N1, N6\nN6 -> terminal\nN0 -> N6\nN4 -> N5, N0\nN3 -> terminal\nN8 -> N7, N9\nN9 -> N0, N3\nN1 -> terminal\nN7 -> terminal\nN5 -> N8, N2\n\nLegal moves: N6assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N0, the only legal move is to N6. N6 leads directly to terminal, so the player who moves to N6 wins immediately (the opponent has no move). Thus, moving to N6 is optimal and results in an immediate win. No other move from N0 is available. Therefore, the best choice is N6.</reason>\n<answer>N6</answer>", "episode_score": 0.05, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N0, the only legal move is to N6. N6 leads directly to terminal, so the player who moves to N6 wins immediately (the opponent has no move). Thus, moving to N6 is optimal and results in an immediate win. No other move from N0 is available. Therefore, the best choice is N6.</reason>\n<answer>N6</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N3\n\nDirected graph:\nN8 -> N6\nN2 -> N7, N5\nN6 -> N2, N0\nN9 -> N4, N0\nN1 -> terminal\nN7 -> N1\nN0 -> terminal\nN4 -> terminal\nN5 -> terminal\nN3 -> N9, N8, N6\n\nLegal moves: N9, N8, N6assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N3, the legal moves are to N9, N8, or N6. We analyze the outcome of each move:  \n- N3 → N9: then to N4 or N0, both terminal → opponent wins immediately.  \n- N3 → N8: then to N6. From N6: options are N2 and N0. N2 leads to N7 or N5 (N7 to N1 terminal, N5 terminal). So N2 is losing for the player who steps into it. N6 → N0 leads to terminal. So N6 → N2 eventually leads to terminal. Either way, the opponent wins.  \n- N3 → N6: same as above; N6 → N0 → terminal. N6 → N2 → N7 → N1 or N2 → N5 → both terminal. So either way, the opponent wins.  \n\nAll moves from N3 lead to opponent winning immediately. So, all moves are equivalent in outcome. However, choosing N6 leads to a path that includes N2 → N5 or N7 → N1, which are terminal. But no matter what, the opponent wins. Thus, any choice leads to loss. But since we must pick one that maximizes outcome, and all are equal, we pick one. N6 is a central node with multiple paths, and may offer more strategic depth, though all lead to loss. Thus, N6 is chosen.</reason>  \n<answer>N6</answer>", "episode_score": -0.6166666666666667, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N3, the legal moves are to N9, N8, or N6. We analyze the outcome of each move:  \n- N3 → N9: then to N4 or N0, both terminal → opponent wins immediately.  \n- N3 → N8: then to N6. From N6: options are N2 and N0. N2 leads to N7 or N5 (N7 to N1 terminal, N5 terminal). So N2 is losing for the player who steps into it. N6 → N0 leads to terminal. So N6 → N2 eventually leads to terminal. Either way, the opponent wins.  \n- N3 → N6: same as above; N6 → N0 → terminal. N6 → N2 → N7 → N1 or N2 → N5 → both terminal. So either way, the opponent wins.  \n\nAll moves from N3 lead to opponent winning immediately. So, all moves are equivalent in outcome. However, choosing N6 leads to a path that includes N2 → N5 or N7 → N1, which are terminal. But no matter what, the opponent wins. Thus, any choice leads to loss. But since we must pick one that maximizes outcome, and all are equal, we pick one. N6 is a central node with multiple paths, and may offer more strategic depth, though all lead to loss. Thus, N6 is chosen.</reason>  \n<answer>N6</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N6\n\nDirected graph:\nN8 -> N6\nN2 -> N7, N5\nN6 -> N2, N0\nN9 -> N4, N0\nN1 -> terminal\nN7 -> N1\nN0 -> terminal\nN4 -> terminal\nN5 -> terminal\nN3 -> N9, N8, N6\n\nLegal moves: N2, N0assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N6, the legal moves are to N2 or N0. N0 leads directly to terminal (win for the player who moves there, as the opponent has no move). Thus, moving to N0 ends the game immediately and wins. Moving to N2 leads to N7 or N5; both N7 and N5 lead to terminal. Therefore, moving to N0 is a winning move. The optimal choice is N0.</reason>\n<answer>N0</answer>", "episode_score": 1.05, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N6, the legal moves are to N2 or N0. N0 leads directly to terminal (win for the player who moves there, as the opponent has no move). Thus, moving to N0 ends the game immediately and wins. Moving to N2 leads to N7 or N5; both N7 and N5 lead to terminal. Therefore, moving to N0 is a winning move. The optimal choice is N0.</reason>\n<answer>N0</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N5\n\nDirected graph:\nN9 -> N1, N3\nN5 -> N9, N2\nN6 -> N7, N2\nN7 -> terminal\nN3 -> N4, N6\nN0 -> terminal\nN1 -> N4, N8\nN4 -> terminal\nN8 -> terminal\nN2 -> N0\n\nLegal moves: N9, N2assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N5, the legal moves are to N9 or N2. We analyze each option:\n\n- Move to N2: then path is N5 → N2 → N0 → terminal (N2 → N0 is terminal). The opponent cannot move after, so this is a winning move for the player who made the first move (current player). However, this leads to immediate terminal state after one of their moves, meaning they lose on their turn. But N2 → N0 leads directly to terminal; opponent has no legal move from there. So, if we go to N2, the opponent has no move and loses. So this is a win.\n\n- Move to N9: N9 → N3 or N1. N3 → N6 → N2 or N4 (N3 → N4 leads to terminal). From N3 → N6 → N7, the game continues. N1 → N4 → terminal or N1 → N8 → terminal. N9 → N1 → N4 is terminal. But N9 → N3 → N6 → N2 → N0: opponent will eventually win if we go that way. However, from N3 → N6 → N7: N7 → terminal, so this path is terminal. All branches from N9 lead to terminal in a few moves. The player who plays second in such sequences will be forced to respond, but due to the structure, any play leads to terminal.\n\nSo N2 leads to an immediate win. Thus, the optimal choice is N2.</reason>\n<answer>N2</answer>", "episode_score": -0.95, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N5, the legal moves are to N9 or N2. We analyze each option:\n\n- Move to N2: then path is N5 → N2 → N0 → terminal (N2 → N0 is terminal). The opponent cannot move after, so this is a winning move for the player who made the first move (current player). However, this leads to immediate terminal state after one of their moves, meaning they lose on their turn. But N2 → N0 leads directly to terminal; opponent has no legal move from there. So, if we go to N2, the opponent has no move and loses. So this is a win.\n\n- Move to N9: N9 → N3 or N1. N3 → N6 → N2 or N4 (N3 → N4 leads to terminal). From N3 → N6 → N7, the game continues. N1 → N4 → terminal or N1 → N8 → terminal. N9 → N1 → N4 is terminal. But N9 → N3 → N6 → N2 → N0: opponent will eventually win if we go that way. However, from N3 → N6 → N7: N7 → terminal, so this path is terminal. All branches from N9 lead to terminal in a few moves. The player who plays second in such sequences will be forced to respond, but due to the structure, any play leads to terminal.\n\nSo N2 leads to an immediate win. Thus, the optimal choice is N2.</reason>\n<answer>N2</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N2\n\nDirected graph:\nN9 -> N1, N3\nN5 -> N9, N2\nN6 -> N7, N2\nN7 -> terminal\nN3 -> N4, N6\nN0 -> terminal\nN1 -> N4, N8\nN4 -> terminal\nN8 -> terminal\nN2 -> N0\n\nLegal moves: N0assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N2, the only legal move is to N0, which is a terminal node. The player who moves to N0 wins immediately, as the opponent has no legal move. Thus, moving to N0 is the only option and results in an immediate win. No other move is available, so this is the optimal choice.</reason>\n<answer>N0</answer>", "episode_score": 0.05, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N2, the only legal move is to N0, which is a terminal node. The player who moves to N0 wins immediately, as the opponent has no legal move