[2026-08-13 15:26:03] [agentic_pipeline.py (516)] [INFO] [DRIVER 0 / 1][PID 1538566] [{"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N2\n\nDirected graph:\nN1 -> N4, N3\nN8 -> N9, N7\nN3 -> terminal\nN2 -> N0, N1\nN0 -> N6, N4\nN9 -> terminal\nN7 -> terminal\nN6 -> N9, N8\nN5 -> terminal\nN4 -> N5\n\nLegal moves: N0, N1assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N2, the legal moves are to N0 or N1. We evaluate both options:\n\n- Move to N0:\n  - N0 → N6 or N4\n  - From N4 → N5 (terminal → lose if opponent moves to N5 or blocks from it)\n    - N6 → N9 or N8\n    - N8 → N9, N7 (N7 leads to terminal, N9 terminal)\n    - N9 → terminal, N4 → N5 → terminal\n\n  So N4 → N5 → terminal, N6 → N9 → terminal → eventually leads to terminal state, which means a player can force the opponent to step into a terminal.\n\n- Move to N1:\n  - N1 → N3 → terminal → opponent loses immediately\n\nSo going to N1 leads to opponent having no move. This is a win.\n\nTherefore, moving to N1 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N1</answer>", "episode_score": -0.95, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N2, the legal moves are to N0 or N1. We evaluate both options:\n\n- Move to N0:\n  - N0 → N6 or N4\n  - From N4 → N5 (terminal → lose if opponent moves to N5 or blocks from it)\n    - N6 → N9 or N8\n    - N8 → N9, N7 (N7 leads to terminal, N9 terminal)\n    - N9 → terminal, N4 → N5 → terminal\n\n  So N4 → N5 → terminal, N6 → N9 → terminal → eventually leads to terminal state, which means a player can force the opponent to step into a terminal.\n\n- Move to N1:\n  - N1 → N3 → terminal → opponent loses immediately\n\nSo going to N1 leads to opponent having no move. This is a win.\n\nTherefore, moving to N1 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N1</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N1\n\nDirected graph:\nN1 -> N4, N3\nN8 -> N9, N7\nN3 -> terminal\nN2 -> N0, N1\nN0 -> N6, N4\nN9 -> terminal\nN7 -> terminal\nN6 -> N9, N8\nN5 -> terminal\nN4 -> N5\n\nLegal moves: N4, N3assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N1, the legal moves are to N4 or N3. N3 leads directly to terminal (win for the player who moves there, as the opponent has no move). Thus, moving to N3 wins immediately. Moving to N4 leads to N5 (terminal) or N4 again (looping). N4 to N5 is terminal, so the opponent wins. Therefore, choosing N3 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N3</answer>", "episode_score": 1.05, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N1, the legal moves are to N4 or N3. N3 leads directly to terminal (win for the player who moves there, as the opponent has no move). Thus, moving to N3 wins immediately. Moving to N4 leads to N5 (terminal) or N4 again (looping). N4 to N5 is terminal, so the opponent wins. Therefore, choosing N3 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N3</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N1\n\nDirected graph:\nN5 -> N6, N2\nN9 -> terminal\nN8 -> terminal\nN4 -> N8\nN7 -> terminal\nN6 -> N0, N9\nN3 -> terminal\nN1 -> N4, N5\nN0 -> N7, N8\nN2 -> N4, N3\n\nLegal moves: N4, N5assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N1, the player can move to N4 or N5.  \n- Moving to N5: leads to N6. From N6, the opponent can move to N9 (terminal, opponent wins) or N0. If to N0, then to N7 or N8 (both terminal). So opponent wins.  \n- Moving to N4: leads to N8 (terminal), so opponent loses immediately. Thus, moving to N4 ends the game immediately and wins.  \nThus, choosing N4 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N4</answer>", "episode_score": -0.95, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N1, the player can move to N4 or N5.  \n- Moving to N5: leads to N6. From N6, the opponent can move to N9 (terminal, opponent wins) or N0. If to N0, then to N7 or N8 (both terminal). So opponent wins.  \n- Moving to N4: leads to N8 (terminal), so opponent loses immediately. Thus, moving to N4 ends the game immediately and wins.  \nThus, choosing N4 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N4</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N4\n\nDirected graph:\nN5 -> N6, N2\nN9 -> terminal\nN8 -> terminal\nN4 -> N8\nN7 -> terminal\nN6 -> N0, N9\nN3 -> terminal\nN1 -> N4, N5\nN0 -> N7, N8\nN2 -> N4, N3\n\nLegal moves: N8assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N4, the legal moves are to N8. N8 is a terminal node, so moving to N8 ends the game immediately. The player who moves to N8 wins because the opponent has no legal move. Thus, moving to N8 is optimal and results in an immediate win. No other move from N4 is available. Therefore, the best choice is N8.</reason>\n<answer>N8</answer>", "episode_score": 0.05, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N4, the legal moves are to N8. N8 is a terminal node, so moving to N8 ends the game immediately. The player who moves to N8 wins because the opponent has no legal move. Thus, moving to N8 is optimal and results in an immediate win. No other move from N4 is available. Therefore, the best choice is N8.</reason>\n<answer>N8</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N7\n\nDirected graph:\nN13 -> N10, N9\nN8 -> N5, N2\nN1 -> N6, N3\nN5 -> terminal\nN11 -> terminal\nN7 -> N8, N4\nN12 -> N9, N3\nN2 -> terminal\nN6 -> N11, N0\nN3 -> terminal\nN9 -> terminal\nN10 -> terminal\nN4 -> N1, N12\nN0 -> N13, N5\n\nLegal moves: N8, N4assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N7, the legal moves are to N8 or N4.  \n- Moving to N8: Then opponent faces N8 → N5 (lose) or N2 (lose). So opponent loses immediately. Good.  \n- Moving to N4: Then opponent faces N4 → N1 or N12.  \n   - From N1 → N6 → N11 (terminal, opponent wins) or N3 (lose), but N1→N3 leads to opponent losing.  \n   - From N4 → N12 → N9 or N3, both terminal (opponent loses). So N4 gives opponent a winning move only if they play well, but both branches lead to terminal. However, N8 gives opponent immediate terminal paths from both options.  \nSince both N8 and N4 lead to opponent eventually facing terminal, but N8 offers no safe continuation (both N5 & N2 are terminal), N8 is a safer and more direct win. Thus, choosing N8 maximizes the chance of forcing opponent to lose immediately. Final move: N8.</reason>\n<answer>N8</answer>", "episode_score": -0.95, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N7, the legal moves are to N8 or N4.  \n- Moving to N8: Then opponent faces N8 → N5 (lose) or N2 (lose). So opponent loses immediately. Good.  \n- Moving to N4: Then opponent faces N4 → N1 or N12.  \n   - From N1 → N6 → N11 (terminal, opponent wins) or N3 (lose), but N1→N3 leads to opponent losing.  \n   - From N4 → N12 → N9 or N3, both terminal (opponent loses). So N4 gives opponent a winning move only if they play well, but both branches lead to terminal. However, N8 gives opponent immediate terminal paths from both options.  \nSince both N8 and N4 lead to opponent eventually facing terminal, but N8 offers no safe continuation (both N5 & N2 are terminal), N8 is a safer and more direct win. Thus, choosing N8 maximizes the chance of forcing opponent to lose immediately. Final move: N8.</reason>\n<answer>N8</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N8\n\nDirected graph:\nN13 -> N10, N9\nN8 -> N5, N2\nN1 -> N6, N3\nN5 -> terminal\nN11 -> terminal\nN7 -> N8, N4\nN12 -> N9, N3\nN2 -> terminal\nN6 -> N11, N0\nN3 -> terminal\nN9 -> terminal\nN10 -> terminal\nN4 -> N1, N12\nN0 -> N13, N5\n\nLegal moves: N5, N2assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N8, the legal moves are to N5 or N2. N2 leads directly to terminal (win for the player who moves there, as the opponent has no move). N5 also leads to terminal. Both N5 and N2 are terminal moves, so the player who moves to either wins immediately. Since both are winning moves, either is optimal. However, N2 is a direct terminal move, while N5 leads to terminal after one step. Both are equivalent in outcome. Choosing N2 is simpler and direct. Thus, the optimal choice is N2.</reason>\n<answer>N2</answer>", "episode_score": 0.05, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N8, the legal moves are to N5 or N2. N2 leads directly to terminal (win for the player who moves there, as the opponent has no move). N5 also leads to terminal. Both N5 and N2 are terminal moves, so the player who moves to either wins immediately. Since both are winning moves, either is optimal. However, N2 is a direct terminal move, while N5 leads to terminal after one step. Both are equivalent in outcome. Choosing N2 is simpler and direct. Thus, the optimal choice is N2.</reason>\n<answer>N2</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N3\n\nDirected graph:\nN0 -> terminal\nN7 -> N2, N1\nN5 -> terminal\nN2 -> terminal\nN9 -> terminal\nN6 -> N7, N8\nN8 -> N2, N0\nN1 -> N4, N9\nN3 -> N4, N6\nN4 -> N5\n\nLegal moves: N4, N6assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N3, the player can move to N4 or N6.  \n- If move to N4: then to N5 (terminal), opponent wins.  \n- If move to N6: opponent can go to N7 or N8.  \n  - If to N7: opponent can go to N2 or N1.  \n    - N2 → terminal (win for opponent)  \n    - N1 → N4, N9: N4 → N5 (win for opponent); N9 → terminal (win for opponent)  \n  - If to N8: then to N2 or N0.  \n    - N2 → terminal (opponent wins)  \n    - N0 → terminal (opponent wins)  \nAll paths from N6 eventually lead to opponent winning. Thus, moving to N4 leads directly to opponent winning immediately. Moving to N6 gives opponent a chance to move but all options lead to terminal nodes for the current player. Therefore, N4 is the only move that ends the game immediately. However, since the player who makes no move loses, choosing N4 (leading to terminal) means the opponent has no move and thus *we* win. Thus, N4 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N4</answer>", "episode_score": -0.95, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N3, the player can move to N4 or N6.  \n- If move to N4: then to N5 (terminal), opponent wins.  \n- If move to N6: opponent can go to N7 or N8.  \n  - If to N7: opponent can go to N2 or N1.  \n    - N2 → terminal (win for opponent)  \n    - N1 → N4, N9: N4 → N5 (win for opponent); N9 → terminal (win for opponent)  \n  - If to N8: then to N2 or N0.  \n    - N2 → terminal (opponent wins)  \n    - N0 → terminal (opponent wins)  \nAll paths from N6 eventually lead to opponent winning. Thus, moving to N4 leads directly to opponent winning immediately. Moving to N6 gives opponent a chance to move but all options lead to terminal nodes for the current player. Therefore, N4 is the only move that ends the game immediately. However, since the player who makes no move loses, choosing N4 (leading to terminal) means the opponent has no move and thus *we* win. Thus, N4 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N4</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A respon