ve to N5 or N7.  \n- Moving to N5: Then opponent can go to N1 (terminal) or N4 (terminal). Both are terminal moves for the opponent, so opponent loses immediately.  \n- Moving to N7: Then opponent can go to N9 or N8.  \n  - From N9: goes to N4 (terminal, opponent wins).  \n  - From N8: goes to N2 (terminal, opponent wins) or N3.  \n    - From N3: goes to N0 (terminal) or N9 (loop back). N9 leads to N4 (terminal), so the opponent eventually wins.  \n\nThus, moving to N5 leads to an immediate win (opponent has no legal move after N5 → N1 or N4). Moving to N7 gives opponent a path to terminal. Therefore, N5 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N5</answer>", "episode_score": -0.95, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N6, the player can move to N5 or N7.  \n- Moving to N5: Then opponent can go to N1 (terminal) or N4 (terminal). Both are terminal moves for the opponent, so opponent loses immediately.  \n- Moving to N7: Then opponent can go to N9 or N8.  \n  - From N9: goes to N4 (terminal, opponent wins).  \n  - From N8: goes to N2 (terminal, opponent wins) or N3.  \n    - From N3: goes to N0 (terminal) or N9 (loop back). N9 leads to N4 (terminal), so the opponent eventually wins.  \n\nThus, moving to N5 leads to an immediate win (opponent has no legal move after N5 → N1 or N4). Moving to N7 gives opponent a path to terminal. Therefore, N5 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N5</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N5\n\nDirected graph:\nN0 -> terminal\nN1 -> terminal\nN5 -> N4, N1\nN8 -> N2, N3\nN9 -> N4\nN3 -> N0, N9\nN2 -> terminal\nN6 -> N5, N7\nN7 -> N9, N8\nN4 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N4, N1assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N5, the legal moves are to N4 or N1. Both N4 and N1 lead to terminal nodes, so the player who moves to either wins immediately (the opponent has no legal move). Thus, both moves end the game immediately. Since the player who makes the move wins (the opponent loses), choosing either N4 or N1 is optimal. However, since both lead to immediate victory, either is valid. We choose one. N1 is a safer choice as it avoids potential loops, but both are winning. However, N4 leads directly to terminal. Both are optimal. We select N1 as it is a direct win.</reason>\n<answer>N1</answer>", "episode_score": 0.05, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N5, the legal moves are to N4 or N1. Both N4 and N1 lead to terminal nodes, so the player who moves to either wins immediately (the opponent has no legal move). Thus, both moves end the game immediately. Since the player who makes the move wins (the opponent loses), choosing either N4 or N1 is optimal. However, since both lead to immediate victory, either is valid. We choose one. N1 is a safer choice as it avoids potential loops, but both are winning. However, N4 leads directly to terminal. Both are optimal. We select N1 as it is a direct win.</reason>\n<answer>N1</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N1\n\nDirected graph:\nN3 -> terminal\nN1 -> N9, N5, N2\nN4 -> terminal\nN2 -> N7\nN5 -> N4\nN9 -> N0\nN0 -> N3, N6\nN7 -> terminal\nN8 -> terminal\nN6 -> N8, N5, N3\n\nLegal moves: N9, N5, N2assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N1, the players can move to N9, N5, or N2. We analyze each path to determine the winning or losing positions.  \n- From N9: moves to N0, which leads to N3 (terminal) or N6. N6 then leads to N8 (terminal) or N5 or N3. N5 leads to N4, which is terminal. So N0 leads to terminal via N3 or N6. Thus N9 leads to a terminal state via N0, meaning N9 is a losing move (first player loses).  \n- From N5: leads to N4 (terminal), so a terminal outcome – also a losing move.  \n- From N2: goes to N7 which is terminal – also a losing move.  \n\nAll initial moves lead to terminal states. So any choice leads to opponent winning. But we must pick one that maximizes outcome. Since all moves lead to immediate terminal, they are equivalent. However, choosing N2 leads to N7 (terminal), which is a losing move for the opponent. All are equal. But since the goal is to maximize outcome, and all are terminal, we pick the first legal one. However, the safest and most direct terminal is via N2 → N7. So N2 is optimal as it directly ends the game. Thus, choosing N2 is best.</reason>\n<answer>N2</answer>", "episode_score": -0.6166666666666667, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N1, the players can move to N9, N5, or N2. We analyze each path to determine the winning or losing positions.  \n- From N9: moves to N0, which leads to N3 (terminal) or N6. N6 then leads to N8 (terminal) or N5 or N3. N5 leads to N4, which is terminal. So N0 leads to terminal via N3 or N6. Thus N9 leads to a terminal state via N0, meaning N9 is a losing move (first player loses).  \n- From N5: leads to N4 (terminal), so a terminal outcome – also a losing move.  \n- From N2: goes to N7 which is terminal – also a losing move.  \n\nAll initial moves lead to terminal states. So any choice leads to opponent winning. But we must pick one that maximizes outcome. Since all moves lead to immediate terminal, they are equivalent. However, choosing N2 leads to N7 (terminal), which is a losing move for the opponent. All are equal. But since the goal is to maximize outcome, and all are terminal, we pick the first legal one. However, the safest and most direct terminal is via N2 → N7. So N2 is optimal as it directly ends the game. Thus, choosing N2 is best.</reason>\n<answer>N2</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N2\n\nDirected graph:\nN3 -> terminal\nN1 -> N9, N5, N2\nN4 -> terminal\nN2 -> N7\nN5 -> N4\nN9 -> N0\nN0 -> N3, N6\nN7 -> terminal\nN8 -> terminal\nN6 -> N8, N5, N3\n\nLegal moves: N7assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N2, the only legal move is to N7. N7 leads to terminal, so the player who moves to N7 wins immediately (the opponent has no move). Thus, moving to N7 is optimal and results in an immediate win. No other move from N2 is available. Therefore, the best choice is N7.</reason>\n<answer>N7</answer>", "episode_score": 0.05, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N2, the only legal move is to N7. N7 leads to terminal, so the player who moves to N7 wins immediately (the opponent has no move). Thus, moving to N7 is optimal and results in an immediate win. No other move from N2 is available. Therefore, the best choice is N7.</reason>\n<answer>N7</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reaso