[2026-08-13 15:36:57] [agentic_pipeline.py (516)] [INFO] [DRIVER 0 / 1][PID 1538566] [{"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N7\n\nDirected graph:\nN6 -> terminal\nN7 -> N1, N9, N5\nN8 -> terminal\nN3 -> N4, N6\nN9 -> N8, N2\nN5 -> N0, N3\nN0 -> terminal\nN1 -> N5\nN4 -> terminal\nN2 -> N0\n\nLegal moves: N1, N9, N5assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N7, the legal moves are to N1, N9, or N5. We analyze each:  \n- N1 → N5 → N0 (win for opponent): N5 is followed by N0 (terminal), so opponent wins.  \n- N9 → N8 or N2: N8 ends (opponent wins), N2 → N0 (opponent wins). Both lead to opponent winning.  \n- N5 → N0 or N3: N0 ends (opponent wins), N3 → N4 or N6. N4 and N6 both end (opponent wins).  \nAll paths from N7 eventually lead to terminal nodes on the opponent's turn. Thus, all moves lose. However, since we must choose one that maximizes outcome and all lead to loss, any move is equivalent. Choosing N5 is safe as it opens N3, but still results in loss. The optimal choice is N9 as it avoids N1’s path to N5 which leads to N0. But all are losing. Thus, any is equal. Pick N9.</reason>  \n<answer>N9</answer>", "episode_score": -0.6166666666666667, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N7, the legal moves are to N1, N9, or N5. We analyze each:  \n- N1 → N5 → N0 (win for opponent): N5 is followed by N0 (terminal), so opponent wins.  \n- N9 → N8 or N2: N8 ends (opponent wins), N2 → N0 (opponent wins). Both lead to opponent winning.  \n- N5 → N0 or N3: N0 ends (opponent wins), N3 → N4 or N6. N4 and N6 both end (opponent wins).  \nAll paths from N7 eventually lead to terminal nodes on the opponent's turn. Thus, all moves lose. However, since we must choose one that maximizes outcome and all lead to loss, any move is equivalent. Choosing N5 is safe as it opens N3, but still results in loss. The optimal choice is N9 as it avoids N1’s path to N5 which leads to N0. But all are losing. Thus, any is equal. Pick N9.</reason>  \n<answer>N9</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N9\n\nDirected graph:\nN6 -> terminal\nN7 -> N1, N9, N5\nN8 -> terminal\nN3 -> N4, N6\nN9 -> N8, N2\nN5 -> N0, N3\nN0 -> terminal\nN1 -> N5\nN4 -> terminal\nN2 -> N0\n\nLegal moves: N8, N2assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N9, the legal moves are to N8 or N2. N8 leads directly to terminal (win for the player who moves there, as the opponent has no move). N2 leads to N0, which is terminal. Thus, moving to N8 wins immediately. Moving to N2 results in the opponent winning. Therefore, the optimal move is to N8.</reason>\n<answer>N8</answer>", "episode_score": 1.05, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N9, the legal moves are to N8 or N2. N8 leads directly to terminal (win for the player who moves there, as the opponent has no move). N2 leads to N0, which is terminal. Thus, moving to N8 wins immediately. Moving to N2 results in the opponent winning. Therefore, the optimal move is to N8.</reason>\n<answer>N8</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N4\n\nDirected graph:\nN10 -> N9, N0\nN6 -> N11, N13\nN12 -> terminal\nN8 -> terminal\nN9 -> terminal\nN5 -> N10, N2\nN0 -> N6, N7\nN13 -> N1, N12\nN11 -> N1, N3\nN3 -> terminal\nN2 -> terminal\nN4 -> N6, N5\nN1 -> terminal\nN7 -> N3, N8\n\nLegal moves: N6, N5assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N4, the legal moves are to N6 and N5.  \n- Moving to N5: leads to N2 (terminal), so the opponent wins immediately.  \n- Moving to N6: leads to N11 or N13.  \n  - N11 → N1 or N3: both terminal → opponent wins  \n  - N13 → N1 or N12: N12 → terminal, so opponent wins.  \nAll paths from N6 or N5 eventually lead to terminal nodes, meaning the player who moves first from N4 loses. However, since the player making the move must choose a destination, both options result in the opponent winning. But since we must choose one that maximizes outcome, and both lead to loss, either is equivalent. Choosing N5 leads to immediate terminal (opponent wins), same as N6. However, N6 offers more branching but still ends in loss. Since both are losing, the optimal choice is arbitrary. We pick N5 as it is simpler and directly leads to terminal. However, strictly speaking, both are equivalent. But per instruction to maximize outcome, and since N5 leads to terminal immediately, it is safer. Thus, N5 is chosen.</reason>\n<answer>N5</answer>", "episode_score": -0.95, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N4, the legal moves are to N6 and N5.  \n- Moving to N5: leads to N2 (terminal), so the opponent wins immediately.  \n- Moving to N6: leads to N11 or N13.  \n  - N11 → N1 or N3: both terminal → opponent wins  \n  - N13 → N1 or N12: N12 → terminal, so opponent wins.  \nAll paths from N6 or N5 eventually lead to terminal nodes, meaning the player who moves first from N4 loses. However, since the player making the move must choose a destination, both options result in the opponent winning. But since we must choose one that maximizes outcome, and both lead to loss, either is equivalent. Choosing N5 leads to immediate terminal (opponent wins), same as N6. However, N6 offers more branching but still ends in loss. Since both are losing, the optimal choice is arbitrary. We pick N5 as it is simpler and directly leads to terminal. However, strictly speaking, both are equivalent. But per instruction to maximize outcome, and since N5 leads to terminal immediately, it is safer. Thus, N5 is chosen.</reason>\n<answer>N5</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N5\n\nDirected graph:\nN10 -> N9, N0\nN6 -> N11, N13\nN12 -> terminal\nN8 -> terminal\nN9 -> terminal\nN5 -> N10, N2\nN0 -> N6, N7\nN13 -> N1, N12\nN11 -> N1, N3\nN3 -> terminal\nN2 -> terminal\nN4 -> N6, N5\nN1 -> terminal\nN7 -> N3, N8\n\nLegal moves: N10, N2assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N5, the legal moves are to N10 or N2. N2 leads directly to terminal (win for the player who moves there, as the opponent has no move). Thus, moving to N2 wins immediately. Moving to N10 leads to N9 or N0. N9 is terminal, so the opponent wins. N0 leads to N6 or N7. N6 leads to N11 or N13; both N11 and N13 eventually lead to terminal. N7 leads to N3 or N8, both terminal. Thus, any move from N10 eventually lets the opponent win. Therefore, choosing N2 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N2</answer>", "episode_score": 1.05, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N5, the legal moves are to N10 or N2. N2 leads directly to terminal (win for the player who moves there, as the opponent has no move). Thus, moving to N2 wins immediately. Moving to N10 leads to N9 or N0. N9 is terminal, so the opponent wins. N0 leads to N6 or N7. N6 leads to N11 or N13; both N11 and N13 eventually lead to terminal. N7 leads to N3 or N8, both terminal. Thus, any move from N10 eventually lets the opponent win. Therefore, choosing N2 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N2</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N1\n\nDirected graph:\nN4 -> N0\nN2 -> N5, N9\nN0 -> terminal\nN7 -> terminal\nN5 -> terminal\nN6 -> terminal\nN8 -> N7, N5\nN3 -> N2\nN1 -> N3, N8, N2\nN9 -> N4, N6\n\nLegal moves: N3, N8, N2assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N1, the player can move to N3, N8, or N2. We analyze each:  \n- N3 → N2 → then to N5 or N9; N5 → terminal (win for opponent), N9 → N4→N0→terminal or N6→terminal. N9 ends with opponent win. So N3 leads to a losing position for the first player.  \n- N8 → N7 (win) or N5 (win). Both terminal; thus N8 leads to opponent win.  \n- N2 → N5 or N9: both terminal. Opponent wins.  \nAll paths lead to opponent winning. Thus, each move results in loss. However, since we must choose a move that maximizes outcome, and all are equivalent in leading to loss, any choice is equal. But N8 leads to N7 or N5 both terminal—immediate loss. N3 and N2 also lead to terminal. All are bad. But N2 allows move to N9 which can go to N6 (terminal), so no advantage. All paths are terminal. The safest is to pick N2 as it is a central node with balanced options. However, since all moves lead to opponent winning, any is equivalent. Choosing N2 is safe and symmetric. Thus, N2 is selected.</reason>  \n<answer>N2</answer>", "episode_score": -0.6166666666666667, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N1, the player can move to N3, N8, or N2. We analyze each:  \n- N3 → N2 → then to N5 or N9; N5 → terminal (win for opponent), N9 → N4→N0→terminal or N6→terminal. N9 ends with opponent win. So N3 leads to a losing position for the first player.  \n- N8 → N7 (win) or N5 (win). Both terminal; thus N8 leads to opponent win.  \n- N2 → N5 or N9: both terminal. Opponent wins.  \nAll paths lead to opponent winning. Thus, each move results in loss. However, since we must choose a move that maximizes outcome, and all are equivalent in leading to loss, any choice is equal. But N8 leads to N7 or N5 both terminal—immediate loss. N3 and N2 also lead to terminal. All are bad. But N2 allows move to N9 which can go to N6 (terminal), so no advantage. All paths are terminal. The safest is to pick N2 as it is a central node with balanced options. However, since all moves lead to opponent winning, any is equivalent. Choosing N2 is safe and symmetric. Thus, N2 is selected.</reason>  \n<answer>N2</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N2\n\nDirected graph:\nN4 -> N0\nN2 -> N5, N9\nN0 -> terminal\nN7 -> terminal\nN5 -> terminal\nN6 -> terminal\nN8 -> N7, N5\nN3 -> N2\nN1 -> N3, N8, N2\nN9 -> N4, N6\n\nLegal moves: N5, N9assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N2, the legal moves are to N5 or N9. N5 leads directly to terminal (win for the player who moves there, as the opponent has no move). N9 leads to N4 or N6; N4 leads to N0 (terminal), and N6 leads to terminal. Thus, moving to N5 wins immediately. Choosing N5 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N5</answer>", "episode_score": 1.05, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N2, the legal moves are to N5 or N9. N5 leads directly to terminal (win for the player who moves there, as the opponent has no move). N9 leads to N4 or N6; N4 leads to N0 (terminal), and N6 leads to terminal. Thus, moving to N5 wins immediately. Choosing N5 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N5</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N9\n\nDirected graph:\nN9 -> N8, N13, N1\nN0 -> N11\nN11 -> terminal\nN13 -> N4, N7\nN3 -> terminal\nN5 -> terminal\nN6 -> terminal\nN1 -> N5, N3, N12\nN7 -> terminal\nN4 -> N10\nN2 -> N6, N3\nN10 -> terminal\nN8 -> N0\nN12 -> N10, N2, N6\n\nLegal moves: N8, N13, N1assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N9, the legal moves are to N8, N13, or N1. We analyze each:  \n- N1 leads to N5, N3, or N12. N5 and N3 lead to terminal (loss for next player). N12 leads to N10, N2, or N6 – all eventually terminal. So N1 leads to terminal moves.  \n- N8 → N0 → N11 → terminal (terminal for next player). So N8 leads to terminal.  \n- N13 → N4 or N7. N7 is terminal. N4 → N10 → terminal. So N13 leads to terminal.  \n\nAll moves from N9 lead to terminal states in one step. Thus, no matter where we go, the opponent wins. However, since we want to maximize our outcome and all options lead to opponent winning, any choice is equal. We pick one. N1 is a safe, balanced move with multiple paths, but all lead to terminal. Choosing N13 avoids immediate terminal and explores more, but still loses. Opt for N8 as it leads directly to N0 → N11 → terminal. All are equivalent. Pick N8.</reason>\n<answer>N8</answer>", "episode_score": 1.3833333333333333, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N9, the legal moves are to N8, N13, or N1. We analyze each:  \n- N1 leads to N5, N3, or N12. N5 and N3 lead to terminal (loss for next player). N12 leads to N10, N2, or N6 – all eventually terminal. So N1 leads to terminal moves.  \n- N8 → N0 → N11 → terminal (terminal for next player). So N8 leads to terminal.  \n- N13 → N4 or N7. N7 is terminal. N4 → N10 → terminal. So N13 leads to terminal.  \n\nAll moves from N9 lead to terminal states in one step. Thus, no matter where we go, the opponent wins. However, since we want to maximize our outcome and all options lead to opponent winning, any choice is equal. We pick one. N1 is a safe, balanced move with multiple paths, but all lead to terminal. Choosing N13 avoids immediate terminal and explores more, but still loses. Opt for N8 as it leads directly to N0 → N11 → terminal. All are equivalent. Pick N8.</reason>\n<answer>N8</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N8\n\nDirected graph:\nN9 -> N8, N13, N1\nN0 -> N11\nN11 -> terminal\nN13 -> N4, N7\nN3 -> terminal\nN5 -> terminal\nN6 -> t