ives opponent a choice but still leads to loss. Since N2 results in an immediate win, it is optimal. Therefore, choose N2.</reason>\n<answer>N2</answer>", "episode_score": -0.95, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N3, the player can move to N2 or N9.  \n- If moving to N2: the opponent faces N2, which leads to N6 or N7, both terminal. So opponent loses immediately.  \n- If moving to N9: the opponent faces N9, which leads to N2 or N1.  \n  - If opponent moves to N1, then to N5 or N0: N5 leads to N7 or N4; N7 is terminal (opponent loses). N4 leads to N8 (terminal). So either way, opponent eventually loses.  \n  - If opponent moves to N2, same as above.  \nThus, both N2 and N9 lead to opponent's loss. However, moving to N2 immediately ends the game with opponent having no move. Moving to N9 gives opponent a choice but still leads to loss. Since N2 results in an immediate win, it is optimal. Therefore, choose N2.</reason>\n<answer>N2</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N2\n\nDirected graph:\nN4 -> N8\nN7 -> terminal\nN6 -> terminal\nN2 -> N6, N7\nN0 -> terminal\nN9 -> N2, N1\nN1 -> N5, N0\nN8 -> terminal\nN3 -> N2, N9\nN5 -> N7, N4\n\nLegal moves: N6, N7assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N2, the legal moves are to N6 or N7. Both N6 and N7 lead to terminal nodes, so the player who moves to either wins immediately (the opponent has no move). Thus, both moves end the game immediately. Since the player who makes the move wins (the opponent loses), choosing either N6 or N7 is optimal. However, since both lead to immediate v