[2026-08-13 15:45:56] [agentic_pipeline.py (516)] [INFO] [DRIVER 0 / 1][PID 1538566] [{"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N10\n\nDirected graph:\nN2 -> N1, N0\nN1 -> N11, N12\nN10 -> N2, N6\nN13 -> N7, N5\nN5 -> N8, N9\nN0 -> N4, N11\nN3 -> terminal\nN12 -> N13, N9\nN4 -> terminal\nN9 -> terminal\nN6 -> N3, N4\nN11 -> terminal\nN7 -> terminal\nN8 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N2, N6assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N10, the player can move to N2 or N6.  \n- Moving to N2: leads to N1 or N0.  \n  - N1 leads to N11 or N12: both eventually end via N11→terminal or N12→N13→terminal.  \n  - N0 leads to N4 (terminal) or N11 (terminal). So all paths from N2 end in terminal moves, meaning opponent wins.  \n- Moving to N6: leads to N3 or N4.  \n  - N3 → terminal, N4 → terminal. Both are terminal. Opponent has no move and loses.  \nThus, moving to N6 wins immediately. The optimal choice is N6.</reason>\n<answer>N6</answer>", "episode_score": -0.95, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N10, the player can move to N2 or N6.  \n- Moving to N2: leads to N1 or N0.  \n  - N1 leads to N11 or N12: both eventually end via N11→terminal or N12→N13→terminal.  \n  - N0 leads to N4 (terminal) or N11 (terminal). So all paths from N2 end in terminal moves, meaning opponent wins.  \n- Moving to N6: leads to N3 or N4.  \n  - N3 → terminal, N4 → terminal. Both are terminal. Opponent has no move and loses.  \nThus, moving to N6 wins immediately. The optimal choice is N6.</reason>\n<answer>N6</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N6\n\nDirected graph:\nN2 -> N1, N0\nN1 -> N11, N12\nN10 -> N2, N6\nN13 -> N7, N5\nN5 -> N8, N9\nN0 -> N4, N11\nN3 -> terminal\nN12 -> N13, N9\nN4 -> terminal\nN9 -> terminal\nN6 -> N3, N4\nN11 -> terminal\nN7 -> terminal\nN8 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N3, N4assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N6, the legal moves are to N3 and N4. N3 and N4 are both terminal nodes, meaning the player who moves to them wins immediately (the opponent has no legal move). Thus, both moves end the game. Since the player who makes the move wins, choosing either N3 or N4 is optimal. However, since both lead to immediate termination, either is valid. But N4 leads to terminal state directly, and N3 also does. Both are equivalent in outcome. Choosing N4 is safe and direct. However, since the goal is to maximize final game outcome and both end the game immediately, either is fine. We pick one. N4 is chosen.</reason>\n<answer>N4</answer>", "episode_score": 0.05, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N6, the legal moves are to N3 and N4. N3 and N4 are both terminal nodes, meaning the player who moves to them wins immediately (the opponent has no legal move). Thus, both moves end the game. Since the player who makes the move wins, choosing either N3 or N4 is optimal. However, since both lead to immediate termination, either is valid. But N4 leads to terminal state directly, and N3 also does. Both are equivalent in outcome. Choosing N4 is safe and direct. However, since the goal is to maximize final game outcome and both end the game immediately, either is fine. We pick one. N4 is chosen.</reason>\n<answer>N4</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N3\n\nDirected graph:\nN10 -> terminal\nN6 -> N0, N9\nN0 -> N10, N9\nN5 -> N8, N11\nN2 -> terminal\nN12 -> N2, N13\nN3 -> N5, N4\nN4 -> N1, N12\nN13 -> terminal\nN1 -> terminal\nN9 -> terminal\nN8 -> N6, N1\nN11 -> N13, N7\nN7 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N5, N4assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N3, the legal moves are to N5 or N4.  \n- Moving to N5 leads to N8 or N11:\n  - N8 → N6 → N0 or N9; N6 → N9 leads to terminal; N9 → terminal (win).\n  - N11 → N13 → terminal, or N7 → terminal (win).\n  So N5 gives opponent a losing position.\n- Moving to N4 leads to N1 or N12:\n  - N1 → terminal, opponent loses immediately. \n  - N12 → N2 (terminal) or N13 (terminal), both end immediately.\n\nBut N4 gives a win now, as moving to N1 or N12 results in terminal. So, N4 is winning immediately. N5 leads to a situation where opponent may have a winning move (e.g., N6 → N0 → N10 → terminal). However, N4 allows immediate end. Thus, choosing N4 wins the game.</reason>\n<answer>N4</answer>", "episode_score": -0.95, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N3, the legal moves are to N5 or N4.  \n- Moving to N5 leads to N8 or N11:\n  - N8 → N6 → N0 or N9; N6 → N9 leads to terminal; N9 → terminal (win).\n  - N11 → N13 → terminal, or N7 → terminal (win).\n  So N5 gives opponent a losing position.\n- Moving to N4 leads to N1 or N12:\n  - N1 → terminal, opponent loses immediately. \n  - N12 → N2 (terminal) or N13 (terminal), both end immediately.\n\nBut N4 gives a win now, as moving to N1 or N12 results in terminal. So, N4 is winning immediately. N5 leads to a situation where opponent may have a winning move (e.g., N6 → N0 → N10 → terminal). However, N4 allows immediate end. Thus, choosing N4 wins the game.</reason>\n<answer>N4</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N4\n\nDirected graph:\nN10 -> terminal\nN6 -> N0, N9\nN0 -> N10, N9\nN5 -> N8, N11\nN2 -> terminal\nN12 -> N2, N13\nN3 -> N5, N4\nN4 -> N1, N12\nN13 -> terminal\nN1 -> terminal\nN9 -> terminal\nN8 -> N6, N1\nN11 -> N13, N7\nN7 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N1, N12assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N4, the legal moves are to N1 or N12. N1 leads directly to terminal (win for the player who moves there, as the opponent has no move). N12 leads to N2 or N13. N2 leads to terminal, and N13 leads to terminal. Thus, moving to N1 wins immediately. Moving to N12 allows the opponent to move to N2 or N13, both of which lead to terminal. So, choosing N1 wins the game. The optimal move is to N1.</reason>\n<answer>N1</answer>", "episode_score": 1.05, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N4, the legal moves are to N1 or N12. N1 leads directly to terminal (win for the player who moves there, as the opponent has no move). N12 leads to N2 or N13. N2 leads to terminal, and N13 leads to terminal. Thus, moving to N1 wins immediately. Moving to N12 allows the opponent to move to N2 or N13, both of which lead to terminal. So, choosing N1 wins the game. The optimal move is to N1.</reason>\n<answer>N1</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N4\n\nDirected graph:\nN13 -> N8, N6\nN8 -> terminal\nN6 -> N1, N12\nN1 -> terminal\nN4 -> N5, N9\nN3 -> terminal\nN2 -> N3, N0\nN0 -> terminal\nN7 -> terminal\nN5 -> N2, N10\nN12 -> N7, N3\nN11 -> terminal\nN9 -> N11, N8\nN10 -> N13, N1\n\nLegal moves: N5, N9assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N4, the legal moves are to N5 or N9.  \n- Moving to N5: then opponent faces N5. From N5, moves are to N2 or N10.  \n   - If to N2: leads to N3 (terminal) or N0 (terminal), both losing.  \n   - If to N10: then N10 → N13 or N1, both eventually lead to terminal (N13 → N8 wins for next, or N8 → terminal).  \n   - N5 → N2 → N13 → N8, N2 → N3 → terminal, so N13 or N8 are safe.  \n   But N5 → N10 → N13 → N6 → N1: leads to loss eventually. \n   So N5 paths may lead to terminal for opponent.  \n- N9: leads to N11 → terminal, N9 → N8 → terminal. So N9 leads directly to terminal.  \nThus, from N9, both paths are terminal, so the opponent loses.  \n\nTherefore, choosing N9 leads to immediate terminal game after the opponent’s move.  \n\nBest move is to N9.</reason>\n<answer>N9</answer>", "episode_score": -0.95, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N4, the legal moves are to N5 or N9.  \n- Moving to N5: then opponent faces N5. From N5, moves are to N2 or N10.  \n   - If to N2: leads to N3 (terminal) or N0 (terminal), both losing.  \n   - If to N10: then N10 → N13 or N1, both eventually lead to terminal (N13 → N8 wins for next, or N8 → terminal).  \n   - N5 → N2 → N13 → N8, N2 → N3 → terminal, so N13 or N8 are safe.  \n   But N5 → N10 → N13 → N6 → N1: leads to loss eventually. \n   So N5 paths may lead to terminal for opponent.  \n- N9: leads to N11 → terminal, N9 → N8 → terminal. So N9 leads directly to terminal.  \nThus, from N9, both paths are terminal, so the opponent loses.  \n\nTherefore, choosing N9 leads to immediate terminal game after the opponent’s move.  \n\nBest move is to N9.</reason>\n<answer>N9</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N9\n\nDirected graph:\nN13 -> N8, N6\nN8 -> terminal\nN6 -> N1, N12\nN1 -> terminal\nN4 -> N5, N9\nN3 -> terminal\nN2 -> N3, N0\nN0 -> terminal\nN7 -> terminal\nN5 -> N2, N10\nN12 -> N7, N3\nN11 -> terminal\nN9 -> N11, N8\nN10 -> N13, N1\n\nLegal moves: N11, N8assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N9, the legal moves are to N11 or N8. N8 leads directly to terminal (win for the player who moves there, as the opponent has no move). N11 leads to terminal immediately. Both moves end the game immediately. Since the player who makes the move wins (the opponent has no legal move), choosing either N8 or N11 results in an immediate win. However, N8 is a direct terminal move, and N11 also terminates the game. Both are winning moves. But since the goal is to maximize outcome and both lead to victory, either is optimal. However, N8 is safer and more direct. Thus, N8 is chosen.</reason>\n<answer>N8</answer>", "episode_score": 0.05, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N9, the legal moves are to N11 or N8. N8 leads directly to terminal (win for the player who moves there, as the opponent has no move). N11 leads to terminal immediately. Both moves end the game immediately. Since the player who makes the move wins (the opponent has no legal move), choosing either N8 or N11 results in an immediate win. However, N8 is a direct terminal move, and N11 also terminates the game. Both are winning moves. But since the goal is to maximize outcome and both lead to victory, either is optimal. However, N8 is safer and more direct. Thus, N8 is chosen.</reason>\n<answer>N8</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N6\n\nDirected graph:\nN0 -> terminal\nN12 -> terminal\nN6 -> N9, N13, N3\nN7 -> N5\nN4 -> terminal\nN10 -> N8\nN8 -> terminal\nN13 -> N10\nN1 -> N4, N11, N7, N0\nN5 -> terminal\nN11 -> N9, N2\nN9 -> N12\nN2 -> terminal\nN3 -> N10, N1, N0\n\nLegal moves: N9, N13, N3assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N6, the legal moves are to N9, N13, or N3.  \n- N9 leads to N12 (terminal) — immediate win for the player who moves here.  \n- N13 leads to N10, then N8 (terminal). So the opponent wins if they move to N10.  \n- N3 leads to N10 or N1 or N0. N0 and N1 lead to terminal nodes (N0 → terminal, N1 → N4 or N7; N4 → terminal; N7 → N5 → terminal). So N3 leads to terminal nodes, meaning the opponent wins immediately or after one step.  \n\nThus, moving to N9 wins immediately. Choosing N9 maximizes the outcome.</reason>\n<answer>N9</answer>", "episode_score": -0.6166666666666667, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N6, the legal moves are to N9, N13, or N3.  \n- N9 leads to N12 (terminal) — immediate win for the player who moves here.  \n- N13 leads to N10, then N8 (terminal). So the opponent wins if they move to N10.  \n- N3 leads to N10 or N1 or N0. N0 and N1 lead to terminal nodes (N0 → terminal, N1 → N4 or N7; N4 → terminal; N7 → N5 → terminal). So N3 leads to terminal nodes, meaning the opponent wins immediately or after one step.  \n\nThus, moving to N9 wins immediately. Choosing N9 maximizes the outcome.</reason>\n<answer>N9</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N9\n\nDirected graph:\nN0 -> terminal\nN12 -> terminal\nN6 -> N9, N13, N3\nN7 -> N5\nN4 -> terminal\nN10 -> N8\nN8 -> terminal\nN13 -> N10\nN1 -> N4, N11, N7, N0\nN5 -> terminal\nN11 -> N9, N2\nN9 -> N12\nN2 -> terminal\nN3 -> N10, N1, N0\n\nLegal moves: N12assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N9, the only legal move is to N12, which is a terminal node. Moving to N12 ends the game immediately, and the player who made the move wins (since the opponent has no legal move). Thus, choosing N12 is optimal and guarantees a win.</reason>\n<answer>N12</answer>", "episode_score": 0.05, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N9, the only legal move is to N12, which is a terminal node. Moving to N12 ends the game immediately, and the player who made the move wins (since the opponent has no legal move). Thus, choosing N12 is optimal and guarantees a win.</reason>\n<answer>N12</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N1\n\nDirected graph:\nN9 -> terminal\nN1 -> N2, N6\nN7 -> N4\nN0 -> N7, N3\nN8 -> terminal\nN6 -> N2, N8\nN2 -> N5, N0\nN4 -> terminal\nN3 -> terminal\nN5 -> N9, N7\n\nLegal moves: N2, N6assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N1, the player can move to N2 or N6. We analyze both options using backward induction, aiming to find the node that leads to a winning position for the first player (assuming optimal play).\n\nFrom N2: moves to N5 or N0.\n- From N5: can go to N9 (terminal → opponent loses) or N7 → N4 (terminal → opponent loses). Both are terminal wins.\n- From N0: goes to N7 or N3. N7 → N4 (terminal), N3 → terminal. All paths end in opponent losing.\n→ So all N2 moves lead to terminal win for the first player.\n\nFrom N6: goes to N2 or N8.\n- N8 → terminal → opponent loses immediately.\n- N2 → as above, leads to terminal opponent loss.\n→ So all N6 moves lead to terminal win for the first player.\n\nEither N2 or N6 leads to a win. Since both are winning moves, the optimal choice is between them. However, N6 gives an immediate path to N8 (terminal), which ends the game immediately. N2 has more branching but also leads to terminal states. However, since both lead to victory, we pick one that maximizes outcome. N6 offers a direct terminal win via N8. Thus, N6 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N6</answer>", "episode_score": -0.95, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N1, the player can move to N2 or N6. We analyze both options using backward induction, aiming to find the node that leads to a winning position for the first player (assuming optimal play).\n\nFrom N2: moves to N5 or N0.\n- From N5: can go to N9 (terminal → opponent loses) or N7 → N4 (terminal → opponent loses). Both are terminal wins.\n- From N0: goes to N7 or N3. N7 → N4 (terminal), N3 → terminal. All paths end in opponent losing.\n→ So all N2 moves lead to terminal win for the first player.\n\nFrom N6: goes to N2 or N8.\n- N8 → terminal → opponent loses immediately.\n- N2 → as above, leads to terminal opponent loss.\n→ So all N6 moves lead to terminal win for the first player.\n\nEither N2 or N6 leads to a win. Since both are winning moves, the optimal choice is between them. However, N6 gives an immediate path to N8 (terminal), which ends the game immediately. N2 has more branching but also leads to terminal states. However, since both lead to victory, we pick one that maximizes outcome. N6 offers a direct terminal win via N8. Thus, N6 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N6</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N6\n\nDirected graph:\nN9 -> terminal\nN1 -> N2, N6\nN7 -> N4\nN0 -> N7, N3\nN8 -> terminal\nN6 -> N2, N8\nN2 -> N5, N0\nN4 -> terminal\nN3 -> terminal\nN5 -> N9, N7\n\nLegal moves: N2, N8assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N6, the legal moves are to N2 or N8. N8 leads directly to terminal (win for the player who moves there, as the opponent has no move). Thus, moving to N8 wins immediately. Moving to N2 leads to N5 or N0. N5 leads to N9 (terminal) or N7; N7 leads to N4 (terminal). So from N2, the opponent can force a win by moving to N5→N7→N4 or N2→N0→N7→N4. Thus, N2 is a losing move. The optimal move is to N8, which ends the game immediately. </reason>\n<answer>N8</answer>", "episode_score": 0.05, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N6, the legal moves are to N2 or N8. N8 leads directly to terminal (win for the player who moves there, as the opponent has no move). Thus, moving to N8 wins immediately. Moving to N2 leads to N5 or N0. N5 leads to N9 (terminal) or N7; N7 leads to N4 (terminal). So from N2, the opponent can force a win by moving to N5→N7→N4 or N2→N0→N7→N4. Thus, N2 is a losing move. The optimal move is to N8, which ends the game immediately. </reason>\n<answer>N8</answer>"}]
