[2026-08-13 15:48:08] [agentic_pipeline.py (516)] [INFO] [DRIVER 0 / 1][PID 1538566] [{"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N4\n\nDirected graph:\nN3 -> terminal\nN0 -> N1, N2\nN9 -> N3, N0\nN1 -> N3\nN4 -> N5, N7\nN8 -> terminal\nN2 -> terminal\nN6 -> terminal\nN7 -> N8, N6\nN5 -> N1, N9\n\nLegal moves: N5, N7assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N4, the legal moves are to N5 and N7.  \n- If we move to N5: the opponent faces N5, which can go to N1 or N9.  \n  - N1 → N3 → terminal, so the opponent will win (we lose).  \n  - N9 → N3 → terminal or N0 → N1 → N3 or N0 → N2. N0 → N2 → terminal or N1 → N3 → terminal. All paths from N5 eventually lead to terminal, so the opponent wins.  \n- If we move to N7: the opponent faces N7, which leads to N8 (terminal) or N6 (terminal). Both are terminal. So the opponent loses immediately.  \nThus, moving to N7 wins immediately for the current player. The optimal move is to N7.</reason>\n<answer>N7</answer>", "episode_score": -0.95, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N4, the legal moves are to N5 and N7.  \n- If we move to N5: the opponent faces N5, which can go to N1 or N9.  \n  - N1 → N3 → terminal, so the opponent will win (we lose).  \n  - N9 → N3 → terminal or N0 → N1 → N3 or N0 → N2. N0 → N2 → terminal or N1 → N3 → terminal. All paths from N5 eventually lead to terminal, so the opponent wins.  \n- If we move to N7: the opponent faces N7, which leads to N8 (terminal) or N6 (terminal). Both are terminal. So the opponent loses immediately.  \nThus, moving to N7 wins immediately for the current player. The optimal move is to N7.</reason>\n<answer>N7</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N7\n\nDirected graph:\nN3 -> terminal\nN0 -> N1, N2\nN9 -> N3, N0\nN1 -> N3\nN4 -> N5, N7\nN8 -> terminal\nN2 -> terminal\nN6 -> terminal\nN7 -> N8, N6\nN5 -> N1, N9\n\nLegal moves: N8, N6assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N7, the legal moves are to N8 and N6. Both N8 and N6 lead to terminal nodes, so the player who moves to either wins immediately (the opponent has no legal move). Thus, both moves end the game immediately. Since the player who makes the move wins, choosing either N8 or N6 is optimal. However, since both lead to terminal states, either is valid. We choose one. N6 is selected as it appears earlier in the list.</reason>\n<answer>N6</answer>", "episode_score": 0.05, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N7, the legal moves are to N8 and N6. Both N8 and N6 lead to terminal nodes, so the player who moves to either wins immediately (the opponent has no legal move). Thus, both moves end the game immediately. Since the player who makes the move wins, choosing either N8 or N6 is optimal. However, since both lead to terminal states, either is valid. We choose one. N6 is selected as it appears earlier in the list.</reason>\n<answer>N6</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N4\n\nDirected graph:\nN9 -> terminal\nN3 -> N7\nN0 -> N5\nN5 -> N6, N1, N8\nN7 -> terminal\nN4 -> N5, N2, N0\nN6 -> terminal\nN1 -> terminal\nN8 -> N3, N1\nN2 -> N9\n\nLegal moves: N5, N2, N0assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N4, the legal moves are to N5, N2, or N0. We analyze each:  \n- N4 → N5: From N5, moves to N6 (terminal), N1 (terminal), or N8. N6 and N1 lead to terminal (loss for next player). N8 → N3 → N7 (terminal) or N8 → N1 (terminal). All paths from N5 lead to terminal. So, N5 loses immediately.  \n- N4 → N2: N2 → N9 (terminal), so the next player loses. This is a win.  \n- N4 → N0: N0 → N5, which we already know leads to terminal paths, so this is a loss.  \n\nMaximizing outcome: Choosing N2 leads to an immediate win. Thus, N2 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N2</answer>", "episode_score": -0.6166666666666667, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N4, the legal moves are to N5, N2, or N0. We analyze each:  \n- N4 → N5: From N5, moves to N6 (terminal), N1 (terminal), or N8. N6 and N1 lead to terminal (loss for next player). N8 → N3 → N7 (terminal) or N8 → N1 (terminal). All paths from N5 lead to terminal. So, N5 loses immediately.  \n- N4 → N2: N2 → N9 (terminal), so the next player loses. This is a win.  \n- N4 → N0: N0 → N5, which we already know leads to terminal paths, so this is a loss.  \n\nMaximizing outcome: Choosing N2 leads to an immediate win. Thus, N2 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N2</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N5\n\nDirected graph:\nN5 -> N1, N6, N2\nN7 -> N0, N2\nN6 -> N3, N1\nN9 -> terminal\nN1 -> N9\nN0 -> terminal\nN2 -> N4\nN8 -> terminal\nN4 -> terminal\nN3 -> N8, N7\n\nLegal moves: N1, N6, N2assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N5, the legal moves are to N1, N6, or N2. We analyze each destination and the resulting game outcomes:\n\n- Moving to N1: N1 → N9 (terminal), so the opponent loses immediately. This is a win.\n- Moving to N6: N6 → N3 or N1. \n  - N6 → N3 → N8 (terminal), or N3 → N7 → N0 or N2\n    - N3 → N7 → N2 → N4 → terminal, opponent wins.\n    - N3 → N7 → N0 → terminal, opponent wins.\n  - N6 → N1 → N9 → opponent wins.\n  So all paths from N6 lead to opponent winning.\n- Moving to N2: N2 → N4 → terminal, opponent wins immediately.\n\nOnly move to N1 leads to immediate win. Thus, choosing N1 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N1</answer>", "episode_score": -0.6166666666666667, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N5, the legal moves are to N1, N6, or N2. We analyze each destination and the resulting game outcomes:\n\n- Moving to N1: N1 → N9 (terminal), so the opponent loses immediately. This is a win.\n- Moving to N6: N6 → N3 or N1. \n  - N6 → N3 → N8 (terminal), or N3 → N7 → N0 or N2\n    - N3 → N7 → N2 → N4 → terminal, opponent wins.\n    - N3 → N7 → N0 → terminal, opponent wins.\n  - N6 → N1 → N9 → opponent wins.\n  So all paths from N6 lead to opponent winning.\n- Moving to N2: N2 → N4 → terminal, opponent wins immediately.\n\nOnly move to N1 leads to immediate win. Thus, choosing N1 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N1</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N2\n\nDirected graph:\nN9 -> terminal\nN3 -> N7\nN0 -> N5\nN5 -> N6, N1, N8\nN7 -> terminal\nN4 -> N5, N2, N0\nN6 -> terminal\nN1 -> terminal\nN8 -> N3, N1\nN2 -> N9\n\nLegal moves: N9assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N2, the only legal move is to N9, which leads to terminal. Thus, the first player (me) moves to N9 and wins immediately. No other move is available, so choosing N9 is the only option. Since the game ends immediately after this move, and the opponent has no legal move, this is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N9</answer>", "episode_score": 0.05, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N2, the only legal move is to N9, which leads to terminal. Thus, the first player (me) moves to N9 and wins immediately. No other move is available, so choosing N9 is the only option. Since the game ends immediately after this move, and the opponent has no legal move, this is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N9</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N1\n\nDirected graph:\nN5 -> N1, N6, N2\nN7 -> N0, N2\nN6 -> N3, N1\nN9 -> terminal\nN1 -> N9\nN0 -> terminal\nN2 -> N4\nN8 -> terminal\nN4 -> terminal\nN3 -> N8, N7\n\nLegal moves: N9assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N1, the legal moves are to N9 or N6. N9 is a terminal node, so moving there ends the game immediately and results in the opponent having no move, meaning the current player wins. Thus, choosing N9 is optimal and guarantees a win. Moving to N6 leads to N3 or N1, both of which can eventually lead to N9 or terminal nodes, but N9 is immediately available from N1. Since the player who moves to N9 wins, the optimal move is to N9.</reason>\n<answer>N9</answer>", "episode_score": 0.05, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N1, the legal moves are to N9 or N6. N9 is a terminal node, so moving there ends the game immediately and results in the opponent having no move, meaning the current player wins. Thus, choosing N9 is optimal and guarantees a win. Moving to N6 leads to N3 or N1, both of which can eventually lead to N9 or terminal nodes, but N9 is immediately available from N1. Since the player who moves to N9 wins, the optimal move is to N9.</reason>\n<answer>N9</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N6\n\nDirected graph:\nN9 -> N1, N7\nN1 -> N0\nN8 -> N5\nN7 -> terminal\nN3 -> terminal\nN5 -> N2\nN0 -> terminal\nN6 -> N9, N5, N8\nN4 -> terminal\nN2 -> N4, N3, N9\n\nLegal moves: N9, N5, N8assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N6, the legal moves are to N9, N5, or N8. We evaluate each destination:  \n- To N9: Next moves are to N1 or N7. N1 leads to N0 (terminal), so the opponent wins. N7 leads to terminal, so the current player wins. Thus, choosing N9 allows a win via N7.  \n- To N5: Moves to N2. From N2: options are N4 (terminal), N3 (terminal), or N9. N9 leads to N1 → N0 or N7. N7 is terminal win for the player who moves there. So from N2, a move to N9 can lead to a win. So from N6 → N5 → N2 → N9 → N7, the current player wins.  \n- To N8: Leads to N5, which we already know leads to N2, then potentially N9 → N7. Again, a win.  \n\nAll options eventually allow a win via N7. However, choosing N7 directly from N9 or N2 is best. Since N6 → N9 → N7 wins immediately, and this is a direct path, it is optimal. Thus, moving to N9 is best as it leads directly to a terminal win via N7.</reason>  \n<answer>N9</answer>", "episode_score": -0.6166666666666667, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N6, the legal moves are to N9, N5, or N8. We evaluate each destination:  \n- To N9: Next moves are to N1 or N7. N1 leads to N0 (terminal), so the opponent wins. N7 leads to terminal, so the current player wins. Thus, choosing N9 allows a win via N7.  \n- To N5: Moves to N2. From N2: options are N4 (terminal), N3 (terminal), or N9. N9 leads to N1 → N0 or N7. N7 is terminal win for the player who moves there. So from N2, a move to N9 can lead to a win. So from N6 → N5 → N2 → N9 → N7, the current player wins.  \n- To N8: Leads to N5, which we already know leads to N2, then potentially N9 → N7. Again, a win.  \n\nAll options eventually allow a win via N7. However, choosing N7 directly from N9 or N2 is best. Since N6 → N9 → N7 wins immediately, and this is a direct path, it is optimal. Thus, moving to N9 is best as it leads directly to a terminal win via N7.</reason>  \n<answer>N9</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N9\n\nDirected graph:\nN9 -> N1, N7\nN1 -> N0\nN8 -> N5\nN7 -> terminal\nN3 -> terminal\nN5 -> N2\nN0 -> terminal\nN6 -> N9, N5, N8\nN4 -> terminal\nN2 -> N4, N3, N9\n\nLegal moves: N1, N7assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N9, the legal moves are to N1 or N7. N7 leads directly to terminal (win for the player who moves there, as the opponent has no move). Thus, moving to N7 wins immediately. Moving to N1 leads to N0, which is terminal, so the opponent wins. Therefore, the optimal move is to N7.</reason>\n<answer>N7</answer>", "episode_score": 1.05, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N9, the legal moves are to N1 or N7. N7 leads directly to terminal (win for the player who moves there, as the opponent has no move). Thus, moving to N7 wins immediately. Moving to N1 leads to N0, which is terminal, so the opponent wins. Therefore, the optimal move is to N7.</reason>\n<answer>N7</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N2\n\nDirected graph:\nN6 -> N4, N3\nN8 -> terminal\nN9 -> terminal\nN0 -> N4, N7\nN5 -> N8\nN1 -> N6, N5\nN3 -> N9, N0\nN7 -> terminal\nN4 -> terminal\nN2 -> N1, N0\n\nLegal moves: N1, N0assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N2, the legal moves are to N1 or N0. We analyze both:  \n- Moving to N1: from N1, moves are to N6 or N5.  \n  - N6 → N4 or N3: N4 is terminal (win for opponent), N3 → N9 or N0. N9 terminal (opponent wins), N0 → N4 or N7; N4/7 terminal. So N6 leads to opponent winning.  \n  - N5 → N8 (terminal, opponent wins). So all paths from N1 lead to opponent winning.  \n- Moving to N0: from N0 → N4 (terminal, opponent wins) or N7 (terminal, opponent wins). So this also leads to opponent winning.  \n\nBoth moves result in the opponent winning. Since the player with no legal move loses, we must choose a move that forces the opponent into a losing position. However, all paths from N2 lead to terminal nodes for the opponent. Thus, no matter which move is taken, the opponent wins. But to maximize outcome, we pick a move that doesn’t immediately lose us. Both are equivalent. Picking N0 or N1 leads to opponent's win. Since both are equivalent, we choose one. N0 is safer as it avoids N1's N6 path which has risk. However, all paths end in terminal. Thus, any move is equivalent. Choose N0.</reason>\n<answer>N0</answer>", "episode_score": -0.95, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N2, the legal moves are to N1 or N0. We analyze both:  \n- Moving to N1: from N1, moves are to N6 or N5.  \n  - N6 → N4 or N3: N4 is terminal (win for opponent), N3 → N9 or N0. N9 terminal (opponent wins), N0 → N4 or N7; N4/7 terminal. So N6 leads to opponent winning.  \n  - N5 → N8 (terminal, opponent wins). So all paths from N1 lead to opponent winning.  \n- Moving to N0: from N0 → N4 (terminal, opponent wins) or N7 (terminal, opponent wins). So this also leads to opponent winning.  \n\nBoth moves result in the opponent winning. Since the player with no legal move loses, we must choose a move that forces the opponent into a losing position. However, all paths from N2 lead to terminal nodes for the opponent. Thus, no matter which move is taken, the opponent wins. But to maximize outcome, we pick a move that doesn’t immediately lose us. Both are equivalent. Picking N0 or N1 leads to opponent's win. Since both are equivalent, we choose one. N0 is safer as it avoids N1's N6 path which has risk. However, all paths end in terminal. Thus, any move is equivalent. Choose N0.</reason>\n<answer>N0</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N0\n\nDirected graph:\nN6 -> N4, N3\nN8 -> terminal\nN9 -> terminal\nN0 -> N4, N7\nN5 -> N8\nN1 -> N6, N5\nN3 -> N9, N0\nN7 -> terminal\nN4 -> terminal\nN2 -> N1, N0\n\nLegal moves: N4, N7assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N0, the legal moves are to N4 or N7. N4 and N7 are terminal nodes, so moving to either ends the game immediately. The player who makes the move to a terminal node wins, as the opponent has no legal move. Thus, choosing either N4 or N7 wins the game. Both are winning moves. Since the goal is to maximize final game outcome and both lead to an immediate win, either is optimal. However, N7 is a direct move with no further play, and N4 also leads to terminal. Both are equivalent. Choosing N7 is safe and direct. But N0 also has a move to N7, which is terminal, so it is optimal. Therefore, the best choice is N7.</reason>\n<answer>N7</answer>", "episode_score": 0.05, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N0, the legal moves are to N4 or N7. N4 and N7 are terminal nodes, so moving to either ends the game immediately. The player who makes the move to a terminal node wins, as the opponent has no legal move. Thus, choosing either N4 or N7 wins the game. Both are winning moves. Since the goal is to maximize final game outcome and both lead to an immediate win, either is optimal. However, N7 is a direct move with no further play, and N4 also leads to terminal. Both are equivalent. Choosing N7 is safe and direct. But N0 also has a move to N7, which is terminal, so it is optimal. Therefore, the best choice is N7.</reason>\n<answer>N7</answer>"}]
