nning. However, since the player who cannot move loses, we must choose a move that delays the terminal state as long as possible or forces the opponent into a losing position. But all paths from N4 eventually lead to terminal nodes. Thus, both N8 and N0 result in the opponent winning. Since both are equivalent in outcome, we pick one. N0 is safer as it avoids N8's immediate risk. But ultimately, all moves lose. However, maximizing outcome means choosing a path that gives the opponent no advantage. Since all paths end immediately, any choice is equal. We choose N0 as it avoids N8's path which leads to N2→N3. But both are losing. The optimal choice is N0.</reason>  \n<answer>N0</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N0\n\nDirected graph:\nN4 -> N8, N0\nN0 -> N5, N9\nN2 -> N3\nN9 -> terminal\nN5 -> N7, N1\nN1 -> terminal\nN3 -> terminal\nN6 -> N1, N2\nN8 -> N6, N2\nN7 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N5, N9assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N0, the legal moves are to N5 or N9. N9 leads directly to terminal (win for the player who moves there, as the opponent has no move). Thus, moving to N9 wins immediately. Moving to N5 leads to N7 or N1, both of which are terminal, so the opponent wins. Therefore, the optimal move is to N9, which ends the game immediately and wins.</reason>\n<answer>N9</answer>", "episode_score": 1.05, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N0, the legal moves are to N5 or N9. N9 leads directly to terminal (win for the player who moves there, as the opponent has no move). Thus, moving to N9 wins immedia