t N6, the legal moves are to N0 or N4. We need to evaluate which move leads to a winning position. \n\nFrom N0: can move to N4 or N3. \n- N4 leads to N7 or N8 (both N7 → N9, N5; N8 → terminal). N9 → N2 → terminal, N5 → terminal → so any from N7 or N4 leads to terminal eventually. So N4 is a losing move if opponent plays optimally.\n- N3 → N1 (terminal) or N8 → terminal → both end immediately. So N0 leads to terminal for the opponent; so N0 is a losing move.\n\nFrom N4:\n- N7 → N9 (→ N2 → terminal), or N8 (→ terminal). Both terminal; opponent loses. So N4 leads to opponent losing immediately. \n\nTherefore, moving to N4 wins because all paths from N4 lead to terminal for the opponent. Thus, choosing N4 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N4</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N4\n\nDirected graph:\nN3 -> N1, N8\nN2 -> terminal\nN7 -> N9, N5\nN6 -> N0, N4\nN0 -> N4, N3\nN1 -> terminal\nN4 -> N7, N8\nN9 -> N2\nN5 -> terminal\nN8 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N7, N8assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N4, the legal moves are to N7 or N8. N8 leads directly to terminal (win for the player who moves there, as the opponent has no move). Thus, moving to N8 wins immediately. Moving to N7 leads to N9 or N5: N9 leads to N2 (terminal), N5 leads to terminal. Either way, the opponent wins. Therefore, choosing N8 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N8</answer>", "episode_score": 1.05, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N4, the legal moves are to N7 or N8. N8 leads directly to terminal (win for the player who moves there, as the opponent has no move). Thus, moving to N8 wins immediately. Moving to N7 leads to N9 or N5: N9 leads to N2 (terminal), N5 leads to terminal. Either way, the opponent wins. Therefore, choosing N8 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N8</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N0\n\nDirected graph:\nN1 -> terminal\nN8 -> N6\nN4 -> N7, N3, N9\nN2 -> terminal\nN7 -> N3\nN5 -> N1\nN3 -> N5, N2\nN0 -> N8, N7, N4\nN6 -> terminal\nN9 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N8, N7, N4assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N0, the players can move to N8, N7, or N4. We evaluate each option:\n\n- Moving to N8: leads to N6 → terminal → current player wins (opponent has no move).\n- Moving to N7: leads to N3 → from N3, opponent can go to N5 → N1 → terminal (opponent wins) or N2 → terminal (opponent wins). So N3 is a losing position for the player moving next. Thus, N7 leads to a winning position for the player who owns N3's turn, so the original player loses.\n- Moving to N4: leads to N7 → N3 → N2 or N5 → both end in terminal. So N4 leads to N7 → N3 → N2 or N5 → terminal, both end with opponent having a move that ends game. So N4 leads to opponent winning.\n\nThus, N8 leads to immediate terminal, so the current player wins. N7 and N4 lead to opponent winning. The best move is to N8.</reason>\n<answer>N8</answer>", "episode_score": -0.6166666666666667, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N0, the players can move to N8, N7, or N4. We evaluate each option:\n\n- Moving to N8: leads to N6 → terminal → current player wins (opponent has no move).\n- Moving to N7: leads to N3 → from N3, opponent can go to N5 → N1 → terminal (opponent wins) or N2 → terminal (opponent wins). So N3 is a losing position for the player moving next. Thus, N7 leads to a winning position for the player who owns N3's turn, so the original player loses.\n- Moving to N4: leads to N7 → N3 → N2 or N5 → both end in terminal. So N4 leads to N7 → N3 → N2 or N5 → terminal, both end with opponent having a move that ends game. So N4 leads to opponent winning.\n\nThus, N8 leads to immediate terminal, so the current player wins. N7 and N4 lead to opponent winning. The best move is to N8.</reason>\n<answer>N8</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N8\n\nDirected graph:\nN1 -> terminal\nN8 -> N6\nN4 -> N7, N3, N9\nN2 -> terminal\nN7 -> N3\nN5 -> N1\nN3 -> N5, N2\nN0 -> N8, N7, N4\nN6 -> terminal\nN9 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N6assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N8, the only legal move is to N6. N6 leads to terminal, so the player who moves to N6 wins immediately (the opponent has no move). Thus, moving to N6 results in an immediate win. No other move from N8 is available. Therefore, the optimal move is to N6.</reason>\n<answer>N6</answer>", "episode_score": 0.05, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N8, the only legal move is to N6. N6 leads to terminal, so the player who moves to N6 wins immediately (the opponent has no move). Thus, moving to N6 results in an immediate win. No other move from N8 is available. Therefore, the optimal move is to N6.</reason>\n<answer>N6</answer>"}]
