[2026-08-13 15:54:42] [agentic_pipeline.py (516)] [INFO] [DRIVER 0 / 1][PID 1538566] [{"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N4\n\nDirected graph:\nN1 -> terminal\nN5 -> terminal\nN0 -> N2, N1\nN8 -> N5, N13\nN12 -> N11, N10\nN6 -> terminal\nN13 -> N9, N6\nN10 -> terminal\nN3 -> N11, N2\nN7 -> N8, N3\nN2 -> terminal\nN4 -> N0, N7\nN9 -> N12, N10\nN11 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N0, N7assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N4, the legal moves are to N0 or N7. We analyze each option:  \n- Moving to N0: From N0, moves are to N2 or N1. Both N2 and N1 lead to terminal, so the player who moves to N0 wins immediately (opponent has no move).  \n- Moving to N7: From N7, moves are to N8 or N3.  \n  - N8 → N5 or N13; both N5 and N13 eventually lead to terminal via N13→N9→N12→N11 (terminal) or N13→N6 (terminal).  \n  - N3 → N11 or N2; both are terminal.  \nThus, from N7, the opponent will eventually lose. But if the player moves to N0, they force a terminal state immediately, meaning the opponent has no move and thus loses. Therefore, moving to N0 is optimal and guarantees a win. Choosing N0 maximizes the outcome.</reason>\n<answer>N0</answer>", "episode_score": -0.95, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N4, the legal moves are to N0 or N7. We analyze each option:  \n- Moving to N0: From N0, moves are to N2 or N1. Both N2 and N1 lead to terminal, so the player who moves to N0 wins immediately (opponent has no move).  \n- Moving to N7: From N7, moves are to N8 or N3.  \n  - N8 → N5 or N13; both N5 and N13 eventually lead to terminal via N13→N9→N12→N11 (terminal) or N13→N6 (terminal).  \n  - N3 → N11 or N2; both are terminal.  \nThus, from N7, the opponent will eventually lose. But if the player moves to N0, they force a terminal state immediately, meaning the opponent has no move and thus loses. Therefore, moving to N0 is optimal and guarantees a win. Choosing N0 maximizes the outcome.</reason>\n<answer>N0</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N0\n\nDirected graph:\nN1 -> terminal\nN5 -> terminal\nN0 -> N2, N1\nN8 -> N5, N13\nN12 -> N11, N10\nN6 -> terminal\nN13 -> N9, N6\nN10 -> terminal\nN3 -> N11, N2\nN7 -> N8, N3\nN2 -> terminal\nN4 -> N0, N7\nN9 -> N12, N10\nN11 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N2, N1assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N0, the legal moves are to N2 or N1. N2 leads directly to terminal (win for the player who moves there), so moving to N2 ends the game immediately. N1 leads to terminal as well. Both N2 and N1 are terminal moves, so the player who moves to either wins. Thus, choosing either N2 or N1 is optimal. Since N2 is a direct move to terminal, it is a valid and optimal choice.</reason>\n<answer>N2</answer>", "episode_score": 0.05, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N0, the legal moves are to N2 or N1. N2 leads directly to terminal (win for the player who moves there), so moving to N2 ends the game immediately. N1 leads to terminal as well. Both N2 and N1 are terminal moves, so the player who moves to either wins. Thus, choosing either N2 or N1 is optimal. Since N2 is a direct move to terminal, it is a valid and optimal choice.</reason>\n<answer>N2</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N9\n\nDirected graph:\nN3 -> terminal\nN2 -> N4, N6\nN0 -> N7, N5\nN4 -> N3\nN5 -> terminal\nN7 -> terminal\nN6 -> N8, N3\nN9 -> N2, N8\nN1 -> terminal\nN8 -> N1, N0\n\nLegal moves: N2, N8assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N9, the player can move to N2 or N8. We analyze each option:\n\n- Moving to N2: Then player can go to N4 or N6. \n  - N4 → N3 → terminal (opponent wins)\n  - N6 → N8 → N0 → N7 → terminal; or N8 → N1 → terminal (both end in opponent win)\n  → So from N2, all paths lead to terminal state, meaning the current player loses.\n\n- Moving to N8: \n  - N8 → N1 → terminal (opponent wins) or N8 → N0 → N7 → terminal\n  → All paths from N8 lead to terminal state. Thus, all moves from N9 lead to the opponent winning.\n\nSince every legal move results in the opponent winning, the optimal choice is to move to N8, as it is the only available node that doesn't immediately lose. However, since all paths eventually end in terminal states, the player who moves first (us) will lose regardless. But we must choose a move that maximizes outcome. As all options lead to loss, the best choice is N8, as it avoids N2 which leads to N4 → N3 (immediate terminal). N8 gives the opponent a chance to move into N0 → N7 or N1, both terminal. Still, N8 is the only option with a slight edge in structure. Thus, N8 is chosen.</reason>\n<answer>N8</answer>", "episode_score": -0.95, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N9, the player can move to N2 or N8. We analyze each option:\n\n- Moving to N2: Then player can go to N4 or N6. \n  - N4 → N3 → terminal (opponent wins)\n  - N6 → N8 → N0 → N7 → terminal; or N8 → N1 → terminal (both end in opponent win)\n  → So from N2, all paths lead to terminal state, meaning the current player loses.\n\n- Moving to N8: \n  - N8 → N1 → terminal (opponent wins) or N8 → N0 → N7 → terminal\n  → All paths from N8 lead to terminal state. Thus, all moves from N9 lead to the opponent winning.\n\nSince every legal move results in the opponent winning, the optimal choice is to move to N8, as it is the only available node that doesn't immediately lose. However, since all paths eventually end in terminal states, the player who moves first (us) will lose regardless. But we must choose a move that maximizes outcome. As all options lead to loss, the best choice is N8, as it avoids N2 which leads to N4 → N3 (immediate terminal). N8 gives the opponent a chance to move into N0 → N7 or N1, both terminal. Still, N8 is the only option with a slight edge in structure. Thus, N8 is chosen.</reason>\n<answer>N8</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N8\n\nDirected graph:\nN3 -> terminal\nN2 -> N4, N6\nN0 -> N7, N5\nN4 -> N3\nN5 -> terminal\nN7 -> terminal\nN6 -> N8, N3\nN9 -> N2, N8\nN1 -> terminal\nN8 -> N1, N0\n\nLegal moves: N1, N0assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N8, the legal moves are to N1 or N0. N1 leads to terminal (win for the player who moves there, as the opponent has no move). N0 leads to N7 or N5, both of which are terminal. Thus, moving to N1 wins immediately. Choosing N1 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N1</answer>", "episode_score": 1.05, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N8, the legal moves are to N1 or N0. N1 leads to terminal (win for the player who moves there, as the opponent has no move). N0 leads to N7 or N5, both of which are terminal. Thus, moving to N1 wins immediately. Choosing N1 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N1</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N3\n\nDirected graph:\nN0 -> N2, N6\nN6 -> terminal\nN1 -> terminal\nN2 -> N9\nN8 -> N5, N4\nN3 -> N7, N2, N5\nN5 -> N0\nN7 -> N1, N8\nN4 -> terminal\nN9 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N7, N2, N5assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N3, the legal moves are to N7, N2, or N5. We analyze each option:\n\n- Moving to N2: N2 → N9 (terminal). Player who moves to N9 wins immediately, so the opponent loses. So, this is a winning move.\n- Moving to N5: N5 → N0. From N0, the next move is to N2 or N6. N6 leads to terminal (win for opponent), N2 leads to N9 (win for opponent). So from N0, opponent can force a win. Thus, moving to N5 leads to opponent winning.\n- Moving to N7: N7 → N1 or N8. N1 leads to terminal (opponent wins). N8 → N5 or N4. N4 leads to terminal (opponent wins). N5 leads back to N0, which as analyzed, leads to opponent winning. So all paths from N7 lead to opponent winning.\n\nThus, only moving to N2 leads to an immediate win (N9 terminal). All other moves let the opponent win. Therefore, the optimal move is to N2.</reason>\n<answer>N2</answer>", "episode_score": -0.6166666666666667, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N3, the legal moves are to N7, N2, or N5. We analyze each option:\n\n- Moving to N2: N2 → N9 (terminal). Player who moves to N9 wins immediately, so the opponent loses. So, this is a winning move.\n- Moving to N5: N5 → N0. From N0, the next move is to N2 or N6. N6 leads to terminal (win for opponent), N2 leads to N9 (win for opponent). So from N0, opponent can force a win. Thus, moving to N5 leads to opponent winning.\n- Moving to N7: N7 → N1 or N8. N1 leads to terminal (opponent wins). N8 → N5 or N4. N4 leads to terminal (opponent wins). N5 leads back to N0, which as analyzed, leads to opponent winning. So all paths from N7 lead to opponent winning.\n\nThus, only moving to N2 leads to an immediate win (N9 terminal). All other moves let the opponent win. Therefore, the optimal move is to N2.</reason>\n<answer>N2</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N2\n\nDirected graph:\nN0 -> N2, N6\nN6 -> terminal\nN1 -> terminal\nN2 -> N9\nN8 -> N5, N4\nN3 -> N7, N2, N5\nN5 -> N0\nN7 -> N1, N8\nN4 -> terminal\nN9 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N9assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N2, the only legal move is to N9. N9 leads to terminal, so the player who moves to N9 wins immediately (the opponent has no move). Thus, moving to N9 is optimal and results in an immediate win. No other move from N2 is available. Therefore, the best choice is N9.</reason>\n<answer>N9</answer>", "episode_score": 0.05, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N2, the only legal move is to N9. N9 leads to terminal, so the player who moves to N9 wins immediately (the opponent has no move). Thus, moving to N9 is optimal and results in an immediate win. No other move from N2 is available. Therefore, the best choice is N9.</reason>\n<answer>N9</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N9\n\nDirected graph:\nN9 -> N8, N7, N2\nN0 -> terminal\nN8 -> N3\nN1 -> terminal\nN6 -> terminal\nN3 -> N1, N4, N6\nN2 -> N0\nN7 -> N8, N2\nN4 -> N5\nN5 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N8, N7, N2assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N9, the legal moves are to N8, N7, or N2. We analyze each:\n\n- Move to N8: Then to N3. From N3, moves are to N1 (terminal loss), N4. N4 leads to N5 (terminal loss). So N3 → N1 or N4 → N5, both terminal. So player who faces N3 loses.\n- Move to N7: Then can go to N8 → N3 (same as above) or N2 → N0 (terminal loss). So N7 → N8 → N3 leads to opponent facing N3, which is bad. N7 → N2 → N0: opponent faces terminal → opponent loses. So choosing N7 → N2 wins.\n- Move to N2: Then to N0 (terminal) → opponent loses immediately.\n\nThus, moving to N2 leads to immediate opponent loss. Moving to N7 gives a chance but risks N3. Moving to N8 leads to N3, which is losing. Best move is N2, which wins immediately.</reason>\n<answer>N2</answer>", "episode_score": -0.6166666666666667, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N9, the legal moves are to N8, N7, or N2. We analyze each:\n\n- Move to N8: Then to N3. From N3, moves are to N1 (terminal loss), N4. N4 leads to N5 (terminal loss). So N3 → N1 or N4 → N5, both terminal. So player who faces N3 loses.\n- Move to N7: Then