 N12: N12 → N13 or N4. N4 is terminal, so opponent wins. N13 → N0, N11, N10. \n  - N0: terminal → opponent wins.\n  - N11 → N7 (terminal) or N6 (terminal) → opponent wins.\n  - N10 → N6 (terminal) or N3 → N5 (terminal). Both terminal → opponent wins.\n  So all paths from N12 lead to opponent winning. Thus, N12 is losing.\n\n- Moving to N13: N13 → N0, N11, or N10 — all lead to terminal nodes or to nodes feeding into terminals. All paths eventually end with opponent winning.\n\nAll moves from N8 lead to the opponent winning. However, the question asks to choose a move that *maximizes* final game outcome. Since all options result in loss, the best choice is the one that delays the loss longest or offers no immediate win. But all paths end in terminal nodes. Thus, no move is winning. Yet, since we must choose one, and all are equivalent in outcome (loss), we select a move that at least avoids immediate terminal. N13 leads to N11 which branches to N7 or N6 — both terminal. Still, no advantage. But N13 offers more options. However, all paths end in terminal. \n\nSince all options lead to opponent's win, and no move is better than another in outcome, pick one. N13 is a safe, active choice. Thus, optimal is N13.</reason>\n<answer>N13</answer>", "episode_score": -0.6166666666666667, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N8, the legal moves are to N13, N12, or N2. We analyze each:\n\n- Moving to N2: N2 → N1 → then N1 has moves to N9 (terminal) or N4 (terminal). Both are terminal, so the opponent wins immediately. Thus, moving to N2 leads to loss.\n\n- Moving to N12: N12 → N13 or N4. N4 is terminal, so opponent wins. N13 → N0, N11, N10. \n  - N0: terminal → opponent wins.\n  - N11 → N7 (terminal) or N6 (terminal) → opponent wins.\n  - N10 → N6 (terminal) or N3 → N5 (terminal). Both terminal → opponent wins.\n  So all paths from N12 lead to opponent winning. Thus, N12 is losing.\n\n- Moving to N13: N13 → N0, N11, or N10 — all lead to terminal nodes or to nodes feeding into terminals. All paths eventually end with opponent winning.\n\nAll moves from N8 lead to the opponent winning. However, the question asks to choose a move that *maximizes* final game outcome. Since all options result in loss, the best choice is the one that delays the loss longest or offers no immediate win. But all paths end in terminal nodes. Thus, no move is winning. Yet, since we must choose one, and all are equivalent in outcome (loss), we select a move that at least avoids immediate terminal. N13 leads to N11 which branches to N7 or N6 — both terminal. Still, no advantage. But N13 offers more options. However, all paths end in terminal. \n\nSince all options lead to opponent's win, and no move is better than another in outcome, pick one. N13 is a safe, active choice. Thus, optimal is N13.</reason>\n<answer>N13</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N13\n\nDirected graph:\nN5 -> terminal\nN10 -> N6, N3\nN11 -> N7, N6\nN9 -> terminal\nN8 -> N13, N12, N2\nN12 -> N13, N4\nN13 -> N0, N11, N10\nN6 -> terminal\nN1 -> N9, N4\nN7 -> terminal\nN4 -> terminal\nN0 -> terminal\nN3 -> N5\nN2 -> N1\n\nLegal moves: N0, N11, N10assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N13, the legal moves are to N0, N11, or N10. N0 leads directly to terminal (win for the player who moves there, as the opponent has no move). Thus, moving to N0 wins immediately. Since the goal is to maximize final game outcome, choosing N0 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N0</answer>", "episode_score": 1.3833333333333333, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N13, the legal