[2026-08-13 16:05:57] [agentic_pipeline.py (516)] [INFO] [DRIVER 0 / 1][PID 1538566] [{"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N11\n\nDirected graph:\nN6 -> N4, N2\nN2 -> N1, N12\nN0 -> N7, N10\nN3 -> N8, N7\nN7 -> terminal\nN13 -> N9, N0\nN4 -> N1, N3\nN5 -> N13, N9\nN11 -> N6, N5\nN8 -> terminal\nN12 -> terminal\nN9 -> terminal\nN10 -> terminal\nN1 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N6, N5assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N11, the player can move to N6 or N5.  \n- From N6: paths are to N4 or N2.  \n  - N4 → N1 (terminal) or N3 → N8 (terminal). Both lead to loss on next move if opponent plays optimally.  \n  - N2 → N1 (loss), N12 (terminal, opponent wins). So N6 leads to a terminal node or opponent wins.  \n- From N5:  \n  - N5 → N13 → N0 → N7 or N9 (terminal)  \n  - N5 → N9 → terminal. Both end in opponent’s loss, i.e., we win.  \n\nMoving to N5 leads to a winning position (opponent eventually loses).  \nThus, choosing N5 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N5</answer>", "episode_score": -0.95, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N11, the player can move to N6 or N5.  \n- From N6: paths are to N4 or N2.  \n  - N4 → N1 (terminal) or N3 → N8 (terminal). Both lead to loss on next move if opponent plays optimally.  \n  - N2 → N1 (loss), N12 (terminal, opponent wins). So N6 leads to a terminal node or opponent wins.  \n- From N5:  \n  - N5 → N13 → N0 → N7 or N9 (terminal)  \n  - N5 → N9 → terminal. Both end in opponent’s loss, i.e., we win.  \n\nMoving to N5 leads to a winning position (opponent eventually loses).  \nThus, choosing N5 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N5</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N5\n\nDirected graph:\nN6 -> N4, N2\nN2 -> N1, N12\nN0 -> N7, N10\nN3 -> N8, N7\nN7 -> terminal\nN13 -> N9, N0\nN4 -> N1, N3\nN5 -> N13, N9\nN11 -> N6, N5\nN8 -> terminal\nN12 -> terminal\nN9 -> terminal\nN10 -> terminal\nN1 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N13, N9assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N5, the legal moves are to N13 or N9. N9 leads directly to terminal (win for the player who moves there, as the opponent has no move). N13 leads to N0 or N9. N9 is terminal, so moving to N13 and then to N9 ends the game immediately. N0 leads to N7 or N10; N7 and N10 are terminal. Thus, moving to N13 allows the opponent to move to N9 or N0, both of which lead to terminal. However, if we move to N9 directly, the game ends immediately and we win. Therefore, choosing N9 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N9</answer>", "episode_score": 1.05, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N5, the legal moves are to N13 or N9. N9 leads directly to terminal (win for the player who moves there, as the opponent has no move). N13 leads to N0 or N9. N9 is terminal, so moving to N13 and then to N9 ends the game immediately. N0 leads to N7 or N10; N7 and N10 are terminal. Thus, moving to N13 allows the opponent to move to N9 or N0, both of which lead to terminal. However, if we move to N9 directly, the game ends immediately and we win. Therefore, choosing N9 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N9</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N6\n\nDirected graph:\nN0 -> N8, N4\nN6 -> N1, N9\nN2 -> terminal\nN4 -> terminal\nN7 -> terminal\nN5 -> terminal\nN1 -> N4, N7\nN9 -> N3, N1\nN8 -> N5\nN3 -> N0, N2\n\nLegal moves: N1, N9assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N6, the legal moves are to N1 or N9. We analyze each option:  \n- Move to N1: From N1, the options are N4 and N7. Both N4 and N7 lead to terminal nodes, so the player who moves to N1 wins immediately (since the opponent has no moves). Thus, from N1, the opponent is forced to move to a terminal node and loses.  \n- Move to N9: From N9, the options are N3 and N1.  \n  - N3 leads to N0 or N2. N2 is terminal; N0 leads to N8 → N5 (terminal). So any move from N3 eventually leads to terminal nodes.  \n  - N1 again leads to terminal nodes. So all moves from N9 lead to the opponent winning.  \n\nThus, moving to N1 gives the current player a winning position. Moving to N9 gives the opponent a winning position. Therefore, the optimal move is to N1.</reason>  \n<answer>N1</answer>", "episode_score": -0.95, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N6, the legal moves are to N1 or N9. We analyze each option:  \n- Move to N1: From N1, the options are N4 and N7. Both N4 and N7 lead to terminal nodes, so the player who moves to N1 wins immediately (since the opponent has no moves). Thus, from N1, the opponent is forced to move to a terminal node and loses.  \n- Move to N9: From N9, the options are N3 and N1.  \n  - N3 leads to N0 or N2. N2 is terminal; N0 leads to N8 → N5 (terminal). So any move from N3 eventually leads to terminal nodes.  \n  - N1 again leads to terminal nodes. So all moves from N9 lead to the opponent winning.  \n\nThus, moving to N1 gives the current player a winning position. Moving to N9 gives the opponent a winning position. Therefore, the optimal move is to N1.</reason>  \n<answer>N1</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N1\n\nDirected graph:\nN0 -> N8, N4\nN6 -> N1, N9\nN2 -> terminal\nN4 -> terminal\nN7 -> terminal\nN5 -> terminal\nN1 -> N4, N7\nN9 -> N3, N1\nN8 -> N5\nN3 -> N0, N2\n\nLegal moves: N4, N7assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N1, the legal moves are to N4 or N7. N4 and N7 both lead to terminal nodes, so the player who moves to either wins immediately (the opponent has no move). Thus, moving to N4 or N7 ends the game immediately, and the current player wins. Since both are winning moves, either is optimal. However, N1 also has a move to N7, which leads to terminal, and N1 → N4 also leads to terminal. Both are equivalent. Choosing either is optimal. But since the question asks to choose one legal destination that maximizes outcome, and both lead to immediate win, either is valid. We pick one. N4 is chosen.</reason>\n<answer>N4</answer>", "episode_score": 0.05, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N1, the legal moves are to N4 or N7. N4 and N7 both lead to terminal nodes, so the player who moves to either wins immediately (the opponent has no move). Thus, moving to N4 or N7 ends the game immediately, and the current player wins. Since both are winning moves, either is optimal. However, N1 also has a move to N7, which leads to terminal, and N1 → N4 also leads to terminal. Both are equivalent. Choosing either is optimal. But since the question asks to choose one legal destination that maximizes outcome, and both lead to immediate win, either is valid. We pick one. N4 is chosen.</reason>\n<answer>N4</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N4\n\nDirected graph:\nN3 -> terminal\nN9 -> N3\nN8 -> terminal\nN1 -> terminal\nN7 -> N8\nN2 -> terminal\nN6 -> N0, N7\nN4 -> N0, N6, N7\nN5 -> N2, N9\nN0 -> N1, N5\n\nLegal moves: N0, N6, N7assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N4, the legal moves are to N0, N6, or N7. We analyze each option using backward induction since the game ends when a player cannot move to a terminal node.  \n- N0: leads to N0→N1 (terminal) or N0→N5. N1 is terminal so player loses if they reach it. N5→N2 or N9: N2 is terminal; N9→N3→terminal. So from N0, the opponent can move to terminal. Therefore, the player who moves to N0 lets opponent win if they can get to terminal.  \n- N6: N6→N0 or N7. N7→N8→terminal, so N7 leads to opponent losing. If N6→N7, opponent moves to N8 → terminal. So N6→N7 is losing for current. N6→N0 is same as earlier; leads to opponent winning. So N6 ends with opponent winning.  \n- N7: N7→N8 → terminal. That's opponent loses, so we win.  \n\nThus, moving to N7 forces opponent to face N8 → terminal, meaning the opponent has no legal move and loses. Hence, N7 is optimal.  \n</reason>\n<answer>N7</answer>", "episode_score": -0.6166666666666667, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N4, the legal moves are to N0, N6, or N7. We analyze each option using backward induction since the game ends when a player cannot move to a terminal node.  \n- N0: leads to N0→N1 (terminal) or N0→N5. N1 is terminal so player loses if they reach it. N5→N2 or N9: N2 is terminal; N9→N3→terminal. So from N0, the opponent can move to terminal. Therefore, the player who moves to N0 lets opponent win if they can get to terminal.  \n- N6: N6→N0 or N7. N7→N8→terminal, so N7 leads to opponent losing. If N6→N7, opponent moves to N8 → terminal. So N6→N7 is losing for current. N6→N0 is same as earlier; leads to opponent winning. So N6 ends with opponent winning.  \n- N7: N7→N8 → terminal. That's opponent loses, so we win.  \n\nThus, moving to N7 forces opponent to face N8 → terminal, meaning the opponent has no legal move and loses. Hence, N7 is optimal.  \n</reason>\n<answer>N7</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N7\n\nDirected graph:\nN4 -> terminal\nN5 -> N8\nN7 -> N5, N0, N1\nN9 -> terminal\nN1 -> N5, N8\nN0 -> N6\nN6 -> terminal\nN8 -> N2, N3, N4\nN2 -> N9\nN3 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N5, N0, N1assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N7, the legal moves are to N5, N0, or N1. We analyze each option:\n\n- N7 → N5: N5 → N8 → then N8 can go to N2 or N3 or N4. N2 → N9 (terminal), N3 → terminal. N4 → terminal. So from N5, the opponent will eventually win, as all paths from N8 lead to a terminal node (i.e., the opponent wins after our move, meaning we lose).\n\n- N7 → N0: N0 → N6 → terminal. So we force a win: opponent cannot move, they lose.\n\n- N7 → N1: N1 → N5 or N8. As above, from N5 and N8, paths lead to terminal states. Thus, opponent will eventually win. So we lose.\n\nThus, choosing N0 leads to an immediate win. N0 → N6 → terminal, opponent has no move. This is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N0</answer>", "episode_score": -0.6166666666666667, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N7, the legal moves are to N5, N0, or N1. We analyze each option:\n\n- N7 → N5: N5 → N8 → then N8 can go to N2 or N3 or N4. N2 → N9 (terminal), N3 → terminal. N4 → terminal. So from N5, the opponent will eventually win, as all paths from N8 lead to a terminal node (i.e., the opponent wins after our move, meaning we lose).\n\n- N7 → N0: N0 → N6 → terminal. So we force a win: opponent cannot move, they lose.\n\n- N7 → N1: N1 → N5 or N8. As above, from N5 and N8, paths lead to terminal states. Thus, opponent will eventually win. So we lose.\n\nThus, choosing N0 leads to an immediate win. N0 → N6 → terminal, opponent has no move. This is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N0</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N0\n\nDirected graph:\nN4 -> terminal\nN5 -> N8\nN7 -> N5, N0, N1\nN9 -> terminal\nN1 -> N5, N8\nN0 -> N6\nN6 -> terminal\nN8 -> N2, N3, N4\nN2 -> N9\nN3 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N6assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N0, the only legal move is to N6. N6 leads to terminal, so the player who moves to N6 wins immediately (the opponent has no move). Thus, moving to N6 is optimal and guarantees a win. No other move from N0 is available, so N6 is the only choice. Therefore, the optimal move is to N6.</reason>\n<answer>N6</answer>", "episode_score": 0.05, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N0, the only legal move is to N6. N6 leads to terminal, so the player who moves to N6 wins immediately (the opponent has no move). Thus, moving to N6 is optimal and guarantees a win. No other move from N0 is available, so N6 is the only choice. Therefore, the optimal move is to N6.</reason>\n<answer>N6</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N7\n\nDirected graph:\nN3 -> terminal\nN9 -> N3\nN8 -> terminal\nN1 -> terminal\nN7 -> N8\nN2 -> terminal\nN6 -> N0, N7\nN4 -> N0, N6, N7\nN5 -> N2, N9\nN0 -> N1, N5\n\nLegal moves: N8assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N7, the only legal move is to N8. N8 leads to terminal, so the player who moves to N8 wins immediately (the opponent has no move). Thus, moving to N8 results in an immediate win. Other moves from N7 are not available. Therefore, the optimal move is to N8.</reason>\n<answer>N8</answer>", "episode_score": 0.05, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N7, the only legal move is to N8. N8 leads to terminal, so the player who moves to N8 wins immediately (the opponent has no move). Thus, moving to N8 results in an immediate win. Other moves from N7 are not available. Therefore, the optimal move is to N8.</reason>\n<answer>N8</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process