minal.\n    - N8 → N5 → terminal.\n  So all paths from N2 lead to terminal. So opponent loses. Good.\n- Moving to N8: N8 → N5 → terminal. Opponent loses immediately. Good.\n\nAll moves lead to terminal for the opponent. However, since the goal is to maximize final game outcome, and all options win, we pick one that maintains strategic balance. N4 and N7 offer more branching, but all lead to terminal. Any move wins. Choosing N7 is safe and leads to N2, which has multiple options but all terminal. Thus, N7 is a solid choice. However, N4 directly leads to N6 (terminal), a simple win. Since all moves win, the choice is arbitrary. Picking the first optimal one: N4.</reason>\n<answer>N4</answer>", "episode_score": -0.6166666666666667, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N9, the player can move to N7, N4, or N8. We analyze each option to determine which maximizes the chance of winning (i.e., forces the opponent into a losing position). \n\n- Moving to N4: N4 → N6 (terminal). Opponent loses immediately. Good.\n- Moving to N7: N7 → N2. Then:\n  - N2 → N0 (terminal) or N3 or N8.\n    - N0 → terminal → opponent loses.\n    - N3 → N1 or N4: both lead to terminal.\n    - N8 → N5 → terminal.\n  So all paths from N2 lead to terminal. So opponent loses. Good.\n- Moving to N8: N8 → N5 → terminal. Opponent loses immediately. Good.\n\nAll moves lead to terminal for the opponent. However, since the goal is to maximize final game outcome, and all options win, we pick one that maintains strategic balance. N4 and N7 offer more branching, but all lead to terminal. Any move wins. Choosing N7 is safe and leads to N2, which has multiple options but all terminal. Thus, N7 is a solid choice. However, N4 directly leads to N6 (terminal), a simple win. Since all moves win, the choice is arbitrary. Picking the first optimal one: N4.</reason>\n<answer>N4</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N4\n\nDirected graph:\nN4 -> N6\nN2 -> N0, N3, N8\nN7 -> N2\nN0 -> terminal\nN9 -> N7, N4, N8\nN1 -> terminal\nN6 -> terminal\nN3 -> N1, N4\nN8 -> N5\nN5 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N6assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N4, the only legal move is to N6. N6 leads to terminal, so the player who moves to N6 wins immediately (the opponent has no move). Thus, moving to N6 results in an immediate win. No other move from N4 is available. Therefore, the optimal move is to N6.</reason>\n<answer>N6</answer>", "episode_score": 0.05, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N4, the only legal move is to N6. N6 leads to terminal, so the player who moves to N6 wins immediately (the opponent has no move). Thus, moving to N6 results in an immediate win. No other move from N4 is available. Therefore, the optimal move is to N6.</reason>\n<answer>N6</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N9\n\nDirected graph:\nN7 -> N5\nN6 -> terminal\nN1 -> N8\nN4 -> N3, N5\nN8 -> N0\nN5 -> terminal\nN9 -> N7, N4, N1\nN2 -> terminal\nN3 -> N7, N2, N6\nN0 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N7, N4, N1assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N9, the player can move to N7, N4, or N1. We analyze each option:  \n- N7 → N5 → terminal (opponent wins immediately).  \n- N4 → N3 or N5:  \n  - N5 → terminal (opponent wins).  \n  - N3 → N7, N2, N6:  \n    - N7 → N5 → terminal (opponent wins).  \n    - N2 or N6 → terminal (opponent wins). So all paths from N3 lead to opponent win.  \n- N1 → N8 → N0 → terminal (opponent wins).  \n\nAll moves lead to the opponent winning. However, since the player who makes no move loses, we must ensure the opponent is in a losing position. But all paths from any of N7, N4, N1 eventually lead to terminal nodes on the next move. Thus, every move allows the opponent to win. Therefore, no move is winning. But to maximize outcome, we choose the move that delays the loss longest or minimizes opponent advantage. N4 offers a branch to N3, which can go to N7 or N6; N6 leads to terminal. But all paths eventually end. Since all options lead to opponent win, the optimal choice is the one with most flexibility. N4 provides more options. Thus, N4 is chosen.</reason>  \n<answer>N4</answer>", "episode_score": -0.6166666666666667, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N9, the player can move to N7, N4, or N1. We analyze each option:  \n- N7 → N5 → terminal (opponent wins immediately).  \n- N4 → N3 or N5:  \n  - N5 → terminal (opponent wins).  \n  - N3 → N7, N2, N6:  \n    - N7 → N5 → terminal (opponent wins).  \n    - N2 or N6 → terminal (opponent wins). So all paths from N3 lead to opponent win.  \n- N1 → N8 → N0 → terminal (opponent wins).  \n\nAll moves lead to the opponent winning. However, since the player who makes no move loses, we must ensure the opponent is in a losing position. But all paths from any of N7, N4, N1 eventually lead to terminal nodes on the next move. Thus, every move allows the opponent to win. Therefore, no move is winning. But to maximize outcome, we choose the move that delays the loss longest or minimizes opponent advantage. N4 offers a branch to N3, which can go to N7 or N6; N6 leads to terminal. But all paths eventually end. Since all options lead to opponent win, the optimal choice is the one with most flexibility. N4 provides more options. Thus, N4 is chosen.</reason>  \n<answer>N4</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N4\n\nDirected graph:\nN7 -> N5\nN6 -> terminal\nN1 -> N8\nN4 -> N3, N5\nN8 -> N0\nN5 -> terminal\nN9 -> N7, N4, N1\nN2 -> terminal\nN3 -> N7, N2, N6\nN0 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N3, N5assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N4, the legal moves are to N3 or N5. N5 leads directly to terminal (win for the player who moves there, as the opponent has no move). Thus, moving to N5 wins immediately. Moving to N3 leads to N7, N2, or N6. N2 and N6 lead to terminal, so those are winning moves for the opponent. N7 leads to N5, which is a winning move for the opponent. Thus, N3 is losing. The optimal move is to N5, which ends the game immediately and wins.</reason>\n<answer>N5</answer>", "episode_score": 1.05, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N4, the legal moves are to N3 or N5. N5 leads directly to terminal (win for the player who moves there, as the opponent has no move). Thus, moving to N5 wins immediately. Moving to N3 leads to N7, N2, or N6. N2 and N6 lead to terminal, so those are winning moves for the opponent. N7 leads to N5, which is a winning move for the opponent. Thus, N3 is losing. The optimal move is to N5, which ends the game immediately and wins.</reason>\n<answer>N5</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A playe