er>N11</answer>", "episode_score": -0.6166666666666667, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N1, the legal moves are to N10, N11, or N7. We analyze each:  \n- N10 → N4 → terminal (lose if opponent moves after). But N4 is terminal, so the player who moves to N4 wins (since the next player can't move). So from N10, next player loses immediately. So moving to N10 means the opponent faces N4 → immediate loss for them. So we win.  \n- N11 → N13 → terminal (same as above). So N11 leads to terminal, opponent has no move, we win.  \n- N7 → N12 or N10.  \n   - N12 → N5 → terminal, or N8 → N3 → N2 → N6 → terminal or N8 → N11 → N13 → terminal. So all paths from N12 or N8 eventually terminate, giving opponent no move. Thus N7 leads to a winning position.  \n\nAll moves from N1 lead to opponent facing a terminal or winning position. But the key is: once you place them on an N-leaf move (N-0,N13,N-5,N4,N9), the opponent faces \"dead end\", they lose. So each path after one transfer leads to a situation of reduced freedom. All three options lead opponent into a terminal or end-cycle. Thus, all are winning. But we must pick one that maximizes outcome. Since all are safe, choosing any is equal. But N11 leads to N13 → terminal; N10 → N4 → terminal; N7 → N10 → N4 or N12 → N5 → N loses game tree. Since N11, N move directly to terminal edge, safe. Choose N11 as it avoids long chains and ensures immediate terminal. So optimal is N11.</reason>\n<answer>N11</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N11\n\nDirected graph:\nN2 -> N6\nN6 -> terminal\nN1