[2026-08-13 16:16:55] [agentic_pipeline.py (516)] [INFO] [DRIVER 0 / 1][PID 1538566] [{"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N6\n\nDirected graph:\nN7 -> N0\nN9 -> N3\nN2 -> terminal\nN5 -> N1, N4\nN1 -> terminal\nN8 -> terminal\nN6 -> N7, N5, N9\nN0 -> N9, N2, N5\nN4 -> N8\nN3 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N7, N5, N9assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N6, the legal moves are to N7, N5, or N9. We analyze each:  \n- N7 → N0: N0 leads to N9 (win), N2 (loss), or N5. N5 → N1 (win) or N4 → N8 (win). So N0 leads to a win for the player.  \n- N5 → N1 or N4: N1 → terminal (win), N4 → N8 (terminal, win). Both are winning.  \n- N9 → N3: N3 → terminal (loss). So N9 leads to a loss.  \n\nThus, N9 is bad. N7 and N5 both lead to winning positions. Between them, N5 offers more options (N1 and N4), both terminal wins. N7 leads to N0, which has paths to N5 (win) or N2 (loss). But since the opponent plays optimally, we must choose a move that guarantees a win. Both N7 and N5 lead to winning positions. However, N5 directly leads to N1 or N4, both terminal wins, so it is safer and more direct. Thus, N5 is optimal.</reason>  \n<answer>N5</answer>", "episode_score": -0.6166666666666667, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N6, the legal moves are to N7, N5, or N9. We analyze each:  \n- N7 → N0: N0 leads to N9 (win), N2 (loss), or N5. N5 → N1 (win) or N4 → N8 (win). So N0 leads to a win for the player.  \n- N5 → N1 or N4: N1 → terminal (win), N4 → N8 (terminal, win). Both are winning.  \n- N9 → N3: N3 → terminal (loss). So N9 leads to a loss.  \n\nThus, N9 is bad. N7 and N5 both lead to winning positions. Between them, N5 offers more options (N1 and N4), both terminal wins. N7 leads to N0, which has paths to N5 (win) or N2 (loss). But since the opponent plays optimally, we must choose a move that guarantees a win. Both N7 and N5 lead to winning positions. However, N5 directly leads to N1 or N4, both terminal wins, so it is safer and more direct. Thus, N5 is optimal.</reason>  \n<answer>N5</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N5\n\nDirected graph:\nN7 -> N0\nN9 -> N3\nN2 -> terminal\nN5 -> N1, N4\nN1 -> terminal\nN8 -> terminal\nN6 -> N7, N5, N9\nN0 -> N9, N2, N5\nN4 -> N8\nN3 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N1, N4assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N5, the legal moves are to N1 or N4. N1 leads directly to terminal (win for the player who moves there, as the opponent has no move). N4 leads to N8, which is terminal. Thus, moving to N1 wins immediately. Moving to N4 results in a terminal position for the opponent, also winning. Both moves win, but choosing N1 is simpler and direct. However, since both are winning moves, the optimal choice is any legal winning move. N1 is a terminal move, so it is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N1</answer>", "episode_score": 1.05, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N5, the legal moves are to N1 or N4. N1 leads directly to terminal (win for the player who moves there, as the opponent has no move). N4 leads to N8, which is terminal. Thus, moving to N1 wins immediately. Moving to N4 results in a terminal position for the opponent, also winning. Both moves win, but choosing N1 is simpler and direct. However, since both are winning moves, the optimal choice is any legal winning move. N1 is a terminal move, so it is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N1</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N7\n\nDirected graph:\nN6 -> N1, N2\nN3 -> terminal\nN9 -> N3\nN4 -> N0, N9\nN0 -> terminal\nN1 -> terminal\nN7 -> N5, N4\nN2 -> N0, N8\nN5 -> N6, N2\nN8 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N5, N4assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N7, the player can move to N5 or N4.  \n\n- Moving to N5:  \n  N5 → N6 or N2  \n  - N6 → N1 (terminal) or N2 → N0 (terminal)  \n    → All paths lead to terminal states. If opponent starts at N1, N2, N0 → their turn ends. So paths from N5 eventually lose if you can't continue.  \n  - So N5 leads to N2 → N0 or N6 → N1 → terminal. Both are losing.  \n\n- Moving to N4:  \n  N4 → N0 or N9  \n  N0 → terminal (loss for the opponent)  \n  N9 → N3 → terminal (loss for the opponent)  \n  So both branches lead to terminal (opponent loses immediately).  \n\nThus, moving to N4 leads to opponent having no move. So N4 is winning. N5 leads to opponent having winning paths. Therefore, N4 is better.  \n\nMaximize outcome: choose N4.</reason>  \n<answer>N4</answer>", "episode_score": -0.95, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N7, the player can move to N5 or N4.  \n\n- Moving to N5:  \n  N5 → N6 or N2  \n  - N6 → N1 (terminal) or N2 → N0 (terminal)  \n    → All paths lead to terminal states. If opponent starts at N1, N2, N0 → their turn ends. So paths from N5 eventually lose if you can't continue.  \n  - So N5 leads to N2 → N0 or N6 → N1 → terminal. Both are losing.  \n\n- Moving to N4:  \n  N4 → N0 or N9  \n  N0 → terminal (loss for the opponent)  \n  N9 → N3 → terminal (loss for the opponent)  \n  So both branches lead to terminal (opponent loses immediately).  \n\nThus, moving to N4 leads to opponent having no move. So N4 is winning. N5 leads to opponent having winning paths. Therefore, N4 is better.  \n\nMaximize outcome: choose N4.</reason>  \n<answer>N4</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N4\n\nDirected graph:\nN6 -> N1, N2\nN3 -> terminal\nN9 -> N3\nN4 -> N0, N9\nN0 -> terminal\nN1 -> terminal\nN7 -> N5, N4\nN2 -> N0, N8\nN5 -> N6, N2\nN8 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N0, N9assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N4, the legal moves are to N0 or N9. N0 leads directly to terminal (win for the player who moves there, as the opponent has no move). N9 leads to N3, which leads to terminal. Thus, moving to N9 results in the opponent winning. Therefore, the optimal move is to N0, which ends the game immediately and wins the game.</reason>\n<answer>N0</answer>", "episode_score": 1.05, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N4, the legal moves are to N0 or N9. N0 leads directly to terminal (win for the player who moves there, as the opponent has no move). N9 leads to N3, which leads to terminal. Thus, moving to N9 results in the opponent winning. Therefore, the optimal move is to N0, which ends the game immediately and wins the game.</reason>\n<answer>N0</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N7\n\nDirected graph:\nN2 -> N4, N9\nN0 -> N2, N1\nN4 -> N9, N3\nN5 -> N6\nN3 -> terminal\nN8 -> N0\nN9 -> terminal\nN6 -> terminal\nN1 -> terminal\nN7 -> N8, N0, N5\n\nLegal moves: N8, N0, N5assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N7, the player can move to N8, N0, or N5. We analyze each option:  \n- N8 → N0: From N0, can move to N2 or N1. N1 is terminal, so if opponent gets to N2, they move to N4 or N9, both terminal. So N0 leads to N1 or N2; N2 → N4 → N9 (terminal) or N2 → N9. Thus, N0 leads to terminal nodes.  \n- N0 → N1, or N2 → N3/N4/N9 (all terminal) → opponent wins.  \n- N5 → N6 → terminal. Immediate loss.  \n- N8 → N0 → N2 → N4 → N3 (terminal) or N2 → N9. All lead to terminal.  \nThus all paths eventually lead to opponent winning. But the aim is to maximize final outcome. Since all moves lead opponent to terminal, the player who moves into a terminal is forced to lose. So, we must avoid giving opponent a move to terminal. But every edge from any visited leads to terminal. Therefore, **all positions from N7 lead to opponent win. But to maximize outcome, we must choose a move which forces the game to end immediately or minimizes opponent advantage. N5 leads to N6 terminal immediately (opponent has no move), so player moves and opponent loses. Thus N5 is best. Final move: N7 → N5.</reason>  \n<answer>N5</answer>", "episode_score": -0.6166666666666667, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N7, the player can move to N8, N0, or N5. We analyze each option:  \n- N8 → N0: From N0, can move to N2 or N1. N1 is terminal, so if opponent gets to N2, they move to N4 or N9, both terminal. So N0 leads to N1 or N2; N2 → N4 → N9 (terminal) or N2 → N9. Thus, N0 leads to terminal nodes.  \n- N0 → N1, or N2 → N3/N4/N9 (all terminal) → opponent wins.  \n- N5 → N6 → terminal. Immediate loss.  \n- N8 → N0 → N2 → N4 → N3 (terminal) or N2 → N9. All lead to terminal.  \nThus all paths eventually lead to opponent winning. But the aim is to maximize final outcome. Since all moves lead opponent to terminal, the player who moves into a terminal is forced to lose. So, we must avoid giving opponent a move to terminal. But every edge from any visited leads to terminal. Therefore, **all positions from N7 lead to opponent win. But to maximize outcome, we must choose a move which forces the game to end immediately or minimizes opponent advantage. N5 leads to N6 terminal immediately (opponent has no move), so player moves and opponent loses. Thus N5 is best. Final move: N7 → N5.</reason>  \n<answer>N5</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N5\n\nDirected graph:\nN2 -> N4, N9\nN0 -> N2, N1\nN4 -> N9, N3\nN5 -> N6\nN3 -> terminal\nN8 -> N0\nN9 -> terminal\nN6 -> terminal\nN1 -> terminal\nN7 -> N8, N0, N5\n\nLegal moves: N6assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N5, the only legal move is to N6. N6 is a terminal node, so the player who moves to N6 wins immediately, as the opponent has no legal move. Thus, moving to N6 is optimal and guarantees a win.</reason>\n<answer>N6</answer>", "episode_score": 0.05, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N5, the only legal move is to N6. N6 is a terminal node, so the player who moves to N6 wins immediately, as the opponent has no legal move. Thus, moving to N6 is optimal and guarantees a win.</reason>\n<answer>N6</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N9\n\nDirected graph:\nN5 -> N0\nN2 -> N1, N6\nN9 -> N2, N1, N7\nN4 -> terminal\nN7 -> N8\nN6 -> N5, N4\nN0 -> terminal\nN8 -> terminal\nN3 -> terminal\nN1 -> N3, N7\n\nLegal moves: N2, N1, N7assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N9, the legal moves are to N2, N1, or N7. We analyze each:\n\n- Move to N2: From N2, moves are to N1 or N6.\n  - N1 leads to N3 (terminal) or N7.\n    - N7 → N8 (terminal) → opponent wins.\n    - N1 → N7 → N8 → opponent wins.\n  - N6 → N5 → N0 (terminal) → opponent wins. All paths from N2 lead to opponent winning.\n\n- Move to N1: N1 → N3 (terminal) or N7.\n  - N3 → terminal: current player wins immediately.\n  - N7 → N8 → terminal: opponent wins.\n  So, choosing N1 allows a move to N3, which ends the game immediately (opponent has no move). Thus, this is a winning move.\n\n- Move to N7: N7 → N8 (terminal) → opponent wins.\n\nBest choice: N1, as it leads to an immediate terminal node (N3) from which the opponent has no move. This wins the game.</reason>\n<answer>N1</answer>", "episode_score": -0.6166666666666667, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N9, the legal moves are to N2, N1, or N7. We analyze each:\n\n- Move to N2: From N2, moves are to N1 or N6.\n  - N1 leads to N3 (terminal) or N7.\n    - N7 → N8 (terminal) → opponent wins.\n    - N1 → N7 → N8 → opponent wins.\n  - N6 → N5 → N0 (terminal) → opponent wins. All paths from N2 lead to opponent winning.\n\n- Move to N1: N1 → N3 (terminal) or N7.\n  - N3 → terminal: current player wins immediately.\n  - N7 → N8 → terminal: opponent wins.\n  So, choosing N1 allows a move to N3, which ends the game immediately (opponent has no move). Thus, this is a winning move.\n\n- Move to N7: N7 → N8 (terminal) → opponent wins.\n\nBest choice: N1, as it leads to an immediate terminal node (N3) from which the opponent has no move. This wins the game.</reason>\n<answer>N1</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N1\n\nDirected graph:\nN5 -> N0\nN2 -> N1, N6\nN9 -> N2, N1, N7\nN4 -> terminal\nN7 -> N8\nN6 -> N5, N4\nN0 -> terminal\nN8 -> terminal\nN3 -> terminal\nN1 -> N3, N7\n\nLegal moves: N3, N7assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N1, the legal moves are to N3 or N7. N3 is a terminal node, so the player who moves to N3 wins immediately (the opponent has no move). N7 leads to N8, which is terminal, so the opponent wins. Thus, moving to N3 is optimal and guarantees an immediate win. Therefore, the best move is to N3.</reason>\n<answer>N3</answer>", "episode_score": 1.05, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N1, the legal moves are to N3 or N7. N3 is a terminal node, so the player who moves to N3 wins immediately (the opponent has no move). N7 leads to N8, which is terminal, so the opponent wins. Thus, moving to N3 is optimal and guarantees an immediate win. Therefore, the best move is to N3.</reason>\n<answer>N3</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N4\n\nDirected graph:\nN8 -> N11, N10\nN3 -> N1, N2\nN13 -> N9, N8\nN10 -> terminal\nN12 -> terminal\nN11 -> terminal\nN9 -> terminal\nN7 -> N11, N12\nN1 -> N7, N5\nN5 -> terminal\nN0 -> N10, N2\nN6 -> N0, N3\nN4 -> N6, N13\nN2 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N6, N13assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N4, the legal moves are to N6 or N13. We evaluate both:\n\n- Move to N6: Then to N0 or N3. \n  - N0 → N10 (terminal or N2). N2 leads to terminal; N10 leads to terminal. So N0 leads to terminal.\n  - N3 → N1 or N2. \n    - N1 → N7, N5 (N5 terminal, N7 → N11 or N12 both terminal)\n    - So from N3 → N1 → N7 → N11 or N12: terminal.\n    - So all paths from N6 lead to terminal.\n  - All paths from N6 end in terminal → opponent wins.\n\n- Move to N13:\n  - N13 → N9 (terminal) or N8.\n  From N13 leads N options immediately:\n     - N13 → N9: terminal\n     So N13 → N9 wins (opponent has no move)\n\nNow, opponent will win if I play into N6 due to full terminal paths. But N13 → N9 → terminal immediately. So my play at move forcing end of opponent play.\n\nThus, N13 leads to terminal on next move, so opponent has no legal move. I win.\n\nSo best move is N13.</reason>\n<answer>N13</answer>", "episode_score": -0.95, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N4, the legal moves are to N6 or N13. We evaluate both:\n\n- Move to N6: Then to N0 or N3. \n  - N0 → N10 (terminal or N2). N2 leads to terminal; N10 leads to terminal. So N0 leads to terminal.\n  - N3 → N1 or N2. \n    - N1 → N7, N5 (N5 terminal, N7 → N11 or N12 both terminal)\n    - So from N3 → N1 → N7 → N11 or N12: terminal.\n    - So all paths from N6 lead to terminal.\n  - All paths from N6 end in terminal → opponent wins.\n\n- Move to N13:\n  - N13 → N9 (terminal) or N8.\n  From N13 leads N options immediately:\n     - N13 → N9: terminal\n     So N13 → N9 wins (opponent has no move)\n\nNow, opponent will win if I play into N6 due to full terminal paths. But N13 → N9 → terminal immediately. So my play at move forcing end of opponent play.\n\nThus, N13 leads to terminal on next move, so opponent has no legal move. I win.\n\nSo best move is N13.</reason>\n<answer>N13</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N13\n\nDirected graph:\nN8 -> N11, N10\nN3 -> N1, N2\nN13 -> N9, N8\nN10 -> terminal\nN12 -> terminal\nN11 -> terminal\nN9 -> terminal\nN7 -> N11, N12\nN1 -> N7, N5\nN5 -> terminal\nN0 -> N10, N2\nN6 -> N0, N3\nN4 -> N6, N13\nN2 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N9, N8assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N13, the legal moves are to N9 or N8. N9 leads directly to terminal (win for the player who moves there, as the opponent has no move). N8 leads to N11 or N10; both N11 and N10 lead to terminal. Thus, moving to N9 wins immediately. Moving to N8 allows the opponent to move to N10 or N11, both terminal, so the opponent wins. Therefore, choosing N9 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N9</answer>", "episode_score": 1.05, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N13, the legal moves are to N9 or N8. N9 leads directly to terminal (win for the player who moves there, as the opponent has no move). N8 leads to N11 or N10; both N11 and N10 lead to terminal. Thus, moving to N9 wins immediately. Moving to N8 allows the opponent to move to N10 or N11, both terminal, so the opponent wins. Therefore, choosing N9 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N9</answer>"}]
