[2026-08-13 16:19:07] [agentic_pipeline.py (516)] [INFO] [DRIVER 0 / 1][PID 1538566] [{"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N6\n\nDirected graph:\nN4 -> terminal\nN2 -> terminal\nN9 -> N0\nN6 -> N1, N7\nN0 -> terminal\nN3 -> terminal\nN5 -> N7, N2\nN8 -> N2, N9\nN7 -> N4, N3\nN1 -> N5, N8\n\nLegal moves: N1, N7assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N6, the player can move to N1 or N7. We analyze each option:  \n- If move to N1: N1 → N5 or N8.  \n  - N5 → N7 or N2; N7 → N4 (terminal) or N3 (terminal). N7 is a terminal move (to N4 or N3). N2 → terminal. So N1 leads to terminal states.  \n  - N8 → N2 or N9; N2 → terminal, N9 → N0 → terminal. So all paths from N1 lead to terminal.  \n  Thus, N1 leads to opponent winning (current player loses).  \n- If move to N7: N7 → N4 or N3, both terminal. So opponent has no move, current player wins.  \nThus, choosing N7 leads to an immediate win. N1 leads to loss. Maximize outcome by choosing N7.</reason>  \n<answer>N7</answer>", "episode_score": -0.95, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N6, the player can move to N1 or N7. We analyze each option:  \n- If move to N1: N1 → N5 or N8.  \n  - N5 → N7 or N2; N7 → N4 (terminal) or N3 (terminal). N7 is a terminal move (to N4 or N3). N2 → terminal. So N1 leads to terminal states.  \n  - N8 → N2 or N9; N2 → terminal, N9 → N0 → terminal. So all paths from N1 lead to terminal.  \n  Thus, N1 leads to opponent winning (current player loses).  \n- If move to N7: N7 → N4 or N3, both terminal. So opponent has no move, current player wins.  \nThus, choosing N7 leads to an immediate win. N1 leads to loss. Maximize outcome by choosing N7.</reason>  \n<answer>N7</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N7\n\nDirected graph:\nN4 -> terminal\nN2 -> terminal\nN9 -> N0\nN6 -> N1, N7\nN0 -> terminal\nN3 -> terminal\nN5 -> N7, N2\nN8 -> N2, N9\nN7 -> N4, N3\nN1 -> N5, N8\n\nLegal moves: N4, N3assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N7, the legal moves are to N4 or N3. Both N4 and N3 lead to terminal nodes, so the player who moves to either wins immediately (the opponent has no move). Thus, both moves end the game immediately. Since the player who makes the move wins, choosing either N4 or N3 is optimal. However, N3 leads directly to terminal, and N4 also leads to terminal. Both are equivalent in outcome. But since N7 -> N3 is a direct move to terminal, and N7 -> N4 is also direct, either is valid. We choose one. N3 is simpler and direct. Therefore, the optimal move is to N3.</reason>\n<answer>N3</answer>", "episode_score": 0.05, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N7, the legal moves are to N4 or N3. Both N4 and N3 lead to terminal nodes, so the player who moves to either wins immediately (the opponent has no move). Thus, both moves end the game immediately. Since the player who makes the move wins, choosing either N4 or N3 is optimal. However, N3 leads directly to terminal, and N4 also leads to terminal. Both are equivalent in outcome. But since N7 -> N3 is a direct move to terminal, and N7 -> N4 is also direct, either is valid. We choose one. N3 is simpler and direct. Therefore, the optimal move is to N3.</reason>\n<answer>N3</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N4\n\nDirected graph:\nN7 -> terminal\nN0 -> terminal\nN3 -> N9, N0, N8\nN2 -> N1\nN8 -> N9\nN6 -> N3, N7\nN9 -> terminal\nN1 -> N5\nN4 -> N2, N6, N1\nN5 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N2, N6, N1assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N4, the player can move to N2, N6, or N1. We analyze each option to determine which leads to a winning position (i.e., forces the opponent into a losing position).  \n- N2 → N1 → N5 → terminal (opponent wins immediately, so this is a losing move for the player who moves here).  \n- N6 → N3 or N7.  \n  - N3 → N9 (win), N0 (lose), N8 → N9 (win). So N3 leads to at least one winning move.  \n  - N7 → terminal (opponent wins). So N6 gives opponent a chance to win via N7. But since N6 leads to N3 which has winning options (like N9), the player can force a win.  \n- N1 → N5 → terminal (opponent wins). Losing move.  \nBest move is N6, because it allows the player to go to N3, then N9 (terminal), winning. Thus, N6 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N6</answer>", "episode_score": -0.6166666666666667, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N4, the player can move to N2, N6, or N1. We analyze each option to determine which leads to a winning position (i.e., forces the opponent into a losing position).  \n- N2 → N1 → N5 → terminal (opponent wins immediately, so this is a losing move for the player who moves here).  \n- N6 → N3 or N7.  \n  - N3 → N9 (win), N0 (lose), N8 → N9 (win). So N3 leads to at least one winning move.  \n  - N7 → terminal (opponent wins). So N6 gives opponent a chance to win via N7. But since N6 leads to N3 which has winning options (like N9), the player can force a win.  \n- N1 → N5 → terminal (opponent wins). Losing move.  \nBest move is N6, because it allows the player to go to N3, then N9 (terminal), winning. Thus, N6 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N6</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N6\n\nDirected graph:\nN7 -> terminal\nN0 -> terminal\nN3 -> N9, N0, N8\nN2 -> N1\nN8 -> N9\nN6 -> N3, N7\nN9 -> terminal\nN1 -> N5\nN4 -> N2, N6, N1\nN5 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N3, N7assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N6, the legal moves are to N3 or N7. N7 leads directly to terminal, so the player who moves to N7 wins immediately (the opponent has no move). Thus, moving to N7 is a winning move. Moving to N3 leads to N9, N0, or N8. N9 and N0 lead to terminal, so the opponent wins. N8 leads to N9, which is terminal, so opponent wins. Thus, moving to N3 results in an immediate loss. Therefore, the optimal move is to N7.</reason>\n<answer>N7</answer>", "episode_score": 1.05, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N6, the legal moves are to N3 or N7. N7 leads directly to terminal, so the player who moves to N7 wins immediately (the opponent has no move). Thus, moving to N7 is a winning move. Moving to N3 leads to N9, N0, or N8. N9 and N0 lead to terminal, so the opponent wins. N8 leads to N9, which is terminal, so opponent wins. Thus, moving to N3 results in an immediate loss. Therefore, the optimal move is to N7.</reason>\n<answer>N7</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N2\n\nDirected graph:\nN2 -> N9, N3, N7\nN8 -> N0\nN3 -> N6, N5\nN7 -> N8, N3\nN6 -> N8, N1\nN5 -> terminal\nN0 -> terminal\nN1 -> terminal\nN4 -> terminal\nN9 -> N4\n\nLegal moves: N9, N3, N7assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N2, the legal moves are to N9, N3, or N7. We analyze each option:  \n- N9 → N4 (terminal): Immediate win for the opponent (I move to terminal, opponent loses). But N4 leads to terminal, so I win.  \n- N3 → N6 or N5: N5 leads to terminal (opponent wins). N6 → N8 or N1: both N8 and N1 lead to terminal. So N3 leads to opponent winning.  \n- N7 → N8 or N3: N8 → terminal, N3 as above leads to opponent winning. So N7 leads to opponent winning.  \n\nThus, only N9 leads to terminal immediately, so I win. Choosing N9 is optimal. Final outcome: win.</reason>  \n<answer>N9</answer>", "episode_score": -0.6166666666666667, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N2, the legal moves are to N9, N3, or N7. We analyze each option:  \n- N9 → N4 (terminal): Immediate win for the opponent (I move to terminal, opponent loses). But N4 leads to terminal, so I win.  \n- N3 → N6 or N5: N5 leads to terminal (opponent wins). N6 → N8 or N1: both N8 and N1 lead to terminal. So N3 leads to opponent winning.  \n- N7 → N8 or N3: N8 → terminal, N3 as above leads to opponent winning. So N7 leads to opponent winning.  \n\nThus, only N9 leads to terminal immediately, so I win. Choosing N9 is optimal. Final outcome: win.</reason>  \n<answer>N9</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N9\n\nDirected graph:\nN2 -> N9, N3, N7\nN8 -> N0\nN3 -> N6, N5\nN7 -> N8, N3\nN6 -> N8, N1\nN5 -> terminal\nN0 -> terminal\nN1 -> terminal\nN4 -> terminal\nN9 -> N4\n\nLegal moves: N4assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N9, the only legal move is to N4, which is a terminal node. Moving to N4 ends the game immediately, and the player who made the move wins (since the opponent has no legal move). Thus, choosing N4 is optimal and guarantees a win.</reason>\n<answer>N4</answer>", "episode_score": 0.05, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N9, the only legal move is to N4, which is a terminal node. Moving to N4 ends the game immediately, and the player who made the move wins (since the opponent has no legal move). Thus, choosing N4 is optimal and guarantees a win.</reason>\n<answer>N4</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N8\n\nDirected graph:\nN1 -> terminal\nN4 -> terminal\nN8 -> N2, N9\nN7 -> terminal\nN5 -> N0, N4\nN9 -> N3, N5\nN0 -> N2, N6\nN3 -> N4\nN6 -> terminal\nN2 -> N7, N1\n\nLegal moves: N2, N9assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N8, the legal moves are to N2 or N9. We analyze each option:\n\n- Moving to N2: From N2, moves are to N7 or N1. Both N7 and N1 lead to terminal nodes, so the player who moves to N2 wins immediately (the opponent has no move). Thus, N2 is a winning move.\n\n- Moving to N9: From N9, moves are to N3 or N5.\n  - N3 → N4 → terminal (opponent wins).\n  - N5 → N0 or N4\n    - N4 → terminal (opponent wins)\n    - N0 → N2 or N6\n      - N2 → leads to terminal (opponent wins)\n      - N6 → terminal (opponent wins)\n  All paths from N9 lead to the opponent winning. So N9 is a losing move.\n\nThus, choosing N2 maximizes the outcome. The player wins.</reason>\n<answer>N2</answer>", "episode_score": -0.95, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N8, the legal moves are to N2 or N9. We analyze each option:\n\n- Moving to N2: From N2, moves are to N7 or N1. Both N7 and N1 lead to terminal nodes, so the player who moves to N2 wins immediately (the opponent has no move). Thus, N2 is a winning move.\n\n- Moving to N9: From N9, moves are to N3 or N5.\n  - N3 → N4 → terminal (opponent wins).\n  - N5 → N0 or N4\n    - N4 → terminal (opponent wins)\n    - N0 → N2 or N6\n      - N2 → leads to terminal (opponent wins)\n      - N6 → terminal (opponent wins)\n  All paths from N9 lead to the opponent winning. So N9 is a losing move.\n\nThus, choosing N2 maximizes the outcome. The player wins.</reason>\n<answer>N2</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N2\n\nDirected graph:\nN1 -> terminal\nN4 -> terminal\nN8 -> N2, N9\nN7 -> terminal\nN5 -> N0, N4\nN9 -> N3, N5\nN0 -> N2, N6\nN3 -> N4\nN6 -> terminal\nN2 -> N7, N1\n\nLegal moves: N7, N1assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N2, the legal moves are to N7 or N1. N7 leads to terminal (win for the player who moves there), so that is a winning move. N1 also leads to terminal. Thus, both moves end the game immediately. Since the player who makes the move wins (the opponent has no legal move), choosing either N7 or N1 is winning. However, we must choose the one that maximizes outcome. As both lead to immediate termination, and neither gives a follow-up move, either is equivalent. But since N7 and N1 both lead to terminal, both are optimal. However, N1 leads to terminal directly, and N7 also. Thus, either is valid. But considering the graph, N2 -> N1 is direct to terminal, same as N2 -> N7. No difference in outcome. We choose one. N1 is a safe, direct move to terminal. Therefore, the optimal choice is N1.</reason>\n<answer>N1</answer>", "episode_score": 0.05, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N2, the legal moves are to N7 or N1. N7 leads to terminal (win for the player who moves there), so that is a winning move. N1 also leads to terminal. Thus, both moves end the game immediately. Since the player who makes the move wins (the opponent has no legal move), choosing either N7 or N1 is winning. However, we must choose the one that maximizes outcome. As both lead to immediate termination, and neither gives a follow-up move, either is equivalent. But since N7 and N1 both lead to terminal, both are optimal. However, N1 leads to terminal directly, and N7 also. Thus, either is valid. But considering the graph, N2 -> N1 is direct to terminal, same as N2 -> N7. No difference in outcome. We choose one. N1 is a safe, direct move to terminal. Therefore, the optimal choice is N1.</reason>\n<answer>N1</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N2\n\nDirected graph:\nN5 -> terminal\nN9 -> terminal\nN6 -> N1, N7\nN4 -> N9, N6\nN2 -> N4, N8, N6\nN0 -> N5\nN3 -> terminal\nN8 -> N0\nN1 -> terminal\nN7 -> N1, N3\n\nLegal moves: N4, N8, N6assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N2, the legal moves are to N4, N8, or N6. We analyze each option:  \n- N4: leads to N9 (terminal) or N6. N6 leads to N1 (terminal) or N7. N7 leads to N1 or N3 (both terminal). So N4 eventually leads to terminal.  \n- N8: leads to N0 → N5 (terminal). So N8 leads to terminal.  \n- N6: leads to N1 or N7. Both N1 and N7 lead to terminal. So N6 leads to terminal.  \n\nAll moves lead to terminal. However, since the player who cannot move loses, we must consider if any move forces the opponent into a losing position. But all paths eventually reach terminal nodes. Thus, every move ends the game immediately. Therefore, all options are equivalent. But since the goal is to maximize final outcome and all result in loss, any move is equal. Choosing N6 is safe as it branches to N1 or N7, both terminal. However, N4 and N8 also lead to terminal. No move improves outcome. The safest and most direct is N6. Final choice: N6.</reason>  \n<answer>N6</answer>", "episode_score": -0.6166666666666667, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N2, the legal moves are to N4, N8, or N6. We analyze each option:  \n- N4: leads to N9 (terminal) or N6. N6 leads to N1 (terminal) or N7. N7 leads to N1 or N3 (both terminal). So N4 eventually leads to terminal.  \n- N8: leads to N0 → N5 (terminal). So N8 leads to terminal.  \n- N6: leads to N1 or N7. Both N1 and N7 lead to terminal. So N6 leads to terminal.  \n\nAll moves lead to terminal. However, since the player who cannot move loses, we must consider if any move forces the opponent into a losing position. But all paths eventually reach terminal nodes. Thus, every move ends the game immediately. Therefore, all options are equivalent. But since the goal is to maximize final outcome and all result in loss, any move is equal. Choosing N6 is safe as it branches to N1 or N7, both terminal. However, N4 and N8 also lead to terminal. No move improves outcome. The safest and most direct is N6. Final choice: N6.</reason>  \n<answer>N6</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N6\n\nDirected graph:\nN5 -> terminal\nN9 -> terminal\nN6 -> N1, N7\nN4 -> N9, N6\nN2 -> N4, N8, N6\nN0 -> N5\nN3 -> terminal\nN8 -> N0\nN1 -> terminal\nN7 -> N1, N3\n\nLegal moves: N1, N7assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N6, the legal moves are to N1 or N7. N1 leads directly to terminal (win for the player who moves there, as the opponent has no move). N7 leads to N1 or N3, both of which are terminal. Thus, moving to N1 wins immediately. Moving to N7 results in the opponent winning. Therefore, the optimal move is to N1.</reason>\n<answer>N1</answer>", "episode_score": 1.05, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N6, the legal moves are to N1 or N7. N1 leads directly to terminal (win for the player who moves there, as the opponent has no move). N7 leads to N1 or N3, both of which are terminal. Thus, moving to N1 wins immediately. Moving to N7 results in the opponent winning. Therefore, the optimal move is to N1.</reason>\n<answer>N1</answer>"}]
