hat maximizes the chance of winning (i.e., forces the opponent into a losing position).  \n\n- Move to N0:  \n  N0 → N3 or N13 (N13 is terminal, so if opponent picks N13, it's a win for them. N3 leads to N5, N11, N7. N5 → N11 → N12 (terminal) or N2 → terminal; N7 → N9 → N4 → terminal. All paths lead to terminal, so if we move to N0, opponent can win on a safe path.)\n\n- Move to N10:  \n  N10 → N6, N4, N8 all lead to terminal. So opponent has no legal move → **winning move**.\n\n- Move to N7:  \n  N7 → N9 → N4 → all terminal, opponent will win next.\n\nThus, moving to N10 leads to terminal nodes immediately. The opponent has no legal moves and loses. This is a winning move. No other move offers such a guarantee.</reason>\n<answer>N10</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N10\n\nDirected graph:\nN2 -> terminal\nN13 -> terminal\nN6 -> terminal\nN3 -> N5, N11, N7\nN1 -> N0, N10, N7\nN10 -> N6, N4, N8\nN9 -> N4\nN5 -> N11, N2\nN8 -> terminal\nN7 -> N9\nN4 -> terminal\nN12 -> terminal\nN11 -> N12\nN0 -> N13, N3\n\nLegal moves: N6, N4, N8assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N10, the legal moves are to N6, N4, or N8. N6, N4, and N8 all lead to terminal nodes, so the player who moves to any of these wins immediately (the opponent has no move). Thus, all moves from N10 win. Since all options lead to immediate victory, any chosen move is optimal. We choose N6 as it is a terminal node and satisfies the condition of maximizing outcome.</reason>\n<answer>N6</answer>", "episode_score": 0.05, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N10, the legal moves are to N6, N4, or N8. N6, N4, and N8 all lead to terminal nodes, so the player who moves to any of these wins immediately (the opponent has no move). Thus, all moves from N10 win. Since all options lead to immediate victory, any chosen move is optimal. We choose N6 as it is a terminal node and satisfies the condition of maximizing outcome.</reason>\n<answer>N6</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N5\n\nDirected graph:\nN8 -> N4\nN9 -> terminal\nN5 -> N6, N8, N4\nN1 -> N2\nN0 -> N7\nN4 -> N6, N9\nN7 -> terminal\nN3 -> terminal\nN6 -> N3, N0, N1\nN2 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N6, N8, N4assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N5, the legal moves are to N6, N8, or N4. We analyze each destination to determine the outcome (win or loss) assuming optimal play.  \n- N4 leads to N6 or N9. N9 is terminal (win for opponent), N6 leads to N3 (terminal) or N0 or N1. N0 leads to N7 (terminal), N1 leads to N2 (terminal). All paths from N6 eventually lead to terminal nodes. So N4 results in a win for the opponent.  \n- N8 leads to N4. Then as above, N4 leads to terminal states. So N8 also leads to opponent win.  \n- N6 leads to N3 (terminal), N0 (to N7 terminal), N1 (to N2 terminal). All are terminal. So N6 is a win for the current player.  \nThus, choosing N6 results in an immediate win. The best move is N6.</reason>  \n<answer>N6</answer>", "episode_score": -0.6166666666666667, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N5, the legal moves are to N6, N8, or N4. We analyze each destination to determine the outcome (win or loss) assuming optimal play.  \n- N4 leads to N6 or N9. N9 is terminal (win for opponent), N6 leads to N3 (terminal) or N0 or N1. N0 leads to N7 (terminal), N1 leads to N2 (terminal). All paths from N6 eventually lead to terminal nodes. So N4 results in a win for the opponent.  \n- N8 leads to N4. Then as above, N4 leads to terminal states. So N8 also leads to opponent win.  \n- N6 leads to N3 (terminal), N0 (to N7 terminal), N1 (to N2 terminal). All are terminal. So N6 is a win for the current player.  \nThus, choosing N6 results in an immediate win. The best move is N6.</reason>  \n<answer>N6</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N6\n\nDirected graph:\nN8 -> N4\nN9 -> terminal\nN5 -> N6, N8, N4\nN1 -> N2\nN0 -> N7\nN4 -> N6, N9\nN7 -> terminal\nN3 -> terminal\nN6 -> N3, N0, N1\nN2 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N3, N0, N1assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N6, the legal moves are to N3, N0, or N1. N3 leads directly to terminal (win for the player who moves there, as the opponent has no move). N0 leads to N7, which is terminal, so the opponent wins. N1 leads to N2, which is terminal, so the opponent wins. Thus, moving to N3 wins immediately. Choosing N3 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N3</answer>", "episode_score": 1.3833333333333333, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N6, the legal moves are to N3, N0, or N1. N3 leads directly to terminal (win for the player who moves there, as the opponent has no move). N0 leads to N7, which is terminal, so the opponent wins. N1 leads to N2, which is terminal, so the opponent wins. Thus, moving to N3 wins immediately. Choosing N3 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N3</answer>"}]
