[2026-08-13 16:28:30] [agentic_pipeline.py (516)] [INFO] [DRIVER 0 / 1][PID 1538566] [{"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N5\n\nDirected graph:\nN1 -> N2\nN7 -> terminal\nN8 -> N9, N4\nN0 -> terminal\nN4 -> N0, N1\nN5 -> N3, N8\nN2 -> terminal\nN3 -> N6, N9\nN6 -> terminal\nN9 -> N0, N7\n\nLegal moves: N3, N8assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N5, the legal moves are to N3 or N8. We analyze each option:\n\n- Moving to N3: From N3, moves are to N6 or N9.\n  - N6 leads to terminal (win for the player who moved there, so opponent loses).\n  - N9 leads to N0 or N7; N0 is terminal, N7 is terminal. So N9 ends in terminal. Thus, N3 leads to a win if the opponent is forced to move into a terminal.\n  - So N3 leads to a win.\n\n- Moving to N8: From N8, moves are to N9 or N4.\n  - N9 → N0 or N7 (both terminal) → opponent loses.\n  - N4 → N0 or N1; N0 terminal, N1 → N2 (terminal). So N4 also leads to terminal.\n  - Thus, N8 also leads to a win.\n\nBoth N3 and N8 lead to a win. However, we must choose the one that maximizes the outcome. Since both are winning, we pick one. N3 leads directly to N6 (terminal) or N9 (safe terminal paths). N8 leads to N9 or N4, both of which eventually end in terminal. But N3 has a direct path to N6 (terminal), which is a quicker win. Thus, N3 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N3</answer>", "episode_score": -0.95, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N5, the legal moves are to N3 or N8. We analyze each option:\n\n- Moving to N3: From N3, moves are to N6 or N9.\n  - N6 leads to terminal (win for the player who moved there, so opponent loses).\n  - N9 leads to N0 or N7; N0 is terminal, N7 is terminal. So N9 ends in terminal. Thus, N3 leads to a win if the opponent is forced to move into a terminal.\n  - So N3 leads to a win.\n\n- Moving to N8: From N8, moves are to N9 or N4.\n  - N9 → N0 or N7 (both terminal) → opponent loses.\n  - N4 → N0 or N1; N0 terminal, N1 → N2 (terminal). So N4 also leads to terminal.\n  - Thus, N8 also leads to a win.\n\nBoth N3 and N8 lead to a win. However, we must choose the one that maximizes the outcome. Since both are winning, we pick one. N3 leads directly to N6 (terminal) or N9 (safe terminal paths). N8 leads to N9 or N4, both of which eventually end in terminal. But N3 has a direct path to N6 (terminal), which is a quicker win. Thus, N3 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N3</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N3\n\nDirected graph:\nN1 -> N2\nN7 -> terminal\nN8 -> N9, N4\nN0 -> terminal\nN4 -> N0, N1\nN5 -> N3, N8\nN2 -> terminal\nN3 -> N6, N9\nN6 -> terminal\nN9 -> N0, N7\n\nLegal moves: N6, N9assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N3, the legal moves are to N6 or N9. N6 leads directly to terminal (win for the player who moves there, as the opponent has no move). N9 leads to N0 or N7. N0 and N7 are terminal, so moving to N9 results in the opponent winning immediately. Thus, choosing N6 wins the game. The optimal move is to N6.</reason>\n<answer>N6</answer>", "episode_score": 1.05, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N3, the legal moves are to N6 or N9. N6 leads directly to terminal (win for the player who moves there, as the opponent has no move). N9 leads to N0 or N7. N0 and N7 are terminal, so moving to N9 results in the opponent winning immediately. Thus, choosing N6 wins the game. The optimal move is to N6.</reason>\n<answer>N6</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N1\n\nDirected graph:\nN1 -> N8, N3\nN3 -> N9, N6\nN7 -> N2, N0\nN2 -> terminal\nN4 -> N5, N8\nN6 -> terminal\nN0 -> terminal\nN8 -> N7\nN5 -> terminal\nN9 -> N4, N5\n\nLegal moves: N8, N3assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N1, the legal moves are to N8 or N3. We analyze each:  \n- Moving to N8: From N8, move to N7. From N7, moves to N2 or N0, both terminal. So the opponent wins immediately.  \n- Moving to N3: From N3, moves to N9 or N6. N6 leads to terminal (opponent wins). N9 leads to N4 or N5; N5 is terminal, N4 leads to N5 (terminal). So all paths from N3 lead to terminal quickly. Thus, whatever move is taken, the opponent wins. Therefore, no matter which legal move is chosen, the current player loses. But since we must choose a move that maximizes outcome, and all lead to loss, the optimal choice is the one that delays the loss longest. N3 leads to N9, which branches to N4 → N5 (terminal), so the opponent faces no move. However, all paths end quickly. Ultimately, all moves lead to opponent winning. Thus, the best (and only) choice is N3, as it avoids immediate terminal and offers a slightly longer game. But since all result in loss, the maximization of outcome is equivalent. However, N3 provides a path with more options. So we choose N3.</reason>  \n<answer>N3</answer>", "episode_score": -0.95, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N1, the legal moves are to N8 or N3. We analyze each:  \n- Moving to N8: From N8, move to N7. From N7, moves to N2 or N0, both terminal. So the opponent wins immediately.  \n- Moving to N3: From N3, moves to N9 or N6. N6 leads to terminal (opponent wins). N9 leads to N4 or N5; N5 is terminal, N4 leads to N5 (terminal). So all paths from N3 lead to terminal quickly. Thus, whatever move is taken, the opponent wins. Therefore, no matter which legal move is chosen, the current player loses. But since we must choose a move that maximizes outcome, and all lead to loss, the optimal choice is the one that delays the loss longest. N3 leads to N9, which branches to N4 → N5 (terminal), so the opponent faces no move. However, all paths end quickly. Ultimately, all moves lead to opponent winning. Thus, the best (and only) choice is N3, as it avoids immediate terminal and offers a slightly longer game. But since all result in loss, the maximization of outcome is equivalent. However, N3 provides a path with more options. So we choose N3.</reason>  \n<answer>N3</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N3\n\nDirected graph:\nN1 -> N8, N3\nN3 -> N9, N6\nN7 -> N2, N0\nN2 -> terminal\nN4 -> N5, N8\nN6 -> terminal\nN0 -> terminal\nN8 -> N7\nN5 -> terminal\nN9 -> N4, N5\n\nLegal moves: N9, N6assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N3, the legal moves are to N9 or N6. N6 leads directly to terminal (win for the player who moves there, as the opponent has no move). Thus, moving to N6 wins immediately. Moving to N9 leads to N4 or N5; N5 leads to terminal, so the opponent wins. N4 leads to N5 or N8. N8 leads to N7, which leads to N2 or N0, both terminal. Thus, N9 leads to a win for the opponent. Therefore, choosing N6 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N6</answer>", "episode_score": 1.05, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N3, the legal moves are to N9 or N6. N6 leads directly to terminal (win for the player who moves there, as the opponent has no move). Thus, moving to N6 wins immediately. Moving to N9 leads to N4 or N5; N5 leads to terminal, so the opponent wins. N4 leads to N5 or N8. N8 leads to N7, which leads to N2 or N0, both terminal. Thus, N9 leads to a win for the opponent. Therefore, choosing N6 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N6</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N4\n\nDirected graph:\nN8 -> N5, N1\nN7 -> N9\nN0 -> terminal\nN3 -> terminal\nN2 -> N3, N6\nN5 -> terminal\nN4 -> N2, N7\nN9 -> N0, N3\nN1 -> terminal\nN6 -> N7, N8\n\nLegal moves: N2, N7assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N4, the legal moves are to N2 or N7. We analyze each option:\n\n- Move to N2: From N2, moves are to N3 (terminal) or N6.\n  - N3 → terminal: opponent loses immediately.\n  - N6 → N7 or N8.\n    - N7 → N9 → N0 or N3 (both terminal).\n    - N8 → N5 (terminal) or N1 (terminal). So N6 leads to terminal states.\n  So N2 leads to a path where the opponent eventually wins (no back options).\n\n- Move to N7: From N7 → N9.\n  - N9 → N0 or N3 (both terminal). Opponent loses immediately.\n\nThus, moving to N7 forces the opponent into a terminal state immediately. Moving to N2 allows opponent to escape to N6 → N7 → N9 → terminal, but opponent can still win. Therefore, N7 is better. Choosing N7 leads to immediate win.</reason>\n<answer>N7</answer>", "episode_score": 1.05, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N4, the legal moves are to N2 or N7. We analyze each option:\n\n- Move to N2: From N2, moves are to N3 (terminal) or N6.\n  - N3 → terminal: opponent loses immediately.\n  - N6 → N7 or N8.\n    - N7 → N9 → N0 or N3 (both terminal).\n    - N8 → N5 (terminal) or N1 (terminal). So N6 leads to terminal states.\n  So N2 leads to a path where the opponent eventually wins (no back options).\n\n- Move to N7: From N7 → N9.\n  - N9 → N0 or N3 (both terminal). Opponent loses immediately.\n\nThus, moving to N7 forces the opponent into a terminal state immediately. Moving to N2 allows opponent to escape to N6 → N7 → N9 → terminal, but opponent can still win. Therefore, N7 is better. Choosing N7 leads to immediate win.</reason>\n<answer>N7</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N7\n\nDirected graph:\nN8 -> N5, N1\nN7 -> N9\nN0 -> terminal\nN3 -> terminal\nN2 -> N3, N6\nN5 -> terminal\nN4 -> N2, N7\nN9 -> N0, N3\nN1 -> terminal\nN6 -> N7, N8\n\nLegal moves: N9assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N7, the only legal move is to N9. From N9, the player can move to N0 or N3, both of which are terminal nodes. Moving to N0 or N3 ends the game immediately, and the opponent has no legal move. Thus, moving to N9 wins the game. No other move from N7 is available. Therefore, the optimal move is to N9.</reason>\n<answer>N9</answer>", "episode_score": 0.05, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N7, the only legal move is to N9. From N9, the player can move to N0 or N3, both of which are terminal nodes. Moving to N0 or N3 ends the game immediately, and the opponent has no legal move. Thus, moving to N9 wins the game. No other move from N7 is available. Therefore, the optimal move is to N9.</reason>\n<answer>N9</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N9\n\nDirected graph:\nN8 -> N5, N1\nN7 -> N9\nN0 -> terminal\nN3 -> terminal\nN2 -> N3, N6\nN5 -> terminal\nN4 -> N2, N7\nN9 -> N0, N3\nN1 -> terminal\nN6 -> N7, N8\n\nLegal moves: N0, N3assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N9, the legal moves are to N0 or N3. Both N0 and N3 are terminal nodes, so moving to either ends the game immediately. The player who makes the move to a terminal node wins, as the opponent has no legal move. Thus, both moves end the game immediately. Since the player who moves first (me) wins by ending the game, I choose one of the legal moves. Both N0 and N3 are terminal, so either is valid. I choose N3 as it appears earlier in the list.</reason>\n<answer>N3</answer>", "episode_score": 0.05, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N9, the legal moves are to N0 or N3. Both N0 and N3 are terminal nodes, so moving to either ends the game immediately. The player who makes the move to a terminal node wins, as the opponent has no legal move. Thus, both moves end the game immediately. Since the player who moves first (me) wins by ending the game, I choose one of the legal moves. Both N0 and N3 are terminal, so either is valid. I choose N3 as it appears earlier in the list.</reason>\n<answer>N3</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N3\n\nDirected graph:\nN0 -> terminal\nN2 -> N1, N7\nN1 -> terminal\nN3 -> N4, N5\nN8 -> N7, N9\nN7 -> terminal\nN4 -> N5, N8\nN9 -> N0\nN5 -> N6, N2\nN6 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N4, N5assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N3, the player can move to N4 or N5. We analyze each option:\n\n- Moving to N4: From N4, moves are to N5 or N8.\n  - N5 → N6 (win) or N2 (N2 → N1 or N7, both terminal → win).\n  - N8 → N7 (terminal) or N9 → N0 → terminal. So N4 leads to a win if opponent is not at terminal.\n  - All paths from N4 eventually lead to terminal; so the current player wins.\n  \n- Moving to N5: From N5, moves are N6 (terminal) or N2:\n  - N2 → N1 (terminal) or N7 (terminal) → both win for the current move.\n\nEither choice leads to terminal on the next opponent's turn. Since the player who makes the last move (i.e., leads to terminal) wins, both N4 and N5 lead to opponent's loss. However, we must pick one that maximizes outcome. Both lead to win. But since N5 leads directly to N6 or N2 (both safe paths to terminal), and N4 branches to N8 which loops to N9 → N0 (safe), both are winning. But N5 offers a more direct route with fewer risks. Thus, N5 is optimal as it leads to terminal quickly and safely. However, since both are winning, and the goal is to maximize outcome, either is valid. But N5 leads to N6 (terminal) immediately, so it's safer and faster. Therefore, choose N5.</reason>\n<answer>N5</answer>", "episode_score": -0.95, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N3, the player can move to N4 or N5. We analyze each option:\n\n- Moving to N4: From N4, moves are to N5 or N8.\n  - N5 → N6 (win) or N2 (N2 → N1 or N7, both terminal → win).\n  - N8 → N7 (terminal) or N9 → N0 → terminal. So N4 leads to a win if opponent is not at terminal.\n  - All paths from N4 eventually lead to terminal; so the current player wins.\n  \n- Moving to N5: From N5, moves are N6 (terminal) or N2:\n  - N2 → N1 (terminal) or N7 (terminal) → both win for the current move.\n\nEither choice leads to terminal on the next opponent's turn. Since the player who makes the last move (i.e., leads to terminal) wins, both N4 and N5 lead to opponent's loss. However, we must pick one that maximizes outcome. Both lead to win. But since N5 leads directly to N6 or N2 (both safe paths to terminal), and N4 branches to N8 which loops to N9 → N0 (safe), both are winning. But N5 offers a more direct route with fewer risks. Thus, N5 is optimal as it leads to terminal quickly and safely. However, since both are winning, and the goal is to maximize outcome, either is valid. But N5 leads to N6 (terminal) immediately, so it's safer and faster. Therefore, choose N5.</reason>\n<answer>N5</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N1\n\nDirected graph:\nN1 -> N9, N0, N3\nN7 -> terminal\nN4 -> N6, N2\nN5 -> terminal\nN0 -> N9\nN8 -> terminal\nN9 -> N8, N4, N3\nN6 -> N7\nN2 -> terminal\nN3 -> N5\n\nLegal moves: N9, N0, N3assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N1, the legal moves are to N9, N0, or N3. We analyze each option to find the one that maximizes the chance of winning (i.e., forces the opponent into a losing position).  \n\n- Moving to N9: From N9, moves are to N8 (terminal), N4, N3.  \n  - N8 leads to terminal (opponent loses immediately). So N9 → N8 is winning.  \n  - N9 → N4: N4 → N6 → N7 (terminal) or N4 → N2 (terminal). Both terminal, so opponent loses.  \n  - N9 → N3: N3 → N5 (terminal). Opponent loses.  \n  So all moves from N9 lead to opponent losing. Thus N1 → N9 is winning.\n\n- Moving to N0: N0 → N9. Then as above, N9 leads to terminal or opponent loses. So N1 → N0 → N9 → ... leads to opponent losing. Winning.\n\n- Moving to N3: N3 → N5 (terminal). Opponent loses immediately. So N1 → N3 is winning.\n\nAll options lead to a win. However, we must pick one that maximizes outcome. Since all lead to win, any is valid. But N3 directly leads to N5 (terminal), ending the game immediately. This is a guaranteed win with minimal risk. Thus, N3 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N3</answer>", "episode_score": -0.6166666666666667, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N1, the legal moves are to N9, N0, or N3. We analyze each option to find the one that maximizes the chance of winning (i.e., forces the opponent into a losing position).  \n\n- Moving to N9: From N9, moves are to N8 (terminal), N4, N3.  \n  - N8 leads to terminal (opponent loses immediately). So N9 → N8 is winning.  \n  - N9 → N4: N4 → N6 → N7 (terminal) or N4 → N2 (terminal). Both terminal, so opponent loses.  \n  - N9 → N3: N3 → N5 (terminal). Opponent loses.  \n  So all moves from N9 lead to opponent losing. Thus N1 → N9 is winning.\n\n- Moving to N0: N0 → N9. Then as above, N9 leads to terminal or opponent loses. So N1 → N0 → N9 → ... leads to opponent losing. Winning.\n\n- Moving to N3: N3 → N5 (terminal). Opponent loses immediately. So N1 → N3 is winning.\n\nAll options lead to a win. However, we must pick one that maximizes outcome. Since all lead to win, any is valid. But N3 directly leads to N5 (terminal), ending the game immediately. This is a guaranteed win with minimal risk. Thus, N3 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N3</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N5\n\nDirected graph:\nN0 -> terminal\nN2 -> N1, N7\nN1 -> terminal\nN3 -> N4, N5\nN8 -> N7, N9\nN7 -> terminal\nN4 -> N5, N8\nN9 -> N0\nN5 -> N6, N2\nN6 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N6, N2assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N5, the legal moves are to N6 or N2. N6 leads directly to terminal (win for the player who moves there, as the opponent has no move). N2 leads to N1 or N7, both of which lead to terminal. Thus, moving to N6 wins immediately. Moving to N2 allows the opponent to win immediately. Therefore, the optimal move is to N6.</reason>\n<answer>N6</answer>", "episode_score": 1.05, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N5, the legal moves are to N6 or N2. N6 leads directly to terminal (win for the player who moves there, as the opponent has no move). N2 leads to N1 or N7, both of which lead to terminal. Thus, moving to N6 wins immediately. Moving to N2 allows the opponent to win immediately. Therefore, the optimal move is to N6.</reason>\n<answer>N6</answer>"}]
