[2026-08-13 16:30:41] [agentic_pipeline.py (516)] [INFO] [DRIVER 0 / 1][PID 1538566] [{"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N5\n\nDirected graph:\nN9 -> terminal\nN4 -> terminal\nN5 -> N6, N1, N3\nN7 -> terminal\nN2 -> terminal\nN0 -> N2, N7\nN6 -> N4\nN8 -> N9, N3\nN1 -> N8\nN3 -> N0, N9\n\nLegal moves: N6, N1, N3assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N5, the legal moves are to N6, N1, or N3. We analyze each option:  \n- N6 → N4 → terminal (player who moves to N4 wins immediately, opponent loses).  \n- N1 → N8 → N3 → N0 → N2 (terminal) or N3 → N0 → N2 (terminal). Both paths lead to terminal nodes.  \n  Deep: N1 → N8 → N3 → N0 → N2, opponent wins.  \n- N3 → N0 → N2 (terminal) or N3 → N9 (terminal). So N3 leads to immediate terminal.  \n\nThus, N6 leads to N4 (terminal), opponent loses. N1 and N3 lead to opponent winning. Choosing N6 is optimal. </reason>  \n<answer>N6</answer>", "episode_score": -0.6166666666666667, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N5, the legal moves are to N6, N1, or N3. We analyze each option:  \n- N6 → N4 → terminal (player who moves to N4 wins immediately, opponent loses).  \n- N1 → N8 → N3 → N0 → N2 (terminal) or N3 → N0 → N2 (terminal). Both paths lead to terminal nodes.  \n  Deep: N1 → N8 → N3 → N0 → N2, opponent wins.  \n- N3 → N0 → N2 (terminal) or N3 → N9 (terminal). So N3 leads to immediate terminal.  \n\nThus, N6 leads to N4 (terminal), opponent loses. N1 and N3 lead to opponent winning. Choosing N6 is optimal. </reason>  \n<answer>N6</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N6\n\nDirected graph:\nN9 -> terminal\nN4 -> terminal\nN5 -> N6, N1, N3\nN7 -> terminal\nN2 -> terminal\nN0 -> N2, N7\nN6 -> N4\nN8 -> N9, N3\nN1 -> N8\nN3 -> N0, N9\n\nLegal moves: N4assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N6, the only legal move is to N4, which leads to terminal. Thus, the player who moves from N6 wins immediately, as the opponent has no move. Therefore, the optimal move is to N4.</reason>\n<answer>N4</answer>", "episode_score": 0.05, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N6, the only legal move is to N4, which leads to terminal. Thus, the player who moves from N6 wins immediately, as the opponent has no move. Therefore, the optimal move is to N4.</reason>\n<answer>N4</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N2\n\nDirected graph:\nN5 -> N4\nN4 -> terminal\nN2 -> N7, N1\nN3 -> terminal\nN6 -> terminal\nN0 -> N3, N7\nN8 -> terminal\nN1 -> N0, N9\nN9 -> N5, N6\nN7 -> N3, N8\n\nLegal moves: N7, N1assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N2, the legal moves are to N7 or N1. We analyze each:\n\n- Moving to N7: From N7, moves are to N3 or N8. Both N3 and N8 lead to terminal nodes. So the player who moves to N7 wins immediately (opponent has no move).\n- Moving to N1: From N1, moves are to N0 or N9.\n  - N0 leads to N3 or N7. N3 and N7 both lead to terminal nodes.\n  - N9 leads to N5 or N6; N5 → N4 → terminal; N6 → terminal. So all paths from N1 eventually end.\n\nThus, moving to N7 ends the game immediately (the opponent has no move), so it is a winning move. Moving to N1 leads to a position where the opponent can force a win. Therefore, choosing N7 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N7</answer>", "episode_score": -0.95, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N2, the legal moves are to N7 or N1. We analyze each:\n\n- Moving to N7: From N7, moves are to N3 or N8. Both N3 and N8 lead to terminal nodes. So the player who moves to N7 wins immediately (opponent has no move).\n- Moving to N1: From N1, moves are to N0 or N9.\n  - N0 leads to N3 or N7. N3 and N7 both lead to terminal nodes.\n  - N9 leads to N5 or N6; N5 → N4 → terminal; N6 → terminal. So all paths from N1 eventually end.\n\nThus, moving to N7 ends the game immediately (the opponent has no move), so it is a winning move. Moving to N1 leads to a position where the opponent can force a win. Therefore, choosing N7 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N7</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N7\n\nDirected graph:\nN5 -> N4\nN4 -> terminal\nN2 -> N7, N1\nN3 -> terminal\nN6 -> terminal\nN0 -> N3, N7\nN8 -> terminal\nN1 -> N0, N9\nN9 -> N5, N6\nN7 -> N3, N8\n\nLegal moves: N3, N8assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N7, the legal moves are to N3 or N8. N3 leads to terminal (win for the player who moves there, as the opponent has no move), and N8 also leads to terminal. Thus, both moves end the game immediately. Since the player who makes the move wins (the opponent has no legal move), choosing either N3 or N8 is winning. Both are terminal moves, so either is optimal. However, since the goal is to maximize final game outcome and both result in an immediate win, either is valid. But N3 is a shared move from N2 and N7, and N8 is only from N7. No strategic advantage differs. Thus, either is fine. We choose N3 as it is accessible and terminal.</reason>\n<answer>N3</answer>", "episode_score": 0.05, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N7, the legal moves are to N3 or N8. N3 leads to terminal (win for the player who moves there, as the opponent has no move), and N8 also leads to terminal. Thus, both moves end the game immediately. Since the player who makes the move wins (the opponent has no legal move), choosing either N3 or N8 is winning. Both are terminal moves, so either is optimal. However, since the goal is to maximize final game outcome and both result in an immediate win, either is valid. But N3 is a shared move from N2 and N7, and N8 is only from N7. No strategic advantage differs. Thus, either is fine. We choose N3 as it is accessible and terminal.</reason>\n<answer>N3</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N8\n\nDirected graph:\nN7 -> N11, N6\nN13 -> N10, N3\nN11 -> terminal\nN9 -> N0, N12\nN1 -> terminal\nN10 -> N4, N1\nN2 -> N7, N13\nN0 -> terminal\nN3 -> terminal\nN8 -> N2, N5\nN4 -> N12, N1\nN6 -> terminal\nN12 -> terminal\nN5 -> N11, N9\n\nLegal moves: N2, N5assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N8, player can move to N2 or N5.  \n- If move to N2:  \n  - N2 → N7 or N13  \n    - N7 → N11 (win) or N6 (loss)  \n      → N7 → N11 (terminal, opponent wins) → loss  \n    - N13 → N10 or N3  \n      - N10 → N4 or N1 (both terminal) → opponent wins  \n      - N3 → terminal → opponent wins  \n  → All moves from N2 lead to opponent winning.  \n- If move to N5:  \n  - N5 → N11 (terminal, opponent wins) or N9  \n    - N9 → N0 or N12 (both terminal) → opponent wins  \n→ All moves from N5 lead to opponent winning.  \nThus, every move from N8 leads to opponent winning. But since we must choose a move that maximizes our outcome, and all result in loss, we pick the move that delays loss longest or minimizes opponent's advantage. N5 leads to N9 which has N0 or N12 (both terminal), so no advantage. N2 leads to N13 → N10 → N4 → N12 (terminal). All paths end quickly. No difference in outcome. However, N5 allows access to N9 → N0 or N12 (terminal), but still opponent wins. Both are losing. Choose N5 as it avoids N2’s path to N7→N11 (immediate terminal). But all paths end with opponent win. Still, N5 is safer. However, since all moves lead to loss, any is equivalent. Pick N5.</reason>  \n<answer>N5</answer>", "episode_score": -0.95, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N8, player can move to N2 or N5.  \n- If move to N2:  \n  - N2 → N7 or N13  \n    - N7 → N11 (win) or N6 (loss)  \n      → N7 → N11 (terminal, opponent wins) → loss  \n    - N13 → N10 or N3  \n      - N10 → N4 or N1 (both terminal) → opponent wins  \n      - N3 → terminal → opponent wins  \n  → All moves from N2 lead to opponent winning.  \n- If move to N5:  \n  - N5 → N11 (terminal, opponent wins) or N9  \n    - N9 → N0 or N12 (both terminal) → opponent wins  \n→ All moves from N5 lead to opponent winning.  \nThus, every move from N8 leads to opponent winning. But since we must choose a move that maximizes our outcome, and all result in loss, we pick the move that delays loss longest or minimizes opponent's advantage. N5 leads to N9 which has N0 or N12 (both terminal), so no advantage. N2 leads to N13 → N10 → N4 → N12 (terminal). All paths end quickly. No difference in outcome. However, N5 allows access to N9 → N0 or N12 (terminal), but still opponent wins. Both are losing. Choose N5 as it avoids N2’s path to N7→N11 (immediate terminal). But all paths end with opponent win. Still, N5 is safer. However, since all moves lead to loss, any is equivalent. Pick N5.</reason>  \n<answer>N5</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N5\n\nDirected graph:\nN7 -> N11, N6\nN13 -> N10, N3\nN11 -> terminal\nN9 -> N0, N12\nN1 -> terminal\nN10 -> N4, N1\nN2 -> N7, N13\nN0 -> terminal\nN3 -> terminal\nN8 -> N2, N5\nN4 -> N12, N1\nN6 -> terminal\nN12 -> terminal\nN5 -> N11, N9\n\nLegal moves: N11, N9assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N5, the legal moves are to N11 and N9. N11 leads directly to terminal (win for the player who moves there, as the opponent has no move). N9 leads to N0 or N12, both of which are terminal. Thus, moving to N11 wins immediately. Moving to N9 results in a immediate win for the opponent. Therefore, choosing N11 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N11</answer>", "episode_score": 1.05, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N5, the legal moves are to N11 and N9. N11 leads directly to terminal (win for the player who moves there, as the opponent has no move). N9 leads to N0 or N12, both of which are 