or N5 (terminal) → both win.  \n  - N6 → N0 or N12. N12 is terminal (win). N0 → N9 or N4. N9 → N7 or N11; N7 → N4 or N5 (both terminal); N11 terminal. So all paths from N1 eventually lead to terminal.  \nBoth N3 and N1 lead to wins. But N3 has a direct move to N8 (terminal) or N12 (terminal), so immediate win. N1 routes involve more complexity but also lead to terminal. Since both options win, we choose the one that maximizes outcome. As N3 leads to immediate terminal moves, it is optimal. Thus, N3 is better.</reason>  \n<answer>N3</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N3\n\nDirected graph:\nN5 -> terminal\nN8 -> terminal\nN9 -> N7, N11\nN2 -> N3, N1\nN7 -> N4, N5\nN1 -> N13, N6\nN3 -> N8, N12\nN0 -> N9, N4\nN6 -> N0, N12\nN11 -> terminal\nN13 -> N10, N5\nN10 -> terminal\nN12 -> terminal\nN4 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N8, N12assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N3, the legal moves are to N8 or N12. N8 leads to terminal (win for the player who moves there, as the opponent has no move). N12 leads to terminal immediately. Both moves end the game immediately. Since the player who makes the move wins (the opponent has no legal move), choosing either N8 or N12 results in an immediate win. Both are winning moves, but N12 is a direct terminal move. However, N8 also leads to terminal. Since both are terminal, either is optimal. But N12 is a shorter path. Thus, N12 is chosen as it is a direct win.</reason>\n<answer>N12</answer>", "episode_score": 0.05, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N3, the legal moves are to N8 or 