[2026-08-13 16:37:17] [agentic_pipeline.py (516)] [INFO] [DRIVER 0 / 1][PID 1538566] [{"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N9\n\nDirected graph:\nN11 -> N8, N2\nN7 -> terminal\nN9 -> N11, N2\nN17 -> N1\nN1 -> terminal\nN2 -> N10, N14\nN15 -> N12, N3\nN16 -> N4, N17\nN6 -> terminal\nN3 -> terminal\nN5 -> N13\nN0 -> terminal\nN10 -> N17, N6\nN14 -> N5\nN4 -> N0, N15\nN13 -> terminal\nN12 -> N7\nN8 -> N4, N16\n\nLegal moves: N11, N2assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N9, the player can move to N11 or N2.  \n- Moving to N2: leads to N10 or N14.  \n  - N10 → N17 → N1 (terminal) → N10 can go to N6 (terminal), so N14 (N to 3) end, \n  -- \n  \n.  \n- 14 leads immediately Ns\n\n  \n \r\n-\r”\n”\n\n<answer>N2</answer>", "episode_score": -1.5, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N9, the player can move to N11 or N2.  \n- Moving to N2: leads to N10 or N14.  \n  - N10 → N17 → N1 (terminal) → N10 can go to N6 (terminal), so N14 (N to 3) end, \n  -- \n  \n.  \n- 14 leads immediately Ns\n\n  \n \r\n-\r”\n”\n\n<answer>N2</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N2\n\nDirected graph:\nN11 -> N9, N13, N6\nN12 -> N5\nN6 -> N7, N3, N1\nN0 -> terminal\nN3 -> N10\nN2 -> N10, N11, N3\nN10 -> N0\nN13 -> N8\nN4 -> terminal\nN7 -> terminal\nN1 -> N0, N4, N12\nN5 -> terminal\nN9 -> terminal\nN8 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N10, N11, N3assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N2, the legal moves are to N10, N11, or N3. N10 leads to N0 (terminal), so the player who moves to N10 wins immediately (the opponent has no move). N11 leads to N9 or N13: N9 is terminal, N13 leads to N8 (terminal), so N11 leads to a terminal node, meaning the opponent wins. N3 leads to N10, which then leads to N0 (terminal), so the opponent wins. Thus, moving to N10 is winning since it ends the game immediately. Therefore, the optimal move is to N10.</reason>\n<answer>N10</answer>", "episode_score": -0.6166666666666667, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N2, the legal moves are to N10, N11, or N3. N10 leads to N0 (terminal), so the player who moves to N10 wins immediately (the opponent has no move). N11 leads to N9 or N13: N9 is terminal, N13 leads to N8 (terminal), so N11 leads to a terminal node, meaning the opponent wins. N3 leads to N10, which then leads to N0 (terminal), so the opponent wins. Thus, moving to N10 is winning since it ends the game immediately. Therefore, the optimal move is to N10.</reason>\n<answer>N10</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N10\n\nDirected graph:\nN11 -> N9, N13, N6\nN12 -> N5\nN6 -> N7, N3, N1\nN0 -> terminal\nN3 -> N10\nN2 -> N10, N11, N3\nN10 -> N0\nN13 -> N8\nN4 -> terminal\nN7 -> terminal\nN1 -> N0, N4, N12\nN5 -> terminal\nN9 -> terminal\nN8 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N0assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N10, the only legal move is to N0, which is a terminal node. The player who moves to N0 wins immediately, as the opponent has no legal move. Thus, moving to N0 ends the game and wins. No other move from N10 is available. Therefore, the optimal move is to N0.</reason>\n<answer>N0</answer>", "episode_score": 0.05, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N10, the only legal move is to N0, which is a terminal node. The player who moves to N0 wins immediately, as the opponent has no legal move. Thus, moving to N0 ends the game and wins. No other move from N10 is available. Therefore, the optimal move is to N0.</reason>\n<answer>N0</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N9\n\nDirected graph:\nN13 -> N0, N12\nN9 -> N13, N11\nN8 -> terminal\nN6 -> N1, N8\nN11 -> N2, N6\nN4 -> terminal\nN2 -> N3, N7\nN5 -> terminal\nN1 -> N10, N0\nN3 -> terminal\nN12 -> N4, N8\nN7 -> N4, N5\nN10 -> terminal\nN0 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N13, N11assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N9, the player can move to N13 or N11. We analyze each option:  \n- Moving to N13 leads to: N13 → N0 (terminal) or N13 → N12.  \n  - N0 → terminal (opponent loses immediately) → good.  \n  - N12 → N4 or N8 (both terminal) → opponent loses. So N13 is winning.  \n- Moving to N11 leads to: N11 → N2 or N6.  \n  - N2 → N3 (terminal) or N7 → N4 or N5 (both terminal) → opponent loses.  \n  - N6 → N1 → N10 or N0 (both terminal) or N8 (terminal). All terminal. So N11 is also winning.  \nBoth N13 and N11 lead to terminal wins for the current player. But we must choose the move that maximizes our outcome. Since both lead to win, we pick one. N13 offers a direct path to N0 or N12, both terminal. N11 routes through more nodes but also leads to terminal. No difference in outcome. However, N13 has a shorter and more direct path. Thus, N13 is optimal.</reason>  \n<answer>N13</answer>", "episode_score": -0.95, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N9, the player can move to N13 or N11. We analyze each option:  \n- Moving to N13 leads to: N13 → N0 (terminal) or N13 → N12.  \n  - N0 → terminal (opponent loses immediately) → good.  \n  - N12 → N4 or N8 (both terminal) → opponent loses. So N13 is winning.  \n- Moving to N11 leads to: N11 → N2 or N6.  \n  - N2 → N3 (terminal) or N7 → N4 or N5 (both terminal) → opponent loses.  \n  - N6 → N1 → N10 or N0 (both terminal) or N8 (terminal). All terminal. So N11 is also winning.  \nBoth N13 and N11 lead to terminal wins for the current player. But we must choose the move that maximizes our outcome. Since both lead to win, we pick one. N13 offers a direct path to N0 or N12, both terminal. N11 routes through more nodes but also leads to terminal. No difference in outcome. However, N13 has a shorter and more direct path. Thus, N13 is optimal.</reason>  \n<answer>N13</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N13\n\nDirected graph:\nN13 -> N0, N12\nN9 -> N13, N11\nN8 -> terminal\nN6 -> N1, N8\nN11 -> N2, N6\nN4 -> terminal\nN2 -> N3, N7\nN5 -> terminal\nN1 -> N10, N0\nN3 -> terminal\nN12 -> N4, N8\nN7 -> N4, N5\nN10 -> terminal\nN0 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N0, N12assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N13, the legal moves are to N0 or N12. N0 leads directly to terminal (win for the player who moves there, as the opponent has no move). N12 leads to N4 (terminal) or N8 (terminal), both of which are terminal. Thus, moving to N0 wins immediately. Moving to N12 results in immediate loss for the opponent. Therefore, choosing N0 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N0</answer>", "episode_score": 1.05, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N13, the legal moves are to N0 or N12. N0 leads directly to terminal (win for the player who moves there, as the opponent has no move). N12 leads to N4 (terminal) or N8 (terminal), both of which are terminal. Thus, moving to N0 wins immediately. Moving to N12 results in immediate loss for the opponent. Therefore, choosing N0 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N0</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N0\n\nDirected graph:\nN6 -> N3\nN5 -> N4\nN1 -> terminal\nN3 -> N7, N8\nN4 -> terminal\nN2 -> terminal\nN7 -> N8, N1, N9\nN8 -> terminal\nN9 -> N2\nN0 -> N5, N3, N6\n\nLegal moves: N5, N3, N6assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N0, the player can move to N5, N3, or N6. We analyze each option to find the one that maximizes the chance of winning.  \n- N5 → N4 → terminal (win for opponent, so losing).  \n- N3 → N7 or N8.  \n  - N7 → N8, N1, N9: N8 → terminal (opponent wins), N1 → terminal (opponent wins), N9 → N2 → terminal (opponent wins). So all moves from N7 lead to opponent win.  \n  - N8 → terminal (opponent wins). So N3 leads to opponent winning.  \n- N6 → N3, which leads to same as above: opponent wins.  \nAll moves from N0 lead to the opponent winning. Thus, no matter which legal move is chosen, the opponent wins. The optimal choice is the one that delays the loss longest or minimizes opponent advantage, but all lead to loss. Since all options result in loss, any move is equivalent. Chose N3 as it is a central node with multiple paths. </reason>  \n<answer>N3</answer>", "episode_score": -0.6166666666666667, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N0, the player can move to N5, N3, or N6. We analyze each option to find the one that maximizes the chance of winning.  \n- N5 → N4 → terminal (win for opponent, so losing).  \n- N3 → N7 or N8.  \n  - N7 → N8, N1, N9: N8 → terminal (opponent wins), N1 → terminal (opponent wins), N9 → N2 → terminal (opponent wins). So all moves from N7 lead to opponent win.  \n  - N8 → terminal (opponent wins). So N3 leads to opponent winning.  \n- N6 → N3, which leads to same as above: opponent wins.  \nAll moves from N0 lead to the opponent winning. Thus, no matter which legal move is chosen, the opponent wins. The optimal choice is the one that delays the loss longest or minimizes opponent advantage, but all lead to loss. Since all options result in loss, any move is equivalent. Chose N3 as it is a central node with multiple paths. </reason>  \n<answer>N3</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N3\n\nDirected graph:\nN6 -> N3\nN5 -> N4\nN1 -> terminal\nN3 -> N7, N8\nN4 -> terminal\nN2 -> terminal\nN7 -> N8, N1, N9\nN8 -> terminal\nN9 -> N2\nN0 -> N5, N3, N6\n\nLegal moves: N7, N8assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N3, the legal moves are to N7 or N8. N8 leads directly to terminal (win for the player who moves there, as the opponent has no move). Thus, moving to N8 wins immediately. Moving to N7 leads to N8, N1, or N9. N8 is terminal, so the opponent wins if they move there. N1 and N9 lead to terminal or N2, which are terminal. Thus, N7 leads to a win for the opponent. Therefore, choosing N8 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N8</answer>", "episode_score": 1.05, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N3, the legal moves are to N7 or N8. N8 leads directly to terminal (win for the player who moves there, as the opponent has no move). Thus, moving to N8 wins immediately. Moving to N7 leads to N8, N1, or N9. N8 is terminal, so the opponent wins if they move there. N1 and N9 lead to terminal or N2, which are terminal. Thus, N7 leads to a win for the opponent. Therefore, choosing N8 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N8</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N4\n\nDirected graph:\nN11 -> N5, N0\nN13 -> N2, N11\nN10 -> N9, N13\nN5 -> terminal\nN6 -> terminal\nN0 -> N6, N12\nN4 -> N10, N3\nN8 -> terminal\nN12 -> N1, N2\nN7 -> terminal\nN2 -> terminal\nN3 -> N8, N7\nN1 -> terminal\nN9 -> N8, N5\n\nLegal moves: N10, N3assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N4, player can move to N10 or N3.  \n- Moving to N10: leads to N9 or N13.  \n  - N9 → N8 or N5 (both terminal → win for opponent)  \n  - N13 → N2 (terminal) or N11 → N5 or N0  \n    - N0 → N6 (terminal) or N12 → N1 or N2 (both terminal)  \n    So N13 leads to terminal states; all paths from N10 end in opponent winning.  \n- Moving to N3:  \n  - N3 → N8 or N7 (both terminal) → immediate win.  \nThus, moving to N3 leads to immediate terminal states for opponent. So N3 is winning. Therefore, choose N3.</reason>  \n<answer>N3</answer>", "episode_score": -0.95, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N4, player can move to N10 or N3.  \n- Moving to N10: leads to N9 or N13.  \n  - N9 → N8 or N5 (both terminal → win for opponent)  \n  - N13 → N2 (terminal) or N11 → N5 or N0  \n    - N0 → N6 (terminal) or N12 → N1 or N2 (both terminal)  \n    So N13 leads to terminal states; all paths from N10 end in opponent winning.  \n- Moving to N3:  \n  - N3 → N8 or N7 (both terminal) → immediate win.  \nThus, moving to N3 leads to immediate terminal states for opponent. So N3 is winning. Therefore, choose N3.</reason>  \n<answer>N3</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N3\n\nDirected graph:\nN11 -> N5, N0\nN13 -> N2, N11\nN10 -> N9, N13\nN5 -> terminal\nN6 -> terminal\nN0 -> N6, N12\nN4 -> N10, N3\nN8 -> terminal\nN12 -> N1, N2\nN7 -> terminal\nN2 -> terminal\nN3 -> N8, N7\nN1 -> terminal\nN9 -> N8, N5\n\nLegal moves: N8, N7assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N3, the legal moves are to N8 or N7. Both N8 and N7 lead to terminal nodes (win for the player who moves there), so the player who moves to either wins immediately. Thus, both moves end the game immediately. Since the player who makes the move wins (the opponent has no legal move), choosing either N8 or N7 is winning. Both are terminal moves, so either is optimal. However, N7 is a direct terminal move, as is N8. Neither gives a follow-up move. Therefore, both are equivalent. But since the question asks to choose one legal destination that maximizes outcome, and both lead to immediate win, either is valid. We choose N7 as it appears earlier in the list.</reason>\n<answer>N7</answer>", "episode_score": 0.05, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N3, the legal moves are to N8 or N7. Both N8 and N7 lead to terminal nodes (win for the player who moves there), so the player who moves to either wins immediately. Thus, both moves end the game immediately. Since the player who makes the move wins (the opponent has no legal move), choosing either N8 or N7 is winning. Both are terminal moves, so either is optimal. However, N7 is a direct terminal move, as is N8. Neither gives a follow-up move. Therefore, both are equivalent. But since the question asks to choose one legal destination that maximizes outcome, and both lead to immediate win, either is valid. We choose N7 as it appears earlier in the list.</reason>\n<answer>N7</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N3\n\nDirected graph:\nN4 -> terminal\nN5 -> N13, N8\nN2 -> terminal\nN0 -> terminal\nN1 -> terminal\nN7 -> N4\nN11 -> N12, N9, N0\nN10 -> terminal\nN12 -> N2\nN9 -> terminal\nN13 -> N10, N6\nN6 -> N8, N1\nN3 -> N7, N5, N11\nN8 -> N9, N1\n\nLegal moves: N7, N5, N11assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N3, the legal moves are to N7, N5, or N11. We analyze each option:  \n- N7 → N4 → terminal: the player to N4 wins immediately.  \n- N5 → N13 → N10 → terminal, or N6 → N8 → N9 (terminal). Either way, opponent has no moves or ends in terminal. N5 leads to a terminal (win).  \n- N11 → N12 → N2 (terminal) or N9 (terminal) or N0 (terminal). All are terminal. So N11 leads to opponent’s win.  \n\nSo, N7 leads to immediate terminal (opponent loses), N5 leads to terminal (opponent loses), N11 leads to opponent win.  \nN7 and N5 both lead to opponent losing. Between them: N5 leads to N13 → N6 → N8 → N9 (all terminal). N7 → N4 → terminal. Both are winning. But since the goal is to maximize outcome, any move leading to opponent's loss is good. Choose one. N5 is safer as it avoids N6 → N8 → N9 (which might be a risk, but all paths end). Both N7 and N5 are winning. Pick N5 as it has more flexibility. However, N7 leads directly to terminal. Both are valid. Yet, N7 leads to N4 terminal (safe). Choose N7 for direct win. But N5 also leads to terminal.  \nSafe and direct: N7 or N5. But N7 → N4 (terminal) is a secure move. So pick N7.</reason>  \n<answer>N7</answer>", "episode_score": -0.6166666666666667, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N3, the legal moves are to N7, N5, or N11. We analyze each option:  \n- N7 → N4 → terminal: the player to N4 wins immediately.  \n- N5 → N13 → N10 → terminal, or N6 → N8 → N9 (terminal). Either way, opponent has no moves or ends in terminal. N5 leads to a terminal (win).  \n- N11 → N12 → N2 (terminal) or N9 (terminal) or N0 (terminal). All are terminal. So N11 leads to opponent’s win.  \n\nSo, N7 leads to immediate terminal (opponent loses), N5 leads to terminal (opponent loses), N11 leads to opponent win.  \nN7 and N5 both lead to opponent losing. Between them: N5 leads to N13 → N6 → N8 → N9 (all terminal). N7 → N4 → terminal. Both are winning. But since the goal is to maximize outcome, any move leading to opponent's loss is good. Choose one. N5 is safer as it avoids N6 → N8 → N9 (which might be a risk, but all paths end). Both N7 and N5 are winning. Pick N5 as it has more flexibility. However, N7 leads directly to terminal. Both are valid. Yet, N7 leads to N4 terminal (safe). Choose N7 for direct win. But N5 also leads to terminal.  \nSafe and direct: N7 or N5. But N7 → N4 (terminal) is a secure move. So pick N7.</reason>  \n<answer>N7</answer>"}]
