win chance.</reason>  \n<answer>N4</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N4\n\nDirected graph:\nN8 -> N7, N4\nN0 -> N6, N9, N2\nN3 -> terminal\nN7 -> N0\nN1 -> terminal\nN5 -> N7, N4, N8\nN9 -> terminal\nN2 -> terminal\nN6 -> N3\nN4 -> N1\n\nLegal moves: N1assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N4, the only legal move is to N1. N1 is a terminal node, so the player who moves to N1 wins immediately (the opponent has no legal move). Thus, moving to N1 is optimal and results in an immediate win. No other move is available from N4. Therefore, the best choice is N1.</reason>\n<answer>N1</answer>", "episode_score": 0.05, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N4, the only legal move is to N1. N1 is a terminal node, so the player who moves to N1 wins immediately (the opponent has no legal move). Thus, moving to N1 is optimal and results in an immediate win. No other move is available from N4. Therefore, the best choice is N1.</reason>\n<answer>N1</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N8\n\nDirected graph:\nN8 -> N5, N2, N0\nN2 -> N4\nN1 -> N7, N3, N9\nN6 -> terminal\nN9 -> terminal\nN7 -> terminal\nN3 -> N6\nN4 -> terminal\nN0 -> N1\nN5 -> N0, N1\n\nLegal moves: N5, N2, N0assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N8, the legal moves are to N5, N2, or N0. We analyze each option to determine which leads to a winning position (i.e., forces the opponent into a losing position).  \n\n- N5: from N5, can move to N0 or N1  \n  - N0 → N1 → (N1 leads to N7, N3, or N9; N7 and N9 are terminal, N3 → N6 is terminal). So N1 is winning for the player who plays next.  N → all paths eventually reach a terminal. So N5 → N0 or N1 leads to opponent wins.  \n- N2 → N4 → terminal → so current player wins (opponent moves last, no moves). So N2 leads to **immediate terminal via N4**, opponent has no move. So **N2 is a winning move.**  \n- N0 → N1 → same as above, leads to N7/N3/N9 or N6 → all terminal, opponent wins.  \n\nThus, N2 is the only move that leads to opponent's loss. Therefore, N2 is optimal.</reason>  \n<answer>N2</answer>", "episode_score": -0.6166666666666667, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N8, the legal moves are to N5, N2, or N0. We analyze each option to determine which leads to a winning position (i.e., forces the opponent into a losing position).  \n\n- N5: from N5, can move to N0 or N1  \n  - N0 → N1 → (N1 leads to N7, N3, or N9; N7 and N9 are terminal, N3 → N6 is terminal). So N1 is winning for the player who plays next.  N → all paths eventually reach a terminal. So N5 → N0 or N1 leads to opponent wins.  \n- N2 → N4 → terminal → so current player wins (opponent moves last, no moves). So N2 leads to **immediate terminal via N4**, opponent has no move. So **N2 is a winning move.**  \n- N0 → N1 → same as above, leads to N7/N3/N9 or N6 → all terminal, opponent wins.  \n\nThus, N2 is the only move that leads to opponent's loss. Therefore, N2 is optimal.</reason>  \n<answer>N2</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N2\n\nDirected graph:\nN8 -> N5, N2, N0\nN2 -> N4\nN1 -> N7, N3, N9\nN6 -> terminal\nN9 -> terminal\nN7 -> terminal\nN3 -> N6\nN4 -> terminal\nN0 -> N1\nN5 -> N0, N1\n\nLegal moves: N4assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N2, the only legal move is to N4. N4 is a terminal node, so the player who moves to N4 wins immediately (the opponent has no move). Thus, moving to N4 is optimal and results in an immediate win. No other move from N2 is available. Therefore, the best choice is N4.</reason>\n<answer>N4</answer>", "episode_score": 0.05, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N2, the only legal move is to N4. N4 is a terminal node, so the player who moves to N4 wins immediately (the opponent has no move). Thus, moving to N4 is optimal and results in an immediate win. No other move from N2 is available. Therefore, the best choice is N4.</reason>\n<answer>N4</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N9\n\nDirected graph:\nN3 -> N5, N7\nN5 -> N0, N2\nN0 -> N2, N1\nN6 -> N8\nN4 -> N3\nN7 -> terminal\nN2 -> terminal\nN9 -> N4, N5, N6\nN8 -> terminal\nN1 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N4, N5, N6assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N9, the player can move to N4, N5, or N6. We analyze each option:  \n- N4 → N3 → (N3 → N5 or N7). N5 leads to N0 or N2 (both terminal), N7 leads to terminal. So N3 leads to terminal. Thus N4 leads to a winning position for the opponent.  \n- N5 → N0 or N2. Both N0 and N2 lead to terminal. So N5 leads to immediate loss (opponent wins).  \n- N6 → N8 (terminal). So N6 leads to immediate loss.  \n\nThus, moving to N4 is the only option that leads to a win (as it forces the opponent into a position where they must move to a terminal node, i.e., they lose). Therefore, N4 is the optimal move.</reason>  \n<answer>N4</answer>", "episode_score": 1.3833333333333333, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N9, the player can move to N4, N5, or N6. We analyze each option:  \n- N4 → N3 → (N3 → N5 or N7). N5 leads to N0 or N2 (both terminal), N7 leads to terminal. So N3 leads to terminal. Thus N4 leads to a winning position for the opponent.  \n- N5 → N0 or N2. Both N0 and N2 lead to terminal. So N5 leads to immediate loss (opponent wins).  \n- N6 → N8 (terminal). So N6 leads to immediate loss.  \n\nThus, moving to N4 is the only option that leads to a win (as it forces the opponent into a position where they must move to a terminal node, i.e., they lose). Therefore, N4 is the optimal move.</reason>  \n<answer>N4</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N4\n\nDirected graph:\nN3 -> N5, N7\nN5 -> N0, N2\nN0 -> N2, N1\nN6 -> N8\nN4 -> N3\nN7 -> terminal\nN2 -> terminal\nN9 -> N4, N5, N6\nN8 -> terminal\nN1 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N3assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N4, the only legal move is to N3. From N3, we can move to N5 or N7. N7 leads directly to terminal (win for the player who moves there, as the opponent has no move). N5 leads to N0 or N2; both N0 and N2 lead to terminal. Thus, moving to N7 wins immediately. Moving to N5 results in the opponent winning. Therefore, the optimal move is to N3 than to N7.</reason>\n<answer>N7</answer>", "episode_score": -1.5, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N4, the only legal move is to N3. From N3, we can move to N5 or N7. N7 leads directly to terminal (win for the player who moves there, as the opponent has no move). N5 leads to N0 or N2; both N0 and N2 lead to terminal. Thus, moving to N7 wins immediately. Moving to N5 results in the opponent winning. Therefore, the optimal move is to N3 than to N7.</reason>\n<answer>N7</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N5\n\nDirected graph:\nN9 -> N1, N8\nN5 -> N4, N9, N0\nN8 -> N6, N2\nN4 -> N8, N0\nN0 -> N3\nN1 -> terminal\nN2 -> terminal\nN6 -> terminal\nN7 -> terminal\nN3 -> N7\n\nLegal moves: N4, N9, N0assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N5, the legal moves are to N4, N9, or N0. We analyze each option to determine which leads to a winning position (i.e., forces the opponent into a terminal node or a losing position).  \n- N5 → N4: From N4, moves are to N8 or N0.  \n  - N4 → N8 leads to N6 (terminal) or N2 (terminal) → opponent wins.  \n  - N4 → N0 leads to N3 → N7 (terminal) → opponent wins. So N4 is a losing move.  \n- N5 → N9: From N9, moves are to N1 or N8.  \n  - N9 → N1 is terminal (opponent wins).  \n  - N9 → N8 leads to N6 or N2 (both terminal). So N9 is a losing move.  \n- N5 → N0: From N0, move to N3 → N7 (terminal). Opponent wins. So N0 is a losing move.  \nAll moves from N5 lead to opponent winning. Thus, every move results in loss. But since we must choose one that maximizes outcome, and all are equivalent in outcome, we pick one. N9 is a valid choice. However, all lead to loss, so any is equal. Choosing N9 is safe and follows the graph. </reason>  \n<answer>N9</answer>", "episode_score": -0.6166666666666667, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N5, the legal moves are to N4, N9, or N0. We analyze each option to determine which leads to a winning position (i.e., forces the opponent into a terminal node or a losing position).  \n- N5 → N4: From N4, moves are to N8 or N0.  \n  - N4 → N8 leads to N6 (terminal) or N2 (terminal) → opponent wins.  \n  - N4 → N0 leads to N3 → N7 (terminal) → opponent wins. So N4 is a losing move.  \n- N5 → N9: From N9, moves are to N1 or N8.  \n  - N9 → N1 is terminal (opponent wins).  \n  - N9 → N8 leads to N6 or N2 (both terminal). So N9 is a losing move.  \n- N5 → N0: From N0, move to N3 → N7 (terminal). Opponent wins. So N0 is a losing move.  \nAll moves from N5 lead to opponent winning. Thus, every move results in loss. But since we must choose one that maximizes outcome, and all are equivalent in outcome, we pick one. N9 is a valid choice. However, all lead to loss, so any is equal. Choosing N9 is safe and follows the graph. </reason>  \n<answer>N9</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N9\n\