[2026-08-13 16:50:54] [agentic_pipeline.py (516)] [INFO] [DRIVER 0 / 1][PID 1538566] [{"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N5\n\nDirected graph:\nN12 -> terminal\nN13 -> N9, N4\nN2 -> terminal\nN11 -> terminal\nN3 -> N2, N7\nN5 -> N3, N1\nN10 -> terminal\nN7 -> N10, N0\nN6 -> terminal\nN8 -> N7, N13\nN0 -> terminal\nN1 -> N8, N3\nN4 -> N11, N6\nN9 -> N4, N12\n\nLegal moves: N3, N1assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N5, player can move to N3 or N1. We analyze each option to determine which leads to a winning position (i.e., forces the opponent into a losing position).  \n\n- Move to N3: From N3, legal moves are N2 (terminal) or N7.  \n  - N2 leads to terminal (opponent loses). So N3 → N2 is a winning move.  \n  - N3 → N7: From N7, moves to N10 (terminal) or N0 (terminal). Both are terminal, so N7 is a losing position.  \n  So N3 is a winning move (player can go to N2 and win immediately).  \n\n- Move to N1: From N1, moves to N8 or N3  \n  - N1 → N3: leads to same as above – player can win via N3 → N2.  \n  - N1 → N8: leads to N7 or N13:  \n    - N7 → terminal options (N10 or N0), both terminal → opponent loses immediately.  \n    - N13 → N9 or N4 → both can lead to N2 or N12/11/6/0, all terminal. So N13 paths end in terminal.  \n  So N1 leads to a winning path too.  \n\nBoth N3 and N1 lead to a win. But since the goal is to choose a move that maximizes outcome, and both are winning, we pick one. N3 leads directly to N2 (immediate terminal for opponent), so it is optimal. </reason>  \n<answer>N3</answer>", "episode_score": -0.95, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N5, player can move to N3 or N1. We analyze each option to determine which leads to a winning position (i.e., forces the opponent into a losing position).  \n\n- Move to N3: From N3, legal moves are N2 (terminal) or N7.  \n  - N2 leads to terminal (opponent loses). So N3 → N2 is a winning move.  \n  - N3 → N7: From N7, moves to N10 (terminal) or N0 (terminal). Both are terminal, so N7 is a losing position.  \n  So N3 is a winning move (player can go to N2 and win immediately).  \n\n- Move to N1: From N1, moves to N8 or N3  \n  - N1 → N3: leads to same as above – player can win via N3 → N2.  \n  - N1 → N8: leads to N7 or N13:  \n    - N7 → terminal options (N10 or N0), both terminal → opponent loses immediately.  \n    - N13 → N9 or N4 → both can lead to N2 or N12/11/6/0, all terminal. So N13 paths end in terminal.  \n  So N1 leads to a winning path too.  \n\nBoth N3 and N1 lead to a win. But since the goal is to choose a move that maximizes outcome, and both are winning, we pick one. N3 leads directly to N2 (immediate terminal for opponent), so it is optimal. </reason>  \n<answer>N3</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N3\n\nDirected graph:\nN12 -> terminal\nN13 -> N9, N4\nN2 -> terminal\nN11 -> terminal\nN3 -> N2, N7\nN5 -> N3, N1\nN10 -> terminal\nN7 -> N10, N0\nN6 -> terminal\nN8 -> N7, N13\nN0 -> terminal\nN1 -> N8, N3\nN4 -> N11, N6\nN9 -> N4, N12\n\nLegal moves: N2, N7assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N3, the legal moves are to N2 or N7. N2 leads directly to terminal (win for the player who moves there, as the opponent has no move). Thus, moving to N2 wins immediately. Moving to N7 leads to N10 or N0, both terminal, so the opponent wins. Therefore, choosing N2 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N2</answer>", "episode_score": 1.05, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N3, the legal moves are to N2 or N7. N2 leads directly to terminal (win for the player who moves there, as the opponent has no move). Thus, moving to N2 wins immediately. Moving to N7 leads to N10 or N0, both terminal, so the opponent wins. Therefore, choosing N2 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N2</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N1\n\nDirected graph:\nN9 -> N4, N7\nN0 -> N9, N5\nN6 -> N0, N8\nN1 -> N9, N6, N2\nN5 -> terminal\nN4 -> terminal\nN2 -> N3\nN8 -> terminal\nN3 -> N5\nN7 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N9, N6, N2assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N1, the legal moves are to N9, N6, or N2. We analyze each option to determine which leads to a winning position (i.e., forces the opponent into a losing position).  \n\n- N9: from N9, moves are to N4 and N7, both terminal. So the player who moves to N9 wins immediately by leaving the opponent with only terminal moves. But from N9, the next player must move to a terminal, so the one who *receives* N9 leads to the opponent having no move. So moving to N9 results in opponent losing immediately. Thus N9 is a winning move.  \n- N6: leads to N0 or N8.  \n  - N8 → terminal: opponent wins immediately.  \n  - N0 → N9, N5. N9 leads to terminal options (win for opponen