o move = current player wins. Thus, N13 → all options are terminal on next reply. So current player **loses** if I move here.  \n\n- Move to N0: N0 → N3 (terminal) or N6 (terminal). Both are terminal. So **opponent has no legal next, I win.**  \n\nHence, moving to N0 wins. Choosing N0 is optimal.</reason>  \n<answer>N0</answer>", "episode_score": -0.95, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N12, the legal moves are to N13 or N0. We analyze each option:  \n- Move to N13: From N13, moves are to N4 or N7.  \n  - N4 → N1 (terminal) or N10 (terminal).  \n  - N7 → N10 (terminal) or N11. N11 → N2 (terminal) or N9 → N8 or N2. N8 → N6 (terminal) or N5 (terminal). All paths eventually terminate; opponent wins if they reach a terminal.  \n  Paths from N7 lead to a win (opponent no legal move).  \n  So N7 is terminal, N4/N1, N4/N10: terminal. Every N4, N7 path leads to opponent no move = current player wins. Thus, N13 → all options are terminal on next reply. So current player **loses** if I move here.  \n\n- Move to N0: N0 → N3 (terminal) or N6 (terminal). Both are terminal. So **opponent has no legal next, I win.**  \n\nHence, moving to N0 wins. Choosing N0 is optimal.</reason>  \n<answer>N0</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N0\n\nDirected graph:\nN0 -> N3, N6\nN8 -> N6, N5\nN13 -> N4, N7\nN12 -> N13, N0\nN1 -> terminal\nN9 -> N8, N2\nN5 -> terminal\nN10 -> terminal\nN4 -> N1, N10\nN2 -> terminal\nN3 -> terminal\nN11 -> N2, N9\nN6 -> terminal\nN7 -> N10, N11\n\nLegal moves: N3, N6assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N0, the legal moves are to N3 or N6. N3 leads to terminal (win for the player who moves there), so the opponent cannot move after. N6 also leads to terminal. Thus, both moves end the game immediately. Since the player who makes the move wins (the opponent has no legal move), choosing either N3 or N6 results in an immediate win. Both are winning moves, but since the goal is to maximize outcome and both lead to immediate win, either is optimal. However, N3 is a direct terminal move, as is N6. Neither gives a follow-up advantage. Therefore, either is valid. We choose one. N3 is simpler and direct.</reason>\n<answer>N3</answer>", "episode_score": 0.05, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N0, the legal moves are to N3 or N6. N3 leads to terminal (win for the player who moves there), so the opponent cannot move after. N6 also leads to terminal. Thus, both moves end the game immediately. Since the player who makes the move wins (the opponent has no legal move), choosing either N3 or N6 results in an immediate win. Both are winning moves, but since the goal is to maximize outcome and both lead to immediate win, either is optimal. However, N3 is a direct terminal move, as is N6. Neither gives a follow-up advantage. Therefore, either is valid. We choose one. N3 is simpler and direct.</reason>\n<answer>N3</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N9\n\nDirected graph:\nN9 -> N6, N1, N0\nN4 -> terminal\nN5 -> terminal\nN1 -> N2\nN6 -> N3, N0, N4\nN7 -> N1\nN8 -> terminal\nN3 -> terminal\nN0 -> N7, N5\nN2 -> N8\n\nLegal moves: N6, N1, N0assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N9, the legal moves are to N6, N1, or N0. We analyze each option to determine which leads to a winning position (i.e., forces the opponent into a losing position).  \n\n- Move to N6: From N6, moves are to N3 (terminal, win for opponent), N0, or N4 (terminal).  \n  - N0 leads to N7 → N1 → N2 → N8 (terminal), so opponent wins.  \n  - N4 terminal: opponent wins immediately.  \n  - N3 terminal: opponent wins.  \n  All paths from N6 lead to opponent winning. So N6 is losing.\n\n- Move to N1: From N1 → N2 → N8 (terminal), opponent wins. So N1 is losing.\n\n- Move to N0: From N0 → N7 → N1 → N2 → N8 (terminal), opponent wins. So N0 is losing.\n\nAll moves lead to opponent winning. Thus, no matter which move is chosen, the opponent wins. But since we must choose one that maximizes outcome, and all are equivalent in outcome, we pick one. N0 is a safe choice as it leads to a terminal path. However, all are losing. The best (i.e., minimizing loss) is any. We choose N0.</reason>  \n<answer>N0</answer>", "episode_score": -0.6166666666666667, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N9, the legal moves are to N6, N1, or N0. We analyze each option to determine which leads to a winning position (i.e., forces the opponent into a losing position).  \n\n- Move to N6: From N6, moves are to N3 (terminal, win for opponent), N0, or N4 (terminal).  \n  - N0 leads to N7 → N1 → N2 → N8 (terminal), so opponent wins.  \n  - N4 terminal: opponent wins immediately.  \n  - N3 terminal: opponent wins.  \n  All paths from N6 lead to opponent winning. So N6 is losing.\n\n- Move to N1: From N1 → N2 → N8 (terminal), opponent wins. So N1 is losing.\n\n- Move to N0: From N0 → N7 → N1 → N2 → N8 (terminal), opponent wins. So N0 is losing.\n\nAll moves lead to opponent winning. Thus, no matter which move is chosen, the opponent wins. But since we must choose one that maximizes outcome, and all are equivalent in outcome, we pick one. N0 is a safe choice as it leads to a terminal path. However, all are losing. The best (i.e., minimizing loss) is any. We choose N0.</reason>  \n<answer>N0</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N0\n\nDirected graph:\nN9 -> N6, N1, N0\nN4 -> terminal\nN5 -> terminal\nN1 -> N2\nN6 -> N3, N0, N4\nN7 -> N1\nN8 -> terminal\nN3 -> terminal\nN0 -> N7, N5\nN2 -> N8\n\nLegal moves: N7, N5assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N0, the legal moves are to N7 or N5. N5 leads to terminal (win for the player who moves there, as the opponent has no move). Thus, moving to N5 wins immediately. Moving to N7 leads to N1, then to N2, then to N8 (terminal), so the opponent wins. Therefore, choosing N5 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N5</answer>", "episode_score": 1.05, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N0, the legal moves are to N7 or N5. N5 leads to terminal (win for the player who moves there, as the opponent has no move). Thus, moving to N5 wins immediately. Moving to N7 leads to N1, then to N2, then to N8 (terminal), so the opponent wins. Therefore, choosing N5 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N5</answer>"}]
