[2026-08-13 16:55:37] [agentic_pipeline.py (516)] [INFO] [DRIVER 0 / 1][PID 1538566] [{"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N8\n\nDirected graph:\nN0 -> N2\nN7 -> N1, N3\nN2 -> terminal\nN1 -> N5, N9\nN4 -> terminal\nN3 -> N9, N6\nN9 -> terminal\nN5 -> N0, N4\nN8 -> N7, N0\nN6 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N7, N0assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N8, the legal moves are to N7 or N0. N0 leads to N2, which is terminal, so the player who moves to N0 wins immediately (the opponent has no move). Thus, moving to N0 is a winning move. Moving to N7 leads to N1 or N3. N1 leads to N5 or N9; N9 is terminal, so the opponent wins if they move there. N5 leads to N0 or N4; N4 is terminal, so the opponent can move to N0 and win. Thus, from N7, the opponent can force a win. Therefore, choosing N0 is optimal and wins the game.</reason>\n<answer>N0</answer>", "episode_score": -0.95, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N8, the legal moves are to N7 or N0. N0 leads to N2, which is terminal, so the player who moves to N0 wins immediately (the opponent has no move). Thus, moving to N0 is a winning move. Moving to N7 leads to N1 or N3. N1 leads to N5 or N9; N9 is terminal, so the opponent wins if they move there. N5 leads to N0 or N4; N4 is terminal, so the opponent can move to N0 and win. Thus, from N7, the opponent can force a win. Therefore, choosing N0 is optimal and wins the game.</reason>\n<answer>N0</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N0\n\nDirected graph:\nN0 -> N2\nN7 -> N1, N3\nN2 -> terminal\nN1 -> N5, N9\nN4 -> terminal\nN3 -> N9, N6\nN9 -> terminal\nN5 -> N0, N4\nN8 -> N7, N0\nN6 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N2assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N0, the only legal move is to N2, which leads directly to terminal (win for the player who moves there). Thus, moving to N2 ends the game immediately, and the opponent has no move. Therefore, choosing N2 is optimal and guarantees a win.</reason>\n<answer>N2</answer>", "episode_score": 0.05, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N0, the only legal move is to N2, which leads directly to terminal (win for the player who moves there). Thus, moving to N2 ends the game immediately, and the opponent has no move. Therefore, choosing N2 is optimal and guarantees a win.</reason>\n<answer>N2</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N2\n\nDirected graph:\nN5 -> terminal\nN4 -> terminal\nN8 -> terminal\nN9 -> terminal\nN6 -> N0\nN1 -> N4\nN3 -> N8, N5, N1\nN2 -> N6, N7, N3\nN0 -> N3, N8\nN7 -> N9\n\nLegal moves: N6, N7, N3assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N2, the legal moves are to N6, N7, or N3. We analyze each option:  \n- N6 → N0 → N3 → (N3 can lead to N8 or N5 or N1, N1 → N4 → terminal; N3→N8 or N5 leads to terminal, so all N3 paths end). So N6 leads to terminal if opponent plays well.  \n- N7 → N9 → terminal immediately: win for the player who moves here.  \n- N3 → leads to N1 (to N4, terminal), N5 (terminal), N8 (terminal). All are terminal. So all paths from N3 lead to opponent winning.  \nThus, N7 leads directly to terminal (opponent has no move), so the current player wins. Choosing N7 is optimal.</reason>  \n<answer>N7</answer>", "episode_score": -0.6166666666666667, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N2, the legal moves are to N6, N7, or N3. We analyze each option:  \n- N6 → N0 → N3 → (N3 can lead to N8 or N5 or N1, N1 → N4 → terminal; N3→N8 or N5 leads to terminal, so all N3 paths end). So N6 leads to terminal if opponent plays well.  \n- N7 → N9 → terminal immediately: win for the player who moves here.  \n- N3 → leads to N1 (to N4, terminal), N5 (terminal), N8 (terminal). All are terminal. So all paths from N3 lead to opponent winning.  \nThus, N7 leads directly to terminal (opponent has no move), so the current player wins. Choosing N7 is optimal.</reason>  \n<answer>N7</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N7\n\nDirected graph:\nN5 -> terminal\nN4 -> terminal\nN8 -> terminal\nN9 -> terminal\nN6 -> N0\nN1 -> N4\nN3 -> N8, N5, N1\nN2 -> N6, N7, N3\nN0 -> N3, N8\nN7 -> N9\n\nLegal moves: N9assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N7, the only legal move is to N9, which is a terminal node. Moving to N9 ends the game immediately, and the opponent has no move. Thus, the current player wins. No other move is available, so choosing N9 is the only option. Since it results in an immediate win, it is the optimal move.</reason>\n<answer>N9</answer>", "episode_score": 0.05, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N7, the only legal move is to N9, which is a terminal node. Moving to N9 ends the game immediately, and the opponent has no move. Thus, the current player wins. No other move is available, so choosing N9 is the only option. Since it results in an immediate win, it is the optimal move.</reason>\n<answer>N9</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N5\n\nDirected graph:\nN4 -> N3\nN2 -> N1, N0\nN0 -> terminal\nN8 -> N4, N2\nN5 -> N4, N8, N7\nN6 -> terminal\nN7 -> N6\nN9 -> terminal\nN1 -> N7, N9\nN3 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N4, N8, N7assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N5, the legal moves are to N4, N8, or N7. We analyze each destination to determine which leads to a winning position (i.e., forces the opponent into a losing position).  \n- N4 → N3: N3 leads to terminal (win for the player who moves there, so opponent loses). So N4 is a winning move.  \n- N8 → N4 or N2:  \n  - N4 → N3 (terminal, opponent wins).  \n  - N2 → N1 or N0:  \n    - N1 → N7 → N6 (terminal); N9 (terminal). Both terminal. So N2 leads to terminal. Thus N8 leads to opponent winning.  \n- N7 → N6 (terminal), so opponent wins immediately.  \nThus, only N4 leads to a terminal win for the current player. Choosing N4 is optimal.</reason>  \n<answer>N4</answer>", "episode_score": -0.6166666666666667, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N5, the legal moves are to N4, N8, or N7. We analyze each destination to determine which leads to a winning position (i.e., forces the opponent into a losing position).  \n- N4 → N3: N3 leads to terminal (win for the player who moves there, so opponent loses). So N4 is a winning move.  \n- N8 → N4 or N2:  \n  - N4 → N3 (terminal, opponent wins).  \n  - N2 → N1 or N0:  \n    - N1 → N7 → N6 (terminal); N9 (terminal). Both terminal. So N2 leads to terminal. Thus N8 leads to opponent winning.  \n- N7 → N6 (terminal), so opponent wins immediately.  \nThus, only N4 leads to a terminal win for the current player. Choosing N4 is optimal.</reason>  \n<answer>N4</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N4\n\nDirected graph:\nN4 -> N3\nN2 -> N1, N0\nN0 -> terminal\nN8 -> N4, N2\nN5 -> N4, N8, N7\nN6 -> terminal\nN7 -> N6\nN9 -> terminal\nN1 -> N7, N9\nN3 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N3assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N4, the only legal move is to N3, which leads to terminal. Thus, the first player (me) moves to N3 and wins immediately. No other move is available from N4. Since N3 is a terminal node, the opposing player has no legal move and loses. Therefore, moving to N3 is optimal and guarantees a win.</reason>\n<answer>N3</answer>", "episode_score": 0.05, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N4, the only legal move is to N3, which leads to terminal. Thus, the first player (me) moves to N3 and wins immediately. No other move is available from N4. Since N3 is a terminal node, the opposing player has no legal move and loses. Therefore, moving to N3 is optimal and guarantees a win.</reason>\n<answer>N3</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N2\n\nDirected graph:\nN0 -> terminal\nN3 -> terminal\nN8 -> N4\nN1 -> terminal\nN4 -> N1, N7\nN7 -> N5\nN9 -> N0, N6\nN5 -> terminal\nN6 -> N3, N0\nN2 -> N7, N8, N9\n\nLegal moves: N7, N8, N9assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N2, the legal moves are to N7, N8, N9. We analyze each option using backward induction:  \n- N7 → N5 → terminal → winning for the opponent, so N7 leads to immediate loss (current player loses).  \n- N8 → N4 → N1 (terminal) or N7 → N5 (terminal). Both lead to terminal. So N8 leads to loss.  \n- N9 → N0 (terminal) or N6. N6 → N3 (terminal) or N0 (terminal). Both lead to terminal. So N9 leads to loss.  \nAll moves from N2 lead to the opponent winning. Thus, every move results in loss. But since we must choose one that maximizes outcome, and all are equivalent in outcome (all lead to loss), we pick any. N7 is the first in the list. However, N9 leads to N6, which can go to N3 or N0, both terminal. All paths end. The safest is to pick N7 as it avoids N4’s branching. But all are losing. Choose N7.</reason>  \n<answer>N7</answer>", "episode_score": -0.6166666666666667, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N2, the legal moves are to N7, N8, N9. We analyze each option using backward induction:  \n- N7 → N5 → terminal → winning for the opponent, so N7 leads to immediate loss (current player loses).  \n- N8 → N4 → N1 (terminal) or N7 → N5 (terminal). Both lead to terminal. So N8 leads to loss.  \n- N9 → N0 (terminal) or N6. N6 → N3 (terminal) or N0 (terminal). Both lead to terminal. So N9 leads to loss.  \nAll moves from N2 lead to the opponent winning. Thus, every move results in loss. But since we must choose one that maximizes outcome, and all are equivalent in outcome (all lead to loss), we pick any. N7 is the first in the list. However, N9 leads to N6, which can go to N3 or N0, both terminal. All paths end. The safest is to pick N7 as it avoids N4’s branching. But all are losing. Choose N7.</reason>  \n<answer>N7</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N7\n\nDirected graph:\nN0 -> terminal\nN3 -> terminal\nN8 -> N4\nN1 -> terminal\nN4 -> N1, N7\nN7 -> N5\nN9 -> N0, N6\nN5 -> terminal\nN6 -> N3, N0\nN2 -> N7, N8, N9\n\nLegal moves: N5assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N7, the only legal move is to N5. N5 leads to terminal, so the player who moves to N5 wins immediately (the opponent has no move). Thus, moving to N5 results in an immediate win. No other move from N7 is available. Therefore, the optimal move is to N5.</reason>\n<answer>N5</answer>", "episode_score": 0.05, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N7, the only legal move is to N5. N5 leads to terminal, so the player who moves to N5 wins immediately (the opponent has no move). Thus, moving to N5 results in an immediate win. No other move from N7 is available. Therefore, the optimal move is to N5.</reason>\n<answer>N5</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N6\n\nDirected graph:\nN7 -> N8, N3\nN5 -> terminal\nN9 -> terminal\nN8 -> N2, N3\nN2 -> terminal\nN0 -> terminal\nN6 -> N7, N1\nN4 -> N9\nN1 -> N5, N7\nN3 -> N4, N0\n\nLegal moves: N7, N1assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N6, the legal moves are to N7 or N1. We analyze each:\n\n- Moving to N7: From N7, moves are to N8 or N3.\n  - N8 → N2 (terminal) or N3\n    - N3 → N4 or N0 (both terminal) → opponent wins.\n  - So N8 leads to terminal moves for the opponent. N3 leads to N4 or N0 (terminal). So all moves from N7 lead to terminal states for the opponent. Thus, player who moves to N7 wins immediately.\n  \n- Moving to N1: From N1, moves are to N5 or N7.\n  - N5 → terminal (opponent wins).\n  - N7 → as above, leads to opponent's loss.\n  - So choosing N1 allows follow-up to N7, which is winning.\n\nThus, moving to N1 gives the player a winning path. N7 leads to opponent winning. Therefore, N1 is better.</reason>\n<answer>N1</answer>", "episode_score": -0.95, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N6, the legal moves are to N7 or N1. We analyze each:\n\n- Moving to N7: From N7, moves are to N8 or N3.\n  - N8 → N2 (terminal) or N3\n    - N3 → N4 or N0 (both terminal) → opponent wins.\n  - So N8 leads to terminal moves for the opponent. N3 leads to N4 or N0 (terminal). So all moves from N7 lead to terminal states for the opponent. Thus, player who moves to N7 wins immediately.\n  \n- Moving to N1: From N1, moves are to N5 or N7.\n  - N5 → terminal (opponent wins).\n  - N7 → as above, leads to opponent's loss.\n  - So choosing N1 allows follow-up to N7, which is winning.\n\nThus, moving to N1 gives the player a winning path. N7 leads to opponent winning. Therefore, N1 is better.</reason>\n<answer>N1</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N1\n\nDirected graph:\nN7 -> N8, N3\nN5 -> terminal\nN9 -> terminal\nN8 -> N2, N3\nN2 -> terminal\nN0 -> terminal\nN6 -> N7, N1\nN4 -> N9\nN1 -> N5, N7\nN3 -> N4, N0\n\nLegal moves: N5, N7assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N1, the legal moves are to N5 or N7. N5 leads directly to terminal (win for the player who moves there, as the opponent has no move). N7 leads to N8 or N3. N8 leads to N2 or N3; N2 is terminal, so moving to N2 ends the game immediately. N3 leads to N4 or N0; N0 is terminal. N4 leads to N9, which is terminal. Thus, from N1, moving to N5 wins immediately. Moving to N7 leads to a position where the opponent can move to a terminal node or force a win. Since N5 is a terminal move for the opponent, choosing N5 wins the game. Therefore, the optimal move is to N5.</reason>\n<answer>N5</answer>", "episode_score": 0.05, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N1, the legal moves are to N5 or N7. N5 leads directly to terminal (win for the player who moves there, as the opponent has no move). N7 leads to N8 or N3. N8 leads to N2 or N3; N2 is terminal, so moving to N2 ends the game immediately. N3 leads to N4 or N0; N0 is terminal. N4 leads to N9, which is terminal. Thus, from N1, moving to N5 wins immediately. Moving to N7 leads to a position where the opponent can move to a terminal node or force a win. Since N5 is a terminal move for the opponent, choosing N5 wins the game. Therefore, the optimal move is to N5.</reason>\n<answer>N5</answer>"}]
