N0 (terminal) or N5 (then to N9, then to N8 or N7, opponent wins).  \n    - So N3 → N4 → N5 leads to opponent winning.  \n  - But N1 → N3 → N0 is terminal, so the opponent has no move and wins.  \nThus, all paths from N6 lead to the opponent winning. However, we must choose a move that maximizes our outcome. Since all moves lead to the opponent winning, the optimal choice is the one that delays the loss longest or avoids immediate terminal. But N1 leads to N3 → N0 (immediate terminal for opponent), so that is a win. Thus, N1 leads to an immediate terminal on opponent's turn. So, N1 is better. Therefore, choose N1.</reason>  \n<answer>N1</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N1\n\nDirected graph:\nN3 -> N0, N4\nN7 -> terminal\nN0 -> terminal\nN4 -> N0, N5\nN1 -> N2, N3\nN6 -> N5, N1\nN5 -> N9\nN2 -> terminal\nN8 -> terminal\nN9 -> N8, N7\n\nLegal moves: N2, N3assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N1, the legal moves are to N2 or N3. N2 leads directly to terminal (win for the player who moves there, as the opponent has no move). N3 leads to N0 or N4. N0 is terminal, so moving to N0 ends the game immediately for the opponent. N4 leads to N0 (terminal) or N5. N5 leads to N9, which leads to N8 (terminal) or N7 (terminal). All paths from N3 eventually lead to terminal. Thus, moving to N2 wins immediately. Choosing N2 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N2</answer>", "episode_score": 1.05, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N1, the legal moves are to N2 or N3. N2 leads directly to terminal (win for the player who moves there, as the opponent has no move). N3 leads to N0 or N4. N0 is terminal, so moving to N0 ends the game immediately for the opponent. N4 leads to N0 (terminal) or N5. N5 leads to N9, which leads to N8 (terminal) or N7 (terminal). All paths from N3 eventually lead to terminal. Thus, moving to N2 wins immediately. Choosing N2 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N2</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N7\n\nDirected graph:\nN8 -> N4, N9\nN7 -> N9, N8, N0\nN9 -> N5\nN4 -> N1\nN5 -> N3\nN2 -> terminal\nN0 -> N6, N2, N8\nN1 -> terminal\nN6 -> terminal\nN3 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N9, N8, N0assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N7, the legal moves are to N9, N8, or N0. We analyze each option to determine which leads to a winning position (i.e., forces the opponent into a losing position).  \n\n- Move to N9: From N9, next move is to N5; from N5 to N3 (terminal), so opponent wins. Thus, N9 leads to immediate loss.  \n- Move to N8: From N8, moves are to N4 and N9.  \n  - N4 → N1 (terminal), opponent wins.  \n  - N9 → N5 → N3 (terminal), opponent wins. So all moves from N8 lead to opponent winning.  \n- Move to N0: From N0, moves are to N6 (terminal), N2 (terminal), or N8.  \n  - N6, N2 are terminal: opponent wins immediately.  \n  - N8 leads to opponent's loss as above. So choosing N0 → N8 is a winning move.  \n\nThus, moving to N0 and then forcing the opponent into N8 (which leads to a win) is optimal. Therefore, the best first move is to N0.</reason>\n<answer>N0</answer>", "episode_score": -0.6166666666666667, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N7, the legal moves are to N9, N8, or N0. We analyze each option to determine which leads to a winning position (i.e., forces the opponent into a losing position).  \n\n- Move to N9: From N9, next move is to N5; from N5 to N3 (terminal), so opponent wins. Thus, N9 leads to immediate loss.  \n- Move to N8: From N8, moves are to N4 and N9.  \n  - N4 → N1 (terminal), opponent wins.  \n  - N9 → N5 → N3 (terminal), opponent wins. So all moves from N8 lead to opponent winning.  \n- Move to N0: From N0, moves are to N6 (terminal), N2 (terminal), or N8.  \n  - N6, N2 are terminal: opponent wins immediately.  \n  - N8 leads to opponent's loss as above. So choosing N0 → N8 is a winning move.  \n\nThus, moving to N0 and then forcing the opponent into N8 (which leads to a win) is optimal. Therefore, the best first move is to N0.</reason>\n<answer>N0</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N0\n\nDirected graph:\nN8 -> N4, N9\nN7 -> N9, N8, N0\nN9 -> N5\nN4 -> N1\nN5 -> N3\nN2 -> terminal\nN0 -> N6, N2, N8\nN1 -> terminal\nN6 -> terminal\nN3 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N6, N2, N8assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N0, the legal moves are to N6, N2, or N8. N2 and N6 lead directly to terminal nodes, so the player who moves to them wins immediately (the opponent has no move). N8 leads to N4 or N9. N4 leads to N1 (terminal), and N9 leads to N5, which leads to N3 (terminal). Thus, moving to N8 results in the opponent being forced into a terminal node. Therefore, moving to N2 or N6 wins immediately. These are bett