s to N13 or N7 (both terminal), so no advantage. All options end in opponent win. Thus, any move is equal. Picking N4 is safe and accessible. Final choice: N4.</reason>  \n<answer>N4</answer>", "episode_score": -0.6166666666666667, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N10, the player can move to N4, N5, or N12. We analyze each:  \n- N4 → N11, N9; N11 is terminal (win for opponent), N9 → N13, N7; N13 → N6 (terminal), N7 terminal. So N9 leads to terminal. Thus N4 leads to terminal nodes.  \n- N5 → N4, N3, N1, N13; all these lead to terminal nodes. So N5 loses.  \n- N12 → N8, N0; N8 → N2 (terminal), N0 → N11 (terminal). Both terminal. So N12 leads to terminal.  \nAll moves lead to terminal immediately or to paths ending in terminal. Thus all are losing. But we must choose one that maximizes outcome. Since all lead to opponent winning, any move is equivalent. However, N4 opens to N9 which leads to N13 or N7 (both terminal), so no advantage. All options end in opponent win. Thus, any move is equal. Picking N4 is safe and accessible. Final choice: N4.</reason>  \n<answer>N4</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N4\n\nDirected graph:\nN5 -> N4, N3, N13, N1\nN3 -> terminal\nN2 -> terminal\nN1 -> terminal\nN4 -> N11, N9\nN7 -> terminal\nN8 -> N2\nN11 -> terminal\nN13 -> N6\nN12 -> N8, N0\nN9 -> N13, N7\nN6 -> terminal\nN10 -> N4, N5, N12\nN0 -> N11\n\nLegal moves: N11, N9assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N4, the legal moves are to N11 or N9. N11 leads directly to terminal (win for the player who moves there, as the opponent has no move). N9 leads to N13 or N7; N13 leads to N6 (terminal), N7 is terminal. Thus, moving to N9 allows the opponent to move to a terminal node immediately, so they win. Therefore, choosing N11 wins immediately. The optimal move is to N11.</reason>\n<answer>N11</answer>", "episode_score": 1.05, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N4, the legal moves are to N11 or N9. N11 leads directly to terminal (win for the player who moves there, as the opponent has no move). N9 leads to N13 or N7; N13 leads to N6 (terminal), N7 is terminal. Thus, moving to N9 allows the opponent to move to a terminal node immediately, so they win. Therefore, choosing N11 wins immediately. The optimal move is to N11.</reason>\n<answer>N11</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N7\n\nDirected graph:\nN1 -> terminal\nN7 -> N2, N4, N8\nN8 -> N5\nN0 -> N3, N4\nN3 -> N1\nN5 -> terminal\nN9 -> terminal\nN6 -> terminal\nN2 -> N6, N9, N0\nN4 -> N3\n\nLegal moves: N2, N4, N8assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N7, the legal moves are to N2, N4, or N8. We analyze each option:  \n- Moving to N4: N4 → N3 → N1 (terminal), so the opponent wins immediately.  \n- Moving to N8: N8 → N5 → terminal, so the opponent wins immediately.  \n- Moving to N2: N2 has moves to N6, N9, N0. All of N6, N9, N0 lead to terminal nodes. Thus, from N2, the opponent wins immediately.  \nAll moves lead to the opponent winning. However, since the player who cannot move loses, each move transfers the token to a position where the opponent can immediately move to terminal and win. Thus, every move results in an immediate loss for the current player. But since we must choose a move that maximizes outcome, and all lead to loss, the optimal choice is the one that delays the loss longest or minimizes opponent advantage. N2 leads to N0, which feeds to N3 → N1, but all paths end in terminal. No move avoids loss. Therefore, all are equivalent. Picking any is equivalent. We choose N2 as it has more options, but all lead to loss. However, since the player after us loses, initiating a move that leads to terminal on next move is bad. All paths end immediately. Thus, no gain. But must pick one. N4 leads to N3 → N1; N8 to N5; N2 to N0 → N3 → N1. All end same. Choose N2.</reason>  \n<answer>N2</answer>", "episode_score": -0.6166666666666667, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N7, the legal moves are to N2, N4, or N8. We analyze each option:  \n- Moving to N4: N4 → N3 → N1 (terminal), so the opponent wins immediately.  \n- Moving to N8: N8 → N5 → terminal, so the opponent wins immediately.  \n- Moving to N2: N2 has moves to N6, N9, N0. All of N6, N9, N0 lead to terminal nodes. Thus, from N2, the opponent wins immediately.  \nAll moves lead to the opponent winning. However, since the player who cannot move loses, each move transfers the token to a position where the opponent can immediately move to terminal and win. Thus, every move results in an immediate loss for the current player. But since we must choose a move that maximizes outcome, and all lead to loss, the optimal choice is the one that delays the loss longest or minimizes opponent advantage. N2 leads to N0, which feeds to N3 → N1, but all paths end in terminal. No move avoids loss. Therefore, all are equivalent. Picking any is equivalent. We choose N2 as it has more options, but all lead to loss. However, since the player after us loses, initiating a move that leads to terminal on next move is bad. All paths end immediately. Thus, no gain. But must pick one. N4 leads to N3 → N1; N8 to N5; N2 to N0 → N3 → N1. All end same. Choose N2.</reason>  \n<answer>N2</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N2\n\nDirected graph:\nN1 -> terminal\nN7 -> N2, N4, N8\nN8 -> N5\nN0 -> N3, N4\nN3 -> N1\nN5 -> terminal\nN9 -> terminal\nN6 -> terminal\nN2 -> N6, N9, N0\nN4 -> N3\n\nLegal moves: N6, N9, N0assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N2, the legal moves are to N6, N9, or N0. N6 and N9 lead directly to terminal nodes, so the player who moves to them wins immediately (the opponent has no move). N0 leads to N3, and N3 leads to N1, which leads to terminal. Thus, moving to N0 results in the opponent winning. Therefore, choosing N6 or N9 is optimal. Since N6 and N9 both end the game immediately, either is winning. Choosing N9 is safe and direct. Thus, the best move is N9.</reason>\n<answer>N9</answer>", "episode_score": 0.7166666666666668, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N2, the legal moves are to N6, N9, or N0. N6 and N9 lead directly to terminal nodes, so the player who moves to them wins immediately (the opponent has no move). N0 leads to N3, and N3 leads to N1, which leads to terminal. Thus, moving to N0 results in the opponent winning. Therefore, choosing N6 or N9 is optimal. Since N6 and N9 both end the game immediately, either is winning. Choosing N9 is safe and direct. Thus, the best move is N9.</reason>\n<answer>N9</answer>"}]
