terminal)\n  So all paths from N6 lead to terminal nodes (opponent loses immediately)\n\nBoth N3 and N6 lead to opponent losing. But we must choose the one that maximizes our outcome. Since both result in win, we pick one. N3 leads to more branching but both end in terminal. Either is winning. However, N6 has a direct move to N0 (immediate terminal for opponent). Thus, N6 is safer and quicker. But since both are winning, and no difference in outcome, we pick the first optimal. But N3 leads to N9 → N11 → N5 → N4 (all terminal). So all paths end opponent’s move loss. Both are equivalent. But N6 has a direct terminal. Choose N6.</reason>\n<answer>N6</answer>", "episode_score": -0.95, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N1, the legal moves are to N3 or N6. We analyze each:\n\n- Moving to N3: From N3, moves are to N12 or N9.\n  - N12 → N7 (terminal, opponent wins) or N10 (terminal, opponent wins)\n  - N9 → N10 (terminal) or N11\n    - N11 → N5 or N8 (N8 terminal, N5 → N4 or N13 both terminal)\n  So all paths from N3 lead to terminal nodes (opponent loses immediately). So player wins.\n\n- Moving to N6: From N6 → N2 or N0\n  - N0 → terminal (opponent loses immediately)\n  - N2 → N0 or N13 (both terminal)\n  So all paths from N6 lead to terminal nodes (opponent loses immediately)\n\nBoth N3 and N6 lead to opponent losing. But we must choose the one that maximizes our outcome. Since both result in win, we pick one. N3 leads to more branching but both end in terminal. Either is winning. However, N6 has a direct move to N0 (immediate terminal for opponent). Thus, N6 is safer and quicker. But since both are winning, and no difference in outcome, we pick the first optimal. But N3 leads to N9 → N11 → N5 → N4 (all terminal). So all paths end opponent’s move loss. Both are equivalent. But N6 has a direct terminal. Choose N6.</reason>\n<answer>N6</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along