 end. So N4 is better. Thus, choose N4.</reason>\n<answer>N4</answer>", "episode_score": -0.95, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N3, the player can move to N2 or N4.  \n- Moving to N2: N2 → N9 → N0 or N6. N0 → N7 → N5 → terminal or N7 → N8 → terminal. N6 → terminal. So from N2, the opponent will win if the chain leads to a terminal.  \n  N2 → N9 → N0 → N7 → N5 (terminal) or N7→ N8 (terminal).  All are terminal.  So N2 leads to immediate win for opponent.  \n- Moving to N4: N4 → N2 or N1. N1 is terminal. N2 → N9 → N0→  N7→  N5/N8 (terminal). All paths lead to terminal. So N4 also leads to opponent winning.  \nBoth moves lead to opponent winning. But we must choose one that maximizes our outcome. Since both lead to opponent losing (i.e., we win), either is valid. However, N4 leads to N1 (terminal) immediately, ending game now. So if N4 → N1, we win immediately. Thus N4 has a winning move. N2 leads to longer game but all paths end. So N4 is better. Thus, choose N4.</reason>\n<answer>N4</answer>"}]
