wer>N1</answer>", "episode_score": -0.6166666666666667, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N11, the legal moves are to N12, N5, or N1. We analyze each:\n\n- N5 → N0 (terminal): player who moves to N0 loses, so the opponent wins. So moving to N5 leads to immediate loss.\n\n- N12 → N3, N4:\n  - N3 → N6 (terminal) or N8 → N5, N₂\n  - N4 → N13 (terminal) or N9 (terminal)\n  So from N12, paths lead to terminal nodes; so opponent wins immediately. So moving to N12 leads to opponent winning.\n\n- N1 → N7 (terminal), N13 (terminal), N10 → N9 (terminal). All moves from N1 lead to terminal. So moving to N1 leads to opponent winning.\n\nAll moves from N11 lead to opponent winning. Thus, no matter which move is chosen, the opponent wins. But since we must choose one that maximizes our outcome, and all result in loss, the optimal choice is the one that delays the loss longest or minimizes opponent advantage. N1 leads to immediate terminal moves, same as others. However, N12 branches to N3 → N8 → N5 (to N0), which is terminal. But N3 → N8 → N2 (terminal) also. All paths end. Thus, all are equivalent. Choose N1 as it is a direct move with no benefit. But safest is to avoid giving opponent an edge. All are terminal. So any is equal. Pick N1.</reason>\n<answer>N1</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N1\n\nDirected graph:\nN4 -> N13, N9\nN13 -> terminal\nN3 -> N6, N8\nN1 -> N7, N13, N10\nN10 -> N9\nN8 -> N5, N2\nN0 -> terminal\nN12 -> N3, N4\nN9 -> terminal\nN6 -> terminal\nN2 -> terminal\nN11 -> N12, N5, N1\nN5 -> N0\nN7 -> terminal\n\n